VALUES (
  'social-science-2-notes',
  'social-science',
  'social-science-2',
  'Nationalism in India',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Nationalism in India","subject":"social-science","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"Chapter ID: sst-02 | Chapter 2: Nationalism in India","content":""},{"heading":"Academic Session: 2026–27 | Target Board Examination: Secondary Examination 2027","content":""},{"heading":"Governing Body: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER / RBSE), Ajmer","content":""},{"heading":"Subject: Social Science (Subject Code: 08 / S-08) | Discipline: History (India and the Contemporary World – Part 2)","content":"---"},{"heading":"SECTION 1: CHAPTER METADATA & OFFICIAL EXAMINATION SCHEME","content":"* **Chapter Working ID**: `sst-02`\n* **Subject ID**: `sst`\n* **Subject Code**: 08 (Social Science / Social Science)\n* **Discipline**: History (*India and the Contemporary World – Part 2*)\n* **Chapter Title (English)**: Nationalism in India\n* **Chapter Title (Hindi)**: Nationalism in India\n* **Prescribed Textbook**: *India and the Contemporary World – Part 2* (NCERT Textbook, copyright adopted and published by Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan, Ajmer)\n* **Official Chapter Marks Allocation**: **04 Marks** (out of total 80 Theory Marks)\n* **Examination Weightage Split**:\n  * **Theory Examination**: 80 Marks\n  * **Sessional / Internal Assessment**: 20 Marks\n  * **Total Subject Evaluation**: 100 Marks\n  * **Exam Duration**: 3 Hours 15 Minutes (195 Minutes)\n* **Target Exam**: BSER Secondary Board Examination 2027 (Academic Session 2026–27)"},{"heading":"Official Examination Question Paper Blueprint Position (BSER Ajmer):","content":"1. **Objective / MCQ / VSA**: 1 Item (1 Mark) – Focus on Dates, Satyagraha locations, or Key Publications/Leaders.\n2. **Essay / Long Answer Question (Section D)**: 1 Question with Internal Choice (3 or 4 Marks) OR 1 Short Answer (2 Marks) + 1 VSA (1 Mark) + 1 MCQ (1 Mark).\n   * *Note*: In BSER 2024, 2023, and 2022 Main Papers, Chapter 2 (*Nationalism in India*) consistently supplied the **Section D 4-Mark Essay Question** (with an internal ''OR'' option), along with map work items in Question 22/28.\n3. **Map Work Allocation**: Map identification items from Chapter 2 (*Indian National Movement Centers*) form a mandatory core of the 4-Mark India Outline Map Question (Question 22/28).\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: OFFICIAL SYLLABUS & PRESCRIPTION VERIFICATION REPORT","content":""},{"heading":"1. Prescription Status:","content":"* **Status**: **100% Fully Prescribed** for the 2026–27 Academic Session & 2027 Examination.\n* **Verification Sources**:\n  1. BSER Official Syllabus Document 2026–27 (`10_2027.pdf`), Page 14.\n  2. NCERT Rationalised Content Inventory for Class X History.\n  3. BSER Class 10 Model Question Paper Blueprint 2026–27 (`Social Science - 08`)."},{"heading":"2. Rationalization & Exclusions Check:","content":"* **Rationalized Topics**: **NONE**. Unlike Chapter 3 (*The Making of a Global World*), where post-19th-century sections were rationalized, Chapter 2 (*Nationalism in India*) is retained in its **entirety**.\n* **Sub-Topic Scope Verification**: All five major sub-sections prescribed in the NCERT/BSER curriculum are active and examinable:\n  1. *The First World War, Khilafat and Non-Cooperation*\n  2. *Differing Strands within the Movement*\n  3. *Towards Civil Disobedience*\n  4. *The Sense of Collective Belonging*\n  5. *Quit India Movement* (Included as a specific thematic appendix/sub-topic in BSER syllabus)\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 3: COMPREHENSIVE SYLLABUS-ALIGNED REVISION NOTES","content":"```\n                                  NATIONALISM IN INDIA (1914–1942)\n                                                 │\n      ┌───────────────────────────┬──────────────┴─────────────┬───────────────────────────┐\n      ▼                           ▼                            ▼                           ▼\n[First World War &        [Differing Strands in        [Towards Civil               [Collective Belonging\n Early Satyagrahas]        Non-Cooperation Movement]   Disobedience Movement]        & Cultural Icons]\n 1914–1919                 1920–1922                    1928–1934                    1920s–1942\n • Economic Distress       • Towns: Boycott & Limits    • Simon Commission (1928)    • Abanindranath''Bharat Mata\\n • Champaran (1917) • Countryside: Baba • Lahore Congress (1929) • Rabindranath & Natesa Sastri\\n • Kheda (1917) Ramchandra (Awadh) (Purna Swaraj) • Tricolor Flag (1921)\\n • Ahmedabad (1918) • Tribal: Alluri Sitarama • Salt March (1930) • Reinterpretation of History\\n • Rowlatt Act (1919) Raju (Gudem Hills) • Dandi (6 April 1930) • Quit India Movement (1942)\\n • Jallianwala Bagh • Plantations: Assam Inland • Round Table Conf. /\\n • Khilafat Movement Emigration Act (1859) Poona Pact (1932)\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### SUB-TOPIC 1: THE FIRST WORLD WAR, KHILAFAT, AND NON-COOPERATION\\n\\n#### 1. Impact of the First World War (1914–1918) on Indian Society & Economy:\\nThe First World War created a completely new economic and political situation in India:\\n* **Defense Expenditure & Taxation**: The British government increased defense expenditure, financed by war loans and increased taxes. Custom duties were raised and income tax was introduced for the first time.\\n* **Price Rise & Inflation**: Prices doubled between 1913 and 1918, leading to extreme hardship for the common people.\\n* **Forced Recruitment**: Rural areas were forced to supply soldiers for the British imperial army, causing widespread anger in villages.\\n* **Crop Failures & Influenza Epidemic (1918–1921)**: Crop failures in 1918–19 and 1920–21 led to acute food shortages, accompanied by an influenza epidemic. According to the 1921 Census, 12 to 13 million (120–130 lakh) people perished due to famines and epidemic.\\n\\n#### 2. The Idea of Satyagraha:\\n* **Concept**: Satyagraha emphasized the **power of truth** and the **need to search for truth**. It suggested that if the cause was true and the struggle was against injustice, physical force was not necessary to fight the oppressor.\\n* **Methodology**: A Satyagrahi could win the battle through non-violence (*Ahimsa*) by appealing to the conscience of the oppressor rather than using coercion.\\n* **Mahatma Gandhi''s Return**: Gandhiji returned to India from South Africa in **January 1915**, where he had successfully fought racist regime with satyagraha."},{"heading":"3. Early Local Satyagrahas led by Gandhiji (1917–1918):","content":"1. **Champaran (Bihar, 1917)**: Inspired peasants to struggle against the oppressive indigo plantation system (*Tinkathia system*).\n2. **Kheda (Gujarat, 1917)**: Supported peasants affected by crop failure and a plague epidemic who could not pay land revenue and demanded revenue relaxation.\n3. **Ahmedabad (Gujarat, 1918)**: Organized a satyagraha movement among cotton mill workers demanding a 35% wage bonus."},{"heading":"4. The Rowlatt Act (1919) & Jallianwala Bagh Massacre:","content":"* **The Rowlatt Act (passed March 1919)**: Hurriedly passed through the Imperial Legislative Council despite united opposition from Indian members. It gave the British government enormous powers to repress political activities and allowed detention of political prisoners **without trial for up to two years**.\n* **Rowlatt Satyagraha**: Gandhiji called for a nationwide satyagraha starting with a *hartal* on **6 April 1919**.\n* **Jallianwala Bagh Incident (13 April 1919)**:\n  * Location: Amritsar, Punjab.\n  * Context: A large crowd gathered in the enclosed ground of Jallianwala Bagh to protest against the arrest of leaders (Dr. Saifuddin Kitchlew and Dr. Satyapal) and to attend the annual Baisakhi fair. Many villagers were unaware of martial law imposed by **General Dyer**.\n  * Execution: Dyer blocked the only exit points and opened fire on the unarmed crowd, killing hundreds. Dyer declared his objective was to *\"produce a moral effect\"* and strike terror in the minds of satyagrahis.\n  * Aftermath: News spread, leading to strikes and clashes. Seeing violence spread, Gandhiji called off the Rowlatt Satyagraha."},{"heading":"5. Khilafat Movement & Alliance with Non-Cooperation:","content":"* **Background**: Ottoman Turkey was defeated in the First World War. Rumors spread that a harsh peace treaty was going to be imposed on the Ottoman Emperor—the **Khalifa** (the spiritual head of the Islamic world).\n* **Khilafat Committee**: Formed in **Bombay in March 1919** by young Muslim leaders **Muhammad Ali** and **Shaukat Ali** (Ali Brothers).\n* **Calcutta Congress Session (September 1920)**: Gandhiji convinced Congress leaders to support Khilafat and launch Non-Cooperation for Swaraj.\n* **Nagpur Congress Session (December 1920)**: A compromise was worked out and the Non-Cooperation Programme was formally adopted."},{"heading":"6. Why Non-Cooperation? (Hind Swaraj, 1909):","content":"In his famous book *Hind Swaraj* (1909), Mahatma Gandhi declared that British rule was established in India with the cooperation of Indians, and had survived only because of this cooperation. If Indians refused to cooperate, British rule in India would collapse within a year, and Swaraj would come.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SUB-TOPIC 2: DIFFERING STRANDS WITHIN THE NON-COOPERATION MOVEMENT","content":"The Non-Cooperation–Khilafat Movement began in **January 1921**. Various social groups participated, each with its own specific aspiration and interpretation of *Swaraj*."},{"heading":"1. Movement in Towns & Cities:","content":"* **Participation**: Thousands of students left government-controlled schools and colleges; headmasters and teachers resigned; lawyers (like Motilal Nehru, C.R. Das) gave up legal practice.\n* **Boycott of Foreign Goods**: Foreign cloth was burnt in huge bonfires, liquor shops were picketed, and Council elections were boycotted (except in Madras by the **Justice Party**, representing non-Brahmans).\n* **Economic Impact**: Imports of foreign cloth fell by half between 1921 and 1922 (value dropped from Rs 102 crore to Rs 57 crore).\n* **Slowdown Reasons**:\n  1. **Khadi cloth was more expensive** than mass-produced mill cloth; poor people could not afford it for long.\n  2. **Lack of alternative Indian institutions**: Students and teachers began trickling back to government schools, and lawyers rejoined government courts."},{"heading":"2. Rebellion in the Countryside (Rural Areas):","content":"* **Awadh Peasant Movement**:\n  * **Leader**: **Baba Ramchandra** (a sanyasi who had earlier been to Fiji as an indentured laborer).\n  * **Targets**: *Talukdars* and landlords who demanded excessively high rents and cesses, and forced peasants into *Begar* (unpaid forced labor).\n  * **Demands**: Reduction of revenue, abolition of *begar*, and social boycott of oppressive landlords (*Nai-Dhobi bandhs*).\n  * **Oudh Kisan Sabha**: Set up in **October 1920** by Jawaharlal Nehru, Baba Ramchandra, and others. Within a month, over 300 branches were set up.\n  * **Deviation**: As the movement spread in 1921, houses of talukdars were attacked, bazaars looted, and grain hoards taken over.\n\n* **Tribal Movement in Gudem Hills (Andhra Pradesh)**:\n  * **Cause**: British government closed large forest areas, preventing tribals from entering forests to graze cattle or collect fuel wood and fruits, and forced them to contribute *begar* for road building.\n  * **Leader**: **Alluri Sitarama Raju**:\n    * Claimed special powers (astrological predictions, healing abilities, bulletproof body).\n    * Inspired by Gandhiji and persuaded people to wear Khadi and give up drinking.\n    * **Difference from Gandhiji**: Raju asserted that India could be liberated only by the use of **force**, not non-violence.\n    * Led guerrilla warfare against police stations; Raju was captured and executed in **1924**."},{"heading":"3. Swaraj in the Plantations (Assam):","content":"* **Inland Emigration Act of 1859**: Plantation workers in Assam were not permitted to leave tea gardens without permission, which was rarely given.\n* **Workers'' Concept of Swaraj**: For plantation workers, Swaraj meant the right to move freely in and out of confined spaces and retaining a link with their native villages.\n* **Action**: Thousands of workers defied authorities, left plantations, and headed home believing *Gandhi Raj* was coming. They were stranded by a railway/steamer strike and caught by police and beaten up.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SUB-TOPIC 3: TOWARDS CIVIL DISOBEDIENCE MOVEMENT","content":""},{"heading":"1. Withdrawal of Non-Cooperation Movement (February 1922):","content":"* **Chauri Chaura Incident**: On **5 February 1922**, at Chauri Chaura (Gorakhpur district, Uttar Pradesh), a peaceful demonstration in a bazaar turned into a violent clash with police, in which peasants set fire to a police station, killing 22 policemen.\n* **Decision**: Gandhiji immediately called off the Non-Cooperation Movement, feeling satyagrahis needed to be properly trained before mass struggles."},{"heading":"2. Internal Debates in Congress:","content":"* **Swaraj Party (1923)**: Formed by **C.R. Das** and **Motilal Nehru** within Congress to participate in council elections and oppose British policies from within councils.\n* **Young Radical Leaders**: **Jawaharlal Nehru** and **Subhas Chandra Bose** pressed for more radical mass agitation and full independence."},{"heading":"3. Two Factors Shaping Late 1920s Politics:","content":"1. **Economic Depression**: Agricultural prices began to fall from 1926 and collapsed after 1930, creating severe distress in rural India.\n2. **Simon Commission (1928)**:\n  * Set up by the British Statutory Commission under Sir John Simon to review the constitutional system.\n  * **Key Flaw**: It did **not have a single Indian member**; all members were British.\n  * **Protest**: When it arrived in India in 1928, it was greeted with slogans: **\"Go Back Simon\"**. Lala Lajpat Rai was police-assaulted during a demonstration in Lahore and later succumbed to injuries."},{"heading":"4. Demand for Purna Swaraj (1929):","content":"* **Lahore Congress Session (December 1929)**: Presided over by **Jawaharlal Nehru**.\n* **Resolution**: Passed the demand for **Purna Swaraj** (Full Independence).\n* **Declaration**: **26 January 1930** was declared as Independence Day, when people were to take a pledge to struggle for complete independence."},{"heading":"5. The Salt March (Dandi March) & Civil Disobedience Movement (1930):","content":"* **Why Salt?**: Salt was consumed by rich and poor alike. The tax on salt and government monopoly over its production revealed the most oppressive face of British rule.\n* **Ultimatum**: On 31 January 1930, Gandhiji sent a letter to Viceroy Irwin stating **11 demands**. Irwin was unwilling to negotiate.\n* **Dandi March Details**:\n  * **Start Date**: 12 March 1930.\n  * **Route**: From Sabarmati Ashram to the coastal town of **Dandi** (Gujarat).\n  * **Distance**: 240 miles, covered in 24 days (10 miles a day) with 78 trusted volunteers.\n  * **Reaching Dandi**: **6 April 1930**, Gandhiji manufactured salt by boiling sea water, breaking the salt law. This marked the official launch of the **Civil Disobedience Movement**."},{"heading":"6. Difference between Non-Cooperation and Civil Disobedience:","content":"* **Non-Cooperation (1920–22)**: People were asked only **not to cooperate** with the British government.\n* **Civil Disobedience (1930–34)**: People were asked not only to refuse cooperation but also to **break colonial laws** (e.g., salt law, forest laws, non-payment of land revenue/chaukidari tax)."},{"heading":"7. Gandhi-Irwin Pact (5 March 1931) & Round Table Conference:","content":"* **Pact Terms**: Gandhiji called off the movement and agreed to attend the **Second Round Table Conference** in London; Viceroy Irwin agreed to release political prisoners.\n* **Outcome**: Negotiations in London broke down; Gandhiji returned disappointed. He restarted Civil Disobedience, but by 1934 it lost its momentum.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SUB-TOPIC 4: PARTICIPATION OF SOCIAL GROUPS & LIMITS OF CIVIL DISOBEDIENCE","content":""},{"heading":"1. How Different Social Groups Saw the Movement:","content":"| Social Group | Prominent Figures / Organizations | Primary Grievance / Motivation | Concept of Swaraj |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Rich Peasants** | Patidars of Gujarat, Jats of Uttar Pradesh | High land revenue rates during trade depression & falling prices | Struggle against high land revenue |\n| **Poor Peasants** | Small tenants & agricultural laborers | Unpayable rent to landlords as cash income dwindled | \"No-Rent\" campaign (remission of rent) |\n| **Business Class** | Purshottamdas Thakurdas, G.D. Birla; FICCI (1927), IICC (1920) | Protection against imports of foreign goods & sterling-rupee exchange ratio | Freedom for business without imperial restrictions |\n| **Industrial Working Class** | Railway workers (1930), Dockworkers (1932), Chotanagpur tin miners | Low wages and poor working conditions | High wages and better working conditions (Limited participation except in Nagpur) |\n| **Women** | Thousands of urban & rural women | Inspired by Gandhiji''s call | Service to the nation as a sacred duty (participated in marches, salt-making, picketing) |"},{"heading":"2. Limits of Civil Disobedience:","content":"1. **Untouchables / Dalits**:\n   * For long, Congress ignored Dalits for fear of offending conservative high-caste Hindus (*Sanatanis*).\n   * Gandhiji called them **Harijans** (\"Children of God\"), organized satyagraha for temple entry, and cleaned toilets himself.\n   * **Dr. B.R. Ambedkar**: Formed the **Depressed Classes Association (1930)** and demanded separate electorates for Dalits at the Second Round Table Conference.\n   * **Poona Pact (September 1932)**: Signed between Gandhiji and Dr. B.R. Ambedkar. Abandoned separate electorates but gave reserved seats to Depressed Classes in provincial and central legislative councils, to be voted in general electorates.\n2. **Muslim Organizations**:\n   * After the decline of Non-Cooperation–Khilafat, a large section of Muslims felt alienated from Congress, which was seen as visibly linked to Hindu religious nationalist groups like the **Hindu Mahasabha**.\n   * **Muhammad Ali Jinnah** (Muslim League) was willing to give up demands for separate electorates if Muslims were assured reserved seats in Central Assembly and representation in Muslim-majority provinces (Bengal & Punjab).\n   * Negotiations failed at the All Parties Conference in 1928 when **M.R. Jayakar** of the Hindu Mahasabha strongly opposed compromises.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SUB-TOPIC 5: THE SENSE OF COLLECTIVE BELONGING & QUIT INDIA MOVEMENT","content":"Nationalism spreads when people begin to believe that they are all part of the same nation and discover unified identity through cultural processes:"},{"heading":"1. Visual Allegories – Bharat Mata (Bharat Mata):","content":"* **Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay**: Created the image of Bharat Mata and wrote **''Vande Mataram''** in the 1870s as a hymn to the motherland (later included in his novel *Anandamath*).\n* **Abanindranath Tagore (1905)**: Painted his famous image of **Bharat Mata** depicted as an ascetic figure—calm, composed, divine, and spiritual."},{"heading":"2. Revival of Indian Folklore:","content":"* **Bengal**: **Rabindranath Tagore** collected ballads, nursery rhymes, and myths, leading the folk revival movement.\n* **Madras**: **Natesa Sastri** published a massive four-volume collection of Tamil folk tales titled ***The Folklore of Southern India***, believing folklore was national literature."},{"heading":"3. Icons and Symbols (Flags):","content":"* **Swadeshi Flag (Bengal, 1857/1905)**: Tricolor flag (red, green, yellow) with eight lotuses representing eight provinces of British India, and a crescent moon representing Hindus and Muslims.\n* **Swaraj Flag (1921)**: Designed by Mahatma Gandhi. Tricolor (red, green, white) with a **spinning wheel** (*Charkha*) in the center, representing the Gandhian ideal of self-help."},{"heading":"4. Reinterpretation of History:","content":"British historians portrayed Indians as backward, primitive, and incapable of governing themselves. In response, Indian historians wrote about India''s glorious past achievements in art, architecture, science, mathematics, religion, and trade, urging readers to take pride in Indian history.\\n\\n#### 5. Quit India Movement (Quit India Movement, 1942):\\n* **Context**: Failure of the Cripps Mission (1942) and World War II pressures.\\n* **Resolution**: Passed by Congress Working Committee at Wardha (July 1942) and endorsed at **Bombay on 8 August 1942**.\\n* **Slogan**: Gandhiji gave the famous mantra: **\"Do or Die\"** (*Do or die*).\\n* **Nature**: A mass movement where farmers, youth, students, and workers participated. Leaders like Jayaprakash Narayan, Ram Manohar Lohia, and Aruna Asaf Ali engaged in underground activities. Women like Matangini Hazra (Bengal), Kanaklata Barua (Assam), and Rama Devi (Odisha) showed heroic resistance.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SECTION 4: MASTER TIMELINE & PRESCRIBED MAP WORK CATALOG\\n\\n### 1. Chronological Master Timeline (1914 – 1942)\\n\\n```\\n1914─────── ► First World War Begins\\n1915─────── ► Jan: Gandhiji Returns to India from South Africa\\n1917─────── ► Champaran (Bihar) Indigo Satyagraha & Kheda Peasant Satyagraha\\n1918─────── ► Ahmedabad Cotton Mill Workers Satyagraha\\n1919─────── ► March: Rowlatt Act Passed; Khilafat Committee Formed (Bombay)\\n            13 April: Jallianwala Bagh Massacre (Amritsar)\\n1920─────── ► Sept: Calcutta Congress Session (Khilafat & Non-Cooperation agreement)\\n            Dec: Nagpur Congress Session (Non-Cooperation Program adopted)\\n1921─────── ► Jan: Non-Cooperation–Khilafat Movement launched\\n1922─────── ► 5 Feb: Chauri Chaura Incident; Movement withdrawn by Gandhiji\\n1923─────── ► Formation of Swaraj Party (Motilal Nehru & C.R. Das)\\n1924─────── ► Execution of Tribal Leader Alluri Sitarama Raju\\n1928─────── ► Arrival of Simon Commission (\"Go Back Simon\"); All Parties Conference\\n1929─────── ► Dec: Lahore Congress Session (Purna Swaraj resolution under J.L. Nehru)\\n1930─────── ► 26 Jan: First Independence Day pledge taken\\n            12 March–6 April: Dandi Salt March & Civil Disobedience launched\\n            Dr. B.R. Ambedkar forms Depressed Classes Association\\n1931─────── ► 5 March: Gandhi-Irwin Pact signed; Second Round Table Conference\\n1932─────── ► Sept: Poona Pact signed between Gandhiji and Dr. B.R. Ambedkar\\n1934─────── ► Civil Disobedience Movement completely fades out\\n1942─────── ► 8 August: Quit India Resolution adopted at Bombay (\"Do or Die\")\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Prescribed Map Work Locations Inventory (History - 4 Marks Map Question)\\n\\nThe BSER Social Science paper includes 1 compulsory Map Question (Question 22/28) carrying 4 marks. Map items from History are drawn strictly from the **6 Key Indian National Movement Centres**:\\n\\n| Location Name | State | Historical Significance / Event Tag | BSER Past Paper Appearance |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Champaran** Bihar | Movement of Indigo Planters (1917) Model Paper 2026–27 |\\n| **Kheda** Gujarat | Peasant Satyagraha against revenue collection (1917) BSER 2023, 2024 |\\n| **Ahmedabad** | Gujarat | Cotton Mill Workers Satyagraha (1918) BSER 2022, 2024 |\\n| **Amritsar** | Punjab | Jallianwala Bagh Massacre site (13 April 1919) BSER 2022, 2023, 2024 |\\n| **Chauri Chaura** Uttar Pradesh | Site of violent clash causing withdrawal of Non-Cooperation Movement (1922) | BSER 2023, 2024 |\\n| **Dandi** | Gujarat | Coastal town where Salt March ended and Civil Disobedience launched (1930) BSER 2022, 2023, 2024 |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SECTION 5: VERIFIED PREVIOUS-YEAR BOARD QUESTIONS (PYQs)\\n\\n### 1. Multiple Choice Questions (1 Mark):\\n\\n* **PYQ 1 (BSER Secondary Main Exam 2022)**\\n  * **Question**: When did the Jallianwala Bagh massacre take place?\\n    (A) 9 April 1919 (B) 10 April 1919 (C) 13 April 1919 (D) 19 April 1919\\n  * **Answer**: **(C) 13 April 1919**\\n  * **Source**: BSER 2022 Main Exam Paper, S-08, Q.1(i).\\n\\n* **PYQ 2 (BSER Secondary Main Exam 2022)**\\n  * **Question**: In which Round Table Conference did Mahatma Gandhi participate?\\n    (A) First (B) Second (C) Third (D) None of these\\n  * **Answer**: **(B) Second**\\n  * **Source**: BSER 2022 Main Exam Paper, S-08, Q.1(ii).\\n\\n* **PYQ 3 (BSER Supplementary Exam 2024 - D&D S-124)**\\n  * **Question**: In which year did Mahatma Gandhi return to India from South Africa?\\n    (A) 1915 AD (B) 1951 AD (C) 1927 AD (D) 1918 AD\\n  * **Answer**: **(A) 1915 AD**\\n  * **Source**: BSER 2024 Supplementary Paper, S-124, Q.1(i).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Short Answer Questions (2 Marks):\\n\\n* **PYQ 4 (BSER Secondary Main Exam 2022)**\\n  * **Question**: For what reasons were the farmers of Kheda district not in a position to pay the land revenue? [2 Marks]\\n  * **Model Answer**:\\n    1. **Crop Failure**: Crops failed in Kheda district of Gujarat in 1917 due to monsoon failure, leaving farmers without produce.\\n    2. **Plague Epidemic**: The region was struck by a severe plague epidemic, disrupting normal livelihoods. Consequently, peasants could not pay the high land revenue demanded by the British government and requested revenue relaxation.\\n  * **Source**: BSER 2022 Main Paper, S-08, Q.5.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. Essay / Long Answer Questions (4 Marks):\\n\\n* **PYQ 5 (BSER Secondary Main Exam 2024)**\\n  * **Question**: How did the Non-Cooperation Movement spread in the towns? Explain. [4 Marks]\\n  * **Model Answer**:\\n    1. **Middle-Class Participation**: The movement began in towns with middle-class participation. Thousands of students left government-controlled schools and colleges. Headmasters and teachers resigned, and lawyers gave up their legal practice.\\n    2. **Boycott of Foreign Goods**: Foreign cloth was boycotted and burnt in huge public bonfires. Liquor shops were picketed across towns.\\n    3. **Boycott of Council Elections**: Council elections were boycotted in most provinces, except in Madras where the Justice Party felt entering councils was a way of gaining power.\\n    4. **Economic Impact**: Imports of foreign cloth halved between 1921 and 1922, its value dropping from Rs 102 crore to Rs 57 crore. Merchants and traders refused to trade in foreign goods or finance foreign trade.\\n  * **Source**: BSER 2024 Main Exam Paper, S-08, Q.20 (OR option).\\n\\n* **PYQ 6 (BSER Secondary Main Exam 2023)**\\n  * **Question**: Explain the events before the Non-Cooperation Movement. [4 Marks]\\n  * **Model Answer**:\\n    1. **First World War Impacts**: Created economic distress, high prices, forced recruitment, and crop failures combined with an influenza epidemic (12–13 million deaths).\\n    2. **Arrival of Gandhiji & Local Satyagrahas**: Gandhiji brought the technique of Satyagraha and organized movements in Champaran (1917), Kheda (1917), and Ahmedabad (1918).\\n    3. **Rowlatt Act & Nationwide Hartal (1919)**: The repressive Rowlatt Act allowed two-year detention without trial. Gandhiji called for a nation-wide *hartal* on 6 April 1919.\\n    4. **Jallianwala Bagh Massacre (13 April 1919)**: General Dyer opened fire on unarmed citizens in Amritsar, causing widespread outrage across the nation.\\n    5. **Khilafat Issue & Alliance**: To unite Hindus and Muslims, Gandhiji supported Ali Brothers on the Khilafat issue, leading to the adoption of Non-Cooperation at the Nagpur Congress Session in December 1920.\\n  * **Source**: BSER 2023 Main Exam Paper, S-08, Q.18.\\n\\n* **PYQ 7 (BSER Secondary Main Exam 2022 / 2023)**\\n  * **Question**: \"Ideas of nationalism also developed through a movement to revive Indian folklore.\" Explain the statement with arguments. [4 Marks]\\n  * **Model Answer**:\\n    1. **Preservation of National Identity**: In late 19th-century India, nationalists began recording folk tales sung by bards and tour villages to gather songs and legends. They believed these tales gave a true picture of traditional culture that had been corrupted by foreign rule.\\n    2. **Contributions in Bengal**: Rabindranath Tagore himself began collecting ballads, nursery rhymes, and myths, and led the movement for folk revival to re-establish pride in national heritage.\\n    3. **Contributions in Madras**: In Madras, Natesa Sastri published a massive four-volume collection of Tamil folk tales, *The Folklore of Southern India*. He believed that folklore was national literature and \"the most trustworthy manifestation of people’s real thoughts\".\\n    4. **Restoring Self-Respect**: Restoring folk traditions helped instill a sense of national pride and restored confidence among Indians against colonial cultural superiority.\\n  * **Source**: BSER 2022 Main Paper Q.21 (OR) & BSER 2023 Main Paper Q.18 (OR).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SECTION 6: PRACTICE & PREDICTED QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS\\n\\n### 1. Short Answer Practice Questions (2–3 Marks):\\n\\n* **Question 1**: Explain the concept of Satyagraha as advocated by Mahatma Gandhi.\\n  * **Answer**:\\n    1. Satyagraha emphasized the power of truth and the need to search for truth.\\n    2. It suggested that if the cause was true and struggle was against injustice, physical force was not necessary to fight the oppressor.\\n    3. Without seeking vengeance or being aggressive, a satyagrahi could win the battle through non-violence by appealing to the conscience of the oppressor.\\n\\n* **Question 2**: Why was the Simon Commission boycotted by Indians?\\n  * **Answer**:\\n    1. The Indian Statutory Commission (Simon Commission) was set up by the British Government to look into the functioning of the constitutional system in India and suggest changes.\\n    2. The main problem was that **it did not have a single Indian member**; all its seven members were British.\\n    3. When it arrived in 1928, all Indian political parties (Congress and Muslim League) boycotted it with the slogan \"Go Back Simon\".\\n\\n* **Question 3**: What was the Inland Emigration Act of 1859? How did plantation workers in Assam react to it?\\n  * **Answer**:\\n    1. Under the Inland Emigration Act of 1859, tea plantation workers in Assam were not permitted to leave the tea gardens without written permission, which was rarely given.\\n    2. When workers heard of the Non-Cooperation Movement, thousands defied authorities, left plantations, and headed for home, believing *Gandhi Raj* was coming.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Original Predicted Long Answer Questions for Board Exam 2027:\\n\\n* **Predicted Question 1**: Describe the Salt March (Dandi March) and explain why it became an effective symbol of resistance against British colonialism. [4 Marks]\\n  * **Answer**:\\n    1. **Selection of Salt as a Symbol**: Gandhiji found in salt a powerful symbol that could unite the nation. Salt was something consumed by the rich and the poor alike, and was an essential item of food. The tax on salt and government monopoly over its production revealed the oppressive nature of British rule.\\n    2. **The Ultimatum**: On 31 January 1930, Gandhiji sent a letter to Viceroy Irwin stating 11 demands. When Irwin refused to negotiate, Gandhiji launched the Salt March.\\n    3. **The Dandi March**: Accompanied by 78 trusted volunteers, Gandhiji walked over 240 miles from Sabarmati Ashram to Dandi in 24 days. Thousands came to hear him along the way.\\n    4. **Breaking the Law & Mass Impact**: On 6 April 1930, Gandhiji reached Dandi and ceremonially manufactured salt by boiling sea water. This broke the salt law and inaugurated the Civil Disobedience Movement across the country, where thousands manufactured salt, picketed liquor shops, and boycotted foreign goods.\\n\\n* **Predicted Question 2**: Analyze the role of different social groups in the Civil Disobedience Movement, highlighting why rich peasants and business classes participated. [4 Marks]\\n  * **Answer**:\\n    1. **Rich Peasants (Patidars & Jats)**:\\n       * Being producers of commercial crops, they were hard hit by the trade depression and falling agricultural prices.\\n       * As their cash income disappeared, they found it impossible to pay the government''s revenue demand.\n       * They became enthusiastic supporters of Civil Disobedience, organizing their communities to demand revenue reduction.\n    2. **Business Classes (FICCI & Industrialists)**:\n       * During WWI, Indian merchants made huge profits and wanted protection against imports of foreign goods and a favorable exchange ratio.\n       * Led by G.D. Birla and Purshottamdas Thakurdas, they formed FICCI (1927) and gave financial assistance to the movement, refusing to buy or sell imported goods.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 7: 15-QUESTION MCQ QUIZ (WITH EXPLANATORY NOTES)","content":"1. **In which year did Mahatma Gandhi return to India from South Africa?**\n   (A) 1912\n   (B) 1914\n   (C) 1915\n   (D) 1919\n   * **Answer**: **(C) 1915**\n   * *Note*: Gandhiji returned to India in January 1915.\n\n2. **Where did Mahatma Gandhi organize his first Satyagraha movement in India in 1917?**\n   (A) Kheda\n   (B) Champaran\n   (C) Ahmedabad\n   (D) Amritsar\n   * **Answer**: **(B) Champaran**\n   * *Note*: Champaran satyagraha in Bihar was organized for indigo planters.\n\n3. **The Rowlatt Act of 1919 allowed the British government to detain political prisoners without trial for up to:**\n   (A) 1 year\n   (B) 2 years\n   (C) 3 years\n   (D) 5 years\n   * **Answer**: **(B) 2 years**\n   * *Note*: Detention without trial for up to two years was the core clause of the Rowlatt Act.\n\n4. **Who was the British military officer responsible for the Jallianwala Bagh Massacre?**\n   (A) Lord Irwin\n   (B) Sir John Simon\n   (C) General Dyer\n   (D) Lord Curzon\n   * **Answer**: **(C) General Dyer**\n   * *Note*: General Dyer ordered firing on the crowd on 13 April 1919 at Amritsar.\n\n5. **In which book did Mahatma Gandhi declare that British rule was established in India with the cooperation of Indians?**\n   (A) My Experiments with Truth\n   (B) Discovery of India\n   (C) Hind Swaraj\n   (D) Anandamath\n   * **Answer**: **(C) Hind Swaraj**\n   * *Note*: *Hind Swaraj* was written by Gandhiji in 1909.\n\n6. **The Khilafat Committee was formed in March 1919 in which city?**\n   (A) Calcutta\n   (B) Bombay\n   (C) Lucknow\n   (D) Amritsar\n   * **Answer**: **(B) Bombay**\n   * *Note*: Formed in Bombay by Ali Brothers (Muhammad Ali & Shaukat Ali).\n\n7. **At which Congress Session in December 1920 was the Non-Cooperation Programme formally adopted?**\n   (A) Calcutta Session\n   (B) Nagpur Session\n   (C) Lahore Session\n   (D) Madras Session\n   * **Answer**: **(B) Nagpur Session**\n   * *Note*: Non-cooperation was adopted at Nagpur in December 1920 after initial approval at Calcutta in Sept 1920.\n\n8. **Who led the peasant movement in Awadh against talukdars and landlords?**\n   (A) Alluri Sitarama Raju\n   (B) Baba Ramchandra\n   (C) Jawaharlal Nehru\n   (D) Motilal Nehru\n   * **Answer**: **(B) Baba Ramchandra**\n   * *Note*: Baba Ramchandra was a sanyasi and former indentured laborer in Fiji.\n\n9. **The Swaraj Party was formed within Congress in 1923 by:**\n   (A) Jawaharlal Nehru and Subhas Chandra Bose\n   (B) C.R. Das and Motilal Nehru\n   (C) Mahatma Gandhi and Sardar Patel\n   (D) Dr. B.R. Ambedkar and Maulana Azad\n   * **Answer**: **(B) C.R. Das and Motilal Nehru**\n   * *Note*: Formed to enter legislative councils and oppose British policies from within.\n\n10. **The resolution of ''Purna Swaraj'' (Full Independence) was passed at the Lahore Congress Session under the presidency of:**\n    (A) Mahatma Gandhi\n    (B) Jawaharlal Nehru\n    (C) Subhas Chandra Bose\n    (D) Sarojini Naidu\n    * **Answer**: **(B) Jawaharlal Nehru**\n    * *Note*: Passed in December 1929 at Lahore.\n\n11. **How many trusted volunteers accompanied Mahatma Gandhi on the Dandi Salt March?**\n    (A) 55\n    (B) 78\n    (C) 100\n    (D) 240\n    * **Answer**: **(B) 78**\n    * *Note*: Gandhiji started the 240-mile march with 78 volunteers.\n\n12. **The Depressed Classes Association was established in 1930 by:**\n    (A) Mahatma Gandhi\n    (B) Jyotirao Phule\n    (C) Dr. B.R. Ambedkar\n    (D) Periyar E.V. Ramasamy\n    * **Answer**: **(C) Dr. B.R. Ambedkar**\n    * *Note*: Dr. Ambedkar established it to demand political rights for Dalits.\n\n13. **The Poona Pact of September 1932 resulted in:**\n    (A) Withdrawal of Civil Disobedience\n    (B) Reservation of seats for Depressed Classes in general electorates\n    (C) Formation of Swaraj Party\n    (D) Launch of Quit India Movement\n    * **Answer**: **(B) Reservation of seats for Depressed Classes in general electorates**\n    * *Note*: Signed between Gandhiji and Dr. B.R. Ambedkar.\n\n14. **Who painted the famous image of Bharat Mata depicted as an ascetic figure in 1905?**\n    (A) Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay\n    (B) Rabindranath Tagore\n    (C) Abanindranath Tagore\n    (D) Raja Ravi Varma\n    * **Answer**: **(C) Abanindranath Tagore**\n    * *Note*: Abanindranath Tagore painted the iconic calm, ascetic Bharat Mata image.\n\n15. **The slogan \"Do or Die\" was given by Mahatma Gandhi during which movement?**\n    (A) Non-Cooperation Movement\n    (B) Civil Disobedience Movement\n    (C) Rowlatt Satyagraha\n    (D) Quit India Movement\n    * **Answer**: **(D) Quit India Movement**\n    * *Note*: Given during the Quit India speech at Bombay on 8 August 1942.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 8: TOPIC-BY-TOPIC SYLLABUS COVERAGE CHECKLIST","content":"| Prescribed Syllabus Sub-Topic | Text Notes Coverage | PYQ Coverage | Practice Qs Coverage | MCQ Quiz Coverage | Status |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **First World War Context & Impact** | Included (Sec 3) | Included (Sec 5) | Included (Sec 6) | Q.1, Q.3 | Verified Prescribed |\n| **Satyagraha & Early Movements (1917–18)** | Included (Sec 3) | Included (Sec 5) | Included (Sec 6) | Q.2 | Verified Prescribed |\n| **Rowlatt Act & Jallianwala Bagh (1919)** | Included (Sec 3) | Included (Sec 5) | Included (Sec 6) | Q.3, Q.4 | Verified Prescribed |\n| **Khilafat & Non-Cooperation Movement** | Included (Sec 3) | Included (Sec 5) | Included (Sec 6) | Q.5, Q.6, Q.7 | Verified Prescribed |\n| **Movement in Towns & Slowdown** | Included (Sec 3) | Included (Sec 5) | Included (Sec 6) | Q.7 | Verified Prescribed |\n| **Rebellion in Countryside (Awadh & Tribal)** | Included (Sec 3) | Included (Sec 5) | Included (Sec 6) | Q.8 | Verified Prescribed |\n| **Swaraj in Plantations (Assam)** | Included (Sec 3) | Included (Sec 5) | Included (Sec 6) | Text Notes | Verified Prescribed |\n| **Chauri Chaura & Swaraj Party** | Included (Sec 3) | Included (Sec 5) | Included (Sec 6) | Q.9 | Verified Prescribed |\n| **Simon Commission & Purna Swaraj (1929)** | Included (Sec 3) | Included (Sec 5) | Included (Sec 6) | Q.10 | Verified Prescribed |\n| **Salt March & Civil Disobedience (1930)** | Included (Sec 3) | Included (Sec 5) | Included (Sec 6) | Q.11 | Verified Prescribed |\n| **Social Groups Participation (Peasants, FICCI)**| Included (Sec 3) | Included (Sec 5) | Included (Sec 6) | Text Notes | Verified Prescribed |\n| **Limits: Dalits, Poona Pact, Muslims** | Included (Sec 3) | Included (Sec 5) | Included (Sec 6) | Q.12, Q.13 | Verified Prescribed |\n| **Sense of Collective Belonging (Folklore, Flag)**| Included (Sec 3) | Included (Sec 5) | Included (Sec 6) | Q.14 | Verified Prescribed |\n| **Quit India Movement (1942)** | Included (Sec 3) | Included (Sec 5) | Included (Sec 6) | Q.15 | Verified Prescribed |\n| **Map Work (6 Movement Centers)** | Included (Sec 4) | Included (Sec 4) | Included (Sec 4) | Map Catalog | Verified Prescribed |\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 9: LIST OF SOURCES & REFERENCE INVENTORY","content":"1. **BSER Official Syllabus Document (2026–27 Session / 2027 Exam)**: `10_2027.pdf`, Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan, Ajmer.\n2. **NCERT Prescribed Textbook**: *India and the Contemporary World – Part 2* (Class X History), Chapter 2 (*Nationalism in India*).\n3. **BSER Secondary Main Examination Question Paper 2024**: Code S-08 (Social Science).\n4. **BSER Secondary Main Examination Question Paper 2023**: Code S-08 (Social Science).\n5. **BSER Secondary Main Examination Question Paper 2022**: Code S-08 (Social Science).\n6. **BSER Supplementary Examination Question Paper 2024**: Code S-124 (Social Science - D&D / CWSN).\n7. **BSER Official Model Question Paper 2026–27**: Class 10 Social Science (Subject Code 08).\n8. **NCERT Rationalised Content List for Class X**: National Council of Educational Research and Training.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 10: EDITORIAL RECORD & DISPUTE RESOLUTION NOTE","content":"* **Date Verification**: All historical dates (1915 return, 13 April 1919 massacre, 5 Feb 1922 Chauri Chaura, 12 March–6 April 1930 Salt March, Sept 1932 Poona Pact, 8 August 1942 Quit India) have been strictly verified against the prescribed NCERT textbook.\n* **Map Work Locations**: Verified against BSER map catalogs. Only the prescribed 6 National Movement centers (Champaran, Kheda, Ahmedabad, Amritsar, Chauri Chaura, Dandi) are included for History map identification.\n* **Exclusions**: Zero sub-topics have been excluded for Chapter 2. All content generated above matches the official 04-mark syllabus distribution for RBSE Class 10 Social Science examination in 2027."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
