INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'social-science-3-notes',
  'social-science',
  'social-science-3',
  'The Making of a Global World',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"The Making of a Global World","subject":"social-science","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"Chapter Metadata & Exam Architecture","content":"* **Chapter ID**: `sst-03`\n* **Subject Code**: 08 (`Social Science` / Social Science)\n* **Discipline**: History (*India and the Contemporary World – Part 2* / *India and the Contemporary World – 2*)\n* **Chapter Title**: The Making of a Global World\n* **Academic Session**: 2026–27 (Intended for the 2027 Secondary Board Examination)\n* **Exam Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan, Ajmer (BSER / RBSE)\n* **Total Subject Weightage**: 100 Marks (80 Written Theory + 20 Internal Sessional)\n* **Chapter Weightage**: **04 Marks** out of 80 Theory Marks\n* **Blueprinted Question Pattern**:\n  * 1 Objective / Very Short Answer Question (1 Mark)\n  * 1 Short / Long Answer Question with Choice (3 Marks)\n  *(OR 2 Short Answer Questions of 2 Marks each = 4 Marks)*\n* **Prescribed Primary Textbook**: *India and the Contemporary World – Part 2* (NCERT, published under copyright by BSER Ajmer)\n\n---"},{"heading":"Official Syllabus & Rationalization Verification Report","content":""},{"heading":"1. Verification of Prescribed Status","content":"* **Official Syllabus Document**: BSER Curriculum Circular `10_2027.pdf` (Subject Code 08, page 38).\n* **Chapter Title**: *3-The Making of a Global World*.\n* **Prescribed Weightage**: **04 Marks**.\n* **Prescription Status**: **100% Prescribed for RBSE Board Examination 2027**."},{"heading":"2. NCERT Rationalization vs. RBSE BSER Exam Policy (Crucial Editorial Clarification)","content":"* **NCERT/CBSE Baseline**: In certain CBSE updates, Sub-sections 2, 3, and 4 (*The Nineteenth Century 1815–1914*, *The Inter-war Economy*, and *Rebuilding a World Economy: The Post-War Era*) were recommended for periodic assessments rather than board papers.\n* **Official RBSE BSER Policy**: The Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER Ajmer) **explicitly includes all four sections** of Chapter 3 in its official syllabus notification (`10_2027.pdf`) and tests all sections in the annual Board Examination.\n* **Evidence from Authentic BSER Board Papers**:\n  * **2025 Main Examination (S-08)**: Direct 2-mark question on *Causes of the Great Depression of 1929* (Section 3).\n  * **2024 Supplementary Examination (S-124)**: Direct long-answer options on *Great Depression* and *End of Bretton Woods and Globalisation* (Section 4).\n  * **2023 Main Examination (S-08)**: Objective questions on *Rinderpest Cattle Disease* (Section 2) and *Bretton Woods Conference Location* (Section 4).\n* **Editorial Directive**: Complete, un-truncated coverage of all four sections (Pre-modern World, 19th Century World Economy, Inter-war Economy, and Post-war Bretton Woods Era) is provided below as **active, examinable content for the 2027 Board Examination**.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes","content":""},{"heading":"Theme 1: The Pre-modern World","content":""},{"heading":"1. Globalisation in History","content":"* Globalisation is not merely a modern economic phenomenon; it has a long history of trade, migration, capital movement, and cultural exchange spanning thousands of years.\n* From ancient times, travellers, traders, priests, and pilgrims walked vast distances for knowledge, opportunities, spiritual fulfillment, or to escape persecution.\n* They carried goods, money, ideas, values, skills, inventions, crops, and even disease-carrying germs.\n* As early as 3000 BCE, an active coastal trade linked the Indus Valley Civilisation with present-day West Asia."},{"heading":"2. Silk Routes Link the World","content":"* **Definition**: The ''Silk Routes'' refer to a vast network of overland and maritime trade routes connecting vibrant regions of Asia with Europe and Northern Africa.\n* **Origins of the Name**: The name highlights the historical prominence of west-bound Chinese silk cargoes carried along these routes.\n* **Goods Traded**:\n  * *West-bound*: Chinese silk, Chinese pottery, Indian spices, and textiles.\n  * *East-bound*: Precious metals (gold and silver) flowed from Europe to Asia.\n* **Cultural & Religious Exchange**: Silk routes served as channels for deep cultural fusion. Christian missionaries, Muslim preachers, and Buddhist monks traversed these routes. Buddhism emerged from eastern India and spread in multiple directions through intersecting points of the silk routes."},{"heading":"3. Food Travels: Spaghetti and Potato","content":"* **Food as Cultural Links**: Traders and travellers introduced new crops to the lands they visited. Even humble foodstuffs share common global origins.\n* **Spaghetti & Noodles**: Long noodles travelled west from China to become spaghetti. Alternatively, Arab traders introduced pasta to 5th-century Sicily (Italy).\n* **The American Contribution**: Many of our common foods—potatoes, soya, groundnuts, maize, tomatoes, chillies, and sweet potatoes—were completely unknown to our ancestors until Christopher Columbus accidentally discovered the Americas.\n* **Impact of the Potato**: The humble potato transformed European diets. It allowed the poor to eat better and live longer. In Ireland, the poorest peasants became so dependent on potatoes that when a disease destroyed the potato crop in 1845–1849, over 1,000,000 people starved to death in the **Irish Potato Famine**.\n\n```\n[Ancient Food & Cultural Exchanges]\n   China (Noodles) ────────► Europe (Spaghetti)\n   Americas (Potato, Tomato, Maize) ────────► Global Consumption & Agricultural Shift\n```"},{"heading":"4. Conquest, Disease, and Trade","content":"* **Discovery of Americas**: In the 16th century, European sailors found a sea route to Asia and crossed the Atlantic to the Americas. Americas''vast lands and abundant crops/minerals transformed global trade.\\n* **Precious Metals**: Silver mined in Peru and Mexico enhanced European wealth and financed its trade with Asia. Legends about South American wealth led to expeditions searching for **El Dorado**, the fabled city of gold.\\n* **Biological Conquest (Smallpox)**:\\n  * The European conquest of America was not caused primarily by superior military firepower.\\n  * The most powerful weapon of the Spanish conquerors was the disease germ **Smallpox**.\\n  * Native Americans had been isolated from the rest of the world for millennia and possessed no immune immunity against smallpox.\\n  * Once introduced, smallpox spread deep into the continent ahead of European troops, decimating whole communities and paving the way for conquest.\\n* **Shift in Global Gravity**: Until the 18th century, China and India were the world''s wealthiest countries and prominent in Asian trade. However, China restricted overseas contacts, shifting the center of world trade westward to Europe.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Theme 2: The Nineteenth Century (1815–1914) – A World Economy Takes Shape","content":""},{"heading":"1. Three Types of Global Economic Flows","content":"Economists identify three distinct types of economic ''flows'' within international economic exchanges:\n1. **Flow of Trade**: Trade in goods such as wheat, cotton, and cloth.\n2. **Flow of Labour**: Migration of people in search of employment opportunities.\n3. **Flow of Capital**: Long-term and short-term investments of capital over long distances.\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│               THREE TYPES OF GLOBAL FLOWS               │\n├────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 1. Flow of Trade       │ Goods (Wheat, Cotton, Textiles)│\n│ 2. Flow of Labour      │ Migrants seeking Employment    │\n│ 3. Flow of Capital     │ Investments (Financial Capital)│\n└────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────┘\n```"},{"heading":"2. A World Economy Takes Shape & Abolition of Corn Laws","content":"* **Population Growth in Britain**: Rapid population growth in late 18th-century Britain pushed up demand for foodgrains, inflating food prices.\n* **The Corn Laws**: Under pressure from landed groups, the British government restricted the import of corn. These laws were known as the **Corn Laws**.\n* **Effects of Repeal**: Unhappy with high food prices, industrialists and urban dwellers forced the abolition of the Corn Laws.\n* **Consequences of Free Trade**:\n  * Food could be imported into Britain far more cheaply than it could be produced domestically.\n  * British agriculture was unable to compete with imports. Vast areas of land were left uncultivated, leaving thousands of farm labourers unemployed, who flocked to cities or migrated overseas.\n  * Lower food prices increased British consumption. Industrial growth led to higher incomes and increased food imports from Eastern Europe, Russia, America, and Australia."},{"heading":"3. Role of Technology","content":"* **Key Innovations**: Railways, steamships, and the telegraph were crucial transformations.\n* **Refrigerated Ships**:\n  * Before the 1870s, live animals were shipped from America to Europe and slaughtered upon arrival. Live animals took up immense ship space, many died in transit, or fell ill and became unfit to eat. Meat was an expensive luxury in Europe.\n  * The development of **refrigerated ships** enabled the transport of perishable foods over long distances.\n  * Animals were slaughtered at the starting point (America, Australia, New Zealand) and transported to Europe as frozen meat.\n  * This reduced shipping costs, lowered European meat prices, improved working-class diets, and fostered social peace in Europe."},{"heading":"4. Late Nineteenth-Century Colonialism & Rinderpest","content":"* **Partition of Africa**: In 1885, big European powers met in Berlin (**Berlin Conference of 1885**) to carve up Africa among themselves using literal straight ruler lines.\n* **Rinderpest or Cattle Plague (*Rinderpest or Cattle Plague*)**:\n  * Rinderpest was a devastating cattle disease that arrived in Africa in the late 1880s.\n  * It was carried by infected cattle imported from British Asia to feed Italian soldiers invading Eritrea in East Africa.\n  * Entering East Africa, Rinderpest moved west and south like wildfire, killing **90 percent of Africa''s cattle population**.\\n  * **Impact**: Cattle loss destroyed African pastoral livelihoods. Colonial governments used the catastrophe to force surviving Africans into the labor market, securing control over African land and labor.\\n\\n```\\nInfected Asian Cattle Imported ── ► Rinderpest Disease Outbreak ── ► Kills 90% African Cattle ── ► Destroys Pastoral Economy ── ► Forces Africans into Colonial Wage Labor\\n```\\n\\n#### 5. Indentured Labor Migration from India\\n* **Definition**: An indentured laborer is a bonded laborer under contract to work for a specific employer for a set period to pay off passage to a new country or plantation.\\n* **Described as a \"New System of Slavery\"**: Recruitment was conducted by agents who used false promises about destinations, modes of travel, nature of work, and living conditions.\\n* **Regions of Origin**: Eastern Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Central India, and dry districts of Tamil Nadu, where cottage industries had collapsed and land rents had risen.\\n* **Destinations**: Caribbean islands (Trinidad, Guyana, Suriname), Mauritius, Fiji, and tea plantations in Assam.\\n* **Cultural Fusion & Hybrid Cultures**:\\n  * In Trinidad, the annual Muharram procession was transformed into a riotous carnival called **Hosay** in which workers of all races joined.\\n  * The religion of **Rastafarianism** reflected Indian links to the Caribbean.\\n  * **Chutney Music** in Trinidad and Guyana became a popular creative expression of cultural fusion.\\n* **Abolition**: Indian nationalist leaders campaigned against the brutal indentured recruitment system, and it was officially abolished in **1921**.\\n\\n#### 6. Indian Entrepreneurs & Trade Under Colonial Rule\\n* **Entrepreneurs Abroad**: Bankers and traders like the *Shikaripuri Shroffs* and *Nattukottai Chettiars* financed export agriculture in Central and Southeast Asia. Hyderabadi Sindhi traders established emporiums at global ports.\\n* **Decline of Indian Textiles**: Fine Indian cotton textiles were hit by high tariffs imposed in Britain to protect local mills. Raw cotton exports from India increased while manufactured cloth exports declined drastically.\\n* **Opium Trade with China**: Britain grew opium in India and exported it to China, using the money to purchase Chinese tea and silk for Britain.\\n* **Trade Surplus & Multilateral Settlement**: Britain had a''trade surplus''with India (exporting manufactured goods of higher value to India than importing goods from India). Britain used this trade surplus to balance its trade deficits with other countries—a system of **multilateral settlement**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Theme 3: The Inter-war Economy\\n\\n#### 1. Economic Impact of the First World War (1914–1918)\\n* **First Modern Industrial War**: Fought with machine guns, tanks, aircraft, chemical weapons, and mass-produced industrial armaments.\\n* **Human Loss**: 9 million dead and 20 million injured, mostly men of working age, drastically reducing household incomes across Europe.\\n* **Shift in Global Financial Power**: The war transformed the US from an international debtor into an international creditor. US overseas assets came to exceed the assets of foreign countries inside the US.\\n\\n#### 2. Post-war Recovery & Agricultural Crisis\\n* **British Struggle**: While Britain was preoccupied with war, industries developed in India and Japan. Post-war Britain struggled to modernize mills and faced stiff competition from Japan.\\n* **Agricultural Overproduction**: During the war, wheat production expanded in Canada, America, and Australia while European wheat fields were disrupted. After the war, European wheat production recovered, causing global wheat surpluses, falling prices, and rural distress.\\n\\n#### 3. Rise of Mass Production & The US Economy\\n* **Henry Ford & Assembly Line**: Car manufacturer Henry Ford pioneered the **Assembly Line** method at his Detroit car plant.\\n  * Workers repeated a single, continuous task (assembling a specific car part) at a conveyor belt''s set pace.\n  * Speeded up output dramatically; the **Model T Ford** became the world''s first mass-produced car.\\n  * Ford doubled daily wages to $5 to prevent high worker turnover, while increasing work intensity.\\n* **Mass Consumption**: Mass production drove down prices, creating a mass consumption boom in the US for cars, refrigerators, washing machines, and radios, financed through hire-purchase (credit paid in weekly installments).\\n\\n```\\nHenry Ford Assembly Line ── ► Mass Production ── ► Lowered Unit Costs ── ► High Daily Wages ($5) ── ► Mass Consumption Boom (Cars & Appliances)\\n```\\n\\n#### 4. The Great Depression of 1929\\n* **Duration**: Began around 1929 and lasted until the mid-1930s.\\n* **Characteristics**: Massive declines in global production, employment, incomes, and international trade.\\n* **Primary Causes**:\\n  1. **Agricultural Overproduction**: Agricultural prices fell, and farmers tried to expand production to maintain total income, worsening the glut and pushing prices further down.\\n  2. **Withdrawal of US Loans**: In the 1920s, global investments relied on US loans. As US lenders panicked, US overseas loans fell from $1 billion to $200 million within a year.\\n  3. **Collapse of Wall Street Stock Exchange (1929)**: The US stock market crash triggered bank failures, business bankruptcies, and massive unemployment.\\n  4. **Tariff Walls**: US attempts to protect its economy by doubling import duties severely damaged global trade.\\n\\n#### 5. Impact of the Great Depression on India\\n* **Immediate Trade Collapse**: Between 1928 and 1934, India''s exports and imports were halved.\n* **Fall in Agricultural Prices**: Wheat prices in India fell by 50%.\n* **Peasant Agony**: While raw material prices tumbled, the British colonial government refused to lower land revenue demands. Jute growers in Bengal suffered as raw jute prices collapsed by over 60%.\n* **Peasant Indebtedness & Gold Exports**: Indian peasants used up savings, mortgaged lands, and sold jewelry. India became an exporter of precious metals (**\"distress gold\"**), which helped finance British sterling payments during the crisis.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Theme 4: Rebuilding a World Economy: The Post-War Era","content":""},{"heading":"1. Post-war Settlement & Bretton Woods Institutions (Bretton Woods Institutions)","content":"* **Lessons from Inter-war Era**:\n  1. An industrial society based on mass production requires mass consumption, which requires stable incomes and full employment.\n  2. Economic stability requires government intervention and control over flows of capital, goods, and labour.\n* **The Bretton Woods Conference (July 1944)**: Held at Bretton Woods in New Hampshire, USA, to establish global economic stability and full employment.\n* **Twin Institutions (The Bretton Woods Twins)**:\n  1. **International Monetary Fund (IMF)**: Established to deal with external surpluses and deficits of member nations.\n  2. **World Bank (IBRD)**: Established to finance post-war reconstruction.\n* **Fixed Exchange Rate System**: National currencies were pegged to the US Dollar at a fixed exchange rate, and the US Dollar was anchored to gold at a fixed price of **$35 per ounce of gold**.\n\n```\n   Bretton Woods System (1944)\n   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐\n   │ Fixed Exchange Rates (Pegged to $ USD)  │\n   │ US Dollar Anchored ($35 = 1 oz Gold)    │\n   ├─────────────────────────────────────────┤\n   │       BRETTON WOODS TWINS INSTITUTIONS  │\n   │  1. IMF (Financial Deficit Balance)     │\n   │  2. World Bank / IBRD (Reconstruction)  │\n   └─────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```"},{"heading":"2. Decolonisation & Emergence of the Group of 77 (G-77)","content":"* **Post-war Decolonisation**: Over two decades after WWII, colonies in Asia and Africa gained political independence, facing poverty and resource scarcity.\n* **Dominance of IMF & World Bank**: Designed to serve developed Western industrial nations; developing nations failed to benefit significantly.\n* **Establishment of G-77**: Developing nations organized as the **Group of 77 (G-77)** to demand a **New International Economic Order (NIEO)** (*New International Economic Order*).\n* **Demands of NIEO**:\n  * Real control over their natural resources.\n  * More development assistance.\n  * Fairer prices for raw materials.\n  * Better access for manufactured goods into developed markets."},{"heading":"3. End of Bretton Woods & Beginning of Globalisation","content":"* **Collapse of Fixed Exchange Rates**: From the 1970s, the US dollar lost confidence as the world''s principal reserve currency due to declining US financial dominance. Fixed exchange rates collapsed and were replaced by **Floating Exchange Rates**.\n* **Shifting Financial Gravity**: Developing nations were forced to borrow from commercial banks rather than international institutions, facing recurring debt crises.\n* **Relocation of Industry to Low-Wage Asian Nations**: From the late 1970s, multinational corporations (MNCs) shifted manufacturing to low-wage Asian countries like China, driven by cheap labor costs. China''s re-entry into the global economy stimulated global trade and investment flows.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Chronological Timeline & Comparative Matrix","content":""},{"heading":"Master Timeline: Evolution of Global Integration (15th Century to 1970s)","content":"| Historical Era / Year | Key Global Event / Phenomenon | Historical Significance & Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **3000 BCE** | Coastal trade connects Indus Valley | Earliest recorded maritime commercial contacts with West Asia. |\n| **15th Century** | Peak of Overland & Maritime Silk Routes | Trans-Eurasian trade linking Chinese silk, Indian spices, and European silver. |\n| **1492** | Christopher Columbus reaches the Americas | Connects Americas to global food, trade, and biological exchanges. |\n| **16th Century** | Smallpox epidemic in the Americas | Spanish conquests facilitated by biological warfare against Native Americans. |\n| **1845–1849** | Irish Potato Famine | Over 1 million Irish die following fungal destruction of potato crops. |\n| **1870s** | Introduction of Refrigerated Ships | Allows frozen meat transport from Americas/Australia to Europe, lowering prices. |\n| **1885** | Berlin Conference | European powers partition the African continent using ruler boundaries. |\n| **1890s** | Rinderpest (Cattle Plague) in Africa | Kills 90% of African cattle, enabling European colonial control over land and labor. |\n| **1914–1918** | First World War | First modern industrial war; shifts financial power from UK to USA. |\n| **1921** | Indentured Labour Migration Abolished | Official end to bonded Indian labour recruitment system following nationalist opposition. |\n| **1929–1930s** | The Great Depression | Global economic crash; agricultural overproduction, bank failures, and trade barriers. |\n| **July 1944** | Bretton Woods Conference (USA) | Establishes IMF, World Bank, and fixed exchange rate system ($35/oz gold). |\n| **1970s** | Collapse of Bretton Woods System | Fixed rates replaced by floating rates; manufacturing shifts to low-cost Asian economies. |\n\n---"},{"heading":"Comparative Matrix: Three Eras of Global Economic Integration","content":"| Key Structural Dimension | Pre-Modern World (Pre-1800) | 19th Century World Economy (1815–1914) | Post-WWII Bretton Woods Era (1944–1970s) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Primary Driver of Integration** | Silk routes, food migrations, conquest, and silver trade | Industrial Revolution, steamships, railways, and colonial trade | Institutional planning, IMF, World Bank, and US dollar dollar-gold peg |\n| **Key Dominant Products** | Silk, spices, porcelain, potatoes, silver | Wheat, cotton, raw materials, textiles, meat | Mass-produced automobiles, industrial goods, capital goods |\n| **Labor Dynamics** | Independent traders, pilgrims, early colonial conquest | Indentured labor migration, urban wage labor | Institutional employment, unionized industrial workforce, MNC relocation |\n| **Crisis Manifestations** | Famines (Ireland), biological disease outbreaks (Smallpox, Rinderpest) | Agricultural collapse due to free imports, colonial exploitation | Wall Street crash, Great Depression, agricultural price collapse |\n\n---"},{"heading":"Verified Previous-Year Board Questions (PYQs)","content":""},{"heading":"Section A: Objective & Very Short Answer PYQs (1 Mark)","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1 [BSER Secondary Main Exam 2023, Code S-08]","content":"**Which disease killed 90 percent of cattle in Africa in the 1890s?**\n* **Answer**: **Rinderpest** (*Rinderpest / cattle plague*).\n* **Source Context**: NCERT History Chapter 3, Page 59."},{"heading":"Question 2 [BSER Secondary Main Exam 2023, Code S-08]","content":"**In which country was the famous Bretton Woods Conference held?**\n*(A) America (B) England (C) South Africa (D) China*\n* **Answer**: **(A) America** (New Hampshire, USA, July 1944).\n* **Source Context**: NCERT History Chapter 3, Page 72."},{"heading":"Question 3 [BSER Secondary Main Exam 2024, Code S-08]","content":"**Mention any two results of the First World War.**\n* **Answer**:\n  1. Huge loss of human life (over 9 million dead and 20 million injured), drastically reducing the working-age population in Europe.\n  2. Shift in global financial power, turning the US from an international debtor into an international creditor.\n* **Source Context**: NCERT History Chapter 3, Page 64.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Section B: Short & Long Answer Board PYQs (2 to 6 Marks)","content":""},{"heading":"Question 4 [BSER Secondary Main Exam 2025, Code S-08]","content":"**Explain any two causes of the Great Depression of 1929. [2 Marks]**\n* **Model Answer**:\n  1. **Agricultural Overproduction**: Agricultural prices fell dramatically after WWI. To maintain total incomes, farmers expanded production, creating a massive market glut that pushed prices down further.\n  2. **Withdrawal of US Loans**: In the 1920s, many countries financed investments through US loans. When US lenders panicked, US overseas loans fell from $1 billion to $200 million within a single year, causing financial panic worldwide.\n* **Source Context**: NCERT History Chapter 3, Page 66."},{"heading":"Question 5 [BSER Secondary Supplementary Exam 2024, Code S-124]","content":"**What did the East India Company do to ensure regular supply of cotton and silk fabrics from Indian weavers? OR Highlight the end of Bretton Woods and the Beginning of ''Globalisation''. [6 Marks (D&D Paper Allocation)]**\n* **Model Answer (For Bretton Woods Option)**:\n  * **Decline of US Dollar**: From the 1960s, rising costs of US overseas commitments weakened the US financial position. The US Dollar could no longer maintain its fixed value relative to gold.\n  * **Collapse of Fixed Exchange Rates**: The system of fixed exchange rates collapsed in the 1970s and was replaced by a system of floating exchange rates.\n  * **Debt Crises in Developing Countries**: The IMF and World Bank shifted focus toward developing countries, forcing them to borrow from private commercial banks at higher interest rates, leading to periodic debt crises.\n  * **Relocation of Manufacturing**: High unemployment in industrial nations led multinational corporations (MNCs) to shift production to low-wage Asian countries like China, sparking modern economic globalisation.\n* **Source Context**: NCERT History Chapter 3, Page 73.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Practice & Original Predicted Board Questions","content":""},{"heading":"Section A: Short Answer Practice Questions (2–3 Marks)","content":""},{"heading":"Question 6 (Conceptual Focus: Role of Technology)","content":"**How did the introduction of refrigerated ships transform the global meat trade in the late 19th century? [3 Marks]**\n* **Model Answer**:\n  1. **Overcoming Perishability**: Before refrigerated ships, live animals were shipped from America/Australia to Europe. Many died or fell ill in transit, making meat an expensive luxury in Europe.\n  2. **Cost Reduction**: Refrigerated ships allowed animals to be slaughtered at the starting point and shipped as frozen meat, cutting space requirements and freight costs.\n  3. **Social Impact**: Lower prices made meat accessible to the European working class, improving diets and promoting social stability."},{"heading":"Question 7 (Conceptual Focus: Indentured Labour & Cultural Fusion)","content":"**Why is the 19th-century system of Indian indentured labour migration described as a ''New System of Slavery''? Give two examples of cultural fusion that emerged from it. [3 Marks]**\n* **Model Answer**:\n  * **Reasons for the Term**: Agents enticed poor peasants using false information regarding destinations, travel conditions, and work nature. Upon arrival, labourers faced harsh living conditions and legal penalties for unauthorized absence.\n  * **Examples of Cultural Fusion**:\n    1. **Hosay Carnival**: In Trinidad, the annual Muharram procession transformed into a carnival called *Hosay*, joined by workers of all races.\n    2. **Chutney Music**: Popular in Trinidad and Guyana, blending Indian folk tunes with Caribbean rhythms.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Section B: Original Predicted Essay/Case-Based Questions (4 Marks)","content":""},{"heading":"Question 8 (2027 Board Predicted Essay Question)","content":"**Explain how the Rinderpest cattle disease helped European colonial powers establish control over Africa in the late 19th century. [4 Marks]**\n* **Model Answer**:\n  1. **Arrival of Disease**: Rinderpest arrived in East Africa in the late 1880s via infected cattle imported from British Asia to feed Italian soldiers in Eritrea.\n  2. **Massive Destruction**: The disease spread south like wildfire, reaching Africa''s Atlantic coast by 1892 and killing **90 percent of the cattle population**.\n  3. **Collapse of Local Economies**: Cattle loss destroyed African pastoral livelihoods, depriving communities of food and wealth.\n  4. **Colonial Exploitation**: European colonizers monopolized remaining cattle resources and used the crisis to force Africans into wage labor on mines and plantations, securing control over African territories."},{"heading":"Question 9 (2027 Board Predicted Competency Question)","content":"**What was the Group of 77 (G-77)? Why did developing countries demand a New International Economic Order (NIEO)? [4 Marks]**\n* **Model Answer**:\n  * **Definition**: The **G-77** was a coalition formed by developing nations to safeguard their economic interests in the post-WWII era.\n  * **Reasons for Demanding NIEO**:\n    1. **Inequity of Bretton Woods**: The IMF and World Bank were designed to serve Western industrial powers and failed to address poverty in newly independent colonies.\n    2. **Real Resource Control**: Developing nations sought sovereignty over their natural resources, which remained exploited by Western MNCs.\n    3. **Fair Trade Conditions**: They demanded fairer prices for raw material exports and better access for their manufactured goods in developed markets.\n\n---"},{"heading":"15-Question Comprehensive MCQ Quiz","content":"```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│              CHAPTER 3: 15 MULTIPLE CHOICE QUIZ         │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n1. **Which ancient trade route network connected Asia with Europe and Northern Africa?**\n   (A) Amber Road  \n   (B) Silk Routes  \n   (C) Trans-Saharan Route  \n   (D) Spice Maritime Route  \n   * **Correct Answer**: **(B) Silk Routes**\n\n2. **Which crop introduced to Europe from the Americas triggered a fatal famine in Ireland in 1845–1849?**\n   (A) Wheat  \n   (B) Maize  \n   (C) Potato  \n   (D) Sweet Potato  \n   * **Correct Answer**: **(C) Potato**\n\n3. **Which biological weapon unintentionally aided the Spanish conquest of the Americas?**\n   (A) Rinderpest  \n   (B) Smallpox  \n   (C) Cholera  \n   (D) Bubonic Plague  \n   * **Correct Answer**: **(B) Smallpox**\n\n4. **In Britain, laws restricting the import of foodgrains were known as:**\n   (A) Grain Acts  \n   (B) Import Duty Laws  \n   (C) Corn Laws  \n   (D) Agricultural Protection Acts  \n   * **Correct Answer**: **(C) Corn Laws**\n\n5. **In which year was the Berlin Conference held by European powers to partition Africa?**\n   (A) 1857  \n   (B) 1885  \n   (C) 1914  \n   (D) 1890  \n   * **Correct Answer**: **(B) 1885**\n\n6. **Rinderpest, a devastating cattle disease, killed what percentage of Africa''s cattle in the 1890s?**\n   (A) 50%  \n   (B) 70%  \n   (C) 80%  \n   (D) 90%  \n   * **Correct Answer**: **(D) 90%**\n\n7. **In Trinidad, the annual Muharram procession transformed into a riotous carnival known as:**\n   (A) Chutney  \n   (B) Hosay  \n   (C) Rastafarianism  \n   (D) Reggae  \n   * **Correct Answer**: **(B) Hosay**\n\n8. **In which year was the indentured labour recruitment system officially abolished in India?**\n   (A) 1911  \n   (B) 1921  \n   (C) 1931  \n   (D) 1947  \n   * **Correct Answer**: **(B) 1921**\n\n9. **Which pioneering industrialist introduced the conveyor-belt Assembly Line for mass car production in Detroit?**\n   (A) Andrew Carnegie  \n   (B) John D. Rockefeller  \n   (C) Henry Ford  \n   (D) J.P. Morgan  \n   * **Correct Answer**: **(C) Henry Ford**\n\n10. **The Wall Street Stock Exchange crash in the USA occurred in which year, triggering the Great Depression?**\n    (A) 1918  \n    (B) 1929  \n    (C) 1939  \n    (D) 1944  \n    * **Correct Answer**: **(B) 1929**\n\n11. **During the Great Depression (1928–1934), agricultural prices in India fell by approximately:**\n    (A) 25%  \n    (B) 33%  \n    (C) 50%  \n    (D) 75%  \n    * **Correct Answer**: **(C) 50%**\n\n12. **Where was the UN Monetary and Financial Conference held in July 1944?**\n    (A) Geneva, Switzerland  \n    (B) Bretton Woods, New Hampshire, USA  \n    (C) London, UK  \n    (D) Paris, France  \n    * **Correct Answer**: **(B) Bretton Woods, New Hampshire, USA**\n\n13. **Which two international financial institutions are famously referred to as the ''Bretton Woods Twins''?**\n    (A) UNO and UNESCO  \n    (B) IMF and World Bank (IBRD)  \n    (C) WTO and GATT  \n    (D) ADB and BRICS Bank  \n    * **Correct Answer**: **(B) IMF and World Bank (IBRD)**\n\n14. **The group of developing countries formed to demand a New International Economic Order (NIEO) was called:**\n    (A) G-8  \n    (B) G-20  \n    (C) G-77  \n    (D) SAARC  \n    * **Correct Answer**: **(C) G-77**\n\n15. **Under the Bretton Woods system, the US dollar was pegged to gold at a fixed price of:**\n    (A) $20 per ounce  \n    (B) $35 per ounce  \n    (C) $50 per ounce  \n    (D) $100 per ounce  \n    * **Correct Answer**: **(B) $35 per ounce**\n\n---"},{"heading":"Topic-by-Topic Syllabus Coverage Checklist","content":"| Sub-topic Code | Prescribed Curriculum Syllabus Topic | Verified Coverage Status | Page / Section Location in Material |\n| :---: | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| **ST-01** | Ancient Global Trade & Silk Routes | **VERIFIED** | Theme 1, Sub-section 2 |\n| **ST-02** | Food Migration: Spaghetti & Potato / Irish Famine | **VERIFIED** | Theme 1, Sub-section 3 |\n| **ST-03** | Conquest, Disease (Smallpox) & El Dorado | **VERIFIED** | Theme 1, Sub-section 4 |\n| **ST-04** | Three Types of Global Flows (Trade, Labour, Capital) | **VERIFIED** | Theme 2, Sub-section 1 |\n| **ST-05** | Corn Laws & Emergence of World Agricultural Economy | **VERIFIED** | Theme 2, Sub-section 2 |\n| **ST-06** | Role of Technology & Refrigerated Ships | **VERIFIED** | Theme 2, Sub-section 3 |\n| **ST-07** | 19th Century Colonialism & Rinderpest Cattle Plague | **VERIFIED** | Theme 2, Sub-section 4 |\n| **ST-08** | Indentured Labour Migration & Cultural Syncretism | **VERIFIED** | Theme 2, Sub-section 5 |\n| **ST-09** | Indian Bankers Abroad & Colonial Multilateral Trade | **VERIFIED** | Theme 2, Sub-section 6 |\n| **ST-10** | First World War Transformation & US Creditor Status | **VERIFIED** | Theme 3, Sub-section 1 |\n| **ST-11** | Mass Production, Henry Ford & Assembly Line | **VERIFIED** | Theme 3, Sub-section 3 |\n| **ST-12** | Great Depression 1929: Causes & Global Impact | **VERIFIED** | Theme 3, Sub-section 4 |\n| **ST-13** | Impact of Great Depression on Indian Economy | **VERIFIED** | Theme 3, Sub-section 5 |\n| **ST-14** | Bretton Woods Conference, IMF & World Bank | **VERIFIED** | Theme 4, Sub-section 1 |\n| **ST-15** | Decolonisation, G-77 & Demand for NIEO | **VERIFIED** | Theme 4, Sub-section 2 |\n| **ST-16** | End of Bretton Woods & Modern Globalisation | **VERIFIED** | Theme 4, Sub-section 3 |\n\n---"},{"heading":"Master Source Inventory & Verification Log","content":"1. **Official BSER Curriculum Circular (10_2027.pdf)**: Issued by BSER Ajmer for 2026–27 session. Prescribes Chapter 3 with 04 Marks weightage and lists all four sub-sections.\n2. **Prescribed NCERT Textbook**: *India and the Contemporary World – Part 2* (History Class 10), Chapter 3 *The Making of a Global World*.\n3. **BSER Main Examination Question Papers**:\n   * `S-08 (2025)`: Question on Great Depression causes (Section 3).\n   * `S-08 (2024)`: Questions on First World War impact & WTO (Section 3 & 4).\n   * `S-08 (2023)`: Objective questions on Rinderpest & Bretton Woods (Section 2 & 4).\n   * `S-124 (2024 D&D)`: Long answer choices on Great Depression & Globalisation.\n4. **Reference Library Architecture Report**: Grounding document for BSER cognitive blueprint, question types, and curriculum mapping."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
