VALUES (
  'social-science-14-notes',
  'social-science',
  'social-science-14',
  'Federalism',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Federalism","subject":"social-science","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"Chapter Metadata & Official Examination Scheme","content":"* **Chapter ID**: `sst-14`\n* **Subject ID**: `sst` (Subject Code: 08)\n* **Discipline**: Political Science (*Democratic Politics – II*)\n* **Chapter Number (Working List)**: 14 (Political Science Chapter 2)\n* **Chapter Title**: Federalism\n* **Hindi Title**: Federalism\n* **Academic Session**: 2026–27 (Intended for the 2027 Secondary Board Examination)\n* **Prescribed Textbook**: *Democratic Politics – II* (Class X), Published by NCERT and Accredited by BSER Ajmer\n* **Exam Duration**: 3 Hours 15 Minutes\n* **Marks Weightage**: **04 Marks** out of 80 Theory Marks (Political Science Total Unit Weightage: 20 Marks across 4 Prescribed Chapters)\n* **Blueprint Question Pattern**: Typically assessed as **1 Short Answer Question (03 Marks) + 1 Objective/MCQ Question (01 Mark)**, OR **2 Short Answer Questions (02 Marks + 02 Marks)**.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Official Syllabus & Prescription Verification Report","content":"* **Prescription Status**: **100% Fully Prescribed** for the 2026–27 academic session and 2027 Secondary Board Examination.\n* **Board Weightage**: **04 Marks** out of 80 Theory Marks.\n* **Rationalisation & Exclusion Check**: Verified against the official Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER, Ajmer) syllabus document (`10_2027.pdf`, p. 38) and the 2026–27 official model question paper (`Social-Science-10.pdf`). **Zero sub-topics have been excluded or rationalised** from Political Science Chapter 2 (*Federalism*). All core thematic areas—Concept and Key Features of Federalism, Routes of Federations, Constitutional Division of Powers (Union, State, Concurrent, Residuary Lists), Special Provisions and Union Territories, Language Policy and Linguistic Reorganisation, Centre-State Relations & Coalition Era, and Rural/Urban Local Self-Government (1992 Decentralisation Amendment)—are active and examinable in full.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Syllabus-Aligned Complete Revision Notes","content":""},{"heading":"1. Concept and Key Features of Federalism","content":"* **Definition**: Federalism is a system of government in which power is divided between a central authority and various constituent units of the country.\n* **Tiers of Government**: Generally, a federation has two or more levels (tiers) of government:\n  1. **Central/Union Government**: Responsible for the entire country and matters of national interest.\n  2. **State/Provincial Government**: Looks after the day-to-day administration of individual states.\n  3. **Local Self-Government** (in India): Third tier added via the 73rd and 74th Constitutional Amendments (1992).\n* **Key Features of Federalism (7 Principles)**:\n  1. **Multiple Tiers**: There are two or more levels of government.\n  2. **Specific Jurisdiction**: Different tiers of government govern the same citizens, but each tier has its own *jurisdiction* (legal authority) in specific matters of legislation, taxation, and administration.\n  3. **Constitutional Guarantee**: The jurisdictions of the respective levels or tiers of government are specified in the Constitution, granting constitutional status and protection.\n  4. **Rigid Constitutional Modification**: The fundamental provisions of the Constitution cannot be unilaterally changed by one level of government. Such changes require the consent of both levels of government.\n  5. **Judicial Umpiring**: Courts (especially the Supreme Court) have the power to interpret the Constitution and the powers of different levels of government. The highest court acts as an umpire if disputes arise between different levels of government in the exercise of their respective powers.\n  6. **Financial Autonomy**: Sources of revenue for each level of government are clearly specified to ensure its financial independence.\n  7. **Dual Objectives**: The federal system has dual objectives:\n     * To safeguard and promote the **unity** of the country.\n     * To accommodate **regional diversity**.\n\n---"},{"heading":"2. Routes of Federation Formation","content":"| Feature / Criteria | \"Coming Together\" Federations | \"Holding Together\" Federations |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Origin & Process** | Independent States voluntarily come together on their own to form a bigger unit to pool sovereignty and retain identity. | A large country decides to divide its power between the constituent states and the national government. |\n| **Objective** | To increase security and efficiency by pooling resources while preserving state sovereignty. | To accommodate regional, cultural, and linguistic diversity within a single nation. |\n| **Power Distribution** | All constituent States usually have equal power and are strong vis-à-vis the central government. | The Central Government tends to be more powerful vis-à-vis the States; constituent units often have unequal powers. |\n| **Examples** | **USA**, **Switzerland**, **Australia**. | **India**, **Spain**, **Belgium**. |\n\n---"},{"heading":"3. What Makes India a Federal Country? (Constitutional Division of Powers)","content":"The Indian Constitution explicitly provides a **three-fold distribution of legislative powers** between the Union Government and the State Governments across three main lists:\n\n1. **Union List**:\n   * Contains subjects of **national importance** requiring a uniform policy throughout the country.\n   * **Key Subjects**: Defence, Foreign Affairs, Banking, Communications, Currency, Atomic Energy.\n   * **Legislative Power**: The **Union Parliament alone** can make laws on these subjects.\n2. **State List**:\n   * Contains subjects of **state and local importance**.\n   * **Key Subjects**: Police, Trade, Commerce, Agriculture, Irrigation, Public Health.\n   * **Legislative Power**: The **State Legislative Assembly alone** can make laws on these subjects under normal circumstances.\n3. **Concurrent List**:\n   * Contains subjects of **common interest** to both the Union Government and State Governments.\n   * **Key Subjects**: Education, Forests, Trade Unions, Marriage, Adoption, Succession.\n   * **Legislative Power**: Both Parliament and State Assemblies can make laws. **Conflict Rule**: If their laws conflict with each other, the law made by the **Union Parliament prevails**.\n4. **Residuary Subjects**:\n   * Subjects that do not fall in any of the three lists, or subjects that came up after the Constitution was drafted (e.g., Computer Software, Information Technology, Cyber Law).\n   * **Legislative Power**: According to the Constitution, the **Union Government** has the sole power to legislate on residuary subjects."},{"heading":"Special Constitutional Status & Union Territories","content":"* **Unequal Powers Among States**: Not all states in India have identical powers. Some states enjoy special status under Article 371 (e.g., Nagaland, Mizoram, Assam, Arunachal Pradesh) due to social/historical circumstances, protecting indigenous cultures and land rights.\n* **Union Territories (UTs)**: These are areas that are too small to become an independent State but could not be merged with any existing State (e.g., Chandigarh, Lakshadweep, Delhi). These territories do not have the powers of a State and are administered directly by the **Central Government**.\n* **Amendment Procedure**: The basic structure of power sharing cannot be easily altered. Any change must first be passed by both Houses of Parliament with at least a **two-thirds majority**, and then ratified by the legislatures of **at least half of the total States**.\n\n---"},{"heading":"4. How is Federalism Practised in India?","content":"Federalism in India has succeeded not merely due to constitutional provisions, but primarily because of the democratic politics of the country.\n\n1. **Linguistic States**:\n   * The creation of Linguistic States was the first and a major test for democratic politics in India.\n   * In 1956 (States Reorganisation Act), borders of several old states were changed to ensure that people who spoke the same language lived in the same State (e.g., Andhra Pradesh, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu).\n   * Some states were created not on language basis but to recognize differences based on culture, ethnicity, or geography (e.g., Nagaland, Uttarakhand, Jharkhand).\n   * **Outcome**: Experience has shown that creating linguistic states actually made the country more united and administration easier.\n2. **Language Policy**:\n   * A second test for Indian federation was the language policy.\n   * **No National Language**: The Constitution did not give the status of national language to any one language. Hindi was identified as the official language (spoken by ~40% of Indians).\n   * **22 Scheduled Languages**: Besides Hindi, 21 other languages are recognised as Scheduled Languages by the Constitution in the **Eighth Schedule**. A candidate in an all-India central government examination may opt to take the examination in any of these languages.\n   * **Official Use of English**: According to the Constitution, the use of English for official purposes was to stop in 1965. However, non-Hindi speaking states (especially Tamil Nadu) demanded that the use of English continue. The Central Government responded by agreeing to continue the use of English along with Hindi for official purposes.\n3. **Centre-State Relations**:\n   * **Pre-1990 Era**: For a long time, the same party ruled both at the Centre and in most States. When rival parties ruled at the State level, the Central Government often misused Article 356 to dismiss State Governments arbitrarily.\n   * **Post-1990 Era (Coalition Era)**: After 1990, regional political parties emerged across many states. Since no single national party secured a clear majority in the Lok Sabha, major national parties had to form **Coalition Governments** with regional parties.\n   * **Impact**: This led to a new culture of power sharing, respect for State autonomy, and was backed by a landmark Supreme Court judgment making it difficult for the Centre to dismiss state governments arbitrarily.\n\n---"},{"heading":"5. Decentralisation in India (3rd Tier of Governance)","content":"* **Definition**: When power is taken away from Central and State governments and given to local government, it is called **Decentralisation**.\n* **Rationale**:\n  * Large number of problems and issues are best settled at the local level.\n  * Local people have better knowledge of problems in their localities, where to spend money, and how to manage things efficiently.\n  * Facilitates direct participation of citizens in decision-making, instilling democratic habits."},{"heading":"The 1992 Constitutional Amendment (73rd & 74th Amendments)","content":"Before 1992, local bodies were under the direct control of state governments, elections were irregular, and local bodies lacked resources. The 1992 Amendment made decentralisation effective through **5 key constitutional mandates**:\n\n1. **Mandatory Regular Elections**: It is constitutionally mandatory to hold regular elections to local government bodies.\n2. **Reserved Seats for Disadvantaged Groups**: Seats are reserved in elected bodies and executive heads for Scheduled Castes (SCs), Scheduled Tribes (STs), and Other Backward Classes (OBCs).\n3. **Women Representation**: At least **one-third (33%)** of all positions are reserved for women.\n4. **State Election Commission (SEC)**: An independent body called the State Election Commission has been created in each State to conduct panchayat and municipal elections.\n5. **State Finance Commission & Power Sharing**: State governments are required to share some powers and revenues with local government bodies.\n\n---"},{"heading":"6. Structure of Local Self-Government in India","content":"```\n                          LOCAL SELF-GOVERNMENT\n                                    |\n          +-------------------------+-------------------------+\n          |                                                   |\n  RURAL LOCAL GOVERNMENT                            URBAN LOCAL GOVERNMENT\n  (Panchayati Raj System)                           (Urban Local Bodies)\n          |                                                   |\n  1. Gram Panchayat (Village Level)                 1. Municipalities (Towns)\n     - Sarpanch & Panchs                               - Municipal Chairperson\n     - Supervised by Gram Sabha                               |\n          |                                         2. Municipal Corporations\n  2. Panchayat Samiti / Block (Block Level)            (Large Cities)\n     - Group of Gram Panchayats                        - Headed by Mayor\n     - BDO & Samiti Chairperson                               |\n          |                                                   |\n  3. Zilla Parishad (District Level)                          |\n     - All Samitis in a District                              |\n     - Headed by Zilla Parishad Chairperson                   |\n```"},{"heading":"A. Rural Local Government (Panchayati Raj)","content":"1. **Gram Panchayat**:\n   * Decision-making body for a village or group of villages.\n   * Consists of several ward members (*Panchs*) and a President (*Sarpanch*), directly elected by all adult citizens residing in that ward/village.\n2. **Gram Sabha**:\n   * The general body consisting of **all adult voters** in a village.\n   * Meets at least 2 to 3 times a year to approve the annual budget of the Gram Panchayat and review its performance.\n3. **Panchayat Samiti / Block / Mandal**:\n   * Formed by grouping a few Gram Panchayats together at the block level.\n   * Members are elected by all the panchayat members in that area.\n4. **Zilla Parishad**:\n   * The apex body at the district level, comprising all Panchayat Samitis in a district.\n   * Members include elected representatives, local Members of Parliament (MPs), Members of Legislative Assembly (MLAs), and district officials.\n   * The **Zilla Parishad Chairperson** is the political head of the Zilla Parishad."},{"heading":"B. Urban Local Government","content":"1. **Municipalities**:\n   * Set up in towns and smaller urban areas.\n   * Headed by a **Municipal Chairperson** (political head).\n2. **Municipal Corporations**:\n   * Set up in big cities (e.g., Jaipur, Jodhpur, Kota, Delhi, Mumbai).\n   * Headed by an elected political head called the **Mayor**.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Technical Glossary","content":"* **Jurisdiction**: The officially defined legal field over which someone has authority. The boundary may be defined in terms of geographical area or in terms of specific subjects.\n* **Coalition Government**: A government formed together by an alliance of two or more political parties when no single party wins a clear majority in elections.\n* **Decentralisation**: The transfer of power, decision-making, and resources from central/state governments to local self-governing bodies.\n* **Residuary Powers**: Legislative powers over topics not enumerated in Union, State, or Concurrent lists, assigned exclusively to the Union Parliament.\n* **Scheduled Languages**: The 22 languages listed in the Eighth Schedule of the Indian Constitution.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Verified Previous-Year Board Questions (PYQs)","content":""},{"heading":"1. Very Short Answer & Objective Questions (1 Mark)","content":"**Q1. How many languages are included in the Eighth Schedule of the Indian Constitution?**  \n*(Source: BSER 2026–27 Official Model Paper, Q1-vii)*  \n* **Answer**: **22 languages** (including Hindi and 21 other scheduled languages).\n\n**Q2. Which level of government in India has the power to legislate on ''Residuary Subjects''?**  \n*(Source: BSER Secondary Examination 2024, Paper Code S-08)*  \n* **Answer**: The **Union / Central Government** (Parliament) has the sole power to legislate on residuary subjects.\n\n**Q3. Which language was identified as the official language of the Indian Union?**  \n*(Source: BSER Secondary Examination 2023, Paper Code S-08)*  \n* **Answer**: **Hindi** was identified as the official language of India.\n\n**Q4. What is the political head of a Municipal Corporation called?**  \n*(Source: BSER Secondary Examination 2022, Paper Code S-08)*  \n* **Answer**: The political head of a Municipal Corporation is called the **Mayor**.\n\n---"},{"heading":"2. Short Answer Questions (2 & 3 Marks)","content":"**Q5. Define ''Decentralisation''. Mention two main steps taken in the 1992 Constitutional Amendment to make decentralisation effective.**  \n*(Source: BSER Secondary Examination 2024, Paper Code S-08, Weightage: 2 Marks)*  \n* **Model Answer**:\n  * **Definition**: When power is taken away from Central and State governments and given to local self-governments, it is called **Decentralisation**.\n  * **Two Steps of 1992 Amendment**:\n    1. **Mandatory Regular Elections**: It was made constitutionally mandatory to hold regular elections to local government bodies.\n    2. **Women Reservation**: At least **one-third (33%)** of all positions in local bodies were reserved for women.\n\n**Q6. Differentiate between ''Coming Together'' Federations and ''Holding Together'' Federations with one example of each.**  \n*(Source: BSER Supplementary Examination 2024, Paper Code S-124, Weightage: 2 Marks)*  \n* **Model Answer**:\n  1. **Coming Together Federations**: Independent states voluntarily come together on their own to form a bigger unit, pooling sovereignty while retaining identity (e.g., **USA**, Switzerland).\n  2. **Holding Together Federations**: A large country decides to divide its power between constituent states and the national government (e.g., **India**, Spain, Belgium).\n\n**Q7. Explain any three key features of the federal system in India.**  \n*(Source: BSER Secondary Examination 2023, Paper Code S-08, Weightage: 3 Marks)*  \n* **Model Answer**:\n  1. **Three-Tier System**: India has three levels of government—Union, State, and Local (Panchayats and Municipalities).\n  2. **Three-Fold Distribution of Legislative Powers**: The Constitution explicitly divides legislative powers between Union and States into Union List, State List, and Concurrent List.\n  3. **Judicial Supremacy**: The Supreme Court acts as an umpire to resolve constitutional disputes between the Union and State governments.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Practice Bank & Original Predicted Questions","content":""},{"heading":"1. Short Answer Questions (2 & 3 Marks)","content":"**Q1. Describe the structure of the Rural Local Government (Panchayati Raj System) in India.**  \n* **Model Answer**:\n  1. **Gram Panchayat**: Operating at the village level, it consists of elected ward members (*Panchs*) and a President (*Sarpanch*), supervised by the **Gram Sabha** (body of all adult voters).\n  2. **Panchayat Samiti**: Formed at the block level by grouping several Gram Panchayats together, headed by a Block Development Officer / Samiti Chairperson.\n  3. **Zilla Parishad**: The apex district-level body comprising all Panchayat Samitis, local MPs, MLAs, and district officials, headed by the **Zilla Parishad Chairperson**.\n\n**Q2. How has the Language Policy of India strengthened national unity? Explain.**  \n* **Model Answer**:\n  * **No Single National Language**: Avoiding imposing Hindi on non-Hindi speaking states prevented linguistic conflict.\n  * **22 Scheduled Languages**: Constitutional recognition of 22 languages ensured equality and regional dignity.\n  * **Bilingual Official Use**: Allowing English alongside Hindi for official communication satisfied non-Hindi speaking regions (like Tamil Nadu) and consolidated national integration.\n\n---"},{"heading":"2. Original Predicted Board Question (2027 Examination Focus - 4 Marks)","content":"**Q3. \"Decentralisation post-1992 transformed local self-governance in India.\" Evaluate this statement by examining the 5 key provisions of the 73rd and 74th Constitutional Amendments.**  \n* **Model Answer**:\n  * **Introduction**: Prior to 1992, local bodies were under direct state control, lacked financial independence, and suffered irregular elections. The 1992 Amendments provided constitutional status to local self-government.\n  * **5 Key Constitutional Provisions**:\n    1. **Mandatory Regular Elections**: State governments must conduct local elections every 5 years without arbitrary delays.\n    2. **Reservation for SCs, STs, and OBCs**: Promotes inclusive social representation at the grass-root level.\n    3. **33% Reservation for Women**: Empowered over 10 lakh women leaders in rural and urban local bodies.\n    4. **State Election Commission (SEC)**: Created an independent constitutional authority in every state to conduct free and fair local elections.\n    5. **State Finance Commission (SFC)**: Mandated state governments to share statutory revenue and administrative powers with local bodies.\n  * **Conclusion**: These provisions transformed local self-government into the world''s largest democratic experiment, deepening democracy at the grassroots level.\n\n---"},{"heading":"15-Question MCQ Quiz","content":"```\nQ1. Which of the following is NOT a subject of the Union List in India?\n    (a) Defence\n    (b) Banking\n    (c) Police\n    (d) Foreign Affairs\n    Answer: (c) Police (Police is a State List subject).\n\nQ2. System of government in which power is divided between a central authority and constituent units is called:\n    (a) Unitary\n    (b) Federalism\n    (c) Autocracy\n    (d) Monarchy\n    Answer: (b) Federalism.\n\nQ3. Which of the following is an example of a \"Coming Together\" Federation?\n    (a) India\n    (b) Spain\n    (c) Belgium\n    (d) USA\n    Answer: (d) USA.\n\nQ4. In case of a conflict between the Central and State laws on a subject in the Concurrent List, whose law prevails?\n    (a) State Government\n    (b) Union Government\n    (c) Supreme Court\n    (d) President\n    Answer: (b) Union Government.\n\nQ5. Computer Software and Information Technology subjects fall under which legislative power?\n    (a) Union List\n    (b) State List\n    (c) Concurrent List\n    (d) Residuary Subjects\n    Answer: (d) Residuary Subjects.\n\nQ6. How many languages are listed in the Eighth Schedule of the Indian Constitution?\n    (a) 18\n    (b) 20\n    (c) 22\n    (d) 24\n    Answer: (c) 22.\n\nQ7. What percentage of seats in local government bodies are reserved for women in India?\n    (a) 20%\n    (b) 25%\n    (c) 33% (One-third)\n    (d) 50%\n    Answer: (c) 33% (One-third).\n\nQ8. Which constitutional amendment gave constitutional status to Rural Local Self-Government in India?\n    (a) 42nd Amendment\n    (b) 44th Amendment\n    (c) 73rd Amendment\n    (d) 86th Amendment\n    Answer: (c) 73rd Amendment.\n\nQ9. Who is the political head of a Municipal Corporation?\n    (a) Sarpanch\n    (b) Municipal Chairperson\n    (c) Mayor\n    (d) Governor\n    Answer: (c) Mayor.\n\nQ10. Which body conducts elections to Panchayats and Municipalities in a State?\n     (a) Election Commission of India\n     (b) State Election Commission\n     (c) Supreme Court\n     (d) High Court\n     Answer: (b) State Election Commission.\n\nQ11. The general body consisting of all adult voters in a village is called the:\n     (a) Gram Panchayat\n     (b) Gram Sabha\n     (c) Panchayat Samiti\n     (d) Zilla Parishad\n     Answer: (b) Gram Sabha.\n\nQ12. Which state in India was created on the basis of culture, ethnicity, or geography rather than language?\n     (a) Andhra Pradesh\n     (b) Nagaland\n     (c) Maharashtra\n     (d) Punjab\n     Answer: (b) Nagaland.\n\nQ13. What is the apex body of the Panchayati Raj system at the district level?\n     (a) Gram Sabha\n     (b) Panchayat Samiti\n     (c) Zilla Parishad\n     (d) Municipal Corporation\n     Answer: (c) Zilla Parishad.\n\nQ14. An alliance formed by two or more political parties to form a government when no single party gets a majority is called:\n     (a) Federal Government\n     (b) Unitary Government\n     (c) Coalition Government\n     (d) Community Government\n     Answer: (c) Coalition Government.\n\nQ15. Which article of the Constitution provides special provisions to some North-Eastern states like Nagaland?\n     (a) Article 356\n     (b) Article 370\n     (c) Article 371\n     (d) Article 360\n     Answer: (c) Article 371.\n```\n\n---"},{"heading":"Syllabus Coverage Checklist & Source Inventory","content":""},{"heading":"Sub-Topic Coverage Matrix","content":"1. [x] Definition & Key Features of Federalism\n2. [x] Routes of Federations (\"Coming Together\" vs. \"Holding Together\")\n3. [x] Constitutional Division of Powers (Union, State, Concurrent, Residuary Lists)\n4. [x] Special Constitutional Status & Union Territories\n5. [x] Amendment Procedure for Federal Provisions\n6. [x] Practice of Federalism: Linguistic States Reorganisation\n7. [x] Practice of Federalism: Language Policy & 22 Scheduled Languages\n8. [x] Practice of Federalism: Centre-State Relations & Coalition Government Era\n9. [x] Decentralisation Concept & Need\n10. [x] 1992 Constitutional Amendments (73rd & 74th) Key Provisions\n11. [x] Rural Local Self-Government (Gram Panchayat, Gram Sabha, Panchayat Samiti, Zilla Parishad)\n12. [x] Urban Local Self-Government (Municipalities & Municipal Corporations / Mayor)"},{"heading":"Master Source Inventory","content":"* **Primary Conceptual Source**: *Democratic Politics – II* (Class X NCERT Textbook), Accredited by BSER Ajmer.\n* **Syllabus & Blueprint Directives**: BSER Secondary Syllabus Notification 2026–27 (`10_2027.pdf`, p. 38) & Official BSER Model Paper (`Social-Science-10.pdf`).\n* **Verified Exam Archives**:\n  * BSER Secondary Board Main Exam Paper 2024 (Subject Code 08 / S-08).\n  * BSER Secondary Board Main Exam Paper 2023 (Subject Code 08 / S-08).\n  * BSER Secondary Board Main Exam Paper 2022 (Subject Code 08 / S-08).\n  * BSER Secondary Board Supplementary Exam Paper 2024 (Subject Code 124 / S-124 D&D)."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'social-science-15-notes',
  'social-science',
  'social-science-15',
  'Gender, Religion and Caste',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Gender, Religion and Caste","subject":"social-science","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"RBSE Class 10 Social Science — Academic Session 2026–27 (2027 Examination)","content":""},{"heading":"Discipline: Political Science (Democratic Politics – II)","content":""},{"heading":"Chapter ID: sst-15 | Chapter Title: Gender, Religion and Caste","content":"---"},{"heading":"SECTION 1: EXECUTIVE SYLLABUS & PRESCRIPTION VERIFICATION REPORT","content":"* **Official Subject Code**: Social Science — Code 08 (Social Science)\n* **Prescribed Textbook**: *Democratic Politics – II*, Class X, published by BSER Ajmer / NCERT.\n* **Prescription Status**: **100% Fully Prescribed** for the 2026–27 Academic Session and the 2027 Secondary Board Examination.\n* **Board Marks Allocation**: **04 Marks** out of 80 Theory Marks in Social Science (Political Science Total Unit Weightage: 20 Marks across 5 prescribed chapters).\n* **Rationalisation & Exclusion Verification**:\n  * Verified against official BSER Curriculum Notification (`10_2027.pdf`, p. 38) and the NCERT Rationalised Content Guide.\n  * **Textual Rationalisation**: Selected decorative images on pages 46, 48, and 49 of the NCERT textbook were omitted in NCERT''s rationalization drive, but **100% of the textual concepts and sub-topics are fully active and examinable**.\\n  * **Strict Non-Contamination Policy**: Non-prescribed/rationalised Civics chapters (*Democracy and Diversity*, *Popular Struggles and Movements*, and *Challenges to Democracy*) are strictly excluded from assessment.\\n* **BSER Board Examination Blueprint Pattern**:\\n  * **04 Marks Total Weightage**, typically formatted as:\\n    * Option A: 1 Short Answer Question (03 Marks) + 1 Objective/MCQ Question (01 Mark)\\n    * Option B: 1 Long/Essay Answer Question (04 Marks) with internal choice\\n    * Option C: 2 Short Answer Questions (02 Marks + 02 Marks)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SECTION 2: CHAPTER METADATA & BOARD BLUEPRINT ARCHITECTURE\\n\\n```\\n===============================================================================================\\n                                 BOARDREADY CHAPTER METADATA MATRIX\\n===============================================================================================\\nSubject Name : Social Science\\nSubject Code : 08\\nDiscipline : Political Science / Civics (Democratic Politics – II)\\nWorking Chapter ID : sst-15\\nNCERT Textbook Ch. No.   : Chapter 3 (formerly Chapter 4 prior to rationalization)\\nChapter Title (English) : Gender, Religion and Caste\\nChapter Title (Hindi) : Caste, Religion and Gender Issues\\nExam Target Session : 2026–27 Academic Session (March 2027 Examination)\\nTheory Paper Marks : 04 Marks (out of 80 Total Theory Marks)\\nSessional Internal Marks : 20 Marks (Internal School Assessment)\\nWritten Exam Time : 3 Hours 15 Minutes\\nQuestion Types Tested : Objective/MCQ (1 Mark), Short Answer (2 or 3 Marks), Essay Choice (4 Marks)\\nPrimary Source Text : NCERT Democratic Politics – II (BSER Prescribed Edition)\\nOfficial Paper References: BSER Main Papers 2024, 2023, 2022; BSER D&D 2024; BSER 2026–27 Model Paper\\n===============================================================================================\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SECTION 3: SYLLABUS-ALIGNED COMPLETE REVISION NOTES\\n\\n### THEME 1: GENDER AND POLITICS\\n\\n#### 1. Public / Private Division of Labor (Gender Division of Labour)\\n* **Sexual Division of Labour**: A system in which all work inside the home is either done by the women of the family or organized by them through domestic helpers, while men work outside to earn a livelihood.\\n* **Nature of the Division**: This division is **not based on biology**, but on **social expectations and stereotypes**.\\n* **Impact on Women**:\\n  * Domestic work (cooking, cleaning, washing, tailoring, child-rearing) is considered non-productive and receives **no monetary value or recognition**.\\n  * Women''s role in public life remains minimal in most societies, leading to their exclusion from political decision-making."},{"heading":"2. Feminist Movements","content":"* **Definition of Feminist**: A woman or a man who believes in equal rights and opportunities for women and men.\n* **Objectives of Feminist Movements**:\n  * Agitations demanding equal voting rights (suffrage) and political status for women.\n  * Demands for enhancing educational and career opportunities.\n  * Movements aiming at equality in private and personal domestic lives (family laws, equal wages, protection against domestic violence).\n* **Outcome**: These movements improved women''s role in public life. In Scandinavian countries like **Sweden, Norway, and Finland**, the participation of women in public life is exceptionally high.\\n\\n#### 3. Status of Women in Indian Society\\nIndia remains a **patriarchal society** (a system that values men more and gives them power over women). Women face discrimination, disadvantage, and oppression in various forms:\\n1. **Literacy Rate Gap**:\\n   * The literacy rate among women is **only 54%** compared to **76% among men** (as per textbook baseline census figures).\\n   * Parents prefer spending resources on their sons'' higher education rather than daughters'', leading to higher female dropout rates.\n2. **Unpaid & Undervalued Work**:\n   * On average, an Indian woman works **one hour more per day** than an average man, yet much of her domestic and agricultural work is unpaid and unrecognized.\n3. **Unequal Wages (Equal Remuneration Act, 1976)**:\n   * Despite the **Equal Remuneration Act 1976** stipulating equal wages for equal work, women are paid less than men in sports, cinema, agriculture, construction, and factories.\n4. **Sex Ratio & Female Foeticide**:\n   * Preference for male children leads to sex-selective abortion (female foeticide).\n   * The child sex ratio in India dropped below 919 girls per 1000 boys in several states (and below 900 in regions like Punjab, Haryana, and parts of Rajasthan).\n5. **Urban Crime & Domestic Violence**:\n   * Urban areas have become increasingly unsafe for women, who face harassment, exploitation, domestic violence, and sexual assault inside and outside the home."},{"heading":"4. Women''s Political Representation (Women''s Political Representation)","content":"* **Under-Representation in India**:\n  * The percentage of elected women MPs in the Lok Sabha crossed **14%** for the first time in 2019, which is far below the global average.\n  * Women''s representation in state legislative assemblies (including Rajasthan Vidhan Sabha) is **less than 10%**.\n* **Institutional Remedy — Local Self-Government**:\n  * Under the 73rd and 74th Constitutional Amendments (1992), **one-third (33%) of seats** in local government bodies (Panchayats and Municipalities) are legally reserved for women.\n  * Today, there are over **10 lakh (1 million) elected women representatives** in rural and urban local bodies across India.\n* **National Legislative Reservation**:\n  * Women''s organizations long demanded a similar 33% reservation in the Lok Sabha and State Assemblies.\n  * Note: The *Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam* (106th Constitutional Amendment Act, 2023) passed by Parliament legally enacts 33% reservation for women in Lok Sabha and Assemblies, set to take effect following delimitation.\n\n---"},{"heading":"THEME 2: RELIGION, COMMUNALISM AND POLITICS (Religion, Communalism and Politics)","content":""},{"heading":"1. Relationship Between Religion and Politics","content":"Religion and politics can interact in both constructive and destructive ways:\n* **Gandhiji''s View**: Mahatma Gandhi stated that *\"Religion can never be separated from politics.\"* By religion, he did not mean any particular religion like Hinduism or Islam, but **moral values and ethics** drawn from all religions that must guide politics.\n* **Human Rights View**: Human rights groups demand that the government take special steps to protect religious minorities against communal violence.\n* **Women''s Movement View**: The women''s movement argues that **Family Laws** (laws dealing with marriage, divorce, adoption, and inheritance) of all religions discriminate against women, demanding that the state reform these laws to ensure gender justice.\\n\\n#### 2. Communalism\\n* **Definition of Communalism**: An ideology that views **religion as the principal basis of social community**, holding that followers of a particular religion belong to one community with identical interests, while people of different religions cannot belong to the same social community.\\n* **Communal Mindset**:\\n  * Assumes that differences among followers of the same religion are trivial, while differences across religions are fundamental.\\n  * In its extreme form, communalism asserts that people of different religions cannot live as equal citizens within one nation — either one group must dominate or they must form separate nations.\\n\\n#### 3. Forms of Communalism in Politics\\n1. **Everyday Prejudices**: Routine religious prejudices, stereotypes of religious communities, and belief in the superiority of one''s religion over others.\n2. **Quest for Political Dominance**: A desire for political dominance of one''s religious community. Majoritarian community members seek majoritarian dominance; minority community members seek separate political units.\\n3. **Political Mobilization on Religious Lines**: Use of sacred symbols, religious leaders, emotional appeals, and plain fear to bring voters of one religion together in the political arena.\\n4. **Communal Violence & Riots**: The most ugly form of communalism involving communal violence, massacres, and riots (e.g., severe communal riots during the 1947 Partition and post-independence incidents).\\n\\n#### 4. Secular State Provisions in the Indian Constitution\\nThe framers of the Indian Constitution recognized communalism as a major threat to democracy and explicitly chose the **model of a secular state**. The constitutional provisions ensuring secularism are:\\n1. **No Official Religion**: Unlike Sri Lanka (Buddhism), Pakistan (Islam), or Great Britain (Christianity), India has **no state or official religion**.\\n2. **Freedom of Religion**: Article 25 guarantees all individuals and communities the freedom to profess, practice, and propagate any religion, or not to follow any.\\n3. **Prohibition of Discrimination**: Article 15 prohibits discrimination against any citizen on grounds only of religion, race, caste, sex, or place of birth.\\n4. **State Power to Intervene**: The Constitution empowers the state to intervene in religious matters to ensure equality within religious communities (e.g., banning **untouchability** under Article 17 and reforming discriminatory personal laws).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### THEME 3: CASTE AND POLITICS\\n\\n#### 1. Caste Inequalities & Hierarchy\\n* **Caste System**: An extreme form of division of labor based on hereditary occupational division, sanctified by social ritual and endogamy (marrying within the caste group).\\n* **Untouchability**: An inhuman practice where members of''outcaste'' or ''lower caste'' groups were excluded and subjected to severe social discrimination.\\n* **Breakdown of the Caste Hierarchy**: Social reformers like **Jyotiba Phule, Mahatma Gandhi, Dr. B.R. Ambedkar, and Periyar E.V. Ramaswamy** advocated a society free of caste prejudices.\\n* **Modern Factors Disrupting Caste Distinctions**:\\n  * Economic growth and industrialisation.\\n  * Large-scale **urbanisation** (impersonal city living where caste identities are less visible).\\n  * Expansion of **literacy and education**.\\n  * **Occupational mobility** (shift away from hereditary occupations).\\n  * Weakening of the position of landlords in villages.\\n  * Constitutional prohibition of castediscrimination.\\n\\n#### 2. Caste IN Politics\\nCaste manifests in politics in several distinct ways:\\n1. **Nomination of Candidates**: Political parties keep the caste composition of the electorate in mind when selecting candidates for elections to secure winning majorities.\\n2. **Cabinet Formation**: When forming governments, political parties ensure that representatives of different castes and tribes find a place in the council of ministers.\\n3. **Appeals to Caste Sentiment**: Political parties and candidates make explicit appeals to caste sentiments during election campaigns (\"caste vote bank\").\\n4. **Political Awakening of Disadvantaged Castes**: Universal adult franchise and''one person, one vote'' compelled political leaders to mobilize lower and backward castes, creating a new politicalconsciousness among Dalits and OBCs.\\n\\n###Critical Myth-Busting regarding Caste in Elections:\\n* **Elections are NOT purely about caste**:\\n * No parliamentary or assembly constituency in India has a clear single-caste majority. Every candidate needs voters from multiple castes. **Coalition of Sub-Castes**: Each caste group tries to become bigger by incorporating within it neighboring sub-castes that were previously excluded.\\n2. **Forming Caste Coalitions**: Various caste groups enter into coalitions or negotiations with other castes, engaging in dialogue and power-sharing.\\n3. **New Caste Groupings**: New political caste categories have emerged in the political vocabulary, such as **''Backward'' and ''Forward''caste groups**, or Dalit and Bahujan alliances.\\n\\n#### 4. Positive and Negative Effects of Caste in Politics\\n* **Positive Impact**:\\n * Gives voice to historically marginalized castes (Dalits, Adivasis, OBCs) to demand their fair share of power, resources, land, and educational access.\\n* **Negative Impact**:\\n * Exclusive focus on caste diverts attention from urgent public issues like poverty, corruption, infrastructure, and development.\\n * Can lead to caste violence, social conflict, and fragmentation of society.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SECTION 4: KEY TECHNICAL GLOSSARY & CONCEPTUAL DEFINITIONS\\n\\n1. **Sexual Division of Labor**: A system in which all work inside the home is done by women or organized by them, while men work outside.\\n2. **Feminist**: A woman or man who believes in equal rights and opportunities for women and men.\\n3. **Patriarchy**: A social system ruled by men, where women are subordinate and men hold primary power.\\n4. **Family Laws**: Laws dealing with family-related matters such as marriage, divorce, adoption, guardianship, and inheritance. In India, different personal laws apply to followers of different religions.\\n5. **Urbanisation**: The shift of population from rural areas to urban settlements.\\n6. **Occupational Mobility**: Shift from one occupation to another, especially away from ancestral or hereditary occupations.\\n7. **Caste Hierarchy**: A ladder-like social structure where all caste groups are placed from highest to lowest.\\n8. **Communalism**: An ideology that considers religion as the principal or sole basis of a social community, fostering hostility toward other religious groups.\\n9. **Secular State**: A state that has no official religion, treats all religions equally, and guarantees freedom of faith to all citizens. Significance\\n===============================================================================================\\n1947 Partition of British India Communal violence and mass displacement shape India''s secular constitutional choice.\n1950 (26 January)      Enactment of Constitution of India                     Guarantees Articles 15 (Non-discrimination), 17 (Abolition of Untouchability), & 25 (Religious Freedom).\n1976                   Equal Remuneration Act, 1976                           Mandates equal wages for equal work regardless of gender.\n1992                   73rd and 74th Constitutional Amendments                Mandates 33% (1/3rd) seat reservation for women in Panchayats and Municipalities.\n2019                   17th Lok Sabha Elections                               Elected women MPs crossed 14% for the first time in Indian parliamentary history.\n2023                   106th Constitutional Amendment Act                     Passes Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam reserving 33% seats for women in Lok Sabha and Assemblies.\n========================================================================================================\n```\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 6: VERIFIED PREVIOUS-YEAR BOARD QUESTIONS (PYQs)","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1 (1 Mark — Objective / MCQ)","content":"**Which of the following statements best defines a ''Feminist''?** [BSER 2026–27 Model Paper Q9; BSER 2022 Main Q1(vii)]  \n(A) A person who believes that men are superior to women  \n(B) A person who believes in equal rights and opportunities for women and men  \n(C) A person who supports dictatorial governance  \n(D) A person who believes caste is the basis of community  \n**Model Answer**: **(B) A person who believes in equal rights and opportunities for women and men.**\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 2 (1 Mark — Objective / MCQ)","content":"**A person who considers ''caste'' as the main basis of social community is called a:** [BSER 2024 Main Paper Q1(xi)]  \n(A) Secularist  \n(B) Casteist  \n(C) Communalist  \n(D) Feminist  \n**Model Answer**: **(B) Casteist** *(Note: If religion is considered the basis of community, it is called a Communalist).*\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 3 (2 Marks — Very Short Answer)","content":"**Explain any two forms of discrimination faced by women in Indian society.** [BSER 2024 Main Paper Q10]  \n**Model Answer**:\n1. **Educational Inequality**: The literacy rate among women (54%) is significantly lower than among men (76%) because parents prioritize spending resources on their sons'' education.\n2. **Wage Discrimination**: Despite the Equal Remuneration Act 1976, women are frequently paid less than men for equal work in agriculture, construction, and manufacturing.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 4 (4 Marks — Essay Question with Choice)","content":"**\"Indian society is still patriarchal, in which women face discrimination.\" Discuss the statement in detail.** [BSER 2024 Main Paper Q19]  \n**OR**  \n**\"The Indian Constitution makers have chosen the model of secular governance.\" Discuss the statement in detail.** [BSER 2024 Main Paper Q19 OR; BSER 2022 Main Paper Q22]  \n\n**Model Answer (Option 1 — Patriarchal Discrimination)**:\nIndian society remains patriarchal, where men hold primary power and women face systemic discrimination across several spheres:\n1. **Low Literacy Rate**: The female literacy rate stands at 54% compared to 76% for males. Higher education for girls is often discouraged, resulting in higher female dropout rates.\n2. **Unpaid Domestic Labour**: Women perform all household chores and child-rearing. On average, Indian women work one hour more per day than men, yet their labour is unpaid and unvalued.\n3. **Wage Inequality**: In sports, cinema, agriculture, and construction, female workers are paid lower wages than male workers for equal work.\n4. **Sex-Selective Abortions & Sex Ratio**: Preference for male heirs leads to illegal female foeticide, causing the child sex ratio to drop below 900 girls per 1000 boys in several states.\n\n**Model Answer (Option 2 — Secular Governance Model)**:\nTo combat communalism and protect religious diversity, the framers of the Indian Constitution adopted a secular state model:\n1. **No State Religion**: Unlike Sri Lanka (Buddhism) or Pakistan (Islam), the Indian Constitution bestows no official status on any religion.\n2. **Freedom of Religion**: Article 25 guarantees all citizens the right to profess, practice, and propagate any religion, or no religion at all.\n3. **Prohibition of Religious Discrimination**: Article 15 prohibits state discrimination against any citizen on grounds of religion.\n4. **State Intervention for Equality**: The Constitution empowers the state to intervene in religious practices to eliminate social evils (e.g., Article 17 banning untouchability and reforming discriminatory family laws).\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 5 (6 Marks — CWSN / Board Essay Format)","content":"**Explain the concept of Communalism and discuss its various forms in politics.** [BSER 2024 D&D Paper Q28 OR]  \n**Model Answer**:\n* **Concept**: Communalism is an ideology based on the belief that religion is the principal basis of social community, and that followers of different religions cannot have shared civic interests.\n* **Forms in Politics**:\n  1. **Everyday Beliefs**: Religious prejudices, stereotypes, and belief in the superiority of one''s own religion.\n  2. **Majoritarian / Political Dominance**: Majoritarian communities seek majoritarian dominance; minority communities seek separate political identity.\n  3. **Political Mobilisation**: Use of sacred symbols, emotional appeals, and fear to gather voters of a single religion.\n  4. **Communal Violence**: Mass riots, violence, and targeted conflicts (e.g., Partition violence).\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 7: PRACTICE BANK & ORIGINAL PREDICTED QUESTIONS (2027 EXAM)","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1 (3 Marks — Short Answer Practice)","content":"**How does caste influence politics in India? State three ways.**  \n**Model Answer**:\n1. **Candidate Selection**: Political parties select candidates based on the caste demographics of constituencies to build winning majorities.\n2. **Cabinet Composition**: Government formations ensure that various caste and tribal groups receive proportional representation in councils of ministers.\n3. **Caste Mobilisation**: Parties make targeted campaign appeals to caste sentiments to consolidate specific \"caste vote banks.\"\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 2 (3 Marks — Short Answer Practice)","content":"**State three factors that have contributed to the weakening of the caste hierarchy in modern India.**  \n**Model Answer**:\n1. **Urbanisation & Economic Growth**: Impersonal city living makes caste identity less visible in daily public life.\n2. **Literacy and Educational Expansion**: Modern education breaks traditional caste prejudices and promotes rational, equal opportunities.\n3. **Occupational Mobility**: Individuals are no longer restricted to their ancestral hereditary occupations.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 3 (4 Marks — Original Predicted Essay Question for 2027)","content":"**\"Communalism can take various forms in politics, while secularism is essential for Indian democracy.\" Evaluate this statement with reference to constitutional provisions.**  \n\n**Model Answer Marking Scheme (4 Marks)**:\n* **Part A: Forms of Communalism (2 Marks)**:\n  * Award 1 mark for defining everyday prejudices and political mobilization using religious symbols.\n  * Award 1 mark for explaining majoritarian dominance and communal violence.\n* **Part B: Constitutional Secular Provisions (2 Marks)**:\n  * Award 1 mark for explaining no official religion and freedom of religion (Article 25).\n  * Award 1 mark for explaining prohibition of discrimination (Article 15) and state intervention against social evils like untouchability.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 8: 15-QUESTION MCQ QUIZ WITH ANSWER KEYS & EXPLANATION NOTES","content":"```\n========================================================================================================\n                                 15-QUESTION COMPREHENSIVE MCQ QUIZ\n========================================================================================================\n```\n\n**Q1. When we talk about gender division in Political Science, we mean:**  \n(A) Biological differences between men and women  \n(B) Inequality in voter turnout during elections  \n(C) Unequal roles assigned to men and women by society  \n(D) Ratio of male to female children in a country  \n* **Answer**: **(C)**  \n* *Explanation*: Gender division is a form of social division based on social expectations and stereotypes, not biological differences.\n\n**Q2. Which official Act in India mandates equal wages for equal work for both men and women?**  \n(A) The Minimum Wages Act, 1948  \n(B) The Equal Remuneration Act, 1976  \n(C) The Maternity Benefit Act, 1961  \n(D) The Factories Act, 1948  \n* **Answer**: **(B)**  \n* *Explanation*: The Equal Remuneration Act 1976 provides for equal remuneration to men and women workers for the same work or work of similar nature.\n\n**Q3. What proportion of seats in local government bodies in India is legally reserved for women?**  \n(A) One-fourth (25%)  \n(B) One-third (33%)  \n(C) One-half (50%)  \n(D) Two-thirds (66%)  \n* **Answer**: **(B)**  \n* *Explanation*: The 73rd and 74th Amendments mandated a minimum 33% (1/3rd) reservation for women in Panchayats and Municipalities.\n\n**Q4. In which of the following regions/countries is the participation of women in public life exceptionally high?**  \n(A) South Asian countries  \n(B) Scandinavian countries (Sweden, Norway, Finland)  \n(C) Arab states  \n(D) Latin American nations  \n* **Answer**: **(B)**  \n* *Explanation*: Scandinavian countries have long prioritized gender equality, leading to very high female representation in public life and parliaments.\n\n**Q5. Who among the following declared that \"Religion can never be separated from politics\"?**  \n(A) Jawaharlal Nehru  \n(B) Mahatma Gandhi  \n(C) Dr. B.R. Ambedkar  \n(D) Karl Marx  \n* **Answer**: **(B)**  \n* *Explanation*: Gandhiji held that politics must be guided by moral ethics and values drawn from religion.\n\n**Q6. What are \"Family Laws\"?**  \n(A) Laws passed by municipal councils regarding housing  \n(B) Laws dealing with family matters like marriage, divorce, adoption, and inheritance  \n(C) Laws prohibiting domestic violence  \n(D) Laws regulating child labour  \n* **Answer**: **(B)**  \n* *Explanation*: Family laws govern personal domestic matters and differ among followers of different religions in India.\n\n**Q7. An ideology that considers religion as the principal basis of social community is called:**  \n(A) Secularism  \n(B) Socialism  \n(C) Communalism  \n(D) Casteism  \n* **Answer**: **(C)**  \n* *Explanation*: Communalism is based on the premise that people of different religions cannot belong to the same social community.\n\n**Q8. Which of the following statements about a ''Secular State'' in India is INCORRECT?**  \n(A) There is no official state religion in India.  \n(B) All citizens have the freedom to profess any religion.  \n(C) The state can never intervene in religious matters under any circumstance.  \n(D) The Constitution prohibits discrimination on grounds of religion.  \n* **Answer**: **(C)**  \n* *Explanation*: Statement C is incorrect because the state CAN intervene in religious matters to ensure intra-religious equality and eradicate evils like untouchability.\n\n**Q9. Which article of the Indian Constitution prohibits discrimination against any citizen on grounds of religion, race, caste, sex, or place of birth?**  \n(A) Article 14  \n(B) Article 15  \n(C) Article 17  \n(D) Article 21  \n* **Answer**: **(B)**  \n* *Explanation*: Article 15 prohibits state discrimination on these specific grounds.\n\n**Q10. Who among the following social reformers fought extensively against caste inequalities in India?**  \n(A) Jyotiba Phule  \n(B) Dr. B.R. Ambedkar  \n(C) Periyar E.V. Ramaswamy  \n(D) All of the above  \n* **Answer**: **(D)**  \n* *Explanation*: Jyotiba Phule, Gandhiji, Dr. B.R. Ambedkar, and Periyar all worked tirelessly to eliminate caste discrimination.\n\n**Q11. What is meant by \"Occupational Mobility\"?**  \n(A) Moving from one city to another for work  \n(B) Shift from one occupation to another, especially away from hereditary occupations  \n(C) Promotion within the same company  \n(D) Retiring from an occupation  \n* **Answer**: **(B)**  \n* *Explanation*: Occupational mobility breaks traditional hereditary caste links to specific occupations.\n\n**Q12. Which of the following is a myth regarding caste in Indian elections?**  \n(A) Political parties consider caste demographics when selecting candidates.  \n(B) Every constituency in India has a clear single-caste majority that decides elections.  \n(C) Sitting MPs and MLAs frequently lose elections.  \n(D) Lower castes have gained a voice through adult franchise.  \n* **Answer**: **(B)**  \n* *Explanation*: Statement B is a myth — no parliamentary constituency in India has an absolute majority of a single caste.\n\n**Q13. How does politics influence caste?**  \n(A) Each caste group tries to become bigger by incorporating sub-castes.  \n(B) Various caste groups enter into coalitions with other castes.  \n(C) New caste groupings like ''Backward'' and ''Forward'' emerge.  \n(D) All of the above  \n* **Answer**: **(D)**  \n* *Explanation*: Politics creates sub-caste coalitions, inter-caste negotiations, and macro political categories.\n\n**Q14. The literacy rate among women in India (as per NCERT baseline figures) is approximately:**  \n(A) 76%  \n(B) 54%  \n(C) 33%  \n(D) 82%  \n* **Answer**: **(B)**  \n* *Explanation*: Female literacy rate is cited at 54% compared to 76% for males.\n\n**Q15. What is ''Patriarchy''?**  \n(A) A system where women hold all decision-making powers  \n(B) A social system ruled by men, where women are subordinate  \n(C) A political system without elections  \n(D) An economic system based on bartering  \n* **Answer**: **(B)**  \n* *Explanation*: Patriarchy literally means ''rule by father'' and refers to male dominance in family and society.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 9: TOPIC-BY-TOPIC SYLLABUS COVERAGE CHECKLIST","content":"```\n========================================================================================================\n                                 BOARDREADY SYLLABUS COVERAGE CHECKLIST\n========================================================================================================\nS.No.  Required Topic / Sub-topic                         Prescription Status  Coverage Location\n========================================================================================================\n1.     Gender Division & Sexual Division of Labour         100% Prescribed      Revision Notes Theme 1\n2.     Feminist Movements & Objectives                     100% Prescribed      Revision Notes Theme 1\n3.     Status of Women & Forms of Discrimination           100% Prescribed      Revision Notes Theme 1\n4.     Women''s Political Representation & 33% Reservation  100% Prescribed      Revision Notes Theme 1\n5.     Religion and Politics (Gandhiji & Human Rights)     100% Prescribed      Revision Notes Theme 2\n6.     Communalism Concept & Mindset                       100% Prescribed      Revision Notes Theme 2\n7.     Four Forms of Communalism in Politics               100% Prescribed      Revision Notes Theme 2\n8.     Secular State Constitutional Provisions             100% Prescribed      Revision Notes Theme 2\n9.     Caste Inequalities & Breakdown Factors              100% Prescribed      Revision Notes Theme 3\n10.    Caste IN Politics (Candidate nomination, etc.)      100% Prescribed      Revision Notes Theme 3\n11.    Politics IN Caste (Coalitions, sub-castes)          100% Prescribed      Revision Notes Theme 3\n12.    Positive & Negative Impacts of Caste in Politics    100% Prescribed      Revision Notes Theme 3\n13.    Exclusion Check (Democracy & Diversity, etc.)       Exclusion Verified   Excluded (0 Marks)\n========================================================================================================\n```\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 10: MASTER SOURCE INVENTORY & REFERENCE LOG","content":"1. **BSER Official Curriculum Document (`10_2027.pdf`)**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan, Ajmer. Confirms Subject Code 08, Political Science weightage (20 marks), and 04-mark allotment for *Gender, Religion and Caste*.\n2. **NCERT Prescribed Textbook**: *Democratic Politics – II* (Democratic Politics – II), Class X, NCERT. Prescribed primary source for all conceptual definitions, statistics, and examples.\n3. **NCERT Rationalised Content Guide**: Confirms textual retention of Chapter 3 (*Gender, Religion and Caste*) with minor image rationalisations, and complete removal of Chapters 3, 5, and 8.\n4. **BSER Secondary Main Examination Papers**:\n   * **2024 Main Paper (S-08)**: Q1(xi) Casteist definition, Q10 Forms of discrimination against women, Q19 Patriarchal discrimination vs. Secular governance choice.\n   * **2023 Main Paper (S-08)**: Secular state provisions and political representation questions.\n   * **2022 Main Paper (S-08)**: Q1(vii) Feminist ideology MCQ, Q22 Secular governance provisions vs. Position of women.\n   * **2024 Supplementary D&D Paper (S-124)**: Q28 Communalism concept and forms.\n   * **2026–27 Official Model Question Paper (Social-Science-10.pdf)**: Q9 Feminist definition MCQ."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
