  'notes',
  '{"title":"Political Parties","subject":"social-science","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"Chapter Metadata & Official Examination Scheme","content":"* **Working List Chapter ID**: `sst-16`\n* **Subject ID**: `sst`\n* **Subject Code**: 08 (Social Science / Social Science)\n* **Discipline**: Political Science (*Democratic Politics – II* / *Democratic Politics – 2*)\n* **Chapter Title**: Political Parties\n* **Hindi Title**: Political Party\n* **Academic Session**: 2026–27 (Intended for the 2027 Secondary Board Examination)\n* **Prescribed Textbook**: *Democratic Politics – II* (Class X), published by NCERT and accredited by the Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER / RBSE), Ajmer.\n* **Official Board Weightage**: **05 Marks** out of 80 Theory Marks (Political Science Total: 20 Marks across 5 prescribed units).\n* **Blueprint Question Allocation**: Typically structured as **1 Long / Essay Answer Question (04 Marks) with internal choice + 1 Objective / MCQ Question (01 Mark)**, OR **1 Short Answer Question (03 Marks) + 1 Short Answer Question (02 Marks)**.\n* **Target Output Language**: English\n\n---"},{"heading":"Part 1: Official Syllabus & Prescription Verification Report","content":""},{"heading":"1. Prescription Status","content":"* **Status**: **100% Fully Prescribed**.\n* **Target Exam**: 2027 Secondary Board Examination (Session 2026–27).\n* **Weightage**: **05 Marks** (highest single-chapter weightage in the Political Science discipline)."},{"heading":"2. Rationalisation & Exclusion Verification","content":"* **BSER Syllabus Document**: Checked against the official BSER Syllabus Notification (`10_2027.pdf`, p. 38) and BSER 2026–27 Official Model Question Paper (`Social-Science-10.pdf`).\n* **NCERT Rationalised Content Guide**: Verified against the NCERT Rationalised Content guide. While certain decorative illustrations or non-evaluative cartoon frames were rationalised in NCERT''s drive, **100% of the textual content, core concepts, national/state party criteria, challenges, and reform proposals remain fully active and examinable**.\n* **Non-Prescribed Chapters Excluded**: Chapters such as *Popular Struggles and Movements* and *Challenges to Democracy* (which were dropped in NCERT/CBSE rationalisations) are not part of the active BSER 2026–27 examination scheme. **Chapter 4: Political Parties** is fully prescribed."},{"heading":"3. Topic-by-Topic Prescription Matrix","content":"| Working List Sub-topic | Prescribed in BSER 2026–27? | Assessment Status | Notes / Blueprint Alignment |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| **Why Do We Need Political Parties?** | Yes | Active | Meaning, 3 Components (Leader, Active Members, Followers) |\n| **Functions of Political Parties** | Yes | Active | 7 Core Functions (Elections, Policies, Laws, Form Govt, Opposition, Public Opinion, Access to Machinery) |\n| **Necessity of Political Parties** | Yes | Active | Representative democracy requirements, direct democracy limitations |\n| **How Many Parties Should We Have?** | Yes | Active | One-party, Two-party, and Multi-party systems with country examples |\n| **National Parties in India** | Yes | Active | Election Commission criteria (6% vote + 4 Lok Sabha seats OR 2% Lok Sabha seats from 3 states) & major recognised national parties |\n| **State / Regional Parties** | Yes | Active | Election Commission criteria (6% vote + 2 Assembly seats) & role in federalism/coalition politics |\n| **Challenges to Political Parties** | Yes | Active | 4 Core Challenges (Internal Democracy, Dynastic Succession, Money & Muscle Power, Meaningful Choice) |\n| **How Can Parties Be Reformed?** | Yes | Active | Constitutional anti-defection laws, SC affidavit orders, IT/Elections orders, & 5 proposed reforms |\n\n---"},{"heading":"Part 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes","content":""},{"heading":"1. Meaning and Core Components of a Political Party","content":""},{"heading":"Definition","content":"A **political party** is a voluntary association of people who come together to contest elections and hold power in the government. They agree on some policies and programmes for the society with a view to promote the collective good."},{"heading":"Three Core Components","content":"Every political party has three essential components:\n1. **The Leaders**: Formulate policies, make key decisions, and guide party strategies.\n2. **The Active Members**: Work at the grassroots level, run campaigns, organize rallies, and mobilize voters.\n3. **The Followers**: Citizens and voters who sympathize with the party''s ideology and vote for its candidates.\n\n```\n                  ┌────────────────────────┐\n                  │    POLITICAL PARTY     │\n                  └───────────┬────────────┘\n                              │\n         ┌────────────────────┼────────────────────┐\n         ▼                    ▼                    ▼\n┌─────────────────┐  ┌─────────────────┐  ┌─────────────────┐\n│   THE LEADERS   │  │ ACTIVE MEMBERS  │  │  THE FOLLOWERS  │\n│ (Policy makers) │  │(Grassroots cadre│  │(Voters/sympath- │\n│                 │  │  & campaigners) │  │     isers)      │\n└─────────────────┘  └─────────────────┘  └─────────────────┘\n```\n\n---"},{"heading":"2. Seven Core Functions of Political Parties","content":"Political parties perform fundamental functions in a representative democracy:\n\n1. **Contesting Elections**: In most democracies, elections are fought mainly among the candidates put up by political parties. Parties select their candidates in different ways (e.g., top leaders choose candidates in India, whereas members choose candidates through primaries in the USA).\n2. **Putting Forward Policies and Programmes**: Parties present different policies and programmes to the electorate. Voters choose from these options. In a democracy, a large number of similar opinions are grouped together to provide a direction in which policies can be formulated by the government.\n3. **Making Laws for the Country**: Parties play a decisive role in making laws for a country. Formally, laws are debated and passed in the legislature, but most members belong to a political party and follow the party whip, regardless of their personal opinions.\n4. **Forming and Running Governments**: Parties recruit leaders, train them, and make them ministers to run the government in the way they want. Executive heads (Prime Minister, Chief Ministers, Cabinet Ministers) are drawn from the ruling party.\n5. **Playing the Role of Opposition**: Parties that lose in elections play the role of opposition to the parties in power. They voice different views, criticize the government for its failures or wrong policies, and mobilize opposition to the government.\n6. **Shaping Public Opinion**: Parties raise and highlight key issues. They have lakhs of active members and activists spread all over the country. Many pressure groups are extensions of political parties. Parties launch movements for the resolution of problems faced by people.\n7. **Providing Access to Government Machinery and Welfare Schemes**: For an ordinary citizen, it is much easier to approach a local party leader than a government officer. Parties serve as an essential link between citizens and the state administration.\n\n---"},{"heading":"3. Necessity of Political Parties","content":"Why are political parties indispensable to modern representative democracies?\n\n* **Limitation of Independent Candidates**: If there were no political parties, every candidate in an election would be independent. No one would be able to make promises to the electorate about major policy changes. The government might be formed, but its utility would remain uncertain.\n* **Lack of Accountability for National Policy**: An elected representative would be accountable to their local constituency for what they do in the village or town, but no one would be responsible for how the country as a whole is run.\n* **Requirement of Representative Democracy**: As societies become large and complex, they need an agency to gather different views on various issues and present these to the government. They need a mechanism to bring various representatives together so that a responsible government can be formed.\n\n---"},{"heading":"4. Party Systems: Types and Comparative Analysis","content":"Different countries adopt different party systems based on their historical, social, and cultural background. A party system is not something a country can choose; it evolves over a long time depending on the nature of society, social and regional divisions, and history of politics.\n\n```\n                      ┌───────────────────────────┐\n                      │    TYPES OF PARTY SYSTEM  │\n                      └─────────────┬─────────────┘\n                                    │\n         ┌──────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┐\n         ▼                          ▼                          ▼\n┌───────────────────┐      ┌───────────────────┐      ┌───────────────────┐\n│ ONE-PARTY SYSTEM  │      │ TWO-PARTY SYSTEM  │      │ MULTI-PARTY SYSTEM│\n│ (e.g., China)     │      │ (e.g., USA, UK)   │      │ (e.g., India)     │\n└───────────────────┘      └───────────────────┘      └───────────────────┘\n```"},{"heading":"Detailed Comparison Table","content":"| Feature | One-Party System | Two-Party System | Multi-Party System |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Definition** | Only one party is allowed to control and run the government. | Power alternates between two major dominant parties. | Several political parties compete, and more than two parties have a reasonable chance of coming to power. |\n| **Democratic Status** | **Non-democratic** (no free competition or choice). | **Democratic** (provides clear choice and stability). | **Highly Democratic** (represents diverse social, regional, and religious interests). |\n| **Government Stability** | High stability, but autocratic. | High political stability with clear majority. | Can lead to political instability or coalition governments. |\n| **Voter Choice** | Zero choice for voters. | Clear binary choice. | Wide variety of ideological choices. |\n| **Country Examples** | **China** (Communist Party of China). | **USA** (Democrats vs. Republicans), **UK** (Labour vs. Conservatives). | **India** (NDA, INDIA bloc), **France**, **Germany**. |"},{"heading":"Coalition Government & Alliances in India","content":"When several parties in a multi-party system join hands for the purpose of contesting elections and winning power, it is called an **alliance** or a **front**.\n* In India, three major alliances emerged in the 2004, 2009, 2014, and 2019/2024 parliamentary elections:\n  1. **National Democratic Alliance (NDA)** led by the BJP.\n  2. **Indian National Developmental Inclusive Alliance (INDIA)** / Formerly United Progressive Alliance (UPA) led by the INC.\n  3. **Left Front**.\n\n---"},{"heading":"5. National and State (Regional) Political Parties in India","content":"In India, political parties are registered and recognized by the **Election Commission of India (ECI)**. The ECI offers special facilities to large and established parties, such as a unique **reserved election symbol** that only official candidates of that party can use. Parties that get this privilege and other special facilities are called **Recognised Political Parties**."},{"heading":"Criteria for Recognition by the Election Commission of India","content":"```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    ELECTION COMMISSION CRITERIA                         │\n└────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┘\n                                     │\n         ┌───────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────┐\n         ▼                                                       ▼\n┌─────────────────────────────────┐             ┌─────────────────────────────────┐\n│     STATE / REGIONAL PARTY      │             │         NATIONAL PARTY          │\n├─────────────────────────────────┤             ├─────────────────────────────────┤\n│ • Secures at least 6% of total  │             │ • Secures at least 6% of total  │\n│   votes in State Assembly       │             │   votes in Lok Sabha OR State   │\n│   elections, AND                │             │   Assembly elections in 4       │\n│ • Wins at least 2 seats in the  │             │   States, AND                   │\n│   State Legislative Assembly.   │             │ • Wins at least 4 seats in the  │\n│                                 │             │   Lok Sabha.                    │\n└─────────────────────────────────┘             └─────────────────────────────────┘\n```"},{"heading":"Key Recognised National Parties in India (Textbook Reference Profile)","content":"1. **Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP)**:\n   * **Founded**: 1980 (re-formed from the erstwhile Bharatiya Jana Sangh, founded by Syama Prasad Mookerjee in 1951).\n   * **Ideology**: Cultural nationalism (or *Hindutva*), integral humanism (Deendayal Upadhyaya), full territorial and political integration of Jammu & Kashmir, uniform civil code for all citizens, and ban on religious conversions.\n   * **Alliance**: Leads the National Democratic Alliance (NDA).\n\n2. **Indian National Congress (INC)**:\n   * **Founded**: 1885 (one of the oldest parties in the world).\n   * **Ideology**: Centrist party, secularism, welfare of weaker sections and minorities, economic reforms with a human face.\n   * **Alliance**: Leads the Indian National Developmental Inclusive Alliance (INDIA) / erstwhile UPA.\n\n3. **Communist Party of India - Marxist (CPI-M)**:\n   * **Founded**: 1964 (split from CPI).\n   * **Ideology**: Marxism-Leninism, supports socialism, secularism, and democracy; opposes imperialism and communalism; strong base in Kerala, West Bengal, and Tripura.\n\n4. **Communist Party of India (CPI)**:\n   * **Founded**: 1925.\n   * **Ideology**: Marxism-Leninism, secularism, and democracy; opposed to forces of secessionism and communalism.\n\n5. **Bahujan Samaj Party (BSP)**:\n   * **Founded**: 1984 under the leadership of Kanshi Ram.\n   * **Ideology**: Represents and secures power for the *Bahujan Samaj* (Dalits, Adivasis, OBCs, and religious minorities); inspired by teachings of Sahu Maharaj, Mahatma Phule, Periyar Ramasami, and Dr. B.R. Ambedkar; strong base in Uttar Pradesh.\n\n6. **National People''s Party (NPP)**:\n   * **Founded**: 2013 by P.A. Sangma.\n   * **Ideology**: Focus on North-East India''s regional identity, tribal rights, and development; first national party from North-Eastern India."},{"heading":"State / Regional Parties and Their Growing Importance","content":"Parties other than national parties are classified by the ECI as ''State Parties'' or regional parties. Examples include:\n* **Aam Aadmi Party (AAP)** (Delhi, Punjab)\n* **Samajwadi Party (SP)** & **Rashtriya Janata Dal (RJD)** (UP, Bihar)\n* **Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam (DMK)** & **AIADMK** (Tamil Nadu)\n* **All India Trinamool Congress (AITC)** (West Bengal)\n* **Shiv Sena** (Maharashtra)\n\n##### Role of Regional Parties in Indian Federalism\nOver the last three decades, state parties have expanded their influence. No single national party was able to secure a majority on its own in the Lok Sabha between 1989 and 2014. As a result, national parties were compelled to form coalitions with state parties. This has made the Parliament of India politically more diverse and strengthened **federalism and democracy** in the country.\n\n---"},{"heading":"6. Four Major Challenges to Political Parties","content":"Popular dissatisfaction and criticism have focused on four problem areas in the working of political parties:\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                     CHALLENGES TO POLITICAL PARTIES                     │\n└────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┘\n                                     │\n         ┌───────────────────┬───────┴───────┬───────────────────┐\n         ▼                   ▼               ▼                   ▼\n┌─────────────────┐ ┌─────────────────┐ ┌─────────────────┐ ┌─────────────────┐\n│1. LACK OF       │ │2. DYNASTIC      │ │3. MONEY & MUSCLE│ │4. LACK OF A     │\n│   INTERNAL      │ │   SUCCESSION    │ │   POWER         │ │   MEANINGFUL    │\n│   DEMOCRACY     │ │                 │ │                 │ │   CHOICE        │\n└─────────────────┘ └─────────────────┘ └─────────────────┘ └─────────────────┘\n```\n\n1. **Lack of Internal Democracy within Parties**:\n   * Concentration of power in one or a few top leaders.\n   * Organizational elections are rarely held regularly.\n   * Parties do not maintain membership registers or hold regular internal meetings.\n   * Ordinary members do not get sufficient information on internal party matters and cannot influence decisions.\n\n2. **Dynastic Succession**:\n   * Top positions in many parties are controlled by members of one family.\n   * Unfair to ordinary workers and harmful to democracy because people without adequate experience or popular support come to occupy positions of power.\n\n3. **Growing Role of Money and Muscle Power**:\n   * During elections, parties tend to nominate candidates who have or can raise lots of money.\n   * Wealthy individuals and corporations who provide funds to parties influence party policies and decisions.\n   * In some cases, parties support criminals who can win elections.\n\n4. **Lack of a Meaningful Choice to Voters**:\n   * In recent years, there has been a decline in the ideological differences among parties in most parts of the world.\n   * For example, the difference between the Labour Party and the Conservative Party in the UK is very narrow.\n   * In India as well, differences among major parties on economic policies have reduced. Voters cannot even elect different leaders because the same set of leaders keeps shifting from one party to another.\n\n---"},{"heading":"7. How Can Political Parties Be Reformed?","content":"Reforming political parties requires both legal measures by the state and active participation by citizens."},{"heading":"A. Legal Measures Already Taken in India","content":"1. **Anti-Defection Law**:\n   * The Indian Constitution was amended (52nd Amendment, 1985 & 91st Amendment, 2003) to prevent elected MLAs and MPs from changing parties.\n   * **Defection** means changing party allegiance from the party on whose ticket a person was elected to another party.\n   * Now, if an elected MP or MLA changes parties, he/she loses his/her seat in the legislature. This has made defecting more difficult, though it has reduced internal dissent.\n2. **Supreme Court Order on Affidavits**:\n   * The Supreme Court passed an order to reduce the influence of money and criminals.\n   * It is now mandatory for every candidate contesting elections to file an **affidavit** giving details of his/her property and criminal cases pending against him/her.\n   * An **affidavit** is a signed document submitted to an officer where a person makes a sworn statement regarding their personal information.\n3. **Election Commission Order on Organizational Elections**:\n   * The Election Commission passed an order making it necessary for political parties to hold organizational elections and file their income tax returns."},{"heading":"B. Five Proposed Reform Suggestions","content":"1. **Regulation of Internal Affairs**: A law should be made to regulate the internal affairs of political parties, making it compulsory to maintain a register of members, follow its own constitution, and hold open organizational elections.\n2. **Mandatory Quota for Women**: It should be made mandatory for political parties to give a minimum number of tickets (about one-third / 33%) to women candidates.\n3. **State Funding of Elections**: The government should give parties money to support their election expenses (in kind: petrol, paper, telephone, or in cash based on votes polled).\n4. **Public Pressure and Campaigns**: Citizens, pressure groups, media, and movements can petition, publicize, and agitate to force political parties to reform.\n5. **Direct Citizen Participation**: People who want reform must join political parties, as the quality of democracy depends on the degree of public participation.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Part 3: Verified Previous-Year Questions (PYQs)","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1 (1 Mark - MCQ)","content":"**Source**: BSER Secondary Board Examination 2024 Main Paper (Subject Code: 08)  \n**Question**: Which one of the following is NOT a component of a political party?  \n(A) The Leaders  \n(B) Active Members  \n(C) Executive Officers  \n(D) The Followers  \n**Official Answer**: **(C) Executive Officers**  \n**Explanation**: Every political party consists of three core components: The Leaders, Active Members, and The Followers. Executive officers are government officials, not components of a political party.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 2 (1 Mark - MCQ)","content":"**Source**: BSER Official Model Paper 2026–27 (`Social-Science-10.pdf`)  \n**Question**: An alliance formed by several political parties to contest elections and form government in a multi-party system is called:  \n(A) Coalition / Front  \n(B) One-party system  \n(C) Federation  \n(D) Regional Party  \n**Official Answer**: **(A) Coalition / Front**  \n**Explanation**: When several parties in a multi-party system join hands to contest elections and win power, it is called an alliance or front (e.g., NDA, INDIA alliance).\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 3 (2 Marks - Short Answer)","content":"**Source**: BSER Secondary Board Examination 2023 Main Paper (Subject Code: 08)  \n**Question**: State any two major functions performed by political parties in a democracy.  \n**Model Answer**:\n1. **Contesting Elections**: Political parties select candidates and contest elections to gain political power and form the government.\n2. **Forming and Running Government**: The party that wins the majority forms the government, recruits ministers, and formulates national policies.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 4 (3 Marks - Short Answer)","content":"**Source**: BSER Secondary Board Supplementary (D&D) Examination 2024 (Subject Code: 124)  \n**Question**: What criteria are set by the Election Commission of India to recognize a party as a ''National Party''?  \n**Model Answer**:\nAccording to the Election Commission of India, a party is recognized as a **National Party** if it fulfills the following criteria:\n1. It secures at least **6% of the total votes** polled in Lok Sabha elections OR Legislative Assembly elections in **four states**.\n2. **AND** it wins at least **four seats in the Lok Sabha**.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 5 (4 Marks - Essay / Long Answer with Internal Choice)","content":"**Source**: BSER Secondary Board Examination 2024 Main Paper (Subject Code: 08)  \n**Question**:  \nExplain the major challenges faced by political parties in modern democracies.  \n**OR**  \nSuggest any four measures to reform political parties in India.\n\n**Model Answer (Option 1 - Challenges)**:\nPolitical parties in modern democracies face four major challenges:\n1. **Lack of Internal Democracy**: Power is concentrated in the hands of one or a few top leaders. Organizational elections and membership registers are rarely maintained.\n2. **Dynastic Succession**: Top party posts are often controlled by members of a single family, depriving ordinary workers of leadership opportunities based on merit.\n3. **Role of Money and Muscle Power**: Parties tend to nominate wealthy candidates or those with criminal backgrounds who can fund election campaigns and win seats.\n4. **Lack of Meaningful Choice**: Ideological differences among major parties have narrowed down, offering voters very little distinct policy choice.\n\n**Model Answer (Option 2 - Reforms)**:\nFour key measures to reform political parties in India include:\n1. **Strict Implementation of Anti-Defection Law**: Prevent elected representatives from switching parties for personal gain.\n2. **Mandatory Quota for Women**: Mandate at least one-third (33%) ticket reservation for women candidates in elections.\n3. **State Funding of Elections**: The government should provide election funds to political parties (in cash or kind) to reduce the influence of black money.\n4. **Mandatory Internal Democracy**: Pass a law compelling parties to hold regular organizational elections, maintain member registers, and abide by their constitution.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Part 4: Practice Bank & Original Predicted Questions","content":""},{"heading":"Practice Question 1 (2 Marks - Short Answer)","content":"**Question**: Differentiate between a One-Party System and a Multi-Party System with examples.  \n**Model Answer**:\n* **One-Party System**: Only one party is legally allowed to control and run the government (e.g., Communist Party in **China**). It is non-democratic as voters have no choice.\n* **Multi-Party System**: Several political parties compete, and more than two parties have a reasonable chance of forming the government (e.g., **India**). It provides broad representation to diverse social and regional interests.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Practice Question 2 (3 Marks - Short Answer)","content":"**Question**: What is meant by ''Defection''? How has the Anti-Defection Law attempted to control it in India?  \n**Model Answer**:\n* **Defection** means changing party allegiance from the political party on whose symbol/ticket a person was elected to another party.\n* **Anti-Defection Law**:\n  1. The Constitution was amended to state that if any elected MP or MLA changes their party after winning, they automatically lose their legislative seat.\n  2. This law has successfully brought down unprincipled floor-crossing and cabinet-shopping by elected representatives.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Original Predicted Board Question 2027 (4 Marks - Long Answer)","content":"**Question**:  \n\"Political parties are a necessary condition for a representative democracy.\" Evaluate this statement with four suitable arguments.\n\n**Model Answer**:\nThe statement is completely valid. Political parties are indispensable to modern representative democracies for the following reasons:\n\n1. **Alternative to Independent Chaos**: Without political parties, every candidate in an election would contest as an independent. No candidate could guarantee uniform national policies, leading to an unstable and fragmented government.\n2. **Formulation of Collective Policies**: In large societies, millions of individual opinions exist. Political parties group similar views together to formulate coherent policies that direct government decision-making.\n3. **Accountability for National Governance**: While independent candidates may look after their local constituency, political parties take collective responsibility for running the entire country.\n4. **Linking Citizens to the State**: Parties provide ordinary citizens with easy access to government machinery, welfare schemes, and policy debates through local cadres.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Part 5: 15-Question MCQ Quiz","content":"1. **Which of the following is NOT one of the three components of a political party?**  \n   (A) The Leaders  \n   (B) Active Members  \n   (C) Government Officers  \n   (D) The Followers  \n   *Answer*: **(C)** | *Rationale*: Government officers belong to the permanent executive branch, not political parties.\n\n2. **A country where only one political party is allowed to control and run the government is:**  \n   (A) India  \n   (B) China  \n   (C) United States of America  \n   (D) United Kingdom  \n   *Answer*: **(B)** | *Rationale*: China operates under a one-party system governed by the Communist Party of China.\n\n3. **In the United States of America, which type of party system exists?**  \n   (A) One-party system  \n   (B) Two-party system  \n   (C) Multi-party system  \n   (D) No-party system  \n   *Answer*: **(B)** | *Rationale*: The USA operates a two-party system dominated by the Democratic and Republican parties.\n\n4. **Which party system is practiced in India?**  \n   (A) One-party system  \n   (B) Two-party system  \n   (C) Multi-party system  \n   (D) Dictatorial system  \n   *Answer*: **(C)** | *Rationale*: India has a multi-party system reflecting its vast social, cultural, and regional diversity.\n\n5. **To be recognized as a ''State Party'', a political party must secure at least what percentage of total votes in a State Legislative Assembly election?**  \n   (A) 4%  \n   (B) 6%  \n   (C) 10%  \n   (D) 12%  \n   *Answer*: **(B)** | *Rationale*: ECI mandates 6% total assembly votes + at least 2 assembly seats for State Party recognition.\n\n6. **How many Lok Sabha seats must a party win (along with 6% votes in 4 states) to be recognized as a National Party?**  \n   (A) 2 seats  \n   (B) 4 seats  \n   (C) 10 seats  \n   (D) 15 seats  \n   *Answer*: **(B)** | *Rationale*: The ECI criterion requires 6% votes in 4 states AND at least 4 Lok Sabha seats.\n\n7. **The Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) was founded in 1980 by reviving which erstwhile political party?**  \n   (A) Swatantra Party  \n   (B) Bharatiya Jana Sangh  \n   (C) Congress (O)  \n   (D) Hindu Mahasabha  \n   *Answer*: **(B)** | *Rationale*: BJP was re-formed in 1980, drawing roots from Bharatiya Jana Sangh (founded by Syama Prasad Mookerjee in 1951).\n\n8. **Who among the following founded the Bahujan Samaj Party (BSP) in 1984?**  \n   (A) Dr. B.R. Ambedkar  \n   (B) Jyotiba Phule  \n   (C) Kanshi Ram  \n   (D) Mayawati  \n   *Answer*: **(C)** | *Rationale*: Kanshi Ram founded the BSP in 1984 to represent the Bahujan Samaj.\n\n9. **The Indian National Congress (INC) was established in which year?**  \n   (A) 1857  \n   (B) 1885  \n   (C) 1905  \n   (D) 1947  \n   *Answer*: **(B)** | *Rationale*: INC was established in December 1885 in Bombay.\n\n10. **Changing party allegiance from the party on whose ticket a person was elected to another party is called:**  \n    (A) Coalition  \n    (B) Defection  \n    (C) Alliance  \n    (D) Partisanship  \n    *Answer*: **(B)** | *Rationale*: Defection refers to floor-crossing by elected representatives.\n\n11. **A signed document submitted to an officer where a person makes a sworn statement regarding their personal information, assets, and legal cases is called:**  \n    (A) Manifesto  \n    (B) Affidavit  \n    (C) Ordinance  \n    (D) Constitution  \n    *Answer*: **(B)** | *Rationale*: The Supreme Court mandated candidates to file affidavits detailing assets and criminal records.\n\n12. **Which of the following is a challenge to political parties where top positions are perpetually controlled by members of one family?**  \n    (A) Lack of internal democracy  \n    (B) Dynastic succession  \n    (C) Money power  \n    (D) Defection  \n    *Answer*: **(B)** | *Rationale*: Dynastic succession prevents ordinary party workers from reaching leadership positions.\n\n13. **What is the term used when several parties in a multi-party system join hands to contest elections and form government?**  \n    (A) One-party state  \n    (B) Alliance / Front  \n    (C) Opposition  \n    (D) Unitary government  \n    *Answer*: **(B)** | *Rationale*: Pre-poll arrangements between political parties are called alliances or fronts (e.g., NDA, INDIA).\n\n14. **The National People''s Party (NPP), recognized as a national party, primarily belongs to which region of India?**  \n    (A) South India  \n    (B) North-East India  \n    (C) Western Ghats  \n    (D) Northern Plains  \n    *Answer*: **(B)** | *Rationale*: NPP, founded by P.A. Sangma, is the first national party from North-East India.\n\n15. **Which body regulates and registers political parties in India?**  \n    (A) Supreme Court of India  \n    (B) Union Public Service Commission  \n    (C) Election Commission of India  \n    (D) NITI Aayog  \n    *Answer*: **(C)** | *Rationale*: The Election Commission of India (ECI) registers and grants recognition to political parties.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Part 6: Syllabus Coverage Checklist & Master Source Inventory","content":""},{"heading":"1. Topic-by-Topic Syllabus Coverage Checklist","content":"- [x] Meaning and definition of a Political Party\n- [x] Three core components (Leaders, Active Members, Followers)\n- [x] Seven core functions of political parties in a democracy\n- [x] Necessity of political parties in representative democracies\n- [x] Party systems: One-party, Two-party, and Multi-party systems with global examples\n- [x] Concept of Alliances and Coalitions (NDA, INDIA, Left Front)\n- [x] Election Commission criteria for Recognised State (Regional) Parties\n- [x] Election Commission criteria for Recognised National Parties\n- [x] Profiles and ideologies of major National Parties (BJP, INC, CPI-M, CPI, BSP, NPP)\n- [x] Growing significance of State/Regional Parties in Indian federalism\n- [x] Four major challenges facing political parties\n- [x] Legal measures enacted in India (Anti-Defection Law, Supreme Court Affidavits, ECI tax orders)\n- [x] Five key proposed reform suggestions for political parties"},{"heading":"2. Master Source Inventory","content":"1. **BSER Syllabus Notification 2026–27** (`10_2027.pdf`, p. 38, Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan, Ajmer).\n2. **BSER Official Model Question Paper 2026–27** (`Social-Science-10.pdf`, BSER Ajmer).\n3. **BSER Main Exam Question Paper 2024 (Subject Code: 08 - Social Science)**.\n4. **BSER Main Exam Question Paper 2023 (Subject Code: 08 - Social Science)**.\n5. **BSER Supplementary (D&D) Exam Question Paper 2024 (Subject Code: 124 - Social Science)**.\n6. **NCERT Class\n7. **NCERT Rationalised Content Guide for Class X Social Science**."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
