  '{"title":"Sectors of the Indian Economy","subject":"social-science","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"Chapter Metadata & Official Board Examination Scheme","content":"* **Chapter ID**: `sst-19`\n* **Subject ID**: `sst` (Social Science / Social Science)\n* **Discipline**: Economics (*Understanding Economic Development* / *Understanding Economic Development*)\n* **Working List Chapter Number**: 19 (Economics Chapter 2)\n* **Chapter Title**: Sectors of the Indian Economy\n* **Hindi Title**: Sectors of Indian Economy\n* **Academic Session**: 2026–27 (Intended for 2027 Secondary Board Examination)\n* **Subject Code**: 08\n* **Prescribed Textbook**: *Understanding Economic Development – Class X* (NCERT / BSER Ajmer)\n* **Board Weightage**: **05 Marks** out of 80 Theory Marks (Highest single-chapter allocation in Economics!)\n* **Blueprint Examination Pattern**:\n  * Total Weightage: **05 Marks**\n  * Typically structured as **1 Long / Essay Question (04 Marks) with internal choice + 1 Objective / MCQ Question (01 Mark)**, OR **1 Short Answer Question (03 Marks) + 1 Short Answer Question (02 Marks)**.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Section 1: Official Syllabus & Prescription Verification Report","content":""},{"heading":"1. Verification of Prescription Status","content":"* **Prescription Status**: **100% Fully Prescribed** for the 2026–27 academic session and 2027 Secondary Board Examination.\n* **Official Syllabus Source**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER / RBSE Ajmer) Syllabus Circular (`10_2027.pdf`, p. 38) and the official BSER 2026–27 Secondary Model Question Paper (`Social-Science-10.pdf`)."},{"heading":"2. Rationalisation and Exclusion Check","content":"* Verified against the NCERT Rationalised Content Guide and BSER official curriculum notifications.\n* **Zero Sub-topics Excluded**: All six core sub-topics prescribed in NCERT Chapter 2 are active, evaluative, and fully prescribed for the 2027 board examination:\n  1. Sectors of Economic Activities (Primary, Secondary, Tertiary)\n  2. Comparing the Three Sectors (Gross Domestic Product, Intermediate vs. Final Goods, Historical Shift)\n  3. Primary, Secondary and Tertiary Sectors in India (Rising Importance of Tertiary Sector, Employment Distribution, Underemployment / Disguised Unemployment)\n  4. How to Create More Employment? (Short-term & Long-term Strategies, MGNREGA 2005)\n  5. Division of Sectors as Organised and Unorganised (Features, Vulnerabilities, Protection Measures)\n  6. Sectors in Terms of Ownership: Public and Private Sectors (Features, Necessity of Public Sector, Role in Infrastructure & Social Welfare)\n\n---"},{"heading":"Section 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes","content":""},{"heading":"Theme 1: Sectors of Economic Activities","content":"Economic activities are classified into three major categories based on the nature of the activity:\n\n1. **Primary Sector (Agriculture and Related Sector)**:\n   * **Definition**: Activities undertaken by directly using natural resources.\n   * **Key Examples**: Agriculture, dairy, fishing, forestry, mining, and extraction of natural products.\n   * **Nomenclature**: Called ''Primary'' because it forms the base for all other products subsequently made; also referred to as the **Agriculture and Related Sector**.\n\n2. **Secondary Sector (Industrial Sector)**:\n   * **Definition**: Activities in which natural products are changed into other forms through manufacturing or industrial processing.\n   * **Key Examples**: Spinning cotton yarn into cloth, manufacturing sugar from sugarcane, processing iron ore into steel, brick making, construction of buildings and roads.\n   * **Nomenclature**: Associated with different kinds of industries, hence termed the **Industrial Sector**.\n\n3. **Tertiary Sector (Service Sector)**:\n   * **Definition**: Activities that do not produce goods by themselves but generate services that aid and support the production process in the primary and secondary sectors.\n   * **Key Examples**: Transport (trucks, trains), storage (godowns), communication (telephones, internet), banking, trade, repair services, software development, healthcare, education, law, administration, and personal services (barbers, tailors, maids).\n   * **Nomenclature**: Commonly called the **Service Sector**.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Theme 2: Comparing the Three Sectors (GDP & Historical Shift)","content":"1. **How Do We Count Various Goods and Services?**:\n   * **Value over Quantity**: Adding physical quantities of millions of items (e.g., wheat, cars, soaps) is impossible and meaningless. Therefore, economic comparison is based on the **total monetary value** of goods and services.\n   * **Precaution Against Double Counting**:\n     * Only the value of **FINAL GOODS AND SERVICES** is included in calculation.\n     * **Intermediate Goods**: Goods used up in producing final goods (e.g., wheat sold to a flour mill at ₹20/kg, flour sold to a biscuit factory at ₹25/kg, and biscuits containing sugar/oil sold to consumers at ₹80/pack).\n     * Including intermediate goods at each stage leads to double or triple counting of raw material value.\n\n2. **Gross Domestic Product (GDP)**:\n   * **Definition**: Gross Domestic Product (GDP) is the **total value of all final goods and services produced within a country during a particular year**.\n   * **Formula**: $\\text{GDP} = \\text{Value of Primary Final Output} + \\text{Value of Secondary Final Output} + \\text{Value of Tertiary Final Output}$.\n   * **Measuring Agency in India**: Calculated by the **Central Government Ministry (Central Statistics Office - CSO / NSO)** in collaboration with state government departments.\n\n3. **Historical Shift in Sectors**:\n   * **Initial Stage (Primary Dominance)**: In early stages of civilization, agriculture was the most prominent sector for both output and employment.\n   * **Intermediate Stage (Industrial Revolution)**: Technological advancements in farming released labour; manufacturing factories expanded, making the Secondary sector dominant in production and employment.\n   * **Modern Stage (Service Sector Dominance)**: In developed nations, a further shift occurred from manufacturing to service activities. The Tertiary sector became the largest contributor to GDP and employment.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Theme 3: Primary, Secondary, and Tertiary Sectors in India","content":"1. **Rising Importance of the Tertiary Sector in Production**:\n   Over the last 50 years (1973–74 to 2013–14), while production in all three sectors expanded, the **Tertiary sector grew the fastest**, emerging as the largest producing sector in India. Key reasons:\n   * **Basic Services Requirement**: Need for hospitals, educational institutions, post/telegraph offices, police stations, courts, defense, transport, and banking which the government must provide in a developing country.\n   * **Development of Agriculture & Industry**: Growth in farming and manufacturing creates demand for trade, transport, and storage services.\n   * **Rise in Income Levels**: As per capita income rises, urban populations demand luxury services like eating out, tourism, shopping, private hospitals, private schools, and professional training.\n   * **Information & Communication Technology (ICT)**: Rapid expansion of IT, software, telecom, and digital services over the past two decades.\n\n2. **Where Are Most People Employed? (The Indian Structural Anomaly)**:\n   * **GDP Share vs. Employment Share Disparity**:\n     * In 2013–14, the Tertiary sector contributed **over 60% of India''s GDP**, but employed less than 30% of the workforce.\\n     * The Primary sector (agriculture) contributed **less than 15–20% of GDP**, but continued to employ **nearly 45–50% of the total national workforce**.\\n   * **Conclusion**: A historical shift in output occurred in India (Primary $\\\\rightarrow$ Tertiary), but a corresponding shift in employment **did NOT occur**. Secondary and Tertiary sectors failed to generate sufficient jobs for agricultural workers.\\n\\n3. **Underemployment and Disguised Unemployment**:\\n   * **Definition**: A situation where people are apparently working, but all of them are made to work less than their full potential.\\n   * **Characteristics**:\\n     * Visible in rural agricultural families where 5–8 members work on a small plot of land when only 3 are required.\\n     * Even if 2–3 members are moved away to work elsewhere, agricultural output does **NOT decline**.\\n     * The marginal productivity of extra workers is **ZERO**.\\n   * **Urban Disguised Unemployment**: Service workers on streets (casual painters, plumbers, repair persons, street vendors) who work all day but earn very little.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Theme 4: How to Create More Employment?\\n\\nTo resolve underemployment in agriculture and absorb rural/urban youth, two types of intervention are necessary:\\n\\n1. **Long-Term Capital & Infrastructure Strategies**:\\n   * **Irrigation Infrastructure**: Constructing canals and check-dams (e.g., digging wells/tube wells) enables multi-cropping (Rabi & Kharif), creating direct farm employment year-round.\\n   * **Rural Transportation & Storage**: Building rural roads (PMGSY) and cold storage facilities allows farmers to transport crops to markets, generating jobs in transport, trade, and logistics.\\n   * **Agricultural Credit**: Providing cheap, easy credit through regional rural banks and co-operatives eliminates high-interest moneylender traps, allowing farmers to purchase seeds, pumps, and fertilizers.\\n   * **Promoting Agro-Processing & Rural Industries**: Establishing pulse mills, cold storages, honey-collection centers, and vegetable processing units in semi-rural areas.\\n   * **Expansion of Education & Healthcare**:\\n     * According to NITI Aayog (formerly Planning Commission), improving education requires thousands of new schools, creating over **20 lakh jobs in the education sector alone**.\\n     * Expanding rural healthcare generates massive employment for doctors, nurses, and health workers.\\n   * **Tourism & Regional Crafts**: Developing regional tourism and handicraft hubs can generate **over 35 lakh additional jobs annually**.\\n\\n2. **Short-Term Guaranteed Employment (MGNREGA 2005)**:\\n   * **Full Title**: **Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act 2005**.\\n   * **Legal Provision**: Enacted by the Central Government to implement the **Right to Work** in rural areas.\\n   * **Key Features**:\\n     1. Guarantees **100 days of assured wage employment** in a financial year to every rural household whose adult members volunteer to do unskilled manual work.\\n     2. If the government fails to provide employment within 15 days of application, it must pay an **Unemployment Allowance** to the applicant.\\n     3. Priority is given to works that help **increase future agricultural production** (water conservation, soil erosion control, afforestation, land levelling).\\n     4. Operational in all rural districts across India; 1/3rd of proposed jobs are reserved for women.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Theme 5: Division of Sectors as Organized and Unorganized\\n\\nEconomic activities are also classified based on working conditions, rules, and security of employment:\\n\\n| Parameter | Organized Sector Unorganized Sector\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Registration & Authority** | Registered with the government; Complies with formal laws. | Outside government control; small, scattered units largely unregistered. |\\n| **Rules & Laws Followed** | Follows Factories Act, Minimum Wages Act, Payment of Gratuity Act, etc. | Rules exist on paper but are rarely implemented or enforced. |\\n| **Employment Terms** | Fixed hours, regular and secure job terms; formal appointment letters. | Insecure terms; job loss without notice; seasonal work vulnerability. |\\n| **Salary & Overtime** | Fixed regular salary; mandatory **Overtime Payment** for extra hours. | Low wages; no extra pay for overtime work. |\\n| **Social Security & Benefits** | Paid leave, provident fund (PF), gratuity, medical benefits, safe environment. | No paid leave, no sick leave, no medical benefits, no provident fund. |\\n| **Examples** Bank officers, government schoolteachers, TISCO factory workers, PSU employees. | Landless farm labourers, construction workers, street vendors, weavers, handloom workers. |\\n\\n#### Protecting Workers in the Unorganized Sector\\nUnorganized sector workers face economic exploitation and social discrimination. Strategies to protect them:\\n* **In Rural Areas**: Provide landless laborers and small/marginal farmers with timely delivery of seeds, agricultural inputs, cheap credit, storage, and market linkages.\\n* **In Urban Areas**: Support small-scale industries with raw material procurement and marketing assistance; enforce minimum wage laws for casual construction, transportation, and street workers.\\n* **Social Protection for Marginalised Groups**: A majority of workers from Scheduled Castes (SC), Scheduled Tribes (ST), and Backward Classes (OBC) are trapped in unorganized, insecure jobs. They require both economic support (fair wages) and social dignity against discrimination.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Theme 6: Sectors in Terms of Ownership (Public and Private)\\n\\nEconomic activities are categorized by asset ownership and service delivery responsibility:\\n\\n1. **Public Sector**:\\n   * **Ownership**: The **Government** owns most of the assets and provides all major services.\\n   * **Primary Objective**: **Public Welfare** and social development, not profit maximization.\\n   * **Financing**: Government meets expenses through revenue raised via taxes and public borrowing.\\n   * **Examples**: Indian Railways, Bharat Petroleum, Post Offices, BHEL, SAIL, AIIMS, State Electricity Boards.\\n\\n2. **Private Sector**:\\n   * **Ownership**: Ownership of assets and delivery of services is in the hands of **private individuals or private companies**.\\n   * **Primary Objective**: **Profit Maximization**.\\n   * **Financing**: Financed through private capital investments and charges levied on consumers for services rendered.\\n   * **Examples**: Tata Iron and Steel Company (TISCO), Reliance Industries Limited (RIL), Infosys, ICICI Bank, private hospitals.\\n\\n3. **Why is the Public Sector Necessary?**:\\n   * **Huge Capital Requirements**: Large infrastructure projects (railways, bridges, dams, nuclear plants, heavy machinery) require massive investments beyond the capacity of private enterprises.\\n   * **Non-Excludable Public Goods**: Services like road construction, national defense, lighting, and flood control cannot easily charge individual usage fees to earn profit.\\n   * **Subsidy Support for Basic Economic Support**:\\n     * *Electricity Supply*: Supplying power at subsidized rates to small industries and farmers (private generation would make power unaffordable).\\n     * *Food Security*: Government buys wheat and rice from farmers at **Minimum Support Price (MSP)** and sells it to the poor through ration shops (PDS) at subsidized rates (e.g., ₹2-3/kg).\\n   * **Primary Responsibility for Health & Education**: Providing quality basic education, literacy, clean drinking water, and healthcare to all citizens is the primary responsibility of the government.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Section 3: Technical Glossary & Analytical Comparative Matrices\\n\\n### Technical Glossary\\n1. **Primary Sector**: Economic activities directly harvesting or extracting natural resources (e.g., agriculture, mining).\\n2. **Secondary Sector**: Economic activities transforming natural raw materials into manufactured industrial goods.\\n3. **Tertiary Sector**: Service-oriented economic activities that support production, distribution, and consumption without producing physical goods.\\n4. **Gross Domestic Product (GDP)**: Total monetary value of all final goods and services produced within a country.''s boundary in a given financial year.\n5. **Final Goods**: Finished goods purchased directly by consumers for consumption or investment, requiring no further processing.\n6. **Intermediate Goods**: Raw materials or semi-finished items used up during the production of final goods.\n7. **Disguised Unemployment**: A condition of underemployment where more workers are engaged in a job than required; removing extra workers does not reduce total output.\n8. **Organised Sector**: Enterprise sector registered with government authorities, offering formal job security, regular wages, and legal social security benefits.\n9. **Unorganised Sector**: Sector characterized by small, unregistered, scattered units lacking job security, paid leave, and social benefits.\n10. **Public Sector**: Economic enterprises owned, operated, and managed by government bodies with the primary motive of public welfare.\n11. **Private Sector**: Enterprises owned and managed by private individuals or corporations focused on earning profit.\n12. **MGNREGA 2005**: Legislation guaranteeing 100 days of legal wage employment per year to rural households willing to do unskilled manual work.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Master Analytical Matrix 1: Comparison of Primary, Secondary, and Tertiary Sectors","content":"| Criteria | Primary Sector | Secondary Sector | Tertiary Sector |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Core Nature** | Direct exploitation of natural resources. | Manufacturing & processing raw materials into goods. | Service provision aiding production and daily life. |\n| **Output Type** | Natural raw materials (crops, milk, minerals). | Manufactured physical goods (cloth, sugar, cars). | Intangible services (transport, banking, teaching). |\n| **Alternate Name** | Agriculture and Related Sector. | Industrial Sector. | Service Sector. |\n| **Indian GDP Share (~2013-14)** | Smallest share (~15–20%). | Moderate share (~25–30%). | Largest share (~60%+). |\n| **Indian Employment Share** | Largest share (~45–50%). | Moderate share (~24%). | Growing share (~27–30%). |\n| **Key Issues** | Disguised unemployment, low productivity. | Inadequate expansion to absorb farm labor. | Concentrated in highly skilled services; informal service gaps. |\n\n---"},{"heading":"Master Analytical Matrix 2: Comparison of Public Sector vs Private Sector","content":"| Basis of Difference | Public Sector | Private Sector |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Asset Ownership** | Owned, operated, and controlled by Central/State Government. | Owned, operated, and controlled by private individuals or corporations. |\n| **Main Objective** | Social welfare and equitable public development. | Earning profit for owners/shareholders. |\n| **Source of Funds** | Public taxes, government budget allocations, loans. | Private capital investments, shares, commercial bank loans. |\n| **Price Determination** | Prices regulated or subsidized for public affordability. | Prices determined by market demand, supply, and profit margins. |\n| **Examples in India** | Indian Railways, Post Offices, BHEL, SAIL, AIIMS. | TISCO, Reliance, Infosys, HDFC Bank, private clinics. |\n\n---"},{"heading":"Section 4: Verified Previous-Year Board Questions (PYQs)","content":""},{"heading":"1. BSER Class 10 Social Science Main Exam 2024 (Subject Code: S-08)","content":"* **Q1 (Objective/MCQ)**: Which of the following is an example of an activity of the Tertiary Sector? **[1 Mark]**\n  * (A) Agriculture\n  * (B) Weaving cloth\n  * (C) Banking\n  * (D) Mining\n  * **Model Answer**: **(C) Banking** [1 Mark]\n* **Q14 (Short Answer Question)**: Differentiate between the Organised Sector and Unorganised Sector by stating any two points. **[2 Marks]**\n  * **Model Answer**:\n    1. *Registration & Rules*: The Organised sector is registered with the government and follows formal labor laws (e.g., Factories Act), whereas the Unorganised sector operates outside direct government control and rarely implements formal rules. [1 Mark]\n    2. *Job Security & Benefits*: Organised sector employees enjoy secure employment, fixed working hours, paid leave, and provident fund, whereas unorganised sector workers face job insecurity, low wages, and no paid leaves or social security. [1 Mark]"},{"heading":"2. BSER Class 10 Social Science Main Exam 2023 (Subject Code: S-08)","content":"* **Q1 (Objective/MCQ)**: In India, the largest producing sector in terms of contribution to GDP is: **[1 Mark]**\n  * (A) Primary Sector\n  * (B) Secondary Sector\n  * (C) Tertiary Sector\n  * (D) Information Technology\n  * **Model Answer**: **(C) Tertiary Sector** [1 Mark]\n* **Q16 (Short Answer Question)**: What is Disguised Unemployment? Explain with an example from the rural agricultural sector. **[2 Marks]**\n  * **Model Answer**:\n    * *Definition*: Disguised unemployment (or underemployment) is a situation where more workers are engaged in an economic activity than actually required. People appear to be working, but their marginal contribution to output is zero. [1 Mark]\n    * *Example*: A farming family with 6 members working on a 2-hectare plot of land. Even if 2 members move out to work in a nearby factory, total crop yield remains exactly the same. Thus, those 2 extra workers are disguisedly unemployed. [1 Mark]"},{"heading":"3. BSER Class 10 Social Science Supplementary (D&D) Exam 2024 (Subject Code: S-124)","content":"* **Q24 (Long Answer Question)**: Explain any four reasons for the rising importance of the Tertiary sector in India''s economy over the last few decades. **[4 Marks]**\n  * **Model Answer**:\n    1. *Requirement of Basic Services*: In a developing country, the government must provide basic services such as hospitals, schools, police stations, defense, courts, banking, and transport, driving service sector expansion. [1 Mark]\n    2. *Growth of Agriculture and Industry*: Industrial and agricultural expansion increases demand for supportive services like trade, transport, storage, and repair networks. [1 Mark]\n    3. *Rising Income Levels*: As per capita income increases, urban populations spend more on discretionary services like eating out, private healthcare, private education, tourism, and shopping. [1 Mark]\n    4. *Expansion of ICT*: Rapid development and adoption of Information and Communication Technology (IT, software, internet, mobile telecom) over the last two decades created new service avenues. [1 Mark]"},{"heading":"4. Official BSER 2026–27 Secondary Model Question Paper (Subject Code: 08)","content":"* **Q13 (Objective/MCQ)**: Under MGNREGA 2005, how many days of guaranteed wage employment are provided in a financial year to rural households? **[1 Mark]**\n  * (A) 50 days\n  * (B) 100 days\n  * (C) 150 days\n  * (D) 200 days\n  * **Model Answer**: **(B) 100 days** [1 Mark]\n* **Q22 (Short Answer Question)**: Why is the Public Sector necessary for national economic development? State any two reasons. **[2 Marks]**\n  * **Model Answer**:\n    1. *Heavy Capital Investment*: Infrastructure projects like railways, dams, power plants, and highways require massive financial investment that private companies cannot afford or risk. [1 Mark]\n    2. *Public Welfare Motive*: The government provides essential services (subsidized food via PDS, healthcare, basic education) at affordable prices for poor citizens, whereas private sectors operate solely for profit. [1 Mark]\n\n---"},{"heading":"Section 5: Practice Bank & Original Predicted Questions","content":""},{"heading":"Short Answer Practice Questions (2 & 3 Marks)","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1: How are intermediate goods different from final goods? Why are intermediate goods excluded when calculating Gross Domestic Product (GDP)? [3 Marks]","content":"* **Model Answer**:\n  * *Difference*: Final goods are purchased directly by end-consumers for consumption (e.g., a packet of biscuits), whereas intermediate goods are used up as raw materials during production (e.g., flour, sugar, oil used by a biscuit manufacturer). [1 Mark]\n  * *Reason for Exclusion*: The value of final goods already includes the monetary value of all intermediate goods used in their production. [1 Mark]\n  * *Prevention of Double Counting*: Counting intermediate goods separately leads to the error of \"double counting,\" artificially inflating the national GDP estimate. [1 Mark]"},{"heading":"Question 2: Describe any three features of the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA 2005). [3 Marks]","content":"* **Model Answer**:\n  1. *Guaranteed Employment*: Guarantees 100 days of legal wage employment per financial year to adult members of rural households willing to do unskilled manual work. [1 Mark]\n  2. *Unemployment Allowance*: If the government fails to provide work within 15 days of receiving an application, it must pay a daily unemployment allowance to the worker. [1 Mark]\n  3. *Focus of Work & Reservations*: Priority is given to works that address drought/soil erosion to increase future land productivity, and 1/3rd of all proposed jobs are legally reserved for women. [1 Mark]\n\n---"},{"heading":"Original 2027 Predicted Essay Questions (4 Marks)","content":""},{"heading":"Predicted Question 1 (Essay Type): \"While production in the Tertiary sector has grown significantly in India, employment generation has not shifted at the same pace.\" Evaluate this statement and suggest four measures to create more employment in India. [4 Marks]","content":"##### Official BoardREADY Model Answer & Marking Scheme:\n* **Part A: Evaluation of the Statement (1 Mark)**:\n  * In India, a unique structural transition occurred. Over the last 50 years, the Tertiary sector''s contribution to GDP surged to over 60%, making it the dominant sector in production.\n  * However, a similar shift did not occur in employment: the Primary sector still employs nearly 45–50% of the workforce despite contributing under 20% to GDP. This proves that industrial and service sectors failed to generate sufficient employment opportunities for farm labor. [1 Mark]\n\n* **Part B: Four Measures to Generate Employment (3 Marks)**:\n  1. **Infrastructure & Irrigation Development**:\n     * Constructing check-dams, canals, and rural roads allows multi-cropping, boosting direct agricultural employment and generating logistics/transport jobs. [0.75 Mark]\n  2. **Promoting Agro-Processing & Small-Scale Industries**:\n     * Establishing pulse mills, cold storage units, honey collection centers, and food processing plants in semi-rural areas absorbs local underemployed labor. [0.75 Mark]\n  3. **Expanding Educational & Health Services**:\n     * Investing in new schools and rural health centers creates over 20 lakh formal jobs for teachers, doctors, nurses, and administrative staff, as highlighted by NITI Aayog. [0.75 Mark]\n  4. **Promoting Tourism and Rural Crafts**:\n     * Regional tourism, heritage preservation, and handicraft development have the potential to generate over 35 lakh additional jobs annually. [0.75 Mark]\n\n---"},{"heading":"Section 6: 15-Question MCQ Quiz","content":""},{"heading":"Q1. Agriculture, dairy, fishing, and forestry are examples of which economic sector?","content":"- (A) Secondary Sector\n- (B) Primary Sector\n- (C) Tertiary Sector\n- (D) Quaternary Sector\n- **Answer**: (B) Primary Sector\n- **Rationale**: These activities directly exploit natural resources to produce raw goods, forming the primary base of production."},{"heading":"Q2. Which of the following is considered a final good?","content":"- (A) Flour bought by a bakery\n- (B) Cotton fiber purchased by a textile mill\n- (C) A car bought by a consumer for personal use\n- (D) Sugarcane sold to a sugar mill\n- **Answer**: (C) A car bought by a consumer for personal use\n- **Rationale**: Final goods are consumed directly by the end-user and undergo no further processing in the production chain."},{"heading":"Q3. Gross Domestic Product (GDP) represents the total monetary value of:","content":"- (A) All goods and services produced in a year\n- (B) All final goods and services produced within a country in a year\n- (C) All intermediate goods produced in a year\n- (D) All exports produced in a country\n- **Answer**: (B) All final goods and services produced within a country in a year\n- **Rationale**: GDP specifically measures the total value of final goods and services produced within national boundaries during a financial year."},{"heading":"Q4. Counting intermediate goods along with final goods while calculating GDP results in:","content":"- (A) Deflation of national income\n- (B) Double counting\n- (C) Accurately reflecting rural output\n- (D) Underestimating service sector output\n- **Answer**: (B) Double counting\n- **Rationale**: The value of final goods already incorporates the value of intermediate inputs; counting them again repeats monetary values."},{"heading":"Q5. In India, which sector contributed the largest share to GDP in 2013–14?","content":"- (A) Primary Sector\n- (B) Secondary Sector\n- (C) Tertiary Sector\n- (D) Foreign Sector\n- **Answer**: (C) Tertiary Sector\n- **Rationale**: The Service (Tertiary) sector expanded rapidly, generating over 60% of India''s GDP."},{"heading":"Q6. A situation where workers are apparently employed, but removing some workers does not reduce total production is known as:","content":"- (A) Structural Unemployment\n- (B) Seasonal Unemployment\n- (C) Disguised Unemployment\n- (D) Frictional Unemployment\n- **Answer**: (C) Disguised Unemployment\n- **Rationale**: Disguised unemployment occurs when excess labor works below full capability, giving a zero marginal product."},{"heading":"Q7. Disguised unemployment is most prevalent in which sector of the Indian economy?","content":"- (A) Urban Banking Sector\n- (B) Rural Agriculture Sector\n- (C) Organised Manufacturing\n- (D) IT and Software Sector\n- **Answer**: (B) Rural Agriculture Sector\n- **Rationale**: Small family holdings engage multiple family members due to lack of alternative job opportunities outside farming."},{"heading":"Q8. Under MGNREGA 2005, how many days of guaranteed wage employment are provided per year to rural households?","content":"- (A) 50 days\n- (B) 100 days\n- (C) 150 days\n- (D) 200 days\n- **Answer**: (B) 100 days\n- **Rationale**: MGNREGA legally guarantees 100 days of unskilled manual wage work annually to eligible rural households."},{"heading":"Q9. If the government fails to provide employment within 15 days under MGNREGA 2005, what is provided to the applicant?","content":"- (A) Free agricultural seeds\n- (B) Unemployment Allowance\n- (C) Bank loan waiver\n- (D) Permanent job in public sector\n- **Answer**: (B) Unemployment Allowance\n- **Rationale**: The act mandates payment of an unemployment allowance if employment is not granted within 15 days of applying."},{"heading":"Q10. What proportion of jobs under MGNREGA 2005 is legally reserved for women?","content":"- (A) One-fourth (25%)\n- (B) One-third (33%)\n- (C) Half (50%)\n- (D) Two-thirds (66%)\n- **Answer**: (B) One-third (33%)\n- **Rationale**: The legislation mandates that at least one-third of all beneficiary workers must be women."},{"heading":"Q11. Which of the following is a characteristic of the Organised Sector?","content":"- (A) Jobs are temporary and insecure\n- (B) Enterprise is registered with the government and follows labor laws\n- (C) No paid leaves or health benefits are provided\n- (D) Overtime work is never paid\n- **Answer**: (B) Enterprise is registered with the government and follows labor laws\n- **Rationale**: Organised enterprises are formally registered and subject to labor laws, offering job security and statutory benefits."},{"heading":"Q12. Workers in the Unorganised Sector are vulnerable because they lack:","content":"- (A) Job security and paid leave\n- (B) Fixed working hours\n- (C) Social security benefits like provident fund\n- (D) All of the above\n- **Answer**: (D) All of the above\n- **Rationale**: Unorganised sector jobs lack job security, paid leaves, fixed hours, overtime pay, and social benefits."},{"heading":"Q13. Which of the following enterprises belongs to the Public Sector in India?","content":"- (A) Tata Iron and Steel Company (TISCO)\n- (B) Reliance Industries Limited (RIL)\n- (C) Indian Railways\n- (D) Infosys\n- **Answer**: (C) Indian Railways\n- **Rationale**: Indian Railways is fully owned, operated, and managed by the Government of India for public welfare."},{"heading":"Q14. What is the primary guiding objective of private sector enterprises?","content":"- (A) Public welfare and social development\n- (B) Maximising financial profit\n- (C) Free distribution of essential goods\n- (D) Regional equity\n- **Answer**: (B) Maximising financial profit\n- **Rationale**: Private individuals and companies invest capital primarily to generate financial profits."},{"heading":"Q15. Why does the government provide subsidies on electricity supplied to small-scale farmers and industries?","content":"- (A) To force private companies out of business\n- (B) To encourage excessive power usage\n- (C) To make production affordable and protect basic livelihoods\n- (D) To reduce GDP contribution\n- **Answer**: (C) To make production affordable and protect basic livelihoods\n- **Rationale**: High commercial power rates would make farming and small manufacturing unviable; government subsidies ensure economic sustainability.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Section 7: Syllabus Coverage Checklist & Master Source Inventory","content":""},{"heading":"Syllabus Coverage Verification Matrix","content":"| Sub-Topic / Theme | Prescribed in RBSE 2026–27 Syllabus? | BoardREADY Coverage Status | Exam Weightage |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Sectors of Economic Activities (Primary, Secondary, Tertiary)** | Yes (Prescribed) | Fully Covered in Theme 1 | 1–2 Marks |\n| **Comparing Sectors (GDP, Final vs Intermediate Goods)** | Yes (Prescribed) | Fully Covered in Theme 2 | 1–3 Marks |\n| **Historical Shift in Sectors & Indian Sectoral Trends** | Yes (Prescribed) | Fully Covered in Themes 2 & 3 | 2–3 Marks |\n| **Rising Importance of Tertiary Sector in India** | Yes (Prescribed) | Fully Covered in Theme 3 | 2–4 Marks |\n| **Underemployment / Disguised Unemployment** | Yes (Prescribed) | Fully Covered in Theme 3 | 2 Marks |\n| **Employment Generation & MGNREGA 2005** | Yes (Prescribed) | Fully Covered in Theme 4 | 3–4 Marks |\n| **Organised vs Unorganised Sector Comparison & Protection** | Yes (Prescribed) | Fully Covered in Theme 5 | 2–4 Marks |\n| **Public vs Private Sector Comparison & Role of Government** | Yes (Prescribed) | Fully Covered in Theme 6 | 2–4 Marks |\n\n---"},{"heading":"Master Source Inventory","content":"1. **BSER Secondary Syllabus 2026–27 Notification Document** (`10_2027.pdf`, p. 38, Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan, Ajmer).\n2. **Official BSER 2026–27 Secondary Social Science Model Question Paper** (`Social-Science-10.pdf`, BSER Ajmer).\n3. **BSER Secondary Class X Main Examination Question Paper 2024 (Social Science, S-08)**.\n4. **BSER Secondary Class X Main Examination Question Paper 2023 (Social Science, S-08)**.\n5. **BSER Secondary Class X Supplementary (D&D) Question Paper 2024 (Social Science, S-124)**.\n6. **NCERT Class X Economics Textbook**: *Understanding Economic Development* (Chapter 2: *Sectors of the Indian Economy*).\n7. **NCERT List of Rationalised Content for Class X Textbooks** (NCERT Publication Division)."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'social-science-20-notes',
  'social-science',
  'social-science-20',
  'Money and Credit',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Money and Credit","subject":"social-science","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"Chapter Metadata & Official Examination Architecture","content":"* **Discipline**: Economics (*Understanding Economic Development*)\n* **Chapter Working List Number**: 20\n* **Chapter Textbook Number**: Chapter 3 (*Money and Credit* / *Mudra and Credit*)\n* **Chapter ID**: `sst-20`\n* **Subject ID**: `sst`\n* **Subject Code**: 08 (Social Science / Social Science)\n* **Target Examination**: 2027 Secondary Board Examination (Academic Session 2026–27)\n* **Examining Body**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER / RBSE), Ajmer\n* **Output Language**: English\n* **Theory Examination Allocation**: **05 Marks** out of 80 Total Theory Marks (Economics Unit Total: 20 Marks across 5 chapters)\n* **Relative Weightage**: 6.25% of the total written examination (Highest single-chapter weightage in Economics alongside *Sectors of the Indian Economy* and *Globalisation*)\n\n---"},{"heading":"Official Syllabus & Prescription Verification Report","content":"* **Prescription Status**: **100% Fully Prescribed** for the 2026–27 academic session and 2027 Secondary Board Examination.\n* **Prescribed Primary Textbook**: *Understanding Economic Development* (Class X Economics textbook published by NCERT under copyright, accredited and distributed by BSER Ajmer).\n* **Rationalisation & Exclusion Check**:\n  * Verified against official BSER syllabus circular (`10_2027.pdf`, p. 38) and NCERT rationalised content notifications.\n  * **Chapter 3: Money and Credit** has **ZERO excluded or rationalised sub-topics**.\n  * All 8 core thematic areas—Barter System & Double Coincidence of Wants, Money as a Medium of Exchange, Modern Forms of Money (Currency, Demand Deposits, Cheques), Loan Activities of Banks, Two Different Credit Situations, Terms of Credit, Formal vs. Informal Sector Credit in India, and Self-Help Groups (SHGs) for the Poor—are active, compulsory, and examinable in full.\n* **Blueprint & Exam Pattern**:\n  * Allocated **05 Marks** in the official BSER blueprint.\n  * Tested in the **2026–27 Official BSER Model Question Paper** as:\n    * **1 Objective / MCQ Question (1 Mark)**: Q16 (Demand Deposits as a medium of exchange).\n    * **1 Essay / Long Answer Question with Internal Choice (4 Marks)**: Q35 (Loan activities of banks vs. Formal and informal sources of credit).\n\n---"},{"heading":"Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes","content":""},{"heading":"1. Money as a Medium of Exchange & The Barter System","content":""},{"heading":"The Barter System & Double Coincidence of Wants","content":"* **Barter System**: An ancient system of economic exchange where goods and services were directly exchanged for other goods and services without the use of money.\n* **Double Coincidence of Wants**: The essential prerequisite for a barter system. It means that what a person desires to sell must be precisely what another person desires to buy.\n  * *Example*: A shoe manufacturer wanting wheat must find a farmer who not only has wheat to sell but also wants to buy shoes in return.\n* **Inconveniences of Barter**:\n  * Finding a trading partner with matching wants was extremely difficult, time-consuming, and inefficient.\n  * Lack of a common unit of value to compare different goods.\n  * Impossibility of storing wealth or deferred payments efficiently."},{"heading":"Money as an Intermediate Medium","content":"* **Definition of Money**: Anything that is generally accepted as a medium of exchange, a measure of value, a store of value, and a standard for deferred payments.\n* **Elimination of Double Coincidence**: Money acts as an intermediate step in the exchange process, eliminating the requirement of double coincidence of wants.\n  * The shoe manufacturer can sell shoes for money to anyone who needs shoes, and then use that money to buy wheat from any farmer.\n\n---"},{"heading":"2. Modern Forms of Money","content":""},{"heading":"Evolution of Money","content":"1. **Pre-modern Forms**: Cattle, food grains, metallic coins of gold, silver, copper (minted by ancient and medieval rulers).\n2. **Modern Forms**: Paper notes, coins, demand deposits, electronic money. Modern money has no intrinsic value of its own (unlike gold/silver coins), yet it is accepted universally as a legal tender.\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                        EVOLUTION OF MONEY                              │\n├──────────────┬──────────────────┬──────────────────┬───────────────────┤\n│ Barter System│ Metallic Currency│ Paper Currency & │ Demand Deposits & │\n│ (Commodities,│ (Gold, Silver,   │ Token Coins      │ Electronic /      │\n│  Cattle)     │  Copper Coins)   │ (Legal Tender)   │ Digital Transfers │\n└──────────────┴──────────────────┴──────────────────┴───────────────────┘\n```"},{"heading":"Paper Notes & Currency","content":"* **Issuing Authority**: In India, the **Reserve Bank of India (RBI)** issues currency notes on behalf of the Central Government.\n* **Legal Tender Status**:\n  * As per Indian law, no other individual or organisation is allowed to issue currency.\n  * The law legalises the use of the **Rupee ($\text{₹}$)** as a medium of payment that cannot be refused in settling transactions in India. No citizen can legally refuse a payment made in Rupees."},{"heading":"Deposits with Banks & Demand Deposits","content":"* **Bank Deposits**: Extra cash held by individuals is deposited in banks by opening a bank account in their name.\n* **Benefits to Depositors**:\n  1. Safety and security of funds.\n  2. Earning interest income paid by the bank on the deposited amount.\n* **Demand Deposits**: Deposits in bank accounts that can be withdrawn by the account holder on demand.\n  * **Medium of Exchange Function**: Since demand deposits can be used as a direct medium of payment via cheques or digital transfers without involving physical cash, they are treated as modern money."},{"heading":"Cheque Facilities","content":"* **Definition of a Cheque**: A paper instructing the bank to pay a specific amount from the drawer''s account to the person in whose name the cheque has been issued.\n* **How Cheque Transactions Work**:\n  1. The buyer draws a cheque for the specific amount and hands it to the seller.\n  2. The seller deposits the cheque in their own bank account.\n  3. Money is transferred from the buyer''s bank account to the seller''s bank account without any physical cash handling.\n\n---"},{"heading":"3. Loan Activities of Banks","content":""},{"heading":"How Banks Intermediary Between Depositors and Borrowers","content":"* Banks act as financial intermediaries between those who have surplus funds (**Depositors**) and those who need funds (**Borrowers**).\n\n```\n                     ┌──────────────────────────────┐\n                     │         B A N K              │\n                     └──────┬────────────────┬──────┘\n                            │                │\n           Deposits Cash    │                │  Grants Loans\n           (Interest Paid)  ▼                ▼  (Interest Charged)\n                     ┌──────────────┐┌──────────────┐\n                     │ DEPOSITORS   ││  BORROWERS   │\n                     │ (Surplus)    ││ (Deficit)    │\n                     └──────────────┘└──────────────┘\n```"},{"heading":"Bank Working Mechanism & Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR)","content":"1. **Holding Cash Reserve**: Banks in India keep only a small fraction of their total deposits as cash reserves—currently around **15%**.\n   * This cash is kept to pay depositors who might come to withdraw money on any given day.\n2. **Utilising Surplus Deposits**: Banks use the major portion (**~85%**) of deposits to extend loans for economic activities (agriculture, industry, trade, housing, education).\n3. **Source of Bank Income**:\n  * Banks charge a higher interest rate on loans than what they pay on deposits.\n  * $\text{Bank Income} = \text{Interest Charged from Borrowers} - \text{Interest Paid to Depositors}$.\n\n---"},{"heading":"4. Two Different Credit Situations","content":""},{"heading":"Credit (Loan) Concept","content":"* **Credit**: An agreement in which the lender supplies the borrower with money, goods, or services in return for the promise of future payment."},{"heading":"Situation 1: Credit as a Positive Asset (Salim''s Case - Festival Season)","content":"* **Context**: Salim, a shoe manufacturer, receives a large order for 3,000 pairs of shoes during festival season.\n* **Credit Utilisation**: Salim takes loans for raw material (leather) and advance cash payment from a trader.\n* **Outcome**: He manufactures and delivers the shoes on time, makes a healthy profit, and repays the loan completely.\n* **Role of Credit**: Credit plays a **vital, positive, and constructive role** in increasing income and economic growth."},{"heading":"Situation 2: Credit as a Debt Trap (Swapna''s Case - Agricultural Risk)","content":"* **Context**: Swapna, a small farmer, cultivates groundnut on 3 acres, taking a loan from a local moneylender.\n* **Credit Utilisation**: Crop failure due to pest attacks despite spraying expensive pesticides.\n* **Outcome**: She is unable to repay the loan. Next year, she takes a fresh loan, but harvest is average. Her debt increases, and she is forced to sell a portion of her land to clear the debt.\n* **Role of Credit**: Credit pushes the borrower into a **Debt Trap**—a painful situation from which recovery is extremely difficult.\n\n---"},{"heading":"5. Terms of Credit","content":"Every loan agreement specifies conditions that the borrower must fulfill. These are collectively called the **Terms of Credit**:\n\n1. **Interest Rate**: The percentage charged by the lender on the principal amount per annum.\n2. **Collateral (Asset Security)**: An asset that the borrower owns (e.g., land, house, vehicle, livestock, bank deposits) and pledges to the lender as a guarantee until the loan is repaid.\n   * If the borrower fails to repay, the lender has the legal right to sell the collateral to recover payment.\n3. **Documentation Requirement**: Valid identity proof, income proof, property deeds, and employment details required by banks before granting a loan.\n4. **Mode of Repayment**: Specified duration, installment structure (monthly/quarterly/yearly), and payment medium (cash, cheque, bank transfer).\n\n---"},{"heading":"6. Formal and Informal Sector Credit in India","content":"```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    CREDIT SECTORS IN INDIA                              │\n├──────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┤\n│      FORMAL SECTOR CREDIT        │       INFORMAL SECTOR CREDIT         │\n├──────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ • Banks (Public & Private)       │ • Moneylenders & Traders             │\n│ • Cooperative Societies          │ • Employers, Relatives, Friends      │\n│ • Supervised by RBI             │ • No Regulatory Supervision          │\n│ • Low, Reasonable Interest Rates │ • Unreasonably High Interest Rates   │\n│ • Strict Collateral & Documents  │ • No Collateral Required             │\n│ • Fair, Legal Recovery Methods   │ • Exploitative & Harsh Recovery      │\n└──────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────┘\n```"},{"heading":"The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) Role in Formal Credit","content":"The RBI supervises the functioning of formal sources of loans:\n1. **Cash Reserve Maintenance**: Ensures banks maintain minimum cash reserves.\n2. **Priority Sector Lending**: Verifies that banks give loans not just to profit-making businesses and traders, but also to small cultivators, small-scale industries, and small borrowers.\n3. **Periodic Reporting**: Receives periodic information from banks on how much they are lending, to whom, and at what interest rate."},{"heading":"Rural Credit Distribution & Inequality","content":"* **Rich Households**: Obtain **85%** of their credit from formal sources and only 15% from informal sources.\n* **Poor Rural Households**: Depend on informal sources for **85%** of their credit needs due to lack of collateral, documentation, and rural bank branches.\n* **Why Formal Credit Must Expand**:\n  * High interest charged by informal lenders leaves little income for borrowers, leading to chronic poverty and debt traps.\n  * Cheap and affordable formal credit is crucial for agricultural development, small scale industries, and poverty alleviation.\n\n---"},{"heading":"7. Self-Help Groups (SHGs) for the Poor","content":""},{"heading":"Origin & Need","content":"* Poor rural households remain excluded from formal bank loans because they lack collateral and formal documentation.\n* To solve this problem, **Self-Help Groups (SHGs)** were introduced as an innovative credit delivery system."},{"heading":"Structure and Functioning of SHGs","content":"* **Composition**: A typical SHG consists of **15 to 20 members**, usually belonging to one neighborhood, who meet and save regularly.\n* **Savings Pool**: Savings per member vary from $\text{₹}25$ to $\text{₹}100$ or more per month, depending on ability.\n* **Internal Loans**: Members can take small loans from the group''s pooled savings to meet urgent needs (buying seeds, fertilizers, cattle, or medical expenses) at very low interest rates set by the group itself.\n* **Bank Linkage**:\n  * After 1 to 2 years of regular savings and functioning, the SHG becomes eligible for a collateral-free loan from a bank in the name of the group.\n  * The bank loan is used to create self-employment opportunities (e.g., buying sewing machines, handlooms, cattle, raw materials).\n* **Group Responsibility**: The group as a whole is responsible for loan repayment. In case of default by one member, other members follow up seriously, which is why commercial banks are willing to lend to SHGs without collateral."},{"heading":"Significance of SHGs","content":"1. Helps rural poor overcome the problem of lack of collateral.\n2. Saves poor villagers from exploitation by informal moneylenders.\n3. Serves as a platform for **women empowerment**, fostering financial self-reliance and regular social discussion on health, nutrition, and domestic issues.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Timelines, Flowcharts & Comparative Matrices","content":""},{"heading":"Master Chronology of Currency & Banking Milestones","content":"| Historical Era / Year | Currency & Financial Milestone | Historical Significance in India |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Ancient & Medieval Period** | Commodity & Metallic Coinage | Use of cattle, grain, punch-marked silver coins, gold *mohrs*, and copper coins. |\n| **1935 (April 1)** | Reserve Bank of India (RBI) Established | Created as the supreme monetary authority to regulate currency and credit in India. |\n| **1949** | Nationalisation of RBI | RBI brought under complete ownership of the Government of India. |\n| **1969** | Nationalisation of 14 Major Private Banks | Expanded formal banking branches to rural and semi-urban India. |\n| **1992** | Launch of SHG-Bank Linkage Programme | NABARD introduced formal bank credit access for rural women Self-Help Groups. |\n| **2005** | Credit Market Reforms & Financial Inclusion | Expansion of priority sector lending and rural banking networks. |\n\n---"},{"heading":"Comparative Matrix: Formal Credit vs. Informal Credit","content":"| Parameter | Formal Sector Credit | Informal Sector Credit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Credit Providers** | Commercial Banks, Regional Rural Banks (RRBs), Cooperative Societies | Local Moneylenders, Merchants, Agricultural Traders, Employers, Relatives |\n| **Supervisory Body** | Reserve Bank of India (RBI) | No overarching regulatory authority |\n| **Interest Rate** | Low and reasonable (typically 7%–12% per annum) | Very high and exorbitant (often 36%–60% per annum or 3%–5% per month) |\n| **Collateral Requirement** | Compulsory collateral asset and legal documentation | Usually informal, based on personal acquaintance; no strict legal paperwork |\n| **Primary Objective** | Social welfare, financial inclusion, and regulated profit | Maximising private profit and extracting land/labour from borrowers |\n| **Impact on Borrower** | Promotes income growth, investment, and economic security | Often leads to chronic debt traps, land alienation, and economic distress |\n\n---"},{"heading":"Verified Previous-Year Board Questions (PYQs)","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1 (Multiple Choice Question)","content":"**Which institution issues currency notes in India on behalf of the Central Government?**  \n*(A) State Bank of India*  \n*(B) Reserve Bank of India*  \n*(C) Ministry of Finance*  \n*(D) Punjab National Bank*  \n* **Source**: BSER Secondary Examination 2024 (S-08, Q1-xiv); BSER 2022 Main Exam (S-08, Q1-x)  \n* **Answer**: **(B) Reserve Bank of India**  \n* **Explanatory Note**: Under Indian law, the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) is the sole authority empowered to issue currency notes on behalf of the Central Government.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 2 (Multiple Choice Question)","content":"**Why is demand deposit considered important in an economy?**  \n*(A) It serves as a medium of exchange*  \n*(B) It is intended solely for government use*  \n*(C) It results in barter exchange*  \n*(D) It is used only by large industries*  \n* **Source**: Official BSER Class 10 Model Paper 2026–27 (Social-Science-10.pdf, Q16)  \n* **Answer**: **(A) It serves as a medium of exchange**  \n* **Explanatory Note**: Demand deposits can be withdrawn or transferred using cheques and digital transactions without handling physical cash, making them a direct medium of exchange.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 3 (Very Short Answer Question)","content":"**Why is money called a medium of exchange?**  \n* **Source**: BSER Secondary Supplementary (D&D) Paper 2024 (S-124, Q10)  \n* **Answer**: Money is called a medium of exchange because it acts as an intermediate instrument in trading transactions, allowing buyers and sellers to exchange goods and services directly without requiring a double coincidence of wants.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 4 (Short Answer Question)","content":"**Ashwin is a student of class tenth. He goes to the bank branch for the first time with his grandmother to withdraw cash. Analyze the necessary details to be filled on a cheque to help Ashwin.**  \n* **Source**: BSER Secondary Main Examination 2023 (S-08, Q17)  \n* **Answer**:  \nA cheque requires the following essential details to be filled correctly:\n1. **Name of the Payee**: The full legal name of the person or self (\"Self\") receiving the money.\n2. **Amount in Words and Figures**: The exact cash amount written clearly in words (e.g., \"Five Thousand Rupees Only\") and numerical figures ($\text{₹}5,000/-$).\n3. **Date**: The specific day, month, and year of cheque issuance (valid for 3 months from date).\n4. **Signature of the Account Holder**: The grandmother''s authorized signature at the bottom right corner matching bank records.\n5. **Account Number**: Preserved or pre-printed account number from which money is to be debited.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 5 (Essay / Long Answer Question with Choice)","content":"**A poor woman living in a small village Manyapura took a loan for farming from a village moneylender. Due to her inability to repay the loan on time, she had to sell some of her ancestral land. In the context of this incident, analyze the credit problem in rural areas.**  \n* **Source**: BSER Secondary Main Examination 2023 (S-08, Q17 OR)  \n* **Answer**:  \nThe incident of Manyapura village highlights the severe **rural credit crisis** in India:\n1. **Over-dependence on Informal Moneylenders**: Rural poor households lack collateral (land deeds, bank deposits) and formal documents, forcing them to rely on local moneylenders who charge exorbitant interest rates (36%–60% per annum).\n2. **Exploitative Terms & Debt Traps**: High interest rates mean that most of the crop earnings go toward paying interest rather than reducing the principal amount. In crop failure situations, the debt compounds rapidly.\n3. **Land Alienation**: Unable to clear the mounting debt, vulnerable rural farmers are forced to sell their productive ancestral land or work as bonded labourers, deepening rural poverty.\n4. **Remedial Measures**:\n   * Expansion of formal commercial bank branches and Regional Rural Banks (RRBs) in villages.\n   * Promotion of **Self-Help Groups (SHGs)** to deliver collateral-free micro-credit to poor women.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Practice Bank & Original Predicted Questions","content":""},{"heading":"Short Answer Practice Question 1","content":"**Explain the meaning of ''Collateral'' and state why formal banks demand it before granting a loan.**\n\n* **Model Answer**:\n  * **Meaning**: Collateral is an asset owned by the borrower (such as land, house, vehicle, livestock, or bank deposits) that is pledged to the lender as a security guarantee until the loan is fully repaid.\n  * **Why Banks Demand Collateral**:\n    1. **Risk Mitigation**: It protects the bank against default risk. If the borrower fails to repay the loan, the bank gains the legal right to sell the collateral asset to recover the unpaid loan amount.\n    2. **Ensuring Borrower Commitment**: Collateral incentivizes the borrower to make regular repayments to avoid losing their valuable property.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Original Predicted Question for 2027 Board Exam (4 Marks)","content":"**Evaluate the role of Self-Help Groups (SHGs) in empowering rural women and improving the rural credit system in India.**\n\n```\n                     ┌──────────────────────────────┐\n                     │   SELF-HELP GROUP (SHG)      │\n                     │   (15 - 20 Rural Members)    │\n                     └──────────────┬───────────────┘\n                                    │\n           ┌────────────────────────┴────────────────────────┐\n           ▼                                                 ▼\n┌──────────────────────────────┐          ┌──────────────────────────────┐\n│  FINANCIAL ADVANTAGES        │          │   SOCIAL ADVANTAGES          │\n├──────────────────────────────┤          ├──────────────────────────────┤\n│ • Small collateral-free loans│          │ • Financial independence for │\n│ • Low interest rates         │          │   rural women                │\n│ • Eliminates moneylenders    │          │ • Platform to discuss health,│\n│ • Group bank linkage credit  │          │   sanitation & domestic issues│\n└──────────────────────────────┘          └──────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n* **Model Answer**:\n  Self-Help Groups (SHGs) have transformed the rural credit landscape and played a pivotal role in women empowerment:\n\n  1. **Overcoming Collateral Barriers**: Formal banks require land deeds or assets as collateral, which poor rural women rarely possess. SHGs provide small loans directly from their collective savings pool without requiring individual collateral.\n  2. **Freedom from Exploitative Moneylenders**: SHGs charge very low, reasonable interest rates set by the members themselves, rescuing poor households from high-interest informal moneylenders and debt traps.\n  3. **Bank Credit Linkage for Self-Employment**: Once an SHG maintains regular savings for 1–2 years, it becomes eligible for a formal bank loan. This capital is utilized for micro-enterprises (buying cattle, handlooms, sewing machines, raw materials), creating sustainable self-employment.\n  4. **Social & Financial Empowerment**: SHGs bring rural women together regularly, fostering financial discipline, decision-making confidence, and providing a collective social platform to address health, nutrition, sanitation, and domestic violence issues.\n\n---"},{"heading":"15-Question MCQ Quiz","content":"```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                      15-QUESTION MCQ QUIZ: SST-20                       │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Instructions: Select the single correct option for each question.       │\n│ Answers and explanations are provided at the end of the quiz.           │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```"},{"heading":"Q1. What essential condition is required for a Barter System to function?","content":"(A) Presence of paper currency  \n(B) Double coincidence of wants  \n(C) Bank supervision  \n(D) High literacy rate"},{"heading":"Q2. Which entity holds the sole legal authority to issue currency notes in India?","content":"(A) State Bank of India  \n(B) Ministry of Finance  \n(C) Reserve Bank of India  \n(D) NITI Aayog"},{"heading":"Q3. Why is the Rupee ($\text{₹}$) universally accepted as a medium of exchange in India?","content":"(A) It is made of precious metals  \n(B) It is authorized by law as a legal tender  \n(C) It is issued by international banks  \n(D) It can be exchanged for gold at any shop"},{"heading":"Q4. Deposits in bank accounts that can be withdrawn by the holder on demand are known as:","content":"(A) Fixed Deposits  \n(B) Recurring Deposits  \n(C) Demand Deposits  \n(D) Post Office Deposits"},{"heading":"Q5. What percentage of total cash deposits do commercial banks in India roughly hold as cash reserve?","content":"(A) 50%  \n(B) 15%  \n(C) 80%  \n(D) 100%"},{"heading":"Q6. What is the main source of income for commercial banks?","content":"(A) Account opening fees charged to customers  \n(B) Government subsidies  \n(C) Difference between interest charged on loans and interest paid on deposits  \n(D) Tax collection commissions"},{"heading":"Q7. In Salim''s shoe manufacturing case, credit played a:","content":"(A) Negative and destructive role  \n(B) Positive and constructive role  \n(C) Irrelevant role  \n(D) Neutral role without profit"},{"heading":"Q8. A situation where credit pushes the borrower into an inescapable painful situation is termed as:","content":"(A) Capital formation  \n(B) Financial growth  \n(C) Debt trap  \n(D) Asset creation"},{"heading":"Q9. Which of the following is NOT part of the ''Terms of Credit''?","content":"(A) Interest rate  \n(B) Collateral  \n(C) Mode of repayment  \n(D) Voter Identity Card number"},{"heading":"Q10. An asset that the borrower owns and pledges to the lender as a security guarantee is called:","content":"(A) Deposit  \n(B) Collateral  \n(C) Currency  \n(D) Credit score"},{"heading":"Q11. Which of the following is a formal source of credit?","content":"(A) Moneylender  \n(B) Agricultural trader  \n(C) Commercial Bank  \n(D) Employer"},{"heading":"Q12. Who supervises the credit activities of formal sector lenders in India?","content":"(A) Finance Minister  \n(B) State Bank of India  \n(C) Reserve Bank of India  \n(D) Supreme Court of India"},{"heading":"Q13. According to data on credit sources, what percentage of credit needs of rich urban households come from formal sources?","content":"(A) 15%  \n(B) 50%  \n(C) 85%  \n(D) 100%"},{"heading":"Q14. What is the typical membership size of a rural Self-Help Group (SHG)?","content":"(A) 5 to 10 members  \n(B) 15 to 20 members  \n(C) 100 to 200 members  \n(D) 500 members"},{"heading":"Q15. Why are commercial banks willing to extend loans to SHGs without individual collateral?","content":"(A) The government pays all interest  \n(B) The group as a whole is responsible for loan repayment  \n(C) Members pledge gold to the bank  \n(D) Members are corporate employees  \n\n---"},{"heading":"MCQ Quiz Answer Key & Explanatory Rationales","content":"| Q.No | Correct Option | Explanatory Rationale |\n| :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| **Q1** | **(B)** | The barter system requires a double coincidence of wants, where both parties agree to sell and buy each other''s commodities. |\n| **Q2** | **(C)** | Under Indian law, only the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) is authorized to issue currency notes on behalf of the Central Government. |\n| **Q3** | **(B)** | The Rupee is legal tender legally authorized by the Government of India, and no citizen can legally refuse payment made in Rupees. |\n| **Q4** | **(C)** | Deposits that can be withdrawn from banks on demand by the account holder are defined as demand deposits. |\n| **Q5** | **(B)** | Banks in India maintain approximately 15% of their deposits as cash reserve to meet daily withdrawal demands. |\n| **Q6** | **(C)** | Bank income is the spread between the higher interest rate charged on loans and the lower interest rate paid to depositors. |\n| **Q7** | **(B)** | In Salim''s case, credit enabled timely shoe production and profit, acting as a positive and constructive asset. |\n| **Q8** | **(C)** | When crop failure or high interest prevents loan repayment, debt compounds, pushing the borrower into a painful debt trap. |\n| **Q9** | **(D)** | Terms of credit consist of interest rate, collateral, documentation, and mode of repayment. Voter ID is not a credit term. |\n| **Q10** | **(B)** | Collateral is an asset pledged by a borrower as security until the loan is fully repaid to the lender. |\n| **Q11** | **(C)** | Commercial banks and cooperative societies are formal sources of credit supervised by the RBI. |\n| **Q12** | **(C)** | The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) monitors cash reserves, priority lending, and interest rates of formal financial institutions. |\n| **Q13** | **(C)** | Rich urban households obtain 85% of their loans from formal sources, whereas poor rural households rely 85% on informal sources. |\n| **Q14** | **(B)** | A standard Self-Help Group (SHG) comprises 15 to 20 members from a local rural neighborhood. |\n| **Q15** | **(B)** | Banks lend to SHGs without collateral because the entire group takes joint responsibility for timely loan repayment. |\n\n---"},{"heading":"Topic-by-Topic Syllabus Coverage Checklist","content":"| S.No | Prescribed Sub-Topic / Concept | BSER Syllabus Mapping | Coverage Status |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- | :---: |\n| 1 | Barter System & Double Coincidence of Wants | NCERT Ch. 3 / Section 1 | **100% Covered** |\n| 2 | Money as a Medium of Exchange | NCERT Ch. 3 / Section 1 | **100% Covered** |\n| 3 | Modern Forms of Money (Currency & RBI Legal Tender) | NCERT Ch. 3 / Section 2 | **100% Covered** |\n| 4 | Demand Deposits & Cheque Payment Mechanism | NCERT Ch. 3 / Section 2 | **100% Covered** |\n| 5 | Loan Activities of Banks & Cash Reserves | NCERT Ch. 3 / Section 3 | **100% Covered** |\n| 6 | Two Different Credit Situations (Salim vs. Swapna) | NCERT Ch. 3 / Section 4 | **100% Covered** |\n| 7 | Terms of Credit (Interest, Collateral, Documents, Repayment) | NCERT Ch. 3 / Section 5 | **100% Covered** |\n| 8 | Formal vs. Informal Sector Credit in India | NCERT Ch. 3 / Section 6 | **100% Covered** |\n| 9 | Role of Reserve Bank of India (RBI) | NCERT Ch. 3 / Section 6 | **100% Covered** |\n| 10 | Self-Help Groups (SHGs) for the Poor | NCERT Ch. 3 / Section 7 | **100% Covered** |\n\n---"},{"heading":"Master Source Inventory & Verification Log","content":"1. **Official BSER Syllabus Document (`10_2027.pdf`, p. 38)**: Authoritative confirmation of Economics Chapter 3 (*Money and Credit*) prescription and **05 Marks** theory allocation for the 2027 examination.\n2. **Official BSER Secondary Model Question Paper 2026–27 (`Social-Science-10.pdf`)**: Verification of blueprint question distribution—Q16 (Demand Deposits MCQ [1 Mark]) and Q35 (Loan activities of banks vs. Formal/Informal credit essay choice [4 Marks]).\n3. **BSER Secondary Main Exam Paper 2024 (S-08)**: Primary source for Q1-xiv (RBI currency issuing authority).\n4. **BSER Secondary Main Exam Paper 2023 (S-08)**: Primary source for Q17 (Cheque details prompt & Manyapura village rural credit case study).\n5. **BSER Secondary Supplementary (D&D) Paper 2024 (S-124)**: Source for Q10 (Currency as medium of exchange) and terms of credit options.\n6. **Prescribed NCERT Class X Economics Textbook (*Understanding Economic Development*)**: Primary conceptual foundation for all revision notes, definitions, credit situations, and SHG frameworks."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'social-science-21-notes',
  'social-science',
  'social-science-21',
  'Globalisation and the Indian Economy',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Globalisation and the Indian Economy","subject":"social-science","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"1. Official Syllabus & Prescription Verification Report","content":"* **Prescription Status**: **100% Fully Prescribed** for the 2026–27 academic session and 2027 Secondary Board Examination.\n* **Board Weightage**: **05 Marks** out of 80 Theory Marks in Social Science (This carries the highest single-chapter weightage in Economics alongside *Sectors of the Indian Economy* and *Money and Credit*).\n* **NCERT Rationalisation vs. BSER Board Policy Analysis**:\n  * Verified against the official Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER, Ajmer) Syllabus Circular (`10_2027.pdf`, p. 38/p. 40) and the 2026–27 Official Model Question Paper (`Social-Science-10.pdf`).\n  * While NCERT''s central rationalisation exercise for CBSE schools reduced some descriptive sections in Chapter 4, **BSER Ajmer explicitly lists all 8 core sub-topics** in its official 2026–27 syllabus circular (`10_2027.pdf`, p. 38) and assigns **05 Marks** to this unit in the written secondary board examination.\n  * **100% Active Sub-topics**: Production across countries (MNCs), Interlinking production across countries, Foreign trade and integration of markets, Meaning of Globalisation, Factors enabling Globalisation (Transport & IT), Liberalisation of foreign trade & foreign investment policy, World Trade Organization (WTO), Impact of Globalisation in India, and The Struggle for a Fair Globalisation.\n* **Blueprint Question Pattern**: Allocated **05 Marks** in the BSER blueprint. Tested in the **2026–27 Official BSER Model Question Paper** as **1 Short Answer Question / Analytical Question (3 Marks)** (Q32 on Impact of Globalisation / How MNCs interlink production) + **1 Short Answer Question (2 Marks)** (Q23 on Foreign Investment & Liberalisation), or a combination with Objective / MCQ Questions (1 Mark).\n\n---"},{"heading":"2. Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes","content":""},{"heading":"2.1 Production Across Countries & Multinational Corporations (MNCs)","content":"* **Trade in the Mid-20th Century**: Until the mid-20th century, production was largely organized within national boundaries. Trade between countries consisted primarily of raw materials, food-stuffs, and finished consumer goods. Foreign trade connected distant countries through merchant channels.\n* **Definition of a Multinational Corporation (MNC)**: A Multinational Corporation is a company that owns or controls production in more than one nation. MNCs set up offices and factories for production in regions where they can get cheap labour, raw materials, and other resources.\n* **Global Dispersal of Production**:\n  * MNCs do not produce the entire product in a single country; they break down the production process into small parts and spread them across the globe.\n  * **Factors Determining Production Locations**:\n    1. *Proximity to Markets*: Factories are set up close to major consumer markets (e.g., Mexico and Eastern Europe for US and European markets).\n    2. *Availability of Cheap Labour*: Manufacturing is located where skilled and unskilled labour is available at low costs (e.g., China as a major cheap manufacturing hub).\n    3. *Technical & Service Expertise*: Countries with large pools of educated, English-speaking youth provide technical and customer care services (e.g., India providing high-tech software engineers and call centres).\n    4. *Government Policies*: Favourable tax laws, industrial infrastructure, and relaxed investment rules.\n* **Cost Advantage**: Dispersing production globally allows MNCs to cut production costs by **50% to 60%**, giving them immense competitive strength in world markets.\n* **NCERT Production Case Study**: An American industrial equipment MNC designs its products in research centres in the United States, manufactures the components in China (cheap manufacturing), ships them to Mexico and Eastern Europe for assembly, sells the finished products worldwide, and handles customer care out of call centres in Bengaluru, India.\n\n---"},{"heading":"2.2 Interlinking Production Across Countries","content":"* **Investment vs. Foreign Investment**:\n  * **Investment**: Money that is spent to buy assets such as land, buildings, machines, and other equipment in order to earn profits.\n  * **Foreign Investment**: Investment made by Multinational Corporations (MNCs) in countries other than their home nation.\n* **Four Major Routes through which MNCs Interlink Production Globally**:\n  1. **Joint Ventures / Partnerships with Local Companies**:\n     * MNCs enter foreign markets by partnering with existing local companies.\n     * *Twofold Benefits for Local Companies*: MNCs provide additional capital for buying modern machinery and bring the latest international technology into production processes.\n     * *Example*: In 1995, **Ford Motors** (US MNC) entered India by setting up a joint venture with **Mahindra & Mahindra** (Indian automobile maker) near Chennai, investing ₹1,700 crore to manufacture cars for both Indian and global markets.\n  2. **Buying Up Local Companies (Direct Takeover)**:\n     * The most common route for MNC expansion. MNCs possess vast wealth exceeding the entire national budgets of many developing countries, allowing them to easily buy up established domestic firms.\n     * *Example*: **Cargill Foods** (a giant American MNC) bought **Parakh Foods** (a large Indian edible oil company). Parakh Foods had a vast marketing network across India and four oil refineries. Cargill took over its operations, becoming the largest producer of edible oil in India with a capacity of 5 million pouches daily.\n  3. **Placing Orders with Small Producers (Sub-Contracting / Outsourcing)**:\n     * MNCs place large orders for finished goods (garments, footwear, sports equipment, toys) with thousands of small, independent manufacturers across developing countries.\n     * The MNCs receive the products, print their own brand names on them, and sell them to consumers worldwide at high prices.\n     * *Power of MNCs*: MNCs determine the price, quality, delivery schedule, and labour working conditions for these distant small suppliers.\n  4. **Technology Transfers and Licensing Agreements**:\n     * Supplying proprietary designs, patents, and manufacturing processes to foreign producers under strict quality control agreements.\n\n---"},{"heading":"2.3 Foreign Trade and Market Integration","content":"* **Primary Function of Foreign Trade**: Foreign trade creates an opportunity for domestic producers to reach markets beyond their national boundaries. Simultaneously, it expands consumer choices beyond domestically produced goods.\n* **Market Integration Process**:\n  * With the opening of trade, goods travel from one country to another.\n  * Choice of goods in the markets rises significantly.\n  * Prices of similar goods in two distant markets tend to equalize due to price competition.\n  * Domestic and foreign producers compete directly with each other, integrating national markets into a single global market.\n* **Textbook Case Study: *Chinese Toys in India***:\n  * Chinese manufacturers began exporting plastic toys to India due to lower manufacturing costs.\n  * Indian buyers got a wide variety of toys at significantly lower prices compared to traditional Indian wooden or clay toys.\n  * Within a single year, Chinese toys replaced Indian toys in **70% to 80%** of toy shops.\n  * *Economic Result*: Indian consumers benefitted from cheaper choices, but domestic Indian toy manufacturers faced severe business losses, forcing many small toy factories to shut down.\n\n---"},{"heading":"2.4 Meaning of Globalisation","content":"* **Formal Definition**: Globalisation is the process of rapid integration or interconnectedness between countries through foreign trade and foreign investment, facilitated by Multinational Corporations (MNCs).\n* **Four Major Dimensions / Global Flows**:\n  1. *Flow of Goods and Services*: Movement of agricultural, manufactured, and service products across borders.\n  2. *Flow of Capital / Investment*: Movement of money and foreign direct investments across nations.\n  3. *Flow of Technology*: Rapid spread of ideas, software, designs, and manufacturing techniques.\n  4. *Flow of People (Migration)*: Movement of people between countries in search of better employment, higher income, or education.\n\n---"},{"heading":"2.5 Factors that Have Enabled Globalisation (Enabler 1: Technology)","content":"1. **Rapid Advances in Transportation Technology**:\n   * Over the past 50 years, faster locomotives, long-haul cargo planes, and supertankers have made long-distance delivery of goods significantly faster and cheaper.\n   * **Containerization**: Goods are packed into standardized steel containers that can be loaded intact onto ships, trains, trucks, and planes. Container handling has dramatically reduced port handling costs, minimized transit damage, and accelerated market delivery worldwide.\n2. **Information and Communication Technology (IT & Telecom)**:\n   * **Telecommunications**: Telegraph, mobile phones, fax, and satellite communication devices allow instant contact across the globe, remote decision-making, and real-time trade monitoring.\n   * **Internet & Computers**: E-mail, World Wide Web, e-commerce, and cloud computing allow administrative, design, financial, and publishing services to be conducted remotely.\n   * *Example of IT-enabled Outsourcing*: A news magazine published for readers in London is designed and printed in Delhi. The text is sent via the Internet to Delhi; editors in Delhi design the layout on computers using instructions received from London; the magazine is printed in Delhi and dispatched by air to London; payments are transferred instantly from London to Delhi via e-banking.\n\n---"},{"heading":"2.6 Factors that Have Enabled Globalisation (Enabler 2: Liberalisation of Foreign Trade & Investment)","content":"* **Trade Barriers**:\n  * A trade barrier is a government restriction or regulation imposed on international trade, such as import duties (taxes), quotas, or licensing requirements.\n  * *Why Tax on Imports acts as a Barrier*: Importing goods attracts tax, making imported items more expensive in domestic markets, thereby protecting domestic producers from foreign price competition.\n* **India''s Trade Barrier Policy Post-Independence (1947–1990)**:\n  * After independence in 1947, the Indian government put barriers on foreign trade and foreign investment to protect young domestic industries from competition by powerful foreign companies.\n  * During the 1950s and 1960s, Indian industries were just coming up, and competition from imports would have killed these infant sectors. India allowed imports of only essential goods such as machinery, fertilizers, and petroleum.\n* **New Economic Policy of 1991 & Liberalisation**:\n  * Around 1991, the Indian government realized that Indian producers had matured and needed to compete with international producers to improve product quality, efficiency, and technology.\n  * **Definition of Liberalisation**: Removing barriers or restrictions set by the government on foreign trade and foreign investment.\n  * Under liberalisation, import licenses were abolished for almost all items, tariffs were drastically reduced, and foreign companies were allowed to invest freely in India.\n\n---"},{"heading":"2.7 World Trade Organization (WTO)","content":"* **Establishment & Headquarters**: Established on **1 January 1995** (succeeding the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade - GATT). Headquartered in Geneva, Switzerland.\n* **Core Objectives**:\n  1. To liberalise international trade by removing trade barriers globally.\n  2. To establish fair rules for international trade among member nations.\n  3. To ensure that member countries strictly adhere to trade agreements and non-discriminatory policies.\n* **Critical Issues & Debates Surrounding WTO**:\n  * Although WTO aims to allow free trade for all, in practice, developed nations have unfairly retained trade barriers and massive subsidies (especially in agriculture), while forcing developing nations to remove their protective tariffs and agricultural subsidies.\n  * *Example*: US farmers receive huge government subsidies, allowing them to dump artificially cheap cotton and wheat in international markets, devastating un-subsidized small farmers in developing countries like India.\n\n---"},{"heading":"2.8 Impact of Globalisation in India","content":"* **Positive Impacts**:\n  1. *Benefits to Consumers*: Urban consumers enjoy a wide variety of high-quality goods (automobiles, cell phones, electronics, fast food) at lower prices, leading to higher standards of living.\n  2. *MNC Investments & Job Creation*: MNCs have invested heavily in Indian industries like cell phones, automobiles, electronics, banking, and services, creating direct employment and boosting local ancillary suppliers.\n  3. *Emergence of Indian MNCs*: Top Indian companies leveraged competition to expand globally into multinationals—**Tata Motors** (automobiles), **Infosys** (IT services), **Ranbaxy** (pharmaceuticals), **Asian Paints** (paints), and **Sundaram Fasteners** (nuts and bolts).\n  4. *Boom in IT & Service Outsourcing*: Millions of jobs created in IT, BPOs, call centres, financial accounting, software development, and administrative services.\n* **Negative Impacts & Structural Challenges**:\n  1. *Severe Blow to Small Producers*: Small-scale industries manufacturing batteries, capacitors, plastics, toys, tyres, and vegetable oil faced fierce import competition. Many small units shut down, rendering lakhs of workers unemployed.\n  2. *Insecurity of Employment & Flexibilisation of Labour*: To cut costs, employers hire workers on a flexible, temporary, seasonal basis without provident fund, medical benefits, or job security (e.g., garment exporters in Surat, Tirupur, and Noida).\n  3. *Widening Disparities*: Benefits of globalisation are concentrated among educated, skilled, and wealthy urban citizens, while unorganized workers, small producers, and landless farmers suffer increased economic vulnerability.\n\n---"},{"heading":"2.9 The Struggle for a Fair Globalisation","content":"* **Concept of Fair Globalisation**: Fair globalisation creates equitable opportunities for all citizens and ensures that the benefits of global economic integration are shared fairly among all social groups, rather than benefitting only the rich and powerful.\n* **How the Government Can Ensure Fair Globalisation**:\n  1. **Enforce Labour Laws Strictly**: Ensure that workers'' legal rights are protected and temporary/informal workers receive fair wages and social security.\n  2. **Support Small Producers**: Provide credit, modern technology, and infrastructure to small-scale industries until they become strong enough to compete with MNCs.\n  3. **Use Trade and Investment Barriers Strategically**: Retain targeted tariffs or import quotas when necessary to protect vulnerable domestic employment sectors.\n  4. **Negotiate at the WTO for Fairer Rules**: Align with other developing nations to challenge agricultural subsidies and protectionist policies maintained by developed countries.\n  5. **People''s Campaign Influence**: Mass campaigns, NGOs, and trade union representations can shape international trade negotiations and force government policy revisions.\n\n---"},{"heading":"3. Master Chronology & Analytical Comparative Tables","content":""},{"heading":"3.1 Historical Milestones of Globalisation & Trade Policies in India","content":"| Period / Year | Event / Milestone | Economic Significance for India & World |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1947–1950s** | Independence & Trade Barrier Policy | India placed strict barriers on foreign trade/investment to protect infant domestic industries. |\n| **1960s–1980s** | Import Substitution Phase | Allowed import of only critical industrial items (machinery, petroleum, fertilizers). |\n| **1991** | New Economic Policy (NEP) & Liberalisation | Removal of major trade barriers, opening doors to foreign investment and private enterprise. |\n| **1995 (1 Jan)** | Formation of World Trade Organization (WTO) | Global trade governing body established; India becomes a founding member. |\n| **1995** | Ford Motors Joint Venture | Ford Motors invested ₹1,700 crore in Chennai with Mahindra & Mahindra, showcasing MNC entry. |\n| **2000s–Present** | Rise of Indian Multinationals & IT Hubs | Indian firms (Tata, Infosys, Ranbaxy) expand globally; India becomes global BPO/IT capital. |\n\n---"},{"heading":"3.2 Foreign Investment vs. Domestic Investment","content":"| Feature | Foreign Investment (FDI) | Domestic Investment |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Definition** | Investment made by foreign entities / MNCs in a host country. | Investment made by domestic citizens, companies, or government within their nation. |\n| **Primary Source** | Foreign capital reserves, foreign banks, or global capital markets. | Domestic household savings, domestic commercial banks, and national financial institutions. |\n| **Main Objective** | To expand international markets, cut production costs, and maximize profits. | To build domestic industrial capacity, generate regional employment, and earn local profit. |\n| **Technology Linkage**| Brings modern international technology and advanced global management practices. | Relies on existing domestic technology or imported technology licenses. |\n| **Example** | Cargill Foods purchasing Parakh Foods or Ford Motors setting up a factory near Chennai. | An Indian entrepreneur setting up a local textile mill using domestic bank loans. |\n\n---"},{"heading":"3.3 Trade Barriers vs. Liberalisation","content":"| Criterion | Trade Barriers | Liberalisation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Definition** | Government restrictions/taxes imposed on foreign trade and investment. | Removal or relaxation of government restrictions on foreign trade and investment. |\n| **Tools / Instruments**| Import duties (tariffs), import quotas, strict licensing rules. | Abolition of import licenses, reduction of customs tariffs, free currency movement. |\n| **Primary Objective**| To protect infant domestic industries from competition by powerful foreign firms. | To increase competition, improve quality, adopt modern tech, and integrate with global markets. |\n| **Impact on Prices** | Makes imported goods expensive, protecting domestic prices. | Lowers prices of imported goods, increasing market choices for consumers. |\n| **Policy Period in India**| Prominently followed from 1947 to 1990. | Adopted under the New Economic Policy from 1991 onwards. |\n\n---"},{"heading":"3.4 Positive vs. Negative Impacts of Globalisation on the Indian Economy","content":"| Aspect | Positive Impacts | Negative Impacts |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Consumer Experience** | Wider choice, superior international quality, lower prices for urban consumers. | Traditional local goods are displaced; small consumers lose access to cheap artisanal products. |\n| **Industrial Growth** | MNC investment in automobiles, cell phones, electronics, and fast food sectors. | Small-scale manufacturers (batteries, toys, tyres, capacitors) suffer heavy losses or shutdown. |\n| **Employment Trends** | Creation of high-paying jobs in IT, BPO, software, and skilled service sectors. | Informalisation of industrial labour; workers hired on flexible, temporary terms without security. |\n| **Domestic Companies** | Indian companies transform into global MNCs (Tata Motors, Infosys, Ranbaxy). | Uncompetitive domestic small units are forced out of business due to cheap foreign imports. |\n| **Economic Disparity** | Rapid income growth for educated, skilled, and wealthy urban population. | Widening gap between prosperous urban beneficiaries and insecure rural/unorganized workers. |\n\n---"},{"heading":"4. Verified Previous-Year Board Questions (PYQs)","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1 (1 Mark - Objective / MCQ) [BSER 2024 Main Exam, Code S-08, Q1(xv)]","content":"**As a trade barrier, tax is imposed on:**\n(A) Export  \n(B) Import  \n(C) Capital Investment  \n(D) Subsidy  \n**Official Model Answer**: **(B) on Import** [Passage 81]\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 2 (2 Marks - Short Answer) [BSER 2024 Main Exam, Code S-08, Q14]","content":"**Write any two main functions of World Trade Organisation (WTO).**  \n**Official Model Answer**:  \n1. **Establishing Global Trade Rules**: To set up non-discriminatory rules and regulations for international trade among member countries. [Passage 84]  \n2. **Promoting Free Trade**: To liberalise international trade by encouraging member nations to reduce custom duties, tariffs, and trade barriers. [Passage 84]\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 3 (1 Mark - Very Short) [BSER 2024 Supplementary (D&D) Exam, Code S-124, Q11]","content":"**Between whom does foreign trade take place?**  \n**Official Model Answer**: Foreign trade takes place between two or more different countries (nations). [Passage 86]\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 4 (2 Marks - Short Answer) [BSER 2024 Supplementary (D&D) Exam, Code S-124, Q19]","content":"**Mention any one loss to India as a result of Globalisation.**  \n**Official Model Answer**: Small-scale producers in India suffered severe losses or closure due to competition from cheap foreign imports (e.g., batteries, capacitors, plastics, and toys), resulting in worker retrenchment and job insecurity. [Passage 104]\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 5 (3 Marks - Short/Analytical) [BSER 2024 Supplementary (D&D) Exam, Code S-124, Q25]","content":"**What do you understand by Globalization? Explain.**  \n**Official Model Answer**: Globalization is the process of rapid integration or interconnectedness between different countries. It involves the movement of goods, services, investments, and technology across national borders, largely driven by Multinational Corporations (MNCs). It allows producers to reach foreign markets and provides consumers with wider choices and competitive prices. [Passage 87]\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 6 (1 Mark - Fill in the Blank) [BSER 2023 Main Exam, Code S-08, Q1(xi)]","content":"**What is the main aim of World Trade Organization?**  \n**Official Model Answer**: The main aim of the World Trade Organization (WTO) is to liberalise international trade and establish fair rules for global trade among nations. [Passage 85]\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 7 (1 Mark - Fill in the Blank) [BSER 2022 Main Exam, Code S-08, Q2(i)]","content":"**Companies that do business in several countries at the same time are called __________ corporations.**  \n**Official Model Answer**: **Multinational** (Multinational Corporations / MNCs) [Passage 88]\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 8 (1 Mark - Fill in the Blank) [BSER 2022 Main Exam, Code S-08, Q2(vi)]","content":"**Rapid improvement in ___________ has stimulated the globalisation process.**  \n**Official Model Answer**: **Technology** (or Transportation and Information-Communication Technology) [Passage 89]\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 9 (2 Marks - Short Answer) [BSER 2022 Main Exam, Code S-08, Q16]","content":"**How do multinational companies work? Explain.**  \n**Official Model Answer**: Multinational Corporations (MNCs) operate by dispersing their production processes across multiple countries to reduce costs. They set up manufacturing in regions with cheap labour and raw materials (e.g., China), establish assembly units close to markets (e.g., Mexico), and outsource technical or customer care services to skilled pools in developing nations (e.g., India). [Passage 91]\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 10 (2 Marks - Short Answer) [BSER 2026–27 Official Model Paper, Code S-08, Q23]","content":"**Explain the concept of Liberalisation in the context of foreign trade and investment policy in India.**  \n**Official Model Answer**: Liberalisation refers to the removal or relaxation of government restrictions and barriers on foreign trade and foreign investment. Adopted under India''s New Economic Policy in 1991, it allowed businesses freedom to import/export goods freely and enabled foreign companies to invest directly in the Indian market. [Passage 98]\n\n---"},{"heading":"Question 11 (3 Marks - Short Answer / Case Study) [BSER 2026–27 Official Model Paper, Code S-08, Q32]","content":"**Analyse the various ways through which Multinational Corporations (MNCs) interlink production across countries.**  \n**Official Model Answer**: MNCs interlink production globally through four main strategies:  \n1. *Joint Ventures*: Partnering with local domestic companies by providing foreign capital and modern technology (e.g., Ford Motors with Mahindra & Mahindra).  \n2. *Direct Takeovers*: Purchasing established local companies to gain immediate manufacturing and distribution networks (e.g., Cargill Foods buying Parakh Foods).  \n3. *Sub-contracting / Outsourcing*: Placing orders with small producers in developing nations for items like garments, shoes, and sports equipment, selling them under MNC brand names.  \n4. *Market Proximity*: Setting up assembly plants close to major world consumer markets. [Passage 98, 114]\n\n---"},{"heading":"5. Practice Bank & Original Predicted Questions","content":""},{"heading":"Short Answer Practice Questions","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1","content":"**Why did the Indian government impose barriers to foreign trade and foreign investment after independence in 1947? Why were these barriers removed in 1991?**  \n**Model Answer**:  \n* **Imposition in 1947**: To protect nascent domestic industries from competition by powerful foreign firms during the 1950s and 1960s, as Indian industries were in their infant stage and needed protection to survive.  \n* **Removal in 1991**: Under the New Economic Policy, the government recognized that Indian producers had matured and needed to compete globally to improve product quality, efficiency, and adopt modern technology."},{"heading":"Question 2","content":"**How has containerization in transportation technology accelerated globalisation?**  \n**Model Answer**: Containerization involves packing goods into standardized steel containers that can be loaded unbroken onto ships, trains, trucks, and planes. This has dramatically reduced port handling costs, eliminated transit damage, and accelerated the delivery of goods to distant global markets at significantly lower transport costs."},{"heading":"Question 3","content":"**Explain the impact of Chinese toys on Indian toy manufacturers.**  \n**Model Answer**: Chinese plastic toys entered Indian markets at significantly lower prices with innovative designs. Indian consumers benefitted from cheaper choices, but Chinese toys captured 70% to 80% of the market within a year. As a result, traditional Indian toy manufacturers suffered massive financial losses, forcing many small toy factories to shut down.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Original Predicted Board Exam Questions (2027 Examination)","content":""},{"heading":"Predicted Essay Question 1 (04 Marks - Essay / Long Answer)","content":"**\"Globalisation has produced mixed results for the Indian economy.\" Critically evaluate this statement by discussing two positive and two negative impacts of globalisation on India.**\n\n**Official BSER Marking Scheme (04 Marks Total)**:\n* *Definition / Contextual Introduction* (01 Mark)\n* *Two Positive Impacts explained with examples* (1.5 Marks)\n* *Two Negative Impacts explained with examples* (1.5 Marks)\n\n**Model Answer**:  \n**Introduction**: Globalisation—the rapid integration of national economies through foreign trade and investment—has brought profound transformations to the Indian economy since the 1991 liberalisation reforms, producing both significant gains and structural challenges.\n\n**Positive Impacts**:\n1. *Consumer Choice & Higher Living Standards*: Urban consumers enjoy access to superior quality, technologically advanced products (automobiles, mobile phones, electronics, fast food) at competitive prices.\n2. *Investment & Global Indian MNCs*: Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) created millions of service and manufacturing jobs. Furthermore, top Indian firms expanded into multinationals globally (e.g., Tata Motors, Infosys, Ranbaxy, Asian Paints).\n\n**Negative Impacts**:\n1. *Destruction of Small-Scale Industries*: Small domestic producers of batteries, capacitors, plastics, toys, tyres, and vegetable oil could not compete with cheap foreign imports, leading to widespread unit closures and job retrenchments.\n2. *Flexibilisation and Insecurity of Labour*: To cut costs, export industries hire workers on a temporary, informal basis without social security, medical benefits, or job permanence (e.g., garment units in Surat and Noida).\n\n**Conclusion**: While globalisation modernized India''s urban service and corporate sectors, it harmed small producers and unorganized workers, highlighting the need for government policy intervention.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Predicted Essay Question 2 (04 Marks - Essay / Long Answer with Choice)","content":"**What is meant by ''Fair Globalisation''? Suggest three concrete measures that the government can adopt to make globalisation fairer in India.**\n\n**Official BSER Marking Scheme (04 Marks Total)**:\n* *Concept of Fair Globalisation* (01 Mark)\n* *Three Government Measures clearly explained* (03 Marks - 01 Mark each)\n\n**Model Answer**:  \n**Concept of Fair Globalisation**: Fair globalisation refers to a system of global economic integration that creates equitable opportunities for all citizens and ensures that the benefits of international trade and investment are shared fairly among all social classes, rather than benefitting only the rich, educated, and powerful.\n\n**Three Government Measures for Fair Globalisation**:\n1. **Strict Enforcement of Labour Laws**: The government must ensure that worker rights are protected under law, enforcing proper wages, safe working conditions, social security, and preventing arbitrary retrenchment of temporary workers.\n2. **Support & Protection for Small Producers**: The government must provide cheap credit, modern infrastructure, technology upgrades, and marketing support to small-scale industries until they become strong enough to compete with giant MNCs.\n3. **Strategic Use of Trade Barriers & WTO Diplomacy**: The government should retain targeted import duties or tariffs on vulnerable agricultural and small industrial products. At the international level, India must align with other developing nations to negotiate at the WTO against unfair agricultural subsidies maintained by developed countries.\n\n---"},{"heading":"6. 15-Question MCQ Quiz","content":""},{"heading":"Q1. A company that owns or controls production in more than one nation is called a:","content":"(A) Private Limited Company  \n(B) Public Sector Undertaking  \n(C) Multinational Corporation (MNC)  \n(D) Joint Liability Group  \n**Answer**: **(C)**  \n*Rationale*: By definition, an MNC is a corporation that operates and controls production facilities in more than one nation."},{"heading":"Q2. Investment made by Multinational Corporations (MNCs) in foreign countries is known as:","content":"(A) Gross Domestic Investment  \n(B) Foreign Direct Investment (FDI)  \n(C) Portfolio Capital Reserve  \n(D) Sessional Investment  \n**Answer**: **(B)**  \n*Rationale*: Money spent by foreign MNCs to acquire assets or build factories in another country is called Foreign Direct Investment."},{"heading":"Q3. In 1995, Ford Motors entered the Indian market by establishing a joint venture with which Indian manufacturer?","content":"(A) Tata Motors  \n(B) Maruti Suzuki  \n(C) Mahindra & Mahindra  \n(D) Ashok Leyland  \n**Answer**: **(C)**  \n*Rationale*: Ford Motors partnered with Mahindra & Mahindra investing ₹1,700 crore near Chennai."},{"heading":"Q4. Cargill Foods, a giant American MNC, bought over which large Indian edible oil company?","content":"(A) Ruchi Soya  \n(B) Parakh Foods  \n(C) Fortune Oils  \n(D) Dhara Edibles  \n**Answer**: **(B)**  \n*Rationale*: Cargill Foods acquired Parakh Foods, gaining control of its four oil refineries and nationwide distribution network."},{"heading":"Q5. Packing goods into standardized steel containers for ship, rail, and truck transport is called:","content":"(A) Containerization  \n(B) Bulk Cargo Handling  \n(C) Palletization  \n(D) Freight Licensing  \n**Answer**: **(A)**  \n*Rationale*: Containerization allows goods to be moved intact across ships, trains, and trucks, reducing port costs and transit times."},{"heading":"Q6. What is the tax imposed on imported goods known as?","content":"(A) Excise Duty  \n(B) Import Duty / Tariff (Trade Barrier)  \n(C) Value Added Tax  \n(D) Service Tax  \n**Answer**: **(B)**  \n*Rationale*: Import duties or tariffs are taxes levied on foreign goods entering a country, acting as a trade barrier."},{"heading":"Q7. In which year did the Indian government introduce major liberalisation reforms under the New Economic Policy?","content":"(A) 1947  \n(B) 1971  \n(C) 1991  \n(D) 2005  \n**Answer**: **(C)**  \n*Rationale*: India adopted liberalisation, privatisation, and globalisation reforms under the New Economic Policy in 1991."},{"heading":"Q8. Removing restrictions or barriers set by the government on foreign trade and foreign investment is called:","content":"(A) Nationalisation  \n(B) Privatisation  \n(C) Liberalisation  \n(D) Disinvestment  \n**Answer**: **(C)**  \n*Rationale*: Liberalisation is the formal removal of trade barriers and regulatory controls on trade and investment."},{"heading":"Q9. Where is the headquarters of the World Trade Organization (WTO) situated?","content":"(A) New York, USA  \n(B) Geneva, Switzerland  \n(C) Paris, France  \n(D) London, UK  \n**Answer**: **(B)**  \n*Rationale*: The World Trade Organization (WTO) is headquartered in Geneva, Switzerland."},{"heading":"Q10. On which date was the World Trade Organization (WTO) established?","content":"(A) 11 April 1919  \n(B) 24 October 1945  \n(C) 1 January 1995  \n(D) 15 August 1947  \n**Answer**: **(C)**  \n*Rationale*: The WTO came into formal existence on 1 January 1995, replacing GATT."},{"heading":"Q11. Which one of the following is an Indian Multinational Corporation?","content":"(A) Cargill Foods  \n(B) Ford Motors  \n(C) Infosys  \n(D) Nike  \n**Answer**: **(C)**  \n*Rationale*: Infosys, Tata Motors, and Ranbaxy are leading Indian companies that expanded globally as MNCs."},{"heading":"Q12. What was the major negative impact of Chinese toy imports on the Indian market?","content":"(A) Toys became very expensive  \n(B) Indian toy makers faced heavy losses and unit closures  \n(C) Toy quality deteriorated completely  \n(D) Imports were banned by the government  \n**Answer**: **(B)**  \n*Rationale*: Cheap Chinese plastic toys captured 70–80% market share, forcing many small Indian toy units to shut down."},{"heading":"Q13. Hiring workers on a temporary, seasonal basis without social security benefits is termed as:","content":"(A) Permanent Employment  \n(B) Flexibilisation of Labour  \n(C) Public Sector Job Security  \n(D) Trade Unionism  \n**Answer**: **(B)**  \n*Rationale*: Flexibilisation of labour laws allows companies to hire temporary workers to cut labour costs."},{"heading":"Q14. What is the primary role of the World Trade Organization (WTO)?","content":"(A) To provide interest-free loans to poor countries  \n(B) To liberalise international trade and enforce trade rules  \n(C) To regulate international currency exchange rates  \n(D) To manage environmental conservation globally  \n**Answer**: **(B)**  \n*Rationale*: WTO''s main role is to facilitate free international trade and administer multilateral trade rules."},{"heading":"Q15. Which of the following measures can ensure ''Fair Globalisation'' in India?","content":"(A) Total removal of all labour welfare laws  \n(B) Strict enforcement of labour laws and support for small producers  \n(C) Banning all foreign investment completely  \n(D) Closing domestic markets to international technology  \n**Answer**: **(B)**  \n*Rationale*: Protecting worker rights under labour laws and assisting small producers ensures fair sharing of globalisation benefits.\n\n---"},{"heading":"7. Syllabus Coverage Checklist & Master Source Inventory","content":""},{"heading":"7.1 Topic-by-Topic Syllabus Coverage Checklist","content":"| Sub-Topic / Theme | Prescribed Status | Coverage Location in File | Verification Source |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Production Across Countries (MNCs)** | Prescribed (5 Marks) | Section 2.1 | BSER Syllabus Circular (`10_2027.pdf`, p. 38) |\n| **Interlinking Production Routes** | Prescribed (5 Marks) | Section 2.2 | NCERT Economics Ch. 4 / BSER Model Paper |\n| **Foreign Trade & Market Integration** | Prescribed (5 Marks) | Section 2.3 | NCERT Economics Ch. 4 / Chinese Toys Case Study |\n| **Meaning of Globalisation** | Prescribed (5 Marks) | Section 2.4 | BSER PYQ 2024 D&D S-124 Q25 |\n| **Enabling Factor: Technology (IT & Transport)**| Prescribed (5 Marks) | Section 2.5 | BSER PYQ 2022 S-08 Fill-in-the-blank |\n| **Enabling Factor: Liberalisation (1991 Policy)**| Prescribed (5 Marks) | Section 2.6 | BSER 2026–27 Official Model Paper Q23 |\n| **World Trade Organization (WTO)** | Prescribed (5 Marks) | Section 2.7 | BSER PYQ 2024 Main Paper S-08 Q14 |\n| **Impact of Globalisation in India** | Prescribed (5 Marks) | Section 2.8 | BSER 2026–27 Official Model Paper Q32 |\n| **Struggle for a Fair Globalisation** | Prescribed (5 Marks) | Section 2.9 | NCERT Economics Ch. 4 / Predicted Essay Q2 |\n| **15-Question MCQ Quiz** | Prescribed | Section 6 | Complete Chapter Concept Evaluation |\n\n---"},{"heading":"7.2 Master Source Reference Inventory","content":"1. **Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER / RBSE), Ajmer**: *Secondary Examination Syllabus 2026–27 Document (`10_2027.pdf`)*, Subject Code 08 (Social Science), Economics Unit Chapter 4 (*Globalisation and the Indian Economy*), p. 38/p. 40.\n2. **BSER Secondary Model Question Paper (2026–27 Examination)**: *Subject: Social Science (08)*, Published by BSER Ajmer (`Social-Science-10.pdf`), Q23, Q32.\n3. **BSER Secondary Main Examination Paper 2024 (S-08.pdf)**: *Social Science (08)*, BSER Ajmer, Q1(xv) [Tax on imports as trade barrier], Q14 [WTO functions].\n4. **BSER Secondary Supplementary (D&D) Examination Paper 2024 (S-124.pdf)**: *Social Science (08)*, BSER Ajmer, Q11 [Foreign trade definition], Q19 [Loss due to Globalisation], Q25 [Globalisation concept].\n5. **BSER Secondary Main Examination Paper 2023 (S-08.pdf)**: *Social Science (08)*, BSER Ajmer, Q1(xi) [Main aim of WTO].\n6. **BSER Secondary Main Examination Paper 2022 (S-08.pdf)**: *Social Science (08)*, BSER Ajmer, Q2(i) [MNC definition fill-in-blank], Q2(vi) [Technology stimulus], Q16 [MNC working mechanism].\n7. **NCERT Prescribed Core Textbook**: *Understanding Economic Development*, Class X, Chapter 4: *Globalisation and the Indian Economy*, Published by NCERT under Copyright / Adopted by BSER Ajmer.\n\n---"}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
