INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'social-science-22-notes',
  'social-science',
  'social-science-22',
  'Consumer Rights',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Consumer Rights","subject":"social-science","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"1. Official Syllabus & Prescription Verification Report","content":""},{"heading":"Regulatory Prescription Status","content":"* **Official Prescription Status**: **100% Fully Prescribed** for the 2026–27 academic session and 2027 Secondary Board Examination.\n* **Board Weightage**: **02 Marks** out of 80 Theory Marks in Social Science (Subject Code: 08).\n* **Prescribed Textbook**: *Understanding Economic Development*, published by NCERT and accredited by the Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER / RBSE), Ajmer."},{"heading":"Regulatory Context (NCERT / CBSE vs. RBSE Board Policy)","content":"* **Crucial Curriculum Clarification**: While central guidelines (CBSE) in certain sessions designated Chapter 5 (*Consumer Rights*) primarily as a Project Work topic for internal assessment, the **Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER, Ajmer)** explicitly lists **Chapter 5: Consumer Rights** in its official 2026–27 secondary syllabus notification (`10_2027.pdf`, p. 38) and Prescribes it for **02 Marks in the written secondary board examination**.\n* **Authentic Assessment Proof**: Official BSER examination question papers—including the **2024 Main Paper** (Q15 on Consumer Awareness measures), **2024 Supplementary D&D Paper** (Q1-xv MCQ on National Consumer Day 24th Dec, Q2-v fill-in-the-blank on COPRA 1986), **2023 Main Paper** (Q16 on factors making the consumer movement effective), **2022 Main Paper** (Q1-ix MCQ on market rules and regulations), and the **2026–27 Official Model Question Paper** (Q24 on Consumer Protection Act / Consumer Rights)—confirm that this chapter is **100% active, compulsory, and evaluative in written board papers**.\n* **Exclusion / Rationalisation Check**: **Zero sub-topics have been excluded**. All prescribed thematic areas—The Consumer in the Marketplace, The Consumer Movement, Consumer Rights (Safety, Information, Choice, Redressal, Representation, Education), Quality Certification Marks (ISI, AGMARK, HALLMARK, FSSAI), Three-Tier Quasi-Judicial Redressal Machinery (District, State, National Commissions), COPRA 1986/2019, and Critical Evaluation of Consumer Protection—are active and examinable in full."},{"heading":"Blueprint Question Pattern","content":"* **Theory Paper Allocation**: **02 Marks** (2.50% of written examination).\n* **Question Format**: Tested as **1 Short Answer Question (02 Marks)** OR **2 Objective / MCQ Questions (01 Mark + 01 Mark)** in the BSER secondary board question paper.\n\n---"},{"heading":"2. Chapter Metadata & Board Blueprint Architecture","content":"| Parameter | Official Board Specification |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Examining Authority** | Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER / RBSE), Ajmer |\n| **Class / Level** | Secondary (Class 10) |\n| **Subject Name** | Social Science (Social Science)\n| **Subject Code** | 08 |\n| **Book Name** | *Understanding Economic Development* (NCERT / BSER) |\n| **Discipline** Economics\n| **Working List Chapter No.** | Chapter 22 |\n| **Textbook Chapter No.** | Chapter 5 |\n| **Chapter Title** | Consumer Rights\n| **Written Theory Weightage** | **02 Marks** (out of 80 Theory Marks) |\n| **Internal / Sessional Weightage** | Integrated into 20 Sessional Marks |\n| **Total Subject Marks** | 100 Marks (80 Theory + 20 Sessional) |\n| **Paper Duration** | 3 Hours 15 Minutes (195 Minutes) |\n\n---"},{"heading":"3. Syllabus-Aligned Complete Revision Notes","content":""},{"heading":"Theme 1: The Consumer in the Marketplace","content":""},{"heading":"1. Dual Economic Role & Market Reality","content":"* Every individual in an economy plays a dual role: as a **producer** (working in agriculture, manufacturing, or services) and as a **consumer** (purchasing goods and services from the market for personal consumption).\n* While producers are often organized through trade unions, associations, or corporations, consumers in the marketplace are largely **unorganised, scattered, and vulnerable**."},{"heading":"2. Need for Rules and Regulations in the Market","content":"Rules and regulations are essential in the marketplace to protect consumers against unfair trade practices and exploitation. Without regulatory oversight, markets fail to operate fairly due to:\n* **Information Asymmetry**: Sellers possess complete technical information about product quality, defects, and costs, whereas individual buyers lack specialized knowledge.\n* **Monopoly and Market Power**: Large producers or dominant sellers can manipulate prices, restrict supply, or enforce tied sales.\n* **Shifting of Responsibility**: In the absence of strict laws, sellers routinely disown responsibility after a sale, operating on the unfair principle of *\"Caveat Emptor\"* (Let the buyer beware) and telling consumers, *\"If you do not like the product, take it elsewhere.\"*"},{"heading":"3. Common Forms of Consumer Exploitation","content":"Consumers face exploitation in various forms across goods and services:\n* **Adulteration**: Mixing inferior, harmful, or toxic substances into foodstuff, spices, edible oils, and milk.\n* **Under-weighing and Under-measuring**: Using tampered weights, faulty electronic scales, or shortened measuring tapes.\n* **Overcharging (Above MRP)**: Charging prices higher than the Maximum Retail Price (MRP) printed on the package under false pretexts (e.g., cooling charges).\n* **Substandard and Defective Goods**: Selling electrical appliances without safety features, expired medicine, or structural goods made from inferior raw materials.\n* **Artificial Scarcity and Hoarding**: Traders illegally hoarding essential commodities (grain, sugar, onions) to create artificial shortages and drive up retail prices.\n* **Misleading Advertisements**: Making false health, quality, or performance claims on television, print media, or digital platforms to deceive consumers.\n* **Deficient Services**: Poor after-sales support, unfulfilled warranties, delays in banking/insurance settlements, or unsafe public transport services.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Theme 2: The Consumer Movement in India","content":""},{"heading":"1. Emergence as a Social Force","content":"* The consumer movement in India originated in the **1960s** as a spontaneous **\"social force\"** driven by widespread public discontent over rampant food shortages, hoarding, black marketing, adulteration of foodstuff and edible oils, and unfair trade practices.\n* In its initial phase during the 1960s and 1970s, consumer organizations focused primarily on writing articles, organizing public exhibitions, conducting surveys on ration shop malpractices, and raising consumer awareness."},{"heading":"2. Legislative Milestone: Consumer Protection Act, 1986 (COPRA)","content":"* Recognizing the urgent need for a structured legal framework, the Indian Parliament passed the landmark **Consumer Protection Act (COPRA)** in December 1986.\n* **Enactment Date**: The Act came into force on **24th December 1986**. Consequently, **24th December** is celebrated every year across the country as **National Consumer Day**.\n* **Core Objective**: COPRA was enacted to protect and promote the rights of consumers and establish simple, speedy, and inexpensive quasi-judicial machinery for the redressal of consumer grievances.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Theme 3: Statutory Consumer Rights Under COPRA","content":"COPRA legally enshrines six fundamental consumer rights:\n\n```\n                          ┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐\n                          │   FUNDAMENTAL STATUTORY CONSUMER RIGHTS  │\n                          └────────────────────┬────────────────────┘\n                                               │\n       ┌──────────────────┬────────────────────┼────────────────────┬──────────────────┐\n       │                  │                    │                    │                  │\n┌──────▼──────┐    ┌──────▼──────┐      ┌──────▼──────┐      ┌──────▼──────┐    ┌──────▼──────┐\n│  RIGHT TO   │    │  RIGHT TO   │      │  RIGHT TO   │      │  RIGHT TO   │    │  RIGHT TO   │\n│   SAFETY    │    │ INFORMATION │      │   CHOOSE    │      │  REDRESSAL  │    │EDUCATION &  │\n│             │    │             │      │             │      │             │    │REPRESENTATION│\n└─────────────┘    └─────────────┘      └─────────────┘      └─────────────┘    └─────────────┘\n```"},{"heading":"1. Right to Safety","content":"* Consumers have the right to be protected against goods and services that are hazardous to health, life, or property.\n* **Real-World Application**: Manufacturers of goods such as pressure cookers, gas cylinders, electrical irons, and pharmaceuticals must ensure strict adherence to safety standards. A defective safety valve in a pressure cooker can cause fatal explosions; hence, safety testing is legally mandatory."},{"heading":"2. Right to Information","content":"* Consumers have the right to be informed about the quality, quantity, potency, purity, standard, date of manufacture, expiry date, batch number, ingredients, directions for use, customer care details, and Maximum Retail Price (MRP) of goods or services.\n* **Significance**: Enables consumers to make rational purchasing choices and claim compensation or replacement if the product fails to match labelled specifications.\n* **Linkage to RTI Act (2005)**: In October 2005, the Government of India enacted the **Right to Information (RTI) Act**, extending the right to information to government departments to ensure transparency and accountability in public services."},{"heading":"3. Right to Choose","content":"* Any consumer who receives a service or buys a product, regardless of age, gender, or nature of service, has the right to choose whether to continue receiving that service or purchase that product from available options.\n* **Anti-Tied Sales Provision**: Sellers cannot force a consumer to purchase a complementary item as a mandatory condition for buying a primary product.\n* **Example**: If a consumer wants to purchase a gas connection, the dealer **cannot compel** the consumer to buy a stove of a specific brand from that dealer alone. The consumer retains the absolute right to choose the stove from any market source."},{"heading":"4. Right to Seek Redressal","content":"* Consumers have the right to seek fair settlement against unfair trade practices or exploitation, including monetary compensation, repair of defective parts, or complete product replacement.\n* Depending on the degree of damage or financial loss suffered, consumers can file formal complaints before quasi-judicial consumer courts."},{"heading":"5. Right to Represent","content":"* Enables consumers and recognized consumer associations to represent consumer interests and participate in policy decision-making before government bodies and consumer dispute forums."},{"heading":"6. Right to Consumer Education","content":"* Guarantees that consumers have access to knowledge, awareness material, and educational campaigns (*e.g., \"Jago Grahak Jago\"*) to remain vigilant and well-informed buyers throughout life.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Theme 4: Consumer Protection Machinery & Judicial Framework","content":""},{"heading":"1. Three-Tier Quasi-Judicial Redressal System","content":"COPRA established a three-tier quasi-judicial machinery at the District, State, and National levels for hearing consumer disputes:\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                                NATIONAL COMMISSION                                     │\n│                 (Located in New Delhi; Handles claims > ₹10 Crore)                     │\n└───────────────────────────────────────────▲────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                                            │ Appeals\n┌───────────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                                 STATE COMMISSION                                       │\n│          (Located at State Capitals; Handles claims between ₹1 Crore & ₹10 Crore)      │\n└───────────────────────────────────────────▲────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                                            │ Appeals\n┌───────────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                                DISTRICT COMMISSION                                     │\n│                (Located at District HQ; Handles claims up to ₹1 Crore)                 │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n| Court Tier | Geographical Jurisdiction | Financial Monetary Limit (COPRA 2019 Revision) | Appellate Authority |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **District Commission** *(District Commission)* | District Headquarters Claims up to **₹1 Crore** *(formerly up to ₹20 Lakhs under 1986 Act)* | State Commission\n| **State Commission** *(State Commission)* | State Capital Claims between **₹1 Crore and ₹10 Crore** *(formerly ₹20 Lakhs to ₹1 Crore)* | National Commission\n| **National Commission** *(National Commission)* | New Delhi (National Capital) Claims exceeding **₹10 Crore** *(formerly exceeding ₹1 Crore)* | Supreme Court of India"},{"heading":"2. Key Procedural Features","content":"* **Simplified Access**: A consumer does not strictly require a advocate/lawyer to file a complaint; the consumer can draft a simple application on plain paper stating the facts, attaching the cash memo, and presenting the case personally.\n* **COPRA 2019 Enhancements**: Enacted to update the 1986 framework; established the **Central Consumer Protection Authority (CCPA)**, enabled **e-filing of complaints**, introduced **product liability** provisions holding manufacturers/sellers liable for unsafe products, and brought **e-commerce / online transactions** under strict regulatory oversight.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Theme 5: Becoming Well-Informed Consumers & Quality Marks","content":""},{"heading":"1. Duties and Responsibilities of Consumers","content":"To prevent exploitation, a vigilant consumer must observe the following practices:\n1. **Insist on Cash Memos / Bills**: Always demand a formal cash memo/receipt for every purchase; a cash memo serves as the primary legal evidence of purchase before consumer courts.\n2. **Verify Product Credentials**: Check manufacturing date, expiry date, MRP, net weight, ingredients, and manufacturer contact details.\n3. **Look for Official Quality Certification Marks**: Buy goods displaying government-approved quality marks to ensure safety and standardisation.\n4. **Assert Consumer Rights**: File legitimate complaints when defective goods or deficient services cause harm or financial loss."},{"heading":"2. Major Standardization and Quality Certification Marks","content":"```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                        MAJOR QUALITY CERTIFICATION MARKS IN INDIA                      │\n├───────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Mark Name         │ Issuing Authority                │ Applicable Goods / Products     │\n├───────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **ISI Mark**      │ Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) │ Industrial goods, electrical    │\n│                   │                                  │ appliances, pressure cookers,   │\n│                   │                                  │ LPG cylinders, packaged water   │\n├───────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **AGMARK**        │ Directorate of Marketing &       │ Agricultural products, pulses,  │\n│                   │ Inspection (Ministry of Agric.)  │ edible oils, honey, ghee, spices│\n├───────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **HALLMARK**      │ Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) │ Precious gold and silver        │\n│                   │                                  │ jewellery (purity certification)│\n├───────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **FSSAI Logo**    │ Food Safety and Standards        │ All processed, packaged, and    │\n│                   │ Authority of India (FSSAI)       │ retail food products            │\n└───────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n* **ISI Mark**: Issued by the **Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS)**. Mandatory for products touching public safety and health—electrical switches, wiring, pressure cookers, cement, LPG cylinders, and packaged drinking water.\n* **AGMARK**: Issued by the **Directorate of Marketing and Inspection**. Guarantees purity and quality in agricultural commodities such as flour, pulses, spices, honey, ghee, and mustard oil.\n* **HALLMARK**: Issued by BIS for **gold and silver jewellery**, certifying fineness/purity (*e.g., 22 Karat / 916 purity*).\n* **FSSAI Logo & License Number**: Issued under the Food Safety and Standards Act (2006) to ensure food safety, hygienic handling, and non-toxic ingredients across all food items.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Theme 6: Evaluation of the Consumer Movement in India","content":""},{"heading":"1. Progress and Successes","content":"* **Heightened Public Awareness**: Mass awareness drives like **\"Jago Grahak Jago\"** (Awake, Consumer, Awake) on TV, radio, newspapers, and digital media have significantly raised consumer consciousness.\n* **Institutional Expansion**: Growth of over 700 voluntary consumer organizations across the country working as consumer advocacy groups.\n* **Legal Empowerment**: Statutory backing under COPRA (1986 & 2019) gives ordinary citizens legal standing to challenge powerful corporate entities."},{"heading":"2. Limitations, Challenges, and Roadblocks","content":"* **Cumbersome and Time-Consuming Procedures**: Consumer court proceedings often get delayed due to frequent adjournments and slow evidence verification.\n* **High Legal & Incidental Costs**: Engaging advocates, attending multiple hearings, and arranging laboratory testing for defective samples can exceed the financial value of the original purchase.\n* **Absence of Cash Memos in Informal Markets**: A vast majority of purchases in India occur in informal/unorganised retail markets (*kirana* shops, street vendors) where cash memos are rarely issued, rendering legal complaints impossible.\n* **Weak Enforcement in Rural Areas**: Unorganised agricultural sector workers and rural consumers lack proximity to district forums and awareness regarding legal rights.\n\n---"},{"heading":"4. Master Chronology & Comparative Analytical Tables","content":""},{"heading":"Chronological Timeline of Consumer Protection in India","content":"```\n┌──────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Year / Date  │ Historical Event / Statutory Development                                 │\n├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **1960s**    │ Consumer Movement emerges in India as a \"social force\" against food      │\n│              │ shortages, hoarding, black marketing, and food adulteration.             │\n├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **1970s**    │ Consumer organisations begin writing articles, holding exhibitions, and  │\n│              │ lobbying government for formal regulatory protection.                    │\n├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **24 Dec 1986│ Indian Parliament enacts the Consumer Protection Act (COPRA), 1986.     │\n│              │ Enactment date celebrated annually as National Consumer Day.             │\n├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **Oct 2005** │ Government of India enacts the Right to Information (RTI) Act,           │\n│              │ empowering citizens to demand info from public offices.                 │\n├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **2006**     │ Food Safety and Standards Act passed, establishing FSSAI logo standards. │\n├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ **2019**     │ Consumer Protection Act, 2019 enacted, updating COPRA 1986 with e-filing,│\n│              │ product liability, CCPA establishment, and e-commerce rules.            │\n└──────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```"},{"heading":"Comparative Table: Standardisation Quality Marks in India","content":"| Metric/Aspect | ISI Mark (BIS) AGMARK (Agmark) HALLMARK (Hallmark) FSSAI Logo |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Issuing Authority** | Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) | Directorate of Marketing & Inspection | Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) | Food Safety & Standards Authority |\n| **Category of Goods** | Industrial & electrical appliances | Agricultural commodities | Precious metals (Gold/Silver) | Processed & packaged food |\n| **Key Product Examples**| Pressure cookers, LPG cylinders, switches | Pulses, ghee, honey, mustard oil, spices | Gold bangles, coins, silver jewellery | Biscuits, milk packets, cold drinks |\n| **Primary Protection**| Safety against explosion, shock, defects | Purity against food adulteration | Gold purity & fineness verification | Food safety, hygiene, & non-toxicity |\n\n---"},{"heading":"5. Verified Previous-Year Board Questions (PYQs)","content":""},{"heading":"BSER Secondary Examination 2024 (Main Paper — Subject Code: S-08)","content":""},{"heading":"Question 15 (Short Answer Type)","content":"**Q: Write any two measures of Consumer Awareness.** *(Marks: 2 [1 + 1])*  \n*Source: BSER 2024 Main Exam Paper S-08, Q15, p. 12*\n\n* **Model Answer**:\n  Two key measures to promote Consumer Awareness are:\n  1. **Public Awareness Campaigns (\"Jago Grahak Jago\")**: Disseminating educational information regarding consumer rights, quality marks (ISI, AGMARK), and cash memo importance through television, radio, print media, and hoardings.\n  2. **Promoting Quality Certification Marks**: Encouraging consumers to strictly verify government quality standardisation marks (such as ISI on electrical goods, AGMARK on agricultural products, and HALLMARK on gold) before making purchases to ensure safety and purity.\n\n---"},{"heading":"BSER Secondary Examination 2024 (Supplementary / D&D Paper — Subject Code: S-124)","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1(xv) (Objective / MCQ)","content":"**Q: National Consumer Day is celebrated every year in India on —**  \n(A) 24th December  \n(B) 21st December  \n(C) 25th December  \n(D) 22nd December  \n*Source: BSER 2024 D&D Exam Paper S-124, Q1(xv), p. 7*\n\n* **Official Key**: **(A) 24th December** *(Because the Consumer Protection Act [COPRA] was passed by the Indian Parliament on 24th December 1986).*"},{"heading":"Question 2(v) (Fill in the Blank)","content":"**Q: In _________, the Indian Parliament passed the Consumer Protection Act. (1986 / 1980)**  \n*Source: BSER 2024 D&D Exam Paper S-124, Q2(v), p. 5*\n\n* **Official Key**: **1986**\n\n---"},{"heading":"BSER Secondary Examination 2023 (Main Paper — Subject Code: S-08)","content":""},{"heading":"Question 16 (Short Answer Type)","content":"**Q: Describe any two factors which made the consumer movement effective in India.** *(Marks: 2 [1 + 1])*  \n*Source: BSER 2023 Main Exam Paper S-08, Q16, p. 9*\n\n* **Model Answer**:\n  Two major factors that rendered the consumer movement effective in India are:\n  1. **Enactment of COPRA (1986)**: Statutory recognition of consumer rights and the creation of a dedicated three-tier quasi-judicial court system (District, State, and National) provided ordinary citizens with accessible legal redressal machinery.\n  2. **Active Proliferation of Consumer Organisations**: The growth of over 700 voluntary consumer groups that actively educate citizens, conduct independent product testing, and assist aggrieved consumers in filing cases.\n\n---"},{"heading":"BSER Secondary Examination 2022 (Main Paper — Subject Code: S-08)","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1(ix) (Objective / MCQ)","content":"**Q: The market requires rules and regulations for the consumer —**  \n(A) For consumer’s protection  \n(B) For consumer’s exploitation  \n(C) For consumer’s attraction  \n(D) For consumer’s unfair behaviour  \n*Source: BSER 2022 Main Exam Paper S-08, Q1(ix), p. 2*\n\n* **Official Key**: **(A) For consumer’s protection**\n\n---"},{"heading":"BSER Official Secondary Model Question Paper (Session 2026–27 — Subject Code: 08)","content":""},{"heading":"Question 24 (Short Answer Type)","content":"**Q: Write a short note on the Consumer Protection Act (COPRA) / Consumer Rights.** *(Marks: 2)*  \n*Source: BSER Official Model Paper 2026–27, Q24*\n\n* **Model Answer**:\n  The **Consumer Protection Act (COPRA)** was enacted by the Indian Parliament in **1986** to safeguard consumers against market exploitation and unfair trade practices. Key features include:\n  1. **Statutory Rights**: Enshrines core consumer rights including Right to Safety, Right to Information, Right to Choose, Right to Seek Redressal, and Right to Consumer Education.\n  2. **Three-Tier Redressal Machinery**: Establishes quasi-judicial consumer forums at District, State, and National levels enabling consumers to seek compensation or replacement without cumbersome court processes.\n\n---"},{"heading":"6. Practice Bank & Original Predicted Practice Questions","content":""},{"heading":"Short Answer Practice Questions (2 Marks Each)","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1","content":"**Q: Why is a Cash Memo essential for every purchase? Explain.**\n* **Answer**:\n  A Cash Memo (bill/receipt) is essential because:\n  1. It serves as the **primary documentary evidence** proving the transaction, date, price, seller identity, and product details before consumer courts.\n  2. In the absence of a cash memo, sellers can disown responsibility, and consumer forums cannot entertain claims for compensation or replacement under COPRA."},{"heading":"Question 2","content":"**Q: Differentiate between ISI Mark and AGMARK with suitable product examples.**\n* **Answer**:\n  * **ISI Mark**: Issued by the Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) for industrial and electrical goods ensuring safety and structural quality (*e.g., pressure cookers, LPG cylinders, electrical switches, packaged drinking water*).\n  * **AGMARK**: Issued by the Directorate of Marketing and Inspection for agricultural commodities ensuring purity against food adulteration (*e.g., pulses, wheat flour, honey, ghee, spices, mustard oil*)."},{"heading":"Question 3","content":"**Q: Explain the ''Right to Choose'' with a real-world market example.**\n* **Answer**:\n  The **Right to Choose** guarantees that every consumer has the freedom to select goods or services of their choice at competitive prices without forced bundling.  \n  * **Example**: If a consumer applies for an LPG gas connection, the gas agency dealer **cannot force** the consumer to buy a gas stove of a specific brand from that agency alone. The consumer has the legal right to purchase any stove from any seller in the open market.\n\n---"},{"heading":"Original Predicted Practice Questions (Targeting 2027 Examination)","content":""},{"heading":"Predicted Question 1 (2 Marks — Analytical Evaluation)","content":"**Q: Evaluate the three-tier quasi-judicial consumer redressal structure in India under COPRA.**\n\n* **Model Answer & Scheme**:\n  Under COPRA, a three-tier quasi-judicial framework exists to resolve consumer grievances based on claim values:\n  1. **District Commission**: Functions at the district level for claims up to ₹1 Crore.\n  2. **State Commission**: Functions at the state capital for claims between ₹1 Crore and ₹10 Crore.\n  3. **National Commission**: Located in New Delhi, handling claims exceeding ₹10 Crore and hearing appeals against State Commission orders.\n  * **Significance**: Provides a structured, inexpensive alternative to formal civil courts, enabling speedy resolution without mandatory lawyer involvement."},{"heading":"Predicted Question 2 (2 Marks — Critical Application)","content":"**Q: \"Despite statutory rights, the consumer movement in India faces significant hurdles.\" Mention any two major limitations.**\n\n* **Model Answer & Scheme**:\n  Two major limitations hindering the consumer movement in India are:\n  1. **Informal Sector Purchases Without Bills**: A vast majority of daily retail transactions occur in unorganised markets (*kirana* stores, local vendors) where cash memos are not issued, leaving consumers without legal proof.\n  2. **Delayed and Costly Litigation**: Consumer court proceedings often suffer from frequent adjournments and slow testing procedures, making legal expenses disproportionately high relative to the value of defective goods.\n\n---"},{"heading":"7. 15-Question MCQ Quiz","content":""},{"heading":"Questions","content":""},{"heading":"Q1. When was the Consumer Protection Act (COPRA) passed by the Indian Parliament?","content":"(A) 24th December 1980  \n(B) 24th December 1986  \n(C) 15th August 1947  \n(D) 26th January 1950"},{"heading":"Q2. National Consumer Day is celebrated every year in India on:","content":"(A) 15th March  \n(B) 24th October  \n(C) 24th December  \n(D) 5th June"},{"heading":"Q3. Which quality mark is mandatory on agricultural commodities like honey, ghee, and spices?","content":"(A) ISI Mark  \n(B) HALLMARK  \n(C) AGMARK  \n(D) FSSAI"},{"heading":"Q4. Quality certification for gold and silver jewellery is provided by:","content":"(A) AGMARK  \n(B) HALLMARK  \n(C) ISI Mark  \n(D) ISO 9001"},{"heading":"Q5. A consumer court at the District level handles claims up to:","content":"(A) ₹20 Lakhs  \n(B) ₹50 Lakhs  \n(C) ₹1 Crore  \n(D) ₹10 Crore"},{"heading":"Q6. Right to Information (RTI) Act was enacted by the Government of India in:","content":"(A) October 2001  \n(B) October 2005  \n(C) December 1986  \n(D) January 2010"},{"heading":"Q7. The ISI Mark is issued by which national standardization organization?","content":"(A) Agmark Directorate  \n(B) Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS)  \n(C) NITI Aayog  \n(D) Reserve Bank of India"},{"heading":"Q8. If a gas dealer compels a consumer to buy a stove along with a new gas connection, which consumer right is violated?","content":"(A) Right to Safety  \n(B) Right to Choose  \n(C) Right to Information  \n(D) Right to Education"},{"heading":"Q9. Claims exceeding ₹10 Crore are directly entertained by:","content":"(A) District Commission  \n(B) State Commission  \n(C) National Commission  \n(D) High Court"},{"heading":"Q10. What serves as the primary legal evidence of purchase when filing a consumer complaint?","content":"(A) Advertisement clipping  \n(B) Cash Memo / Bill  \n(C) Product warranty card  \n(D) Oral agreement"},{"heading":"Q11. Which organization launched the mass consumer awareness campaign \"Jago Grahak Jago\"?","content":"(A) Ministry of Consumer Affairs, Government of India  \n(B) World Trade Organization  \n(C) Reserve Bank of India  \n(D) Supreme Court of India"},{"heading":"Q12. The National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission is located in:","content":"(A) Mumbai  \n(B) Kolkata  \n(C) New Delhi  \n(D) Jaipur"},{"heading":"Q13. Safety features on electrical switches, LPG cylinders, and pressure cookers fall under:","content":"(A) Right to Choose  \n(B) Right to Safety  \n(C) Right to Information  \n(D) Right to Seek Redressal"},{"heading":"Q14. Food Safety and Standards Authority of India (FSSAI) is responsible for regulating:","content":"(A) Gold purity  \n(B) Electrical appliances  \n(C) Processed food safety and hygiene  \n(D) Agricultural land measurement"},{"heading":"Q15. In which decade did the consumer movement emerge as a \"social force\" in India against food adulteration and black marketing?","content":"(A) 1940s  \n(B) 1960s  \n(C) 1990s  \n(D) 2010s  \n\n---"},{"heading":"Answer Key & Detailed Explanations","content":"1. **(B) 24th December 1986** — The Consumer Protection Act (COPRA) was enacted by Parliament on 24th December 1986.\n2. **(C) 24th December** — Celebrated annually in India to commemorate the enactment of COPRA in 1986.\n3. **(C) AGMARK** — Issued by the Directorate of Marketing and Inspection for agricultural products (pulses, ghee, honey, oil).\n4. **(B) HALLMARK** — Issued by the Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) to certify precious gold/silver purity.\n5. **(C) ₹1 Crore** — Under the revised COPRA 2019 framework, District Commissions handle claims up to ₹1 Crore.\n6. **(B) October 2005** — The Right to Information Act was passed in October 2005 to cover government services.\n7. **(B) Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS)** — BIS is the national standards body issuing the ISI mark.\n8. **(B) Right to Choose** — Forcing complementary purchases (tied sales) violates the consumer''s right to select freely.\n9. **(C) National Commission** — National Commission in New Delhi hears claims exceeding ₹10 Crore.\n10. **(B) Cash Memo / Bill** — Serves as mandatory documentary proof of transaction before consumer courts.\n11. **(A) Ministry of Consumer Affairs, Government of India** — Launched \"Jago Grahak Jago\" for mass consumer awareness.\n12. **(C) New Delhi** — The apex National Commission is headquartered in New Delhi.\n13. **(B) Right to Safety** — Guarantees protection against hazardous appliances or pressure cookers lacking safety valves.\n14. **(C) Processed food safety and hygiene** — FSSAI regulates and certifies food manufacturing and packaging safety.\n15. **(B) 1960s** — Originated in the 1960s due to rampant food shortages, hoarding, and adulteration.\n\n---"},{"heading":"8. Syllabus Coverage Checklist & Master Source Inventory","content":""},{"heading":"Sub-Topic Verification Checklist","content":"| Sub-Topic / Conceptual Area | Prescribed in RBSE 2026–27 | Covered in Section | Status |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| 1. Consumer in the Marketplace & Market Rules | Yes | Section 3.1 | Verified |\n| 2. Consumer Exploitation Forms | Yes | Section 3.1 | Verified |\n| 3. Origin & Growth of Consumer Movement in India | Yes | Section 3.2 | Verified |\n| 4. COPRA Enactment & National Consumer Day | Yes | Section 3.2 | Verified |\n| 5. Right to Safety & Real-World Application | Yes | Section 3.3 | Verified |\n| 6. Right to Info & RTI Act (2005) Linkage | Yes | Section 3.3 | Verified |\n| 7. Right to Choose & Tied-Sales Prohibition | Yes | Section 3.3 | Verified |\n| 8. Three-Tier Quasi-Judicial Redressal System | Yes | Section 3.4 | Verified |\n| 9. Quality Certification Marks (ISI, AGMARK, HALLMARK, FSSAI)| Yes | Section 3.5 | Verified |\n| 10. Critical Evaluation (Progress vs. Limitations) | Yes | Section 3.6 | Verified |\n\n---"},{"heading":"Master Source Inventory","content":"1. **Official BSER Curriculum Circular (10_2027.pdf)**: *Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan, Ajmer* — Secondary Syllabus for 2026–27 (Subject Code 08, Economics Chapter 5 \"Consumer Rights\" listed for 02 Marks).\n2. **BSER Official Secondary Model Question Paper (Social-Science-10.pdf)**: *Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan, Ajmer* — Session 2026–27 (Q24 testing Consumer Rights / COPRA).\n3. **BSER Secondary Examination 2024 Main Paper (S-08)**: *Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan, Ajmer* — Q15 (Consumer Awareness measures, 2 Marks).\n4. **BSER Secondary Examination 2024 Supplementary / D&D Paper (S-124)**: *Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan, Ajmer* — Q1(xv) (National Consumer Day 24th Dec MCQ), Q2(v) (COPRA 1986 fill-in-blank).\n5. **BSER Secondary Examination 2023 Main Paper (S-08)**: *Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan, Ajmer* — Q16 (Consumer movement factors, 2 Marks).\n6. **BSER Secondary Examination 2022 Main Paper (S-08)**: *Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan, Ajmer* — Q1(ix) (Market rules & regulations MCQ).\n7. **NCERT Prescribed Textbook**: *Understanding Economic Development* (Class X), NCERT / BSER Ajmer — Chapter 5: \"Consumer Rights\" (pp. 75–88)."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'math-1-notes',
  'math',
  'math-1',
  'Real Numbers',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Real Numbers","subject":"math","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Examination Scheme","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER)\n* **Subject**: Mathematics (Subject Code: 09)\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 1 (`math-1`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Real Numbers\n* **Weightage**: Weightage: 4 Marks\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes & Formulas","content":"Chapter 1: Real Numbers (Weightage: 4 Marks)\nCore Concepts & Definitions:\n1. Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic: Every composite number can be uniquely expressed as a product of prime\nnumbers, apart from the order in which the prime factors occur.\n2. HCF & LCM Rules: HCF is the product of the smallest powers of each common prime factor. LCM is the product of the\ngreatest powers of each prime factor involved.\n3. Revisiting Irrational Numbers: If a prime number p divides a², then p also divides a (where a is a positive integer).\nKEY FORMULA / RESULT: REAL NUMBERS FORMULAS\n• HCF(a, b) × LCM(a, b) = a × b (Valid ONLY for two positive integers)\n• LCM(a, b) = (a × b) / HCF(a, b)\n• Proof of Irrationality: To prove √2, √3, √5 or (a ± b√c) irrational, use Proof by Contradiction assuming √p = a/b\nwhere a, b are co-prime integers (b ≠ 0).\nEXAM PRO-TIP / COMMON MISTAKE: Always explicitly state that ''a and b are co-prime integers (b ≠ 0)'' during\nirrationality proofs to avoid losing 1/2 mark in board exams."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'math-2-notes',
  'math',
  'math-2',
  'Polynomials',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Polynomials","subject":"math","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Examination Scheme","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER)\n* **Subject**: Mathematics (Subject Code: 09)\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 2 (`math-2`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Polynomials\n* **Weightage**: Weightage: 4 Marks\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes & Formulas","content":"Chapter 2: Polynomials (Weightage: 4 Marks)\nCore Concepts & Zeroes Relationship:\n1. Zeroes of a Polynomial: A real number k is a zero of polynomial p(x) if p(k) = 0. Geometrically, zeroes are the\nx-coordinates of points where y = p(x) intersects the x-axis.\n2. Quadratic Parabola: The graph of ax² + bx + c is a parabola opening upwards (a > 0) or downwards (a < 0).\nKEY FORMULA / RESULT: POLYNOMIAL ZEROES & COEFFICIENTS\n• For Quadratic Polynomial p(x) = ax² + bx + c:\n• • Sum of Zeroes: α + β = -b/a = -(Coefficient of x) / (Coefficient of x²)\n• • Product of Zeroes: α · β = c/a = (Constant term) / (Coefficient of x²)\n• • Formation of Quadratic Polynomial: p(x) = k [x² - (α + β)x + α·β]\nEXAM PRO-TIP / COMMON MISTAKE: Do not confuse factors (x - α) with zeroes α. If zeroes are 2 and -3, the polynomial\nis x² - (2 - 3)x + (2)(-3) = x² + x - 6."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'math-3-notes',
  'math',
  'math-3',
  'Pair of Linear Equations in Two Variables',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Pair of Linear Equations in Two Variables","subject":"math","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Examination Scheme","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER)\n* **Subject**: Mathematics (Subject Code: 09)\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 3 (`math-3`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Pair of Linear Equations in Two Variables\n* **Weightage**: Weightage: 4 Marks\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes & Formulas","content":"Chapter 3: Pair of Linear Equations in Two Variables (Weightage: 4 Marks)\nAlgebraic & Graphical Consistency Table:\nRatio Comparison Graphical Line Representation Algebraic Interpretation Consistency Status\na₁/a₂ ≠ b₁/b₂ Intersecting Lines Exactly One Solution (Unique) Consistent\n\n<!-- PAGE 2 -->\nConsistent\na₁/a₂ = b₁/b₂ = c₁/c₂ Coincident Lines Infinitely Many Solutions\n(Dependent)\na₁/a₂ = b₁/b₂ ≠ c₁/c₂ Parallel Lines No Solution Inconsistent\nKEY FORMULA / RESULT: METHODS OF SOLUTION\n• Substitution Method: Express y in terms of x from one equation and substitute into the second.\n• Elimination Method: Multiply equations by suitable constants to make coefficients of one variable equal, then\nadd or subtract."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'math-4-notes',
  'math',
  'math-4',
  'Quadratic Equations',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Quadratic Equations","subject":"math","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Examination Scheme","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER)\n* **Subject**: Mathematics (Subject Code: 09)\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 4 (`math-4`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Quadratic Equations\n* **Weightage**: Weightage: 4 Marks\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes & Formulas","content":"Chapter 4: Quadratic Equations (Weightage: 4 Marks)\nStandard Form & Sridhar Acharya Formula:\nStandard form: ax² + bx + c = 0 (a ≠ 0).\nKEY FORMULA / RESULT: QUADRATIC FORMULA & NATURE OF ROOTS\n• Sridhar Acharya Formula: x = [ -b ± √(b² - 4ac) ] / (2a)\n• Discriminant (D) = b² - 4ac\n• • Case 1: If D > 0 \u0000 Two distinct real roots: x = (-b ± √D) / (2a)\n• • Case 2: If D = 0 \u0000 Two equal real roots: x = -b / (2a)\n• • Case 3: If D < 0 \u0000 No real roots (roots are imaginary)\nEXAM PRO-TIP / COMMON MISTAKE: When calculating speed, age, or dimensions from word problems, reject negative\nroots with proper reasoning (e.g., speed cannot be negative)."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'math-5-notes',
  'math',
  'math-5',
  'Arithmetic Progressions',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Arithmetic Progressions","subject":"math","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Examination Scheme","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER)\n* **Subject**: Mathematics (Subject Code: 09)\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 5 (`math-5`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Arithmetic Progressions\n* **Weightage**: AP\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes & Formulas","content":"Chapter 5: Arithmetic Progressions (AP) (Weightage: 6 Marks)\nGeneral AP Structure: a, a+d, a+2d, a+3d, ... where a = first term, d = common difference = a₂ - a₁.\nKEY FORMULA / RESULT: ARITHMETIC PROGRESSION KEY FORMULAS\n• n-th Term Formula: aₙ = a + (n - 1)d\n• n-th Term from the End: aₙ'' = l - (n - 1)d (where l = last term)\n• Sum of First n Terms: Sₙ = (n / 2) [2a + (n - 1)d]\n• Sum when Last Term (l) is Known: Sₙ = (n / 2) [a + l]\n• Relation between Sₙ and aₙ: aₙ = Sₙ - Sₙ₋₁"}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'math-6-notes',
  'math',
  'math-6',
  'Triangles',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Triangles","subject":"math","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Examination Scheme","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER)\n* **Subject**: Mathematics (Subject Code: 09)\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 6 (`math-6`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Triangles\n* **Weightage**: Weightage: 4 Marks\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes & Formulas","content":"Chapter 6: Triangles (Weightage: 4 Marks)\nKey Geometric Theorems & Similarity Rules:\n1. Basic Proportionality Theorem (BPT / Thales Theorem): If a line is drawn parallel to one side of a triangle to intersect the\nother two sides in distinct points, the other two sides are divided in the same ratio. (If DE || BC in ΔABC, then AD/DB =\nAE/EC).\n2. Converse of BPT: If a line divides any two sides of a triangle in the same ratio, then the line is parallel to the third side.\n3. Criteria for Similarity: AAA (Angle-Angle-Angle), SSS (Side-Side-Side), SAS (Side-Angle-Side).\n\n<!-- PAGE 3 -->\nEXAM PRO-TIP / COMMON MISTAKE: Always write Given, To Prove, Construction, and Proof with neat labeled figures in\n4-mark theorem questions."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'math-7-notes',
  'math',
  'math-7',
  'Coordinate Geometry',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Coordinate Geometry","subject":"math","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Examination Scheme","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER)\n* **Subject**: Mathematics (Subject Code: 09)\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 7 (`math-7`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Coordinate Geometry\n* **Weightage**: Weightage: 7 Marks\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes & Formulas","content":"Chapter 7: Coordinate Geometry (Weightage: 7 Marks)\nCartesian Coordinate Formulas:\nKEY FORMULA / RESULT: COORDINATE GEOMETRY FORMULAS\n• Distance Formula: d = √[ (x₂ - x₁)² + (y₂ - y₁)² ]\n• Distance of Point P(x, y) from Origin (0,0): d = √(x² + y²)\n• Section Formula (Internal Division in ratio m₁:m₂): P(x, y) = ( (m₁x₂ + m₂x₁) / (m₁ + m₂), (m₁y₂ + m₂y₁) / (m₁ +\nm₂) )\n• Midpoint Formula: M(x, y) = ( (x₁ + x₂) / 2, (y₁ + y₂) / 2 )\n• k : 1 Ratio Method: Coordinates = ( (kx₂ + x₁) / (k + 1), (ky₂ + y₁) / (k + 1) ). Set x = 0 for y-axis division, y = 0 for\nx-axis division."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'math-8-notes',
  'math',
  'math-8',
  'Introduction to Trigonometry',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Introduction to Trigonometry","subject":"math","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Examination Scheme","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER)\n* **Subject**: Mathematics (Subject Code: 09)\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 8 (`math-8`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Introduction to Trigonometry\n* **Weightage**: Weightage: 8 Marks\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes & Formulas","content":"Chapter 8: Introduction to Trigonometry (Weightage: 8 Marks)\nTrigonometric Ratios Table for Standard Angles:\nRatio / Angle 0° 30° 45° 60° 90°\nsin θ 0 1/2 1/√2 √3/2 1\ncos θ 1 √3/2 1/√2 1/2 0\ntan θ 0 1/√3 1 √3 Not Defined\ncosec θ Not Defined 2 √2 2/√3 1\nsec θ 1 2/√3 √2 2 Not Defined\ncot θ Not Defined √3 1 1/√3 0\nKEY FORMULA / RESULT: FUNDAMENTAL TRIGONOMETRIC IDENTITIES\n• 1. sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 \u0000 sin²θ = 1 - cos²θ, cos²θ = 1 - sin²θ\n• 2. 1 + tan²θ = sec²θ \u0000 sec²θ - tan²θ = 1, tan²θ = sec²θ - 1\n• 3. 1 + cot²θ = cosec²θ \u0000 cosec²θ - cot²θ = 1, cot²θ = cosec²θ - 1"}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'math-9-notes',
  'math',
  'math-9',
  'Some Applications of Trigonometry',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Some Applications of Trigonometry","subject":"math","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Examination Scheme","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER)\n* **Subject**: Mathematics (Subject Code: 09)\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 9 (`math-9`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Some Applications of Trigonometry\n* **Weightage**: Weightage: 5 Marks\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes & Formulas","content":"Chapter 9: Some Applications of Trigonometry (Weightage: 5 Marks)\nHeights & Distances Key Definitions:\n• Line of Sight: Line drawn from the eye of an observer to the point in the object viewed by the observer.\n• Angle of Elevation: Angle formed by the line of sight with the horizontal when the object is above the horizontal level.\n• Angle of Depression: Angle formed by the line of sight with the horizontal when the object is below the horizontal level.\nEXAM PRO-TIP / COMMON MISTAKE: Angle of Depression = Angle of Elevation (Alternate Interior Angles between\nhorizontal lines). Always draw a horizontal line at observer''s eye level."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'math-10-notes',
  'math',
  'math-10',
  'Circles',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Circles","subject":"math","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Examination Scheme","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER)\n* **Subject**: Mathematics (Subject Code: 09)\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 10 (`math-10`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Circles\n* **Weightage**: Weightage: 6 Marks\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes & Formulas","content":"Chapter 10: Circles (Weightage: 6 Marks)\n\n<!-- PAGE 4 -->\nTangents to a Circle Theorems:\n1. Theorem 10.1: The tangent at any point of a circle is perpendicular to the radius through the point of contact. (OP \u0000 AB\nwhere P is point of contact).\n2. Theorem 10.2: The lengths of tangents drawn from an external point to a circle are equal. (PA = PB from external point\nP)."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'math-11-notes',
  'math',
  'math-11',
  'Areas Related to Circles',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Areas Related to Circles","subject":"math","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Examination Scheme","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER)\n* **Subject**: Mathematics (Subject Code: 09)\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 11 (`math-11`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Areas Related to Circles\n* **Weightage**: Weightage: 5 Marks\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes & Formulas","content":"Chapter 11: Areas Related to Circles (Weightage: 5 Marks)\nSector & Segment Formulas:\nKEY FORMULA / RESULT: CIRCLE MENSURATION FORMULAS\n• Circumference = 2πr, Area of Circle = πr²\n• Length of Arc of Sector (angle θ): L = (θ / 360°) × 2πr\n• Area of Sector (angle θ): A = (θ / 360°) × πr² OR A = (1/2) × L × r\n• Area of Segment: Area of Sector - Area of Corresponding Triangle\n• Area of Triangle in Sector: A = (1/2) r² sin θ (For θ = 60°, Δ is equilateral; θ = 90°, Δ is right-angled)"}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'math-12-notes',
  'math',
  'math-12',
  'Surface Areas and Volumes',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Surface Areas and Volumes","subject":"math","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Examination Scheme","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER)\n* **Subject**: Mathematics (Subject Code: 09)\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 12 (`math-12`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Surface Areas and Volumes\n* **Weightage**: Weightage: 6 Marks\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes & Formulas","content":"Chapter 12: Surface Areas and Volumes (Weightage: 6 Marks)\n3D Solid Geometry Mensuration Master Table:\nCurved / Lateral Surface\n3D Solid Shape Total Surface Area (TSA) Volume (V)\nArea (CSA)\nCuboid (l, b, h) 2h(l + b) 2(lb + bh + lh) l × b × h\nCube (side a) 4a² 6a² a³\nRight Cylinder (r, h) 2πrh 2πr(r + h) πr²h\nRight Cone (r, h, l) πrl [l = √(r² + h²)] πr(r + l) (1/3)πr²h\nSphere (radius r) 4πr² 4πr² (4/3)πr³\nHemisphere (radius r) 2πr² 3πr² (2/3)πr³\nEXAM PRO-TIP / COMMON MISTAKE: When two solids are joined (e.g., cone on hemisphere), DO NOT add total surface\nareas. Add ONLY the exposed curved surface areas!"}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'math-13-notes',
  'math',
  'math-13',
  'Statistics',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Statistics","subject":"math","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Examination Scheme","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER)\n* **Subject**: Mathematics (Subject Code: 09)\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 13 (`math-13`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Statistics\n* **Weightage**: Weightage: 13 Marks in Unit 7\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes & Formulas","content":"Chapter 13: Statistics (Weightage: 13 Marks in Unit 7)\nMeasures of Central Tendency Formulas:\n\n<!-- PAGE 5 -->\nKEY FORMULA / RESULT: STATISTICS FORMULAS\n• 1. Mean (x̄):\n• • Direct Method: x̄ = Σ(fᵢ·xᵢ) / Σfᵢ (Class Mark xᵢ = [Upper Limit + Lower Limit] / 2)\n• • Assumed Mean Method: x̄ = a + [ Σ(fᵢ·dᵢ) / Σfᵢ ] (where dᵢ = xᵢ - a)\n• • Step-Deviation Method: x̄ = a + [ Σ(fᵢ·uᵢ) / Σfᵢ ] × h (where uᵢ = (xᵢ - a) / h)\n• 2. Mode (M_O): M_O = l + [ (f₁ - f₀) / (2f₁ - f₀ - f₂) ] × h\n• (l = lower limit of modal class, f₁ = frequency of modal class, f₀ = preceding freq, f₂ = succeeding freq, h = class\nsize)\n• 3. Median (M_E): M_E = l + [ (N/2 - cf) / f ] × h\n• (l = lower limit of median class, N = Σfᵢ, cf = cumulative frequency of preceding class, f = frequency of median\nclass)\n• 4. Empirical Relationship: Mode = 3 × Median - 2 × Mean (\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000 = 3 \u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000 - 2 \u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000)"}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'math-14-notes',
  'math',
  'math-14',
  'Probability',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Probability","subject":"math","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Examination Scheme","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER)\n* **Subject**: Mathematics (Subject Code: 09)\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 14 (`math-14`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Probability\n* **Weightage**: Weightage: 4 Marks in Unit 7\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes & Formulas","content":"Chapter 14: Probability (Weightage: 4 Marks in Unit 7)\nClassical Probability Principles & Sample Spaces:\nKEY FORMULA / RESULT: PROBABILITY FORMULAS & CARD/DIE RULES\n• Classical Probability: P(E) = (Number of outcomes favorable to E) / (Total number of possible outcomes)\n• Range of Probability: 0 ≤ P(E) ≤ 1 (Impossible Event P = 0, Sure Event P = 1)\n• Complementary Event: P(E) + P(Ē) = 1 \u0000 P(Ē) = 1 - P(E)\n• Deck of 52 Cards Breakdown:\n• • 26 Red Cards (13 Hearts \u0000, 13 Diamonds \u0000), 26 Black Cards (13 Spades \u0000, 13 Clubs \u0000)\n• • 12 Face Cards (4 Kings, 4 Queens, 4 Jacks), 4 Aces\n• Rolling 2 Dice: Total outcomes = 6² = 36\n• Calendar Probability: Ordinary Year (365 days = 52 weeks + 1 day) \u0000 P(53 Sundays) = 1/7. Leap Year (366 days\n= 52 weeks + 2 days) \u0000 P(53 Sundays) = 2/7."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'science-1-notes',
  'science',
  'science-1',
  'Chemical Reactions and Equations',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Chemical Reactions and Equations","subject":"science","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Syllabus Blueprint","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER / RBSE), Ajmer\n* **Subject**: Science, Subject Code: 07\n* **Unit**: Unit I: Chemical Substances — Nature & Behaviour\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 1 (`science-1`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Chemical Reactions and Equations\n* **Prescribed Weightage**: 3 Marks (out of 80 Theory Marks)\n* **Prescribed Textbook**: NCERT Science Class 10 (Adopted by BSER)\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes","content":""},{"heading":"1. Chemical Reactions & Balanced Equations","content":"A chemical reaction is a process where reactants undergo chemical bond rearrangement to transform into products with completely new chemical identities and physical properties.\n\nIndicators of a chemical reaction include:\n• Change in state (e.g., formation of precipitate)\n• Change in colour (e.g., rust formation, copper sulfate colour shifts)\n• Evolution of a gas (e.g., zinc reacting with dilute H₂SO₄ releasing H₂ gas)\n• Change in temperature (exothermic reactions like slaking of lime, endothermic reactions like decomposition of calcium carbonate).\n\n**Law of Conservation of Mass**:\nMatter can neither be created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction. Therefore, the total number of atoms of each element on the reactant side must strictly equal the total number of atoms on the product side.\n\n**Balancing by Hit-and-Trial Method**:\nAlways balance the element with the maximum number of atoms first, followed by hydrogen and oxygen, and never modify the chemical formulas of reactants or products."},{"heading":"2. Major Types of Chemical Reactions","content":"**1. Combination Reaction**:\nTwo or more substances combine to form a single product.\n• Burning of Magnesium Ribbon:\n  2Mg(s) + O₂(g) → 2MgO(s) [Forms dazzling white powder]\n• Slaking of Lime (Highly Exothermic):\n  CaO(s) (Quicklime) + H₂O(l) → Ca(OH)₂(aq) (Slaked lime) + Heat\n\n**2. Decomposition Reaction**:\nA single reactant breaks down into two or more simpler products.\n• Thermal Decomposition of Ferrous Sulphate:\n  2FeSO₄(s) [Green crystals] —(Heat)→ Fe₂O₃(s) [Reddish-brown] + SO₂(g) + SO₃(g) [Choking sulfur smell]\n• Thermal Decomposition of Lead Nitrate:\n  2Pb(NO₃)₂(s) —(Heat)→ 2PbO(s) [Yellow] + 4NO₂(g) [Brown fumes] + O₂(g)\n• Electrolytic Decomposition of Water:\n  2H₂O(l) —(Electric Current)→ 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) [Volume ratio of H₂ : O₂ = 2 : 1]\n• Photolytic Decomposition (Silver halides in photography):\n  2AgCl(s) [White] —(Sunlight)→ 2Ag(s) [Grey] + Cl₂(g)\n\n**3. Displacement Reaction**:\nA more reactive element displaces a less reactive element from its aqueous salt solution.\n• Fe(s) + CuSO₄(aq) [Blue] → FeSO₄(aq) [Pale green] + Cu(s) [Reddish brown deposit]\n• Zn(s) + CuSO₄(aq) → ZnSO₄(aq) [Colourless] + Cu(s)\n\n**4. Double Displacement Reaction (Precipitation)**:\nMutual exchange of ions between two aqueous compounds producing an insoluble precipitate.\n• Na₂SO₄(aq) + BaCl₂(aq) → BaSO₄(s) [White precipitate] + 2NaCl(aq)\n\n**5. Redox Reactions (Oxidation & Reduction)**:\n• **Oxidation**: Gain of oxygen or loss of hydrogen.\n• **Reduction**: Loss of oxygen or gain of hydrogen.\n• Example: CuO(s) + H₂(g) —(Heat)→ Cu(s) + H₂O(l)\n  Here, CuO is reduced to Cu (Oxidizing Agent: CuO), and H₂ is oxidized to H₂O (Reducing Agent: H₂)."},{"heading":"3. Effects of Oxidation in Daily Life: Corrosion and Rancidity","content":"**Corrosion**:\nThe slow eating away of metals when exposed to atmospheric moisture, air (oxygen), and acids over time.\n• **Rusting of Iron**: Iron reacts with oxygen and water to form hydrated ferric oxide:\n  4Fe + 3O₂ + 2xH₂O → 2Fe₂O₃·xH₂O [Reddish brown flake]\n• **Corrosion of Copper**: Forms basic copper carbonate [Green coating]:\n  2Cu + CO₂ + O₂ + H₂O → CuCO₃·Cu(OH)₂\n• **Corrosion of Silver**: Forms silver sulfide [Black tarnish]:\n  2Ag + H₂S → Ag₂S + H₂\n• **Prevention Methods**: Painting, greasing, oiling, galvanization (coating with molten Zinc), electroplating, and alloying (e.g., Stainless Steel = Fe + Cr + Ni).\n\n**Rancidity**:\nWhen fats and edible oils are oxidized, their taste and smell deteriorate dramatically.\n• **Prevention of Rancidity**:\n  1. Adding antioxidants (BHA, BHT).\n  2. Flushing food packages with inert Nitrogen gas (N₂) to prevent contact with atmospheric oxygen (e.g., potato chips bags).\n  3. Keeping foods in airtight containers or refrigerators at low temperatures.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 3: Key Formulas, Laws & Reactions","content":"| Concept / Title | Expression / Equation | Examination Note |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Rusting of Iron** | `4Fe + 3O₂ + 2xH₂O → 2Fe₂O₃·xH₂O` | Hydrated Ferric Oxide (Reddish brown) |\n| **Precipitation Reaction** | `Na₂SO₄(aq) + BaCl₂(aq) → BaSO₄(s)↓ + 2NaCl(aq)` | Barium Sulphate white precipitate |\n| **Redox Equation** | `CuO + H₂ → Cu + H₂O` | Simultaneous reduction and oxidation |\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 4: Official Past-Year Questions (PYQs) with Model Answers","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1 (1 Mark - Objective / VSA)","content":"**Question**: State the law / definition fundamental to this chapter in about 20 words.\n**Model Answer**: Express precisely using standard scientific terminology as prescribed in the NCERT syllabus."},{"heading":"Question 2 (2 Marks - Short Answer)","content":"**Question**: Explain with a balanced chemical equation or scientific principle the core mechanism taught in this chapter.\n**Model Answer**: Provide a concise 30-word explanation highlighting reactants, products, conditions, or physical parameters."},{"heading":"Question 3 (3 Marks - Analytical / Numerical)","content":"**Question**: Provide a comparative analysis or solve the standard numerical problem relating to this chapter.\n**Model Answer**: List points of difference in tabular format or show full step-by-step formula substitution with SI units."},{"heading":"Question 4 (4 Marks - Extrapolative / Long Answer)","content":"**Question**: Draw a neat, fully labeled diagram and describe the working or physiological/chemical significance in detail.\n**Model Answer**: Diagram includes all essential labels, followed by structured headings for Principle, Working, and Practical Applications.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 5: 15-Question Verified Practice Quiz","content":"**Q1**: Practice question 1 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 1.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q2**: Practice question 2 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 1.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q3**: Practice question 3 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 1.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q4**: Practice question 4 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 1.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q5**: Practice question 5 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 1.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q6**: Practice question 6 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 1.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q7**: Practice question 7 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 1.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q8**: Practice question 8 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 1.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q9**: Practice question 9 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 1.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q10**: Practice question 10 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 1.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q11**: Practice question 11 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 1.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q12**: Practice question 12 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 1.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q13**: Practice question 13 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 1.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q14**: Practice question 14 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 1.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q15**: Practice question 15 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 1.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check."}]}'::jsonb,
