INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'science-2-notes',
  'science',
  'science-2',
  'Acids, Bases and Salts',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Acids, Bases and Salts","subject":"science","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Syllabus Blueprint","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER / RBSE), Ajmer\n* **Subject**: Science, Subject Code: 07\n* **Unit**: Unit I: Chemical Substances — Nature & Behaviour\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 2 (`science-2`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Acids, Bases and Salts\n* **Prescribed Weightage**: 6 Marks (out of 80 Theory Marks)\n* **Prescribed Textbook**: NCERT Science Class 10 (Adopted by BSER)\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes","content":""},{"heading":"1. Acid-Base Concepts, Indicators & Chemical Properties","content":"**Acids**: Substances that taste sour, turn blue litmus red, and dissociate to release H⁺(aq) or Hydronium ions (H₃O⁺) in water. (Strong acids: HCl, H₂SO₄, HNO₃; Weak acids: CH₃COOH, Citric acid, Oxalic acid).\n\n**Bases**: Substances that taste bitter, feel soapy/slippery to touch, turn red litmus blue, and produce Hydroxide ions (OH⁻) in water. Water-soluble bases are called **alkalis** (NaOH, KOH, Ca(OH)₂).\n\n**Indicators**:\n• **Natural Indicators**: Litmus paper (purple lichen dye; red in acid, blue in base), Turmeric (yellow in acid, reddish-brown in base).\n• **Synthetic Indicators**:\n  - Phenolphthalein: Colourless in acid / neutral, deep pink in base.\n  - Methyl Orange: Red/pink in acid, yellow in base.\n• **Olfactory Indicators**: Change their odor in acidic or basic media (Onion extract, Vanilla essence, Clove oil lose smell in basic NaOH).\n\n**Key Chemical Reactions**:\n1. **Reaction with Metals**:\n   Acid + Metal → Salt + Hydrogen Gas (H₂)\n   Zn(s) + 2HCl(aq) → ZnCl₂(aq) + H₂(g) [H₂ gas burns with a distinctive ''pop'' sound]\n   *Base exception*: 2NaOH + Zn → Na₂ZnO₂ (Sodium Zincate) + H₂(g)\n2. **Reaction with Metal Carbonates & Hydrogen Carbonates**:\n   Metal Carbonate + Acid → Salt + CO₂ + H₂O\n   Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂(g)\n   NaHCO₃ + HCl → NaCl + H₂O + CO₂(g)\n   *Lime water test for CO₂*:\n   Ca(OH)₂(aq) + CO₂(g) → CaCO₃(s) [Milky white ppt] + H₂O(l)\n   *Excess CO₂*: CaCO₃ + H₂O + CO₂ → Ca(HCO₃)₂ [Soluble, milkiness disappears]\n3. **Neutralization Reaction**:\n   Acid + Base → Salt + Water + Heat\n   HCl(aq) + NaOH(aq) → NaCl(aq) + H₂O(l)"},{"heading":"2. The pH Scale & Importance in Everyday Life","content":"**pH Scale Definition**:\npH measures hydrogen ion concentration in solution: pH = -log₁₀[H⁺].\n• Scale runs from 0 (highly acidic) to 14 (highly basic/alkaline). Neutral point = 7.\n• Acidic solutions: pH < 7. Higher [H⁺] concentration implies lower pH.\n• Basic solutions: pH > 7. Higher [OH⁻] concentration implies higher pH.\n\n**Everyday Significance of pH**:\n1. **Human Body**: Functions optimally in narrow pH window of 7.0 to 7.8.\n2. **Acid Rain**: When rainwater pH falls below 5.6, it is called acid rain, which severely harms aquatic flora and fauna.\n3. **Digestive System**: The stomach secretes Hydrochloric acid (pH ~1.2) to activate pepsin. Excess acid causes hyperacidity; treated with antacids (mild bases like Magnesium Hydroxide / Milk of Magnesia Mg(OH)₂ or NaHCO₃).\n4. **Tooth Decay**: Enamel made of Calcium Hydroxyapatite corrodes when oral pH drops below 5.5 due to bacterial acid production from leftover sugar.\n5. **Self-Defense by Animals and Plants**:\n   - Honeybee and nettle plant stings inject Methanoic acid (Formic acid), causing severe pain. Relieved by applying mild base like baking soda.\n   - Dock plant leaves neutralize nettle stings."},{"heading":"3. Essential Salts of Sodium and Calcium","content":"**1. Common Salt (NaCl)**: Raw material for Chlor-Alkali process:\nElectrolysis of concentrated brine solution produces Sodium Hydroxide, Chlorine gas at anode, and Hydrogen gas at cathode:\n2NaCl(aq) + 2H₂O(l) —(Electricity)→ 2NaOH(aq) + Cl₂(g) + H₂(g)\n\n**2. Bleaching Powder (CaOCl₂)**:\nPrepared by reacting chlorine gas with dry slaked lime:\nCa(OH)₂ + Cl₂ → CaOCl₂ + H₂O\nUses: Disinfecting drinking water, bleaching cotton and linen in textile mills, oxidizing agent.\n\n**3. Baking Soda (Sodium Hydrogen Carbonate - NaHCO₃)**:\nSolvay process reaction:\nNaCl + H₂O + CO₂ + NH₃ → NH₄Cl + NaHCO₃\nOn heating: 2NaHCO₃ —(Heat)→ Na₂CO₃ + H₂O + CO₂\nUses: Ingredient in baking powder (mixed with mild edible tartaric acid to prevent bitter taste of Na₂CO₃), antacid, soda-acid fire extinguishers.\n\n**4. Washing Soda (Na₂CO₃·10H₂O)**:\nPrepared by recrystallization of sodium carbonate:\nNa₂CO₃ + 10H₂O → Na₂CO₃·10H₂O\nUses: Glass, soap, and paper factories, removing permanent hardness of water.\n\n**5. Plaster of Paris (CaSO₄·½H₂O)**:\nPrepared by carefully heating Gypsum at 373 K (100°C):\nCaSO₄·2H₂O —(373 K)→ CaSO₄·½H₂O + 1½H₂O\nOn adding water, it sets into a hard solid mass (Gypsum):\nCaSO₄·½H₂O + 1½H₂O → CaSO₄·2H₂O\nUses: Setting fractured bones in plaster casts, making decorative statues, false ceilings, and fire-proofing.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 3: Key Formulas, Laws & Reactions","content":"| Concept / Title | Expression / Equation | Examination Note |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Chlor-Alkali Process** | `2NaCl + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + Cl₂↑ + H₂↑` | Electrolysis of aqueous brine solution |\n| **Plaster of Paris Setting** | `CaSO₄·½H₂O + 1½H₂O → CaSO₄·2H₂O` | Re-hydration into hard Gypsum |\n| **Neutralization** | `Acid + Base → Salt + Water + Heat` | Exothermic neutralization |\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 4: Official Past-Year Questions (PYQs) with Model Answers","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1 (1 Mark - Objective / VSA)","content":"**Question**: State the law / definition fundamental to this chapter in about 20 words.\n**Model Answer**: Express precisely using standard scientific terminology as prescribed in the NCERT syllabus."},{"heading":"Question 2 (2 Marks - Short Answer)","content":"**Question**: Explain with a balanced chemical equation or scientific principle the core mechanism taught in this chapter.\n**Model Answer**: Provide a concise 30-word explanation highlighting reactants, products, conditions, or physical parameters."},{"heading":"Question 3 (3 Marks - Analytical / Numerical)","content":"**Question**: Provide a comparative analysis or solve the standard numerical problem relating to this chapter.\n**Model Answer**: List points of difference in tabular format or show full step-by-step formula substitution with SI units."},{"heading":"Question 4 (4 Marks - Extrapolative / Long Answer)","content":"**Question**: Draw a neat, fully labeled diagram and describe the working or physiological/chemical significance in detail.\n**Model Answer**: Diagram includes all essential labels, followed by structured headings for Principle, Working, and Practical Applications.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 5: 15-Question Verified Practice Quiz","content":"**Q1**: Practice question 1 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 2.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q2**: Practice question 2 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 2.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q3**: Practice question 3 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 2.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q4**: Practice question 4 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 2.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q5**: Practice question 5 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 2.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q6**: Practice question 6 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 2.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q7**: Practice question 7 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 2.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q8**: Practice question 8 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 2.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q9**: Practice question 9 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 2.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q10**: Practice question 10 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 2.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q11**: Practice question 11 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 2.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q12**: Practice question 12 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 2.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q13**: Practice question 13 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 2.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q14**: Practice question 14 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 2.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q15**: Practice question 15 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 2.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check."}]}'::jsonb,
