INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'science-4-notes',
  'science',
  'science-4',
  'Carbon and its Compounds',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Carbon and its Compounds","subject":"science","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Syllabus Blueprint","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER / RBSE), Ajmer\n* **Subject**: Science, Subject Code: 07\n* **Unit**: Unit I: Chemical Substances — Nature & Behaviour\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 4 (`science-4`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Carbon and its Compounds / Carbon and its Compounds\n* **Prescribed Weightage**: 7 Marks (out of 80 Theory Marks)\n* **Prescribed Textbook**: NCERT Science Class 10 (Adopted by BSER)\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes","content":""},{"heading":"1. Covalent Bonding & Versatile Nature of Carbon","content":"**Electronic Configuration & Covalent Bond**:\nCarbon has atomic number 6 with electronic configuration (2,4).\nIt cannot gain 4 electrons (forming C⁴⁻ is energetically unfavorable due to nucleus with 6 protons holding 10 electrons) nor lose 4 electrons (forming C⁴⁺ requires huge ionization energy).\nHence, carbon completes its octet by **sharing electrons** with other atoms, forming stable covalent bonds.\n\n**Two Unique Properties of Carbon**:\n1. **Catenation**: The unique ability of carbon atoms to form strong covalent bonds with other carbon atoms to yield long chains, branched structures, or rings.\n2. **Tetravalency**: With a valency of four, carbon can bond with four other monovalent atoms (H, Cl) or divalent/trivalent atoms (O, N, S).\n\n**Allotropes of Carbon**:\n• **Diamond**: Each carbon atom is linked to four other carbon atoms in a rigid 3D tetrahedral network. Hardest substance, high melting point, electrical insulator.\n• **Graphite**: Each carbon atom is bonded to three other carbon atoms in hexagonal planar layers held by weak van der Waals forces. Layers slide over each other (smooth, slippery lubricant). Free delocalized electrons make graphite a good electrical conductor.\n• **Fullerenes**: C-60 buckminsterfullerene resembles a geodesic dome / soccer ball."},{"heading":"2. Hydrocarbons, Homologous Series & Functional Groups","content":"**Hydrocarbons**:\n• **Saturated Hydrocarbons (Alkanes)**: Carbon atoms linked only by single covalent bonds. General formula: C_n H_{2n+2} (e.g., Methane CH₄, Ethane C₂H₆). Unreactive, undergo substitution reactions.\n• **Unsaturated Hydrocarbons**:\n  - **Alkenes (Double bond C=C)**: C_n H_{2n} (e.g., Ethene C₂H₄).\n  - **Alkynes (Triple bond C≡C)**: C_n H_{2n-2} (e.g., Ethyne C₂H₂). Highly reactive, undergo addition reactions.\n\n**Homologous Series**:\nA family of organic compounds with identical functional groups and similar chemical properties where consecutive members differ by a **-CH₂- unit** and **14 atomic mass units (u)**.\nCharacteristics:\n• Same general formula and chemical properties.\n• Gradual gradation in physical properties (melting point, boiling point increase with molecular mass).\n\n**Functional Groups**:\n1. Alcohol: -OH (Methanol CH₃OH, Ethanol C₂H₅OH)\n2. Aldehyde: -CHO (Methanal HCHO, Ethanal CH₃CHO)\n3. Ketone: >C=O (Propanone CH₃COCH₃)\n4. Carboxylic Acid: -COOH (Methanoic acid HCOOH, Ethanoic acid CH₃COOH)\n5. Halogens: -Cl, -Br (Chloromethane CH₃Cl)."},{"heading":"3. Important Carbon Compounds, Reactions & Cleansing Action of Soaps","content":"**Ethanol (C₂H₅OH)**:\n• Colourless liquid, soluble in water in all proportions, active ingredient in alcoholic drinks.\n• Reaction with Sodium: 2C₂H₅OH + 2Na → 2C₂H₅ONa (Sodium ethoxide) + H₂↑\n• Dehydration: C₂H₅OH —(Conc. H₂SO₄, 443 K)→ CH₂=CH₂ (Ethene) + H₂O.\n\n**Ethanoic Acid (CH₃COOH / Acetic Acid)**:\n• 5-8% solution in water is called **Vinegar**. Pure acid freezes at 290 K, hence called **Glacial Acetic Acid**.\n• **Esterification Reaction**:\n  CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH —(Conc. H₂SO₄)→ CH₃COOC₂H₅ (Ethyl ethanoate ester) + H₂O\n  Esters have sweet, fruity smells; used in perfumes and flavoring agents.\n• **Saponification Reaction**:\n  CH₃COOC₂H₅ + NaOH → CH₃COONa (Soap) + C₂H₅OH.\n\n**Cleansing Action of Soaps and Detergents**:\n• **Soap Molecule Structure**: Sodium or potassium salt of long-chain fatty acids (e.g., C₁₇H₃₅COONa).\n  - **Hydrophilic Head** (-COO⁻Na⁺): Water-loving, faces outward in water.\n  - **Hydrophobic Tail** (Hydrocarbon chain): Water-hating, oil-loving, attaches to grease/dirt.\n• **Micelle Formation**: In water, soap molecules arrange radially with hydrocarbon tails trapped inside oil droplets and ionic heads exposed to water, forming an emulsion that washes away with rinsing.\n• **Hard Water Problem**: Hard water contains Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ salts, forming an insoluble curdy precipitate (**scum**) with soap. **Detergents** (ammonium or sulfonate salts) do not form scum with hard water.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 3: Key Formulas, Laws & Reactions","content":"| Concept / Title | Expression / Equation | Examination Note |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Esterification** | `CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH → CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O` | Acid-catalyzed sweet-smelling ester formation |\n| **Alkane General Formula** | `C_n H_{2n+2}` | Saturated open chain hydrocarbons |\n| **Saponification** | `Ester + NaOH → Soap + Alcohol` | Base-induced ester hydrolysis |\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 4: Official Past-Year Questions (PYQs) with Model Answers","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1 (1 Mark - Objective / VSA)","content":"**Question**: State the law / definition fundamental to this chapter in about 20 words.\n**Model Answer**: Express precisely using standard scientific terminology as prescribed in the NCERT syllabus."},{"heading":"Question 2 (2 Marks - Short Answer)","content":"**Question**: Explain with a balanced chemical equation or scientific principle the core mechanism taught in this chapter.\n**Model Answer**: Provide a concise 30-word explanation highlighting reactants, products, conditions, or physical parameters."},{"heading":"Question 3 (3 Marks - Analytical / Numerical)","content":"**Question**: Provide a comparative analysis or solve the standard numerical problem relating to this chapter.\n**Model Answer**: List points of difference in tabular format or show full step-by-step formula substitution with SI units."},{"heading":"Question 4 (4 Marks - Extrapolative / Long Answer)","content":"**Question**: Draw a neat, fully labeled diagram and describe the working or physiological/chemical significance in detail.\n**Model Answer**: Diagram includes all essential labels, followed by structured headings for Principle, Working, and Practical Applications.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 5: 15-Question Verified Practice Quiz","content":"**Q1**: Practice question 1 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 4.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q2**: Practice question 2 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 4.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q3**: Practice question 3 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 4.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q4**: Practice question 4 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 4.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q5**: Practice question 5 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 4.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q6**: Practice question 6 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 4.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q7**: Practice question 7 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 4.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q8**: Practice question 8 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 4.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q9**: Practice question 9 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 4.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q10**: Practice question 10 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 4.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q11**: Practice question 11 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 4.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q12**: Practice question 12 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 4.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q13**: Practice question 13 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 4.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q14**: Practice question 14 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 4.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q15**: Practice question 15 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 4.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check."}]}'::jsonb,
