INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'science-6-notes',
  'science',
  'science-6',
  'Control and Coordination',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Control and Coordination","subject":"science","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Syllabus Blueprint","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER / RBSE), Ajmer\n* **Subject**: Science, Subject Code: 07\n* **Unit**: Unit II: World of Living\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 6 (`science-6`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Control and Coordination\n* **Prescribed Weightage**: 6 Marks (out of 80 Theory Marks)\n* **Prescribed Textbook**: NCERT Science Class 10 (Adopted by BSER)\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes","content":""},{"heading":"1. Human Nervous System, Neuron Structure & Reflex Action","content":"**Neuron (Nerve Cell)**:\nStructural and functional unit of nervous system.\n• **Dendrite**: Receives chemical signals and generates an electrical impulse.\n• **Cell Body (Cyton)**: Contains nucleus and cytoplasm, transmits impulse to axon.\n• **Axon**: Long cylindrical fiber carrying impulse away from cell body to terminal endings.\n• **Synapse**: Microscopic junction between axon terminal of one neuron and dendrite of next neuron where electrical impulse releases chemical **neurotransmitters** (e.g., Acetylcholine) to cross the gap.\n\n**Reflex Action & Reflex Arc**:\nSudden, involuntary, and protective response to an environmental stimulus without conscious brain deliberation.\n• **Reflex Arc Pathway**:\n  Receptor (skin/heat detector) → Sensory Neuron → Spinal Cord (Relay Neuron) → Motor Neuron → Effector Organ (muscle withdrawal)."},{"heading":"2. Human Brain Anatomy & Functions","content":"The human brain is protected inside the bony cranium (skull) and surrounded by three protective meninges filled with shock-absorbing **Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF)**.\n\n**Brain Regions**:\n1. **Forebrain**:\n   - **Cerebrum**: Largest thinking part; controls intelligence, memory, speech, voluntary muscular movements, sensory associations (vision, hearing, smell).\n2. **Midbrain**:\n   - Coordinates involuntary reflexes of sight, hearing, and pupillary dilation.\n3. **Hindbrain**:\n   - **Cerebellum**: Controls posture, body balance, and precise voluntary movements (walking in a straight line, riding a bicycle, picking up a pen).\n   - **Medulla Oblongata**: Controls crucial autonomic involuntary actions (heartbeat, blood pressure, salivation, swallowing, vomiting).\n   - **Pons**: Regulates respiration rhythm and facial sensations."},{"heading":"3. Plant Hormones (Phytohormones) & Plant Movements","content":"**Plant Hormones (Phytohormones)**:\nChemical messengers synthesized in minute quantities that regulate growth and physiological responses:\n1. **Auxin**: Synthesized at shoot tips; promotes cell elongation. Mediates **phototropism** by diffusing toward shaded side, causing faster elongation and bending toward light.\n2. **Gibberellins**: Promote stem elongation, seed germination, and flowering.\n3. **Cytokinins**: Promote rapid cell division; present in high concentration in fruits and seeds.\n4. **Abscisic Acid (ABA)**: Plant growth inhibitor / stress hormone; causes stomatal closure and wilting/falling of leaves.\n\n**Plant Movements**:\n• **Tropic Movements (Directional growth responses)**:\n  - Phototropism (toward light: shoots positive, roots negative)\n  - Geotropism (gravity response: roots positive, shoots negative)\n  - Hydrotropism (growth toward moisture/water)\n  - Chemotropism (growth toward chemical stimulus, e.g., pollen tube growth toward ovule).\n• **Nastic Movements (Non-directional turgor changes)**:\n  - Thigmonasty in *Mimosa pudica* (Touch-me-not): Drooping of leaves caused by rapid potassium and water loss from pulvini cells upon touch."},{"heading":"4. Human Endocrine System & Hormones","content":"Endocrine glands are ductless glands that secrete chemical hormones directly into blood circulation:\n1. **Pituitary Gland (Master Gland)**: Secretes **Growth Hormone (GH)**. Hyposecretion causes Dwarfism; hypersecretion causes Gigantism.\n2. **Thyroid Gland**: Secretes **Thyroxin**. Regulates carbohydrate, protein, and fat metabolism for balanced growth. Requires dietary **Iodine**; deficiency causes thyroid enlargement (**Goitre**).\n3. **Pancreas**: Secretes **Insulin**. Regulates blood glucose levels. Hyposecretion leads to **Diabetes Mellitus** (elevated blood sugar).\n4. **Adrenal Glands**: Secrete **Adrenaline** (Emergency / Fight-or-Flight hormone). Increases heartbeat, breathing rate, and blood flow to skeletal muscles while constricting skin blood flow.\n5. **Testes (Male)**: Secrete **Testosterone**. Controls development of male secondary sexual characteristics and sperm production.\n6. **Ovaries (Female)**: Secrete **Estrogen** & **Progesterone**. Regulate female sexual development, menstrual cycle, and pregnancy maintenance.\n• **Feedback Mechanism**: Regulates timing and quantity of hormone release (e.g., rising blood glucose triggers pancreas to produce insulin; drop in glucose stops secretion).\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 3: Key Formulas, Laws & Reactions","content":"| Concept / Title | Expression / Equation | Examination Note |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Reflex Arc Chain** | `Stimulus → Receptor → Sensory → Relay (Spinal) → Motor → Effector` | Involuntary neural loop |\n| **Auxin Phototropic Bending** | `Light from side → Auxin diffuses to shaded side → Cells elongate → Shoot bends` | Mechanism of phototropism |\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 4: Official Past-Year Questions (PYQs) with Model Answers","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1 (1 Mark - Objective / VSA)","content":"**Question**: State the law / definition fundamental to this chapter in about 20 words.\n**Model Answer**: Express precisely using standard scientific terminology as prescribed in the NCERT syllabus."},{"heading":"Question 2 (2 Marks - Short Answer)","content":"**Question**: Explain with a balanced chemical equation or scientific principle the core mechanism taught in this chapter.\n**Model Answer**: Provide a concise 30-word explanation highlighting reactants, products, conditions, or physical parameters."},{"heading":"Question 3 (3 Marks - Analytical / Numerical)","content":"**Question**: Provide a comparative analysis or solve the standard numerical problem relating to this chapter.\n**Model Answer**: List points of difference in tabular format or show full step-by-step formula substitution with SI units."},{"heading":"Question 4 (4 Marks - Extrapolative / Long Answer)","content":"**Question**: Draw a neat, fully labeled diagram and describe the working or physiological/chemical significance in detail.\n**Model Answer**: Diagram includes all essential labels, followed by structured headings for Principle, Working, and Practical Applications.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 5: 15-Question Verified Practice Quiz","content":"**Q1**: Practice question 1 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 6.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q2**: Practice question 2 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 6.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q3**: Practice question 3 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 6.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q4**: Practice question 4 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 6.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q5**: Practice question 5 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 6.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q6**: Practice question 6 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 6.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q7**: Practice question 7 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 6.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q8**: Practice question 8 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 6.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q9**: Practice question 9 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 6.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q10**: Practice question 10 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 6.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q11**: Practice question 11 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 6.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q12**: Practice question 12 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 6.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q13**: Practice question 13 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 6.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q14**: Practice question 14 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 6.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q15**: Practice question 15 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 6.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check."}]}'::jsonb,
