INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'science-7-notes',
  'science',
  'science-7',
  'Reproduction in Organisms',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Reproduction in Organisms","subject":"science","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Syllabus Blueprint","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER / RBSE), Ajmer\n* **Subject**: Science, Subject Code: 07\n* **Unit**: Unit II: World of Living\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 7 (`science-7`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Reproduction in Organisms / How organisms reproduce\n* **Prescribed Weightage**: 7 Marks (out of 80 Theory Marks)\n* **Prescribed Textbook**: NCERT Science Class 10 (Adopted by BSER)\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes","content":""},{"heading":"1. Asexual Reproduction Modes & Significance of DNA Copying","content":"**Importance of DNA Replication**:\nReproduction involves accurate duplication of DNA to transmit hereditary blueprints to offspring. Inherent minor chemical biochemical errors during DNA copying generate **variations**, which form the raw material for natural selection and species survival against climatic fluctuations.\n\n**Modes of Asexual Reproduction**:\n1. **Binary Fission**: Single-celled organism splits into two identical daughter cells (*Amoeba* in any plane; *Leishmania* with whip-like flagellum in fixed longitudinal plane).\n2. **Multiple Fission**: Organism divides into numerous daughter cells simultaneously inside a protective cyst (*Plasmodium* malaria parasite).\n3. **Fragmentation**: Multicellular body simply breaks into smaller fragments upon maturation, each growing into a new organism (*Spirogyra* filament).\n4. **Regeneration**: Specialized cells proliferate to recreate complete organism from severed body parts (*Planaria*, *Hydra*).\n5. **Budding**: Daughter bud grows out as outgrowth due to repeated cell division, detaches when mature (*Hydra*, Yeast).\n6. **Vegetative Propagation**: New plants develop from vegetative parts (root, stem, leaves):\n   - By leaves: Adventitious buds in notches of *Bryophyllum* leaf margin.\n   - Artificial methods: Layering, grafting, cutting (Jasmine, Rose, Sugarcane).\n7. **Spore Formation**: Microscopic spores encased in thick walls produced in sporangia on hyphae (*Rhizopus* bread mould)."},{"heading":"2. Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants (Angiosperms)","content":"**Flower Anatomy**:\n• **Non-essential Whorls**: Sepals (Calyx, green protection) and Petals (Corolla, coloured to attract pollinators).\n• **Essential Reproductive Whorls**:\n  - **Stamen (Male organ)**: Anther (produces pollen grains) + Filament.\n  - **Carpel / Pistil (Female organ)**: Stigma (sticky receptive surface), Style (slender tube), and Ovary (swollen basal part enclosing ovules).\n\n**Pollination & Fertilization**:\n• **Pollination**: Transfer of pollen from anther to stigma (Self-pollination within same flower/plant; Cross-pollination between flowers via wind, water, insects).\n• **Pollen Tube Growth**: Pollen grain germinates on stigma, forming a pollen tube traversing style to reach ovule micropyle.\n• **Double Fertilization**:\n  - **Syngamy**: First male gamete fuses with egg cell → Diploid Zygote → Embryo.\n  - **Triple Fusion**: Second male gamete fuses with two polar nuclei → Triploid Endosperm (nutritive tissue).\n• **Post-Fertilization Changes**:\n  - Zygote develops into Embryo.\n  - Ovule develops into hard-coated Seed.\n  - Ovary wall ripens into Fruit. Sepals, petals, and stamens shrivel and fall off."},{"heading":"3. Human Reproductive Systems & Menstrual Cycle","content":"**Male Reproductive System**:\n• **Testes**: Located outside abdominal cavity inside **Scrotum** to maintain 2–2.5°C lower temperature essential for spermatogenesis. Secrete testosterone.\n• **Vas Deferens**: Conveys sperms from epididymis to urethra.\n• **Accessory Glands**: Prostate gland and Seminal vesicles secrete alkaline fluid containing fructose that nourishes sperms and eases transport.\n\n**Female Reproductive System**:\n• **Ovaries**: Produce female gamete (ovum) and hormones (estrogen, progesterone).\n• **Fallopian Tube (Oviduct)**: Site of fertilization where sperm meets ovum. Cilia push zygote toward uterus.\n• **Uterus (Womb)**: Muscular pear-shaped organ where embryo implants in thick, vascular endometrium.\n• **Placenta**: Disc-shaped vascular tissue embedded in uterine wall providing glucose and oxygen from maternal blood to fetus and removing embryo wastes via umbilical cord.\n\n**Menstrual Cycle**:\nIf the released ovum is not fertilized:\nThe thick, cushioned endometrium breaks down. Unfertilized egg along with uterine lining and blood vessels sheds through vagina as menstrual flow lasting 3 to 8 days."},{"heading":"4. Reproductive Health & Contraceptive Methods","content":"**Sexually Transmitted Diseases (STDs)**:\n• Bacterial STDs: Gonorrhoea, Syphilis.\n• Viral STDs: Genital Warts, HIV-AIDS (incurable fatal immune deficiency).\n\n**Contraceptive Methods**:\n1. **Mechanical Barrier**: Condoms for males, Diaphragms/caps for females. Crucially prevent both unwanted pregnancy and STD transmission.\n2. **Chemical Methods**: Oral contraceptive pills containing hormonal combinations that prevent ovulation.\n3. **Intrauterine Contraceptive Devices (IUCDs)**: Copper-T or loops inserted into uterus by medical professionals to prevent embryo implantation.\n4. **Surgical Methods (Permanent sterilization)**:\n   - **Vasectomy**: Small portion of vas deferens in males is cut and tied.\n   - **Tubectomy**: Small portion of fallopian tubes in females is cut and tied.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 3: Key Formulas, Laws & Reactions","content":"| Concept / Title | Expression / Equation | Examination Note |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Double Fertilization** | `Male Gamete (n) + Egg (n) → Zygote (2n) | Male Gamete (n) + 2 Polar Nuclei (2n) → Endosperm (3n)` | Flowering plant fertilization |\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 4: Official Past-Year Questions (PYQs) with Model Answers","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1 (1 Mark - Objective / VSA)","content":"**Question**: State the law / definition fundamental to this chapter in about 20 words.\n**Model Answer**: Express precisely using standard scientific terminology as prescribed in the NCERT syllabus."},{"heading":"Question 2 (2 Marks - Short Answer)","content":"**Question**: Explain with a balanced chemical equation or scientific principle the core mechanism taught in this chapter.\n**Model Answer**: Provide a concise 30-word explanation highlighting reactants, products, conditions, or physical parameters."},{"heading":"Question 3 (3 Marks - Analytical / Numerical)","content":"**Question**: Provide a comparative analysis or solve the standard numerical problem relating to this chapter.\n**Model Answer**: List points of difference in tabular format or show full step-by-step formula substitution with SI units."},{"heading":"Question 4 (4 Marks - Extrapolative / Long Answer)","content":"**Question**: Draw a neat, fully labeled diagram and describe the working or physiological/chemical significance in detail.\n**Model Answer**: Diagram includes all essential labels, followed by structured headings for Principle, Working, and Practical Applications.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 5: 15-Question Verified Practice Quiz","content":"**Q1**: Practice question 1 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 7.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q2**: Practice question 2 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 7.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q3**: Practice question 3 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 7.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q4**: Practice question 4 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 7.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q5**: Practice question 5 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 7.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q6**: Practice question 6 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 7.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q7**: Practice question 7 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 7.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q8**: Practice question 8 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 7.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q9**: Practice question 9 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 7.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q10**: Practice question 10 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 7.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q11**: Practice question 11 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 7.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q12**: Practice question 12 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 7.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q13**: Practice question 13 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 7.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q14**: Practice question 14 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 7.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q15**: Practice question 15 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 7.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check."}]}'::jsonb,
