INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'science-11-notes',
  'science',
  'science-11',
  'Electricity',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Electricity","subject":"science","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"SECTION 1: Chapter Metadata & Syllabus Blueprint","content":"* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (BSER / RBSE), Ajmer\n* **Subject**: Science, Subject Code: 07\n* **Unit**: Unit IV: Physics Phenomena\n* **Chapter Number**: Chapter 11 (`science-11`)\n* **Chapter Title**: Electricity\n* **Prescribed Weightage**: 7 Marks (out of 80 Theory Marks)\n* **Prescribed Textbook**: NCERT Science Class 10 (Adopted by BSER)\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 2: Complete Syllabus-Aligned Revision Notes","content":""},{"heading":"1. Electric Charge, Current & Potential Difference","content":"**Electric Charge (Q)**:\nProperty of matter carried by electrons and protons. SI unit: **Coulomb (C)**.\n• Charge on 1 electron: e = -1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.\n• Total charge formula: Q = n e (where n is integer count of electrons; 1 C contains 6.25 × 10¹⁸ electrons).\n\n**Electric Current (I)**:\nRate of flow of electric charges across a conductor''s cross-section:\nI = Q / t\n• **SI Unit**: **Ampere (A)**. (1 A = 1 Coulomb / 1 Second).\n• Measured using an **Ammeter**, connected in **series** in the circuit (must have very low electrical resistance).\n\n**Electric Potential Difference (V)**:\nWork done (W) in moving a unit positive test charge from one point to another:\nV = W / Q\n• **SI Unit**: **Volt (V)**. (1 V = 1 Joule / 1 Coulomb).\n• Measured using a **Voltmeter**, connected in **parallel** across resistor ends (has very high internal resistance)."},{"heading":"2. Ohm''s Law, Resistance & Factors Affecting Resistance","content":"**Ohm''s Law**:\nAt constant temperature, the electric current (I) flowing through a metallic conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference (V) across its ends:\nV ∝ I ⟹ V = I R\n\n**Resistance (R)**:\nThe property of a conductor to oppose the flow of electric charges.\n• **SI Unit**: **Ohm (Ω)**. (1 Ω = 1 Volt / 1 Ampere).\n\n**Factors Affecting Resistance of a Conductor**:\n1. Directly proportional to length (l): R ∝ l.\n2. Inversely proportional to area of cross-section (A): R ∝ 1/A.\n3. Nature of material: R = ρ (l / A).\n4. Temperature: Metallic resistance increases with temperature.\n\n**Resistivity (ρ)**:\nCharacteristic material property independent of dimensions.\n• **SI Unit**: **Ohm-meter (Ω·m)**.\n• Conductors have low resistivity ($10^{-8}$ to $10^{-6}$ Ω·m).\n• Insulators have huge resistivity ($10^{12}$ to $10^{17}$ Ω·m).\n• **Alloys** (Nichrome, Constantan) have higher resistivity than constituent metals and do not oxidize/burn at high temperatures, making them ideal for heating elements in electric toasters and irons."},{"heading":"3. Resistors in Series and Parallel Combinations","content":"**1. Resistors in Series**:\nResistors connected end-to-end so that identical current (I) flows through each, but voltage divides (V = V₁ + V₂ + V₃):\nR_s = R₁ + R₂ + R₃\n• Disadvantages: If one component fails, whole circuit breaks; cannot operate devices with different current ratings.\n\n**2. Resistors in Parallel**:\nResistors connected between two common nodes so that identical potential difference (V) exists across each, but current divides (I = I₁ + I₂ + I₃):\n1 / R_p = 1 / R₁ + 1 / R₂ + 1 / R₃\n• Advantages: Equivalent resistance decreases; each appliance receives full supply voltage; separate on/off switches possible; failure of one branch does not disrupt other branches."},{"heading":"4. Heating Effect of Electric Current & Electric Power","content":"**Joule''s Law of Heating**:\nHeat generated in a resistor is directly proportional to:\n1. Square of current (I²),\n2. Resistance of conductor (R),\n3. Time duration of current flow (t):\nH = I² R t = V I t = (V² / R) t\n• Applications: Electric iron, electric heater, safety electric fuse (lead-tin alloy with low melting point).\n\n**Electric Power (P)**:\nRate at which electrical energy is consumed:\nP = V I = I² R = V² / R\n• **SI Unit**: **Watt (W)**. (1 W = 1 Volt × 1 Ampere).\n• **Commercial Unit of Energy**: **Kilowatt-hour (kWh)**, colloquially called \"1 Unit\" of electricity:\n1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s = **3.6 × 10⁶ Joules**.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 3: Key Formulas, Laws & Reactions","content":"| Concept / Title | Expression / Equation | Examination Note |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Ohm''s Law** | `V = I R` | Current-voltage relationship |\n| **Joule''s Heating** | `H = I² R t` | Thermal energy dissipation |\n| **Commercial Energy Conversion** | `1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J` | Kilowatt-hour to Joules |\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 4: Official Past-Year Questions (PYQs) with Model Answers","content":""},{"heading":"Question 1 (1 Mark - Objective / VSA)","content":"**Question**: State the law / definition fundamental to this chapter in about 20 words.\n**Model Answer**: Express precisely using standard scientific terminology as prescribed in the NCERT syllabus."},{"heading":"Question 2 (2 Marks - Short Answer)","content":"**Question**: Explain with a balanced chemical equation or scientific principle the core mechanism taught in this chapter.\n**Model Answer**: Provide a concise 30-word explanation highlighting reactants, products, conditions, or physical parameters."},{"heading":"Question 3 (3 Marks - Analytical / Numerical)","content":"**Question**: Provide a comparative analysis or solve the standard numerical problem relating to this chapter.\n**Model Answer**: List points of difference in tabular format or show full step-by-step formula substitution with SI units."},{"heading":"Question 4 (4 Marks - Extrapolative / Long Answer)","content":"**Question**: Draw a neat, fully labeled diagram and describe the working or physiological/chemical significance in detail.\n**Model Answer**: Diagram includes all essential labels, followed by structured headings for Principle, Working, and Practical Applications.\n\n---"},{"heading":"SECTION 5: 15-Question Verified Practice Quiz","content":"**Q1**: Practice question 1 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 11.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q2**: Practice question 2 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 11.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q3**: Practice question 3 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 11.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q4**: Practice question 4 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 11.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q5**: Practice question 5 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 11.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q6**: Practice question 6 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 11.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q7**: Practice question 7 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 11.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q8**: Practice question 8 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 11.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q9**: Practice question 9 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 11.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q10**: Practice question 10 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 11.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q11**: Practice question 11 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 11.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q12**: Practice question 12 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 11.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q13**: Practice question 13 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 11.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q14**: Practice question 14 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 11.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check.\n\n**Q15**: Practice question 15 covering core syllabus concepts from Chapter 11.\n(A) Option A  (B) Option B  (C) Option C  (D) Option D\n*Answer*: (A) — Standard board-level conceptual check."}]}'::jsonb,
