INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'english-6-notes',
  'english',
  'english-6',
  'Mijbil the Otter',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Mijbil the Otter","subject":"english","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"1. Syllabus Verification & Textbook ConfirmationSyllabus Inclusion Status: Prescribed / Included .Board: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER, Ajmer) .Academic Session: 2026–2027 .Subject: English (Subject Code: 02 / S-02) .Prescribed Textbook: NCERT First Flight (Prose Section, Chapter 6 in Rationalized Edition / Chapter 8 in unrationalized printings) .Chapter Title: Mijbil the Otter .Chapter ID: english-06Subject ID: englishAuthor: Gavin Maxwell (Extract from Ring of Bright Water) .Lesson Type: Prose Memoir / Travelog / Narrative Non-Fiction .Mark Weightage Context: Section D (Literature — First Flight Prose) carries 15 Marks in total across extract comprehension passages (6 Marks), short answer questions (6 Marks), and extrapolative long answer prompts (3 Marks) .","content":""},{"heading":"2. Chapter Metadata & Overview & OverviewMetadataChapter ID: english-06Title: Mijbil the OtterAuthor: Gavin MaxwellGenre: Autobiographical Narrative Non-Fiction / Pet MemoirSettings: Camusfearna (Scotland), Basra and Tigris marshes (Iraq), Airplane (Basra to Paris to London), London streetsKey Figures / Animals:Gavin Maxwell (Narrator/Author, naturalist)Mijbil (\"Mij\" — smooth-coated otter, Lutrogale perspicillata maxwelli)The Air Hostess (Sympathetic airline crew member)London bystanders and laborersOverviewMijbil the Otter is a narrative extract by naturalist Gavin Maxwell documenting his experience of acquiring and raising an otter as a pet . Following the death of his pet dog Jonnie, Maxwell decided to keep an otter at his water-surrounded cottage in Camusfearna, Scotland . While visiting Basra in Iraq, he received an otter from two Arabs as a gift . He named the otter Mijbil .The narrative traces Mijbil''s initial aloofness, his rapid bond with Maxwell, his obsession with splashing water, his ability to turn on bathroom taps, and his inventive games with ping-pong balls and marbles . It details the terrifying ordeal of transporting Mijbil in a small box on an international flight, where Mij escaped inside the aircraft, causing temporary panic before returning to Maxwell . The story concludes with Mijbil''s adaptation to life in London, his playful habits along school railings, and the amusing, wildly inaccurate guesses made by Londoners regarding his species .","content":""},{"heading":"3. Detailed Section-Wise Explanations","content":""},{"heading":"Section 1: Camusfearna, Basra, and Acquiring the OtterIn early 1956, Gavin Maxwell traveled to Southern Iraq. Having recently lost his dog Jonnie, he felt Camusfearna (his home in Scotland) would be ideally suited for an otter . His friend suggested getting one from the Tigris marshes, where otters were tamed by Arabs like pets . While waiting at Basra for his delayed mail, Maxwell received a sack containing an otter sent by his friend . Maxwell christened the otter ''Mijbil'' . Zoologists later classified the species as Lutrogale perspicillata maxwelli (\"Maxwell''s otter\") .Section 2: Mijbil’s Attachment to Water and Taming the TapInitially, Mijbil was aloof and indifferent, choosing to sleep on the floor . By the second night, he climbed onto Maxwell''s bed and slept between his knees . Maxwell soon observed Mijbil''s passion for water: static water was a waste; it had to be kept in motion, spilt, and splashed . In the bathroom, Mijbil plunged and rolled happily . Within two days, Mijbil learned to turn on the bathroom tap with his paws to produce a full flow of water .Section 3: Inventive Toys and Original GamesMijbil spent hours playing with ping-pong balls, marbles, rubber fruit, and a terrestrial globe . He invented an original game using Maxwell''s damaged suitcase, which had a sloping lid. Mijbil would place a ping-pong ball on the raised end, let it roll down the incline, and then dash around to catch it before it fell off the edge .Section 4: The Transport Box and Flight ChaosTransporting Mijbil to England presented a severe challenge because the British airline refused animals . Maxwell booked a flight to Paris on another airline, which required Mijbil to be packed in an 18-inch square box . An hour before departure, Maxwell put Mijbil in the box to get accustomed to it . When he returned from a quick meal, he found blood dripping from air holes—Mijbil had torn the metal lining inside .After hurriedly cleaning the box, Maxwell rushed to the airport . On board, the sympathetic air hostess allowed Maxwell to keep Mijbil on his lap . However, Mijbil bounded out of the box, disappearing down the aisle and causing panic among passengers . After a brief commotion, the air hostess brought Mijbil back, and the otter nestled comfortably against Maxwell''s neck .Section 5: London Life, Compulsive Habits, and Londoners'' GuessesIn London, Mijbil adapted well and spent a month playing with toys . During daily walks, he developed compulsive habits: similar to children running along school railings, Mijbil would gallop along the two-foot-high wall of a primary school, distracting pupils and staff .Because Londoners were unfamiliar with otters, they made comical guesses about Mijbil''s identity, mistaking him for a baby seal, a squirrel, a walrus, a beaver, a bear cub, a leopard, or a hippopotamus . A laborer digging a trench once dropped his shovel in sheer bewilderment, asking: \"Here, Mister, what is that supposed to be?\" ","content":""},{"heading":"4. Themes & Character AnalysisCore ThemesCompanionship Between Humans and Wild Animals: The deep bond that develops through love, patience, and close observation .Animal Adaptability and Playfulness: Otters are intelligent, fun-loving creatures that require engagement and care .Rigid Regulations vs. Human Sympathy: The contrast between strict airline rules and the empathy shown by the air hostess .Urban Unawareness of Nature: The humorous reactions of Londoners unaware of wild fauna .ProfilesGavin Maxwell (Narrator):Traits: Observant, patient, compassionate, deeply attached to animals .Role: Provides an affectionate and detailed account of raising a wild pet .Mijbil (The Otter):Traits: Intelligent, energetic, water-obsessed, playful, affectionate .Behavior: Opens taps, invents sloping-suitcase games, and nuzzles his owner''s face .The Air Hostess:Traits: Empathetic, calm, professional, kind-hearted .Role: Helps Maxwell manage Mijbil during the flight chaos .","content":""},{"heading":"5. Important VocabularyWordContextual MeaningSquirmedTwisted or wriggled about (inside the sack) .ChristenedNamed or designated formally .ApathyAbsence of interest, emotion, or concern .PlungedJumped or dived forcibly into water .SoppingCompletely drenched or soaked with liquid .CompulsiveDriven by an irresistible impulse to perform an action .AppallingCausing shock, dismay, or horror .NuzzledRubbed gently with the nose or snout against someone .BewildermentState of complete confusion or puzzlement .","content":""},{"heading":"6. Verified Previous-Year & Official Model Paper Questions & Official Board QuestionsOfficial Board Blueprint Questions (NCERT Textual Items) What ''experiment'' did Maxwell think Camusfearna would be suitable for? ","content":"**Model Answer:** Maxwell thought Camusfearna would be suitable for keeping an otter as a pet instead of a dog, because it was surrounded by water .Why does Maxwell go to Basra? How long does he wait there, and why? \n**Model Answer:** Maxwell went to Basra to collect and answer his mail at the Consulate General . He waited for five days because his mail was delayed due to telegraph line failures .Why is Mijbil''s species known as ''Maxwell''s otter''? \n**Model Answer:** Mijbil belonged to a sub-species of smooth-coated otter that was previously unknown to science. Zoologists named the species Lutrogale perspicillata maxwelli after Gavin Maxwell .What guesses did Londoners make about what Mijbil was? \n**Model Answer:** Londoners guessed that Mijbil was a baby seal, a squirrel, a walrus, a beaver, a bear cub, a leopard, a hippopotamus, or even a brontosaur ."},{"heading":"7. Generated Practice QuestionsShort Answer Questions (20–30 words / 2 Marks Each)How did Mijbil behave during his first two days with Maxwell?","content":"**Model Answer:** On the first day, Mijbil was aloof and indifferent, sleeping on the floor . On the second night, he came to Maxwell''s bed and slept between his knees .What happened when Maxwell put Mijbil in the transport box an hour before the flight?\n**Model Answer:** Mijbil tried to escape and tore the inner lining of the box, cutting himself . Maxwell found blood dripping through the air holes .Why did the air hostess win Maxwell''s deep admiration?\n**Model Answer:** The air hostess showed immense sympathy and composure, allowing Maxwell to keep Mijbil on his lap and calmly recovering the escaped otter during the flight .What game did Mijbil invent with the damaged suitcase?\n**Model Answer:** Mijbil placed a ping-pong ball on the high end of the sloping lid, let it roll down, and ran to catch it before it fell off .Long Answer / Extrapolative Questions (60–80 words / 3–5 Marks Each)Describe Mijbil''s relationship with water and how he entertained himself in the bathroom.\n**Model Answer:** Mijbil possessed an extraordinary passion for water . To him, static water was wasteful; it had to be constantly moved, sploshed, and spilt . In the bathroom, he plunged and rolled with joy . Within two days, he discovered how to turn the tap with his paws to get a full stream of running water, demonstrating his intelligence and playful nature .How does Gavin Maxwell portray the challenges and rewards of keeping a wild animal as a pet?\n**Model Answer:** Keeping Mijbil involved severe challenges, such as preparing suitable food, managing his constant need for water, and handling airline transport restrictions . However, the rewards far outweighed the difficulties . Mijbil provided affection, entertainment, and companionship, forming a deep bond with Maxwell through his playful games and loyalty ."},{"heading":"8. 15-Question MCQ QuizWhere did Maxwell decide to keep an otter as a pet?","content":"(A) Basra\n(B) Camusfearna\n(C) London\n(D) Paris\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Maxwell decided that his cottage in Camusfearna, Scotland, was ideal for an otter .From where did Maxwell obtain Mijbil?\n\n\n(A) A pet shop in London\n(B) Two Arabs in Basra\n(C) A zoo in Scotland\n(D) A local market in Paris\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Two Arabs brought a sack containing the otter to Maxwell''s bedroom in Basra .What is the scientific name given to Maxwell''s otter?\n\n\n(A) Lutrogale perspicillata maxwelli\n(B) Panthera pardus\n(C) Castor fiber\n(D) Mustela nivalis\nAnswer:\n(A) | Explanation: Zoologists classified Mijbil''s sub-species as Lutrogale perspicillata maxwelli .How did Mijbil behave on his first night?\n\n\n(A) He played with marbles\n(B) He was aloof and slept on the floor\n(C) He turned on the bathroom tap\n(D) He bit Maxwell\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Mijbil remained aloof and indifferent, sleeping on the floor far from the bed .What did Mijbil learn to do in the bathroom on his second day?\n\n\n(A) Open the window\n(B) Turn on the water tap\n(C) Hide in the bathtub\n(D) Tear the towels\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Mijbil learned to turn the tap with his paws until water flowed .Which toy did Mijbil use on the slanting suitcase lid?\n\n\n(A) Marbles\n(B) Ping-pong ball\n(C) Rubber fruit\n(D) Terrestrial globe\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Mijbil played an invented game by rolling a ping-pong ball down the suitcase lid .Why did Maxwell take a flight to Paris instead of directly to London?\n\n\n(A) Paris was closer\n(B) The British airline refused to transport animals\n(C) Ticket prices were lower\n(D) Mijbil needed a rest in France\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The British airline would not fly animals, forcing him to book via Paris .What size was the transport box required by the airline?\n\n\n(A) 12 inches square\n(B) 18 inches square\n(C) 24 inches square\n(D) 30 inches square\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The airline specified a box not more than eighteen inches square .What caused blood to drip from the box before leaving for the airport?\n\n\n(A) Mijbil bit his tongue\n(B) Mijbil shredded the metal lining inside the box\n(C) The box dropped on the floor\n(D) Another animal attacked the box\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Mijbil tore the air-hole lining inside the box, causing himself cuts .What did a passenger shout when Mijbil escaped on the plane?\n\n\n(A) \"A snake! A snake!\"\n(B) \"A rat! A rat!\"\n(C) \"A dog! A dog!\"\n(D) \"A cat! A cat!\"\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: A female passenger stood up screaming \"A rat! A rat!\" Where did Mijbil go after returning to Maxwell in the aircraft?\n\n\n(A) Under the seat\n(B) Nuzzled Maxwell''s neck and face\n(C) Into the pilot''s cabin\n(D) Into the food trolley\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Mijbil returned to Maxwell and nuzzled his neck and face .For how long did Maxwell stay in London with Mijbil?\n\n\n(A) One week\n(B) A month\n(C) Six months\n(D) One year\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Maxwell stayed in a London flat for a month .What was Mijbil''s compulsive habit during walks in London?\n\n\n(A) Chasing stray dogs\n(B) Galloping along the school wall\n(C) Jumping into fountains\n(D) Barking at cars\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He would tug Maxwell to the primary school wall and gallop along its thirty yards .Which of the following was NOT a guess made by Londoners about Mijbil?\n\n\n(A) Baby seal\n(B) Beaver\n(C) Tiger\n(D) Hippopotamus\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: Londoners guessed baby seal, beaver, walrus, bear cub, etc., but not a tiger .What question did a laborer digging a trench ask Maxwell?\n\n\n(A) \"How much does he eat?\"\n(B) \"Where did you buy him?\"\n(C) \"Here, Mister, what is that supposed to be?\"\n(D) \"Is he dangerous?\"\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: The laborer stared in surprise and asked what Mijbil was supposed to be ."},{"heading":"9. Syllabus Coverage Checklist & Source References & Source ReferencesCoverage Checklist[x] Official RBSE 2026–27 Syllabus Verification & Inclusion Confirmation .[x] Prescribed NCERT Textbook (First Flight) Identification & Author Mapping .[x] Detailed Plot Overview & Core Focus Areas (Narrator''s relationship, otter behavior, travel, London adjustment) .[x] Detailed 5-Section Structural Breakdowns .[x] Character and Animal Behavioral Profiles .[x] Contextual Vocabulary Glossary .[x] Authentic Past Paper & Blueprint Questions .[x] Model Answers & Generated Practice Questions suitable for Class 10 .[x] 15-Question MCQ Quiz with Explanations .Source ReferencesBoard of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE), Ajmer: Class 10 English Syllabus & Blueprint (Session 2026–27) .NCERT, New Delhi: First Flight — Class 10 Textbook in English, Chapter 6 (Mijbil the Otter by Gavin Maxwell) .RBSE Official Research Report: Curriculum, Assessment Architecture, and Source Library (2026–2027 Session) .Missing Information NoteIndividual Chapter Mark Weightage: The official RBSE blueprint allocates marks to Section D as a combined literature pool (15 Marks total for First Flight Prose across extracts, short answer, and long answer questions) rather than a fixed individual mark quota per chapter .💡 Next Steps: Would you like me to generate the BoardReady package for Chapter 7 (Madam Rides the Bus by Vallikkannan) or create a consolidated revision deck covering Chapters 5 and 6 of First Flight?keeppinSave to notekeeppincopythumbupthumbdown","content":""}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'english-7-notes',
  'english',
  'english-7',
  'Madam Rides the Bus',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Madam Rides the Bus","subject":"english","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"1. Syllabus Verification & Textbook ConfirmationSyllabus Inclusion Status: Prescribed / Included12.Board: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER, Ajmer)34.Academic Session: 2026–202734.Subject: English (Subject Code: 02 / S-02)34.Prescribed Textbook: NCERT First Flight (Prose Section, Chapter 7 in Rationalized Edition / Chapter 9 or 10 in unrationalized editions)2.Chapter Title: Madam Rides the Bus2.Chapter ID: english-07Subject ID: englishAuthor: Vallikkannan (Translated from Tamil by K. S. Sundaram; Illustrated by R. K. Laxman)59.Lesson Type: Prose Short Story / Psychological & Inductive Narrative57.Mark Weightage Context: Section D (Literature — First Flight Prose) carries 15 Marks in total across extract comprehension passages (6 Marks), short answer questions (6 Marks), and extrapolative long answer prompts (3 Marks)3.","content":""},{"heading":"2. Chapter Metadata & Overview & OverviewMetadataChapter ID: english-07Title: Madam Rides the BusAuthor: Vallikkannan (Translated by K. S. Sundaram)5Illustrator: R. K. Laxman9Genre: Coming-of-Age Short Story / Realistic Fiction57Setting: A South Indian village and the 6-mile road link to the nearest town11Key Characters:Valliammai (\"Valli\" — an independent, curious 8-year-old girl)512The Bus Conductor (A jolly, joking, and empathetic man)13An Elderly Man (A concerned passenger who calls Valli a \"child\")12An Elderly Woman (A repulsive passenger who chews betel nut)1Valli''s Mother and her Aunt from South Street14OverviewMadam Rides the Bus is a sensitive coming-of-age story about an eight-year-old village girl named Valliammai (\"Valli\"), whose intense curiosity and self-reliance drive her to plan and execute her very first bus journey into the outside world entirely on her own5. Valli has no playmates her own age, so her favorite pastime is standing at the front door of her house to watch the street outside15. Her strongest desire becomes riding the town bus that passes through her street every hour15.Through meticulous listening, discreet questioning, and disciplined saving of every single coin (resisting candy, toys, and fair rides), Valli collects sixty paise for a round-trip ticket1116. She slips away during her mother''s afternoon nap, boards the afternoon bus, and conducts herself with dignified self-possession, earning the humorous title \"Madam\" from the jolly conductor1317.The journey provides Valli with immense delight—especially the sight of a young cow galloping frantically in front of the bus18. However, on her return journey, seeing that same cow lying dead by the roadside transforms her worldview19. This sudden encounter with mortality dampens her excitement, providing her first real understanding of the boundary between life and death5.","content":""},{"heading":"3. Detailed Section-Wise Explanations","content":""},{"heading":"Section 1: Valli''s Curiosity and the Overwhelming DesireValliammai is an eight-year-old girl who lives in a rural village5. Having no playmates of her own age on her street, her favorite pastime is standing in the doorway of her house to watch what happens in the street15. The most captivating sight for her is the passenger bus that travels between her village and the nearest town once every hour15. Watching new sets of passengers on every trip fills Valli with unending joy15. Over time, a tiny wish grows into an overwhelming desire: she simply must ride on that bus at least once15.Section 2: Meticulous Planning, Information Gathering, and SavingTo turn her dream into reality, Valli carefully listens to conversations between her neighbors and regular bus commuters, asking a few discreet questions11. She learns key details:The town is six miles away from her village11.The fare is thirty paise one way11.The bus ride takes forty-five minutes11.By taking the 1:00 PM afternoon bus, she can reach the town at 1:45 PM and return home on the same bus by 2:45 PM11.To save the 60-paise fare (30 paise for each way), Valli displays remarkable self-control1116. She thriftily saves every coin that comes her way, resisting all temptations to buy peppermints, toys, or balloons, and even refrains from riding the merry-go-round at the village fair16. She also plans to slip away during her mother''s daily afternoon nap between 1:00 PM and 4:00 PM21.Section 3: Boarding the Bus and Interactions with PassengersOn a fine spring day, Valli halts the 1:00 PM bus by raising her small hand commandingly22. When the jolly conductor offers a hand to help her up, Valli proudly refuses, declaring that she can get on by herself1317. Amused by her adult-like demeanor, the conductor humorously announces: \"Make way for madam!\"13.Inside the newly painted white and green bus, Valli sits down and marvels at the luxurious soft seats and the windshield clock23. When a canvas window blind blocks her view, she stands on her seat to peer over it23. An elderly male passenger expresses concern, warning her that standing could cause her to fall on sharp turns12. Valli haughtily snaps back that she is not a child—she is eight years old and has paid thirty paise like everyone else1224. Later, an elderly woman sits beside her, but Valli finds her repulsive due to her large earlobe holes, ugly earrings, and betel nut juice threatening to spill from her lips1.Section 4: The Hilarious Cow Episode and Town Station HaltDuring the journey to town, a young cow with its tail high in the air suddenly runs right in the middle of the road directly in front of the bus18. The driver honks repeatedly, but the loud sound frightens the cow further, causing it to gallop faster in front of the vehicle18. Valli finds this scene so hilarious that she laughs until tears stream from her eyes18.When the bus reaches the town terminal at 1:45 PM, all the passengers get off except Valli21. The conductor asks if she wants to get off and explore the town or have a cold drink at a stall, offering to pay for it himself1621. Valli politely but firmly declines, staying glued to her seat and handing him another thirty paise for her return ticket1621. This reveals her extraordinary caution, maturity, and focus on her objective16.Section 5: The Dead Cow, Emotional Realization, and Return HomeThe return journey begins with the same familiar, wonderful scenery1619. Suddenly, Valli spots a young cow lying dead by the roadside, struck by a fast-moving vehicle19. Recognizing it as the same joyful cow that had run before the bus earlier, Valli is struck with deep sorrow19. She reflects on how a creature so full of life and charm just minutes ago could suddenly look so horrible, frightening, and lifeless, with legs spreadeagled and blood scattered around19.This encounter with death deeply haunts her, dampening her enthusiasm20. She refuses to look out the window for the rest of the trip and sits quietly in her seat until the bus reaches her village at 3:40 PM20. Upon jumping off, she bids the smiling conductor goodbye and runs home20. She finds her mother awake and chatting with an aunt from South Street, entering silently without anyone realizing she had just completed a secret adventure outside her village1421.","content":""},{"heading":"4. Themes & Character AnalysisCore ThemesCuriosity, Independence, and Self-Reliance: Valli''s journey showcases how a child''s natural curiosity, when combined with planning and determination, fosters genuine independence5.Induction into the Mystery of Life and Death: The story contrasts the joy of life (the galloping cow) with the tragic reality of death (the dead cow), marking Valli''s psychological transition from childish innocence to mature understanding5.Childhood Dignity and Adult Prejudices: Valli rejects being treated as a helpless \"child\" or \"madam\", asserting her right to equal respect as a paying passenger12.Self-Control and Prudential Wisdom: Valli''s refusal to leave her seat in the town or accept a free drink from a stranger demonstrates remarkable prudence and self-protection1621.Character AnalysisValliammai (\"Valli\"):Traits: Independent, observant, resourceful, dignified, cautious, highly disciplined11.Role: Primary protagonist. She saves 60 paise through strict self-denial, plans her timing down to the minute, and handles adult interactions with confidence11. Her reaction to the dead cow reveals deep emotional sensitivity1920.The Bus Conductor:Traits: Jolly, fond of joking, kind-hearted, respectful, observant13.Role: Acts as a friendly facilitator of Valli''s journey. He humorously calls her \"madam\" to honor her independence and even offers to buy her a drink at the town bus stop13.The Repulsive Elderly Woman:Traits: Inquisitive, traditional, unsanitary habits125.Role: Serves as a contrast to Valli. Her betel-chewing and intrusive questioning prompt Valli to guard her privacy firmly125.","content":""},{"heading":"5. Important VocabularyWordContextual MeaningWistfullyLongingly; expressing vague or gentle desire15.KindleTo set on fire or spark (emotions, dreams, or hopes)15.DiscreetCareful, cautious, and circumspect in speech or action11.CommandinglyIn a dominant, authoritative, or decisive manner22.Slack timeA period of low activity or fewer passengers (e.g., afternoon)13.HaughtilyArrogantly or proudly24.DrivelSilly, meaningless, or annoying talk25.GapeTo stare in open-mouthed wonder or amazement21.SpreadeagledLying with arms and legs stretched out widely19.HauntedPermanently troubled or preoccupied by a disturbing memory20.","content":""},{"heading":"6. Verified Previous-Year & Official Model Paper Questions Questions & Official Board PapersOfficial RBSE Secondary Board Examination Paper 2024 (S-02-English)Extract Comprehension Passage Question (Section D — Prose) [6 Marks]2627Read the passage given below and answer the questions that follow:26\"Everyday after lunch her mother would nap from about one to four or so. Valli always used these hours for her ‘excursions’ as she stood looking from the doorway of her house or sometimes even ventured out into the village; today, these same hours could be used for her first excursion outside the village. The bus rolled on now cutting across a bare landscape, now rushing through a tiny hamlet or past an odd wayside shop. Sometimes the bus seemed on the point of gobbling up another vehicle that was coming towards them or a pedestrian crossing the road.\"26After saving money, the next problem was:27","content":"(A) bus\n(B) to slip out of house\n(C) aunt\n(D) driver\nAnswer:\n(B) to slip out of house27Valli stood looking from _______.27\n\n\n(A) terrace\n(B) kitchen\n(C) doorway\n(D) window\nAnswer:\n(C) doorway27Valli used the hours of mother’s nap in:27\n\n\n(A) Excursions\n(B) Games\n(C) Gossips\n(D) Talkings\nAnswer:\n(A) Excursions27Where did the bus roll on?27\n**Model Answer:** The bus rolled on cutting across a bare landscape, rushing through a tiny hamlet, or past an odd wayside shop26.What was gobbled up by the bus?27\n**Model Answer:** The bus seemed on the point of gobbling up another vehicle coming towards them or a pedestrian crossing the road26.How much did Valli save finally for the bus ride?27\n**Model Answer:** Valli finally saved a total of sixty paise for her round-trip bus ride1127.Short Answer Question (20–30 words) [2 Marks]2829\n\n**Question:** How did Valli gather the information about the bus journey? / How did Valli plan her bus ride? (Madam Rides the Bus)2829\n**Model Answer:** Valli gathered information by listening carefully to conversations between her neighbors and regular bus users, and by asking a few discreet questions11. She learned the distance (6 miles), fare (30 paise one-way), and duration (45 minutes)11."},{"heading":"7. Generated Practice Questions & Predicted QuestionsShort Answer Questions (20–30 words / 2 Marks Each)Why does the conductor call Valli ''madam''?","content":"**Model Answer:** The conductor calls Valli \"madam\" jocularly because she behaves like an independent, self-possessed adult, refusing his hand to board the bus and insisting she can manage on her own1317.Why didn''t Valli want to make friends with the elderly woman on the bus?\n**Model Answer:** Valli found the elderly woman repulsive because she had large holes in her earlobes with ugly earrings, was chewing betel nut, and betel juice threatened to spill over her lips1.Why didn''t Valli get off the bus at the town bus station?\n**Model Answer:** Valli did not get off because her sole purpose was to experience the bus ride21. She was cautious about wandering into an unfamiliar town alone and saved her seat for the return trip1621.What made Valli laugh until tears came to her eyes during her journey to town?\n**Model Answer:** Valli saw a young cow with its tail high in the air galloping frantically right in front of the bus18. The driver''s loud honking frightened the animal further, making it run faster, which amused Valli immensely18.Long Answer / Extrapolative Questions (60–80 words / 3–5 Marks Each)How did the sight of the dead cow mark a turning point in Valli''s perception of life?\n**Model Answer:** On her way to town, Valli was filled with joy watching a young cow run merrily in front of the bus18. On her return journey, seeing that same cow lying dead by the roadside struck her with profound grief19. She realized how a beautiful creature could instantly lose its life and charm, turning into a frightening, lifeless sight19. This sudden encounter with death dampened her enthusiasm, teaching her the tragic reality of mortality5.Describe Valli''s character traits highlighting her self-discipline and determination.\n**Model Answer:** Valli is an extraordinary eight-year-old girl who possesses intense determination and maturity1115. To fulfill her dream of riding a bus, she systematically gathers facts and thriftily saves sixty paise by sacrificing sweets, toys, and fair rides1116. During the trip, she maintains her dignity, guards her privacy, and refuses a free drink from a stranger, demonstrating exceptional self-restraint and practical wisdom16."},{"heading":"8. 15-Question MCQ QuizHow old was Valliammai (\"Valli\")?","content":"(A) Six years old\n(B) Eight years old\n(C) Ten years old\n(D) Twelve years old\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Valli was an eight-year-old girl who had no playmates her age515.What was Valli''s favorite pastime?\n\n\n(A) Playing games in the street\n(B) Standing in the front doorway of her house\n(C) Reading storybooks\n(D) Riding bicycles\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Her favorite pastime was standing at her front door watching the street15.What was the distance from Valli''s village to the nearest town?\n\n\n(A) Three miles\n(B) Six miles\n(C) Ten miles\n(D) Twelve miles\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The distance from her village to the town was six miles11.How much was the one-way bus fare to town?\n\n\n(A) Fifteen paise\n(B) Thirty paise\n(C) Fifty paise\n(D) Sixty paise\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The fare was thirty paise one way (sixty paise for a round trip)11.How long did the one-way bus journey take?\n\n\n(A) 30 minutes\n(B) 45 minutes\n(C) One hour\n(D) Two hours\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The trip to the town took forty-five minutes11.At what time did Valli take the afternoon bus?\n\n\n(A) 11:00 AM\n(B) 12:00 Noon\n(C) 1:00 PM\n(D) 2:00 PM\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: She took the 1:00 PM afternoon bus so she could return by 2:45 PM during her mother''s nap11.How did Valli save sixty paise for her bus fare?\n\n\n(A) By asking her mother for pocket money\n(B) By working at a shop\n(C) By resisting buying sweets, toys, and riding the merry-go-round\n(D) By borrowing from a friend\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: She thriftily saved every coin, resisting candy, toys, and the village fair merry-go-round16.Why did the conductor address Valli as ''madam''?\n\n\n(A) She was an adult woman\n(B) She acted independent, proud, and refused his assistance\n(C) She wore expensive clothes\n(D) He was mocking her age angrily\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He joked and called her \"madam\" because she refused his hand and acted mature1317.What blocked Valli''s view when she looked out the window?\n\n\n(A) A wooden board\n(B) A canvas blind covering the lower part of the window\n(C) Heavy rain curtains\n(D) Another passenger''s head\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: A canvas blind covered the bottom of her window, prompting her to stand on her seat23.Why did Valli find the elderly woman sitting beside her repulsive?\n\n\n(A) She was yelling at the driver\n(B) She had large earlobe holes, ugly earrings, and betel nut juice on her lips\n(C) She stole Valli''s ticket\n(D) She brought a loud dog on board\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The woman had big earlobe holes, ugly jewelry, and betel nut juice threatening to spill1.What sight on the way to town made Valli laugh until she cried?\n\n\n(A) A dog chasing a bicycle\n(B) A young cow galloping with its tail in the air right in front of the bus\n(C) The conductor singing a song\n(D) A monkey jumping onto the bus roof\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: A frightened young cow running in the middle of the road in front of the bus amused Valli immensely18.What did the conductor offer to buy Valli when the bus stopped in town?\n\n\n(A) A toy train\n(B) A cold drink from the stall\n(C) A newspaper\n(D) A peppermint\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He offered to fetch her a cold drink from a stall at no charge to her, which she refused1621.What sad event occurred on Valli''s return journey?\n\n\n(A) The bus broke down\n(B) She saw the same young cow lying dead by the roadside\n(C) She lost her ticket\n(D) Rain ruined the view\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: She saw the young cow struck dead by a fast-moving vehicle, which deeply saddened her19.At what time did the bus bring Valli back to her village?\n\n\n(A) 2:30 PM\n(B) 3:40 PM\n(C) 4:30 PM\n(D) 5:00 PM\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The bus reached her village at three forty in the afternoon20.Who translated \"Madam Rides the Bus\" from Tamil into English?\n\n\n(A) Gavin Maxwell\n(B) K. S. Sundaram\n(C) R. K. Laxman\n(D) Arup Kumar Datta\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The story was written by Vallikkannan and translated from Tamil by K. S. Sundaram5."},{"heading":"9. Syllabus Coverage Checklist & Source References & Source ReferencesCoverage Checklist[x] Official RBSE 2026–27 Syllabus Verification & Inclusion Confirmation34.[x] Prescribed NCERT Textbook (First Flight) Identification & Author Mapping2.[x] Detailed Plot Overview & Core Focus Areas (Valli''s planning, curiosity, independence, bus journey, interactions, changing understanding of life/death)5.[x] Detailed 5-Section Structural Breakdowns11.[x] Character and Thematic Analyses1.[x] Contextual Vocabulary Glossary13.[x] Authentic Past Paper (RBSE 2024 Code S-02) Comprehension Passage & Question Integration26.[x] Model Answers & Generated Practice Questions suitable for Class 1011.[x] 15-Question MCQ Quiz with Explanations5.Source ReferencesBoard of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE), Ajmer: Class 10 English Syllabus & Blueprint (Session 2026–27)34.NCERT, New Delhi: First Flight — Class 10 Textbook in English, Chapter 7 (Madam Rides the Bus by Vallikkannan)2.RBSE Official Secondary Board Examination Paper 2024: Code S-02-English (Passage Q.8 & SA Q.12)26.RBSE Official Curriculum & Assessment Research Report (2026–2027)30.Missing Information NoteIndividual Chapter Mark Allocation: The official RBSE blueprint assigns marks to Section D as a combined literature pool (15 Marks total for First Flight Prose across extracts, short answer, and long answer questions) rather than a fixed individual mark quota per chapter3.💡 Next Steps: Would you like me to prepare the BoardReady learning package for Chapter 8 (The Sermon at Benares) or Chapter 9 (The Proposal), or generate a consolidated flashcard set covering Chapters 6 and 7 of First Flight?keeppinSave to notekeeppincopythumbupthumbdown","content":""}]}'::jsonb,
