INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'english-8-notes',
  'english',
  'english-8',
  'The Sermon at Benares',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"The Sermon at Benares","subject":"english","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"1. Syllabus Verification & Textbook ConfirmationSyllabus Inclusion Status: Prescribed / Included.Board: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER, Ajmer).Academic Session: 2026–2027.Subject: English (Subject Code: 02 / S-02).Prescribed Textbook: NCERT First Flight (Prose Section, Chapter 8 in Rationalized Edition / Chapter 10 in older printings).Chapter Title: The Sermon at Benares.Chapter ID: english-08Subject ID: englishSource / Adaptation: Adapted from Betty Renshaw''s Values and Voices: A College Reader (1975).Lesson Type: Spiritual Narrative / Historical Prose / Sermon.Mark Weightage Context: Section D (Literature — First Flight Prose) carries 15 Marks in total across extract comprehension passages (6 Marks), short answer questions (6 Marks), and extrapolative long answer prompts (3 Marks).","content":""},{"heading":"2. Chapter Metadata & Overview & OverviewMetadataChapter ID: english-08Title: The Sermon at BenaresSource: Betty Renshaw (Values and Voices: A College Reader, 1975)Genre: Spiritual Literature / Historical Non-Fiction / Moral DiscourseSetting: Northern India (Kapilavastu, Bodh Gaya) and the holy city of Benares (Varanasi) on the River GangesKey Figures:Prince Siddhartha Gautama / Gautama Buddha (563 B.C. – 483 B.C.)Kisa Gotami (A grieving mother who loses her only son)OverviewThe Sermon at Benares narrates the spiritual transformation of Prince Siddhartha Gautama into the Buddha and records his first sermon delivered at Benares, the holy city on the banks of the River Ganges. Shielded from world suffering until age twenty-five, Prince Siddhartha encountered four sights—a sick man, an aged man, a funeral procession, and a begging monk—which moved him to renounce royal life in pursuit of spiritual truth. After seven years of wandering and seven days of deep meditation under a peepal tree (renamed the Bodhi Tree), he achieved enlightenment and became known as the Buddha (\"The Awakened One\").The core narrative illustrates Buddha''s wisdom through the parable of Kisa Gotami, whose only son had died. Unable to accept her loss, Kisa Gotami searched frantically for medicine to revive her child. A villager directed her to Sakyamuni Buddha. To teach her the inescapable reality of death, Buddha asked her to bring a handful of mustard seed from a household where no child, spouse, parent, or friend had ever died. As she went from door to door, every family shared stories of past loss, making her realize that death is universal. Watching the city lights flicker and extinguish at nightfall, she understood that human life is brief and that true peace comes from letting go of selfish grief.","content":""},{"heading":"3. Detailed Section-Wise Explanations","content":""},{"heading":"Section 1: Prince Siddhartha’s Renunciation and EnlightenmentSiddhartha Gautama (563 B.C. – 483 B.C.) was born as a prince in northern India. At age twelve, he was sent away to study Hindu sacred scriptures. Returning four years later, he married a princess, had a son, and lived ten years in royal luxury, completely shielded from worldly suffering.At age twenty-five, while out hunting, he witnessed four sights for the first time: a sick man, an aged man, a funeral procession, and a monk begging for alms. Deeply shaken by these sights of human sorrow, he abandoned his palace to seek enlightenment. After wandering for seven years, he sat under a peepal tree, vowing not to rise until enlightenment came. Achieving enlightenment after seven days, he named the tree the Bodhi Tree (\"Tree of Wisdom\") and became known as the Buddha. He delivered his first sermon at Benares, a sacred pilgrimage site on the River Ganges.Section 2: Kisa Gotami’s Grief and Search for MedicineKisa Gotami suffered the tragedy of losing her only son. Overwhelmed by grief, she carried the dead child to her neighbors, begging for medicine to bring him back to life. Seeing her distress, the neighbors believed she had lost her mind because the child was visibly dead. At length, a compassionate man informed her that while he had no medicine, he knew a physician who could help—Sakyamuni, the Buddha. Kisa Gotami immediately rushed to the Buddha, crying out for medicine to cure her boy.Section 3: The Task of the Mustard SeedBuddha agreed to help but set a specific condition: Kisa Gotami must bring him a handful of mustard seed. As she joyfully promised to procure it, Buddha added a crucial rule:\"The mustard-seed must be taken from a house where no one has lost a child, husband, parent or friend.\"Kisa Gotami traveled from house to house. People pitied her and willingly offered mustard seeds. However, whenever she asked if a family member had ever died in their house, they sorrowfully replied that the living were few, but the dead were many, pleading with her not to remind them of their deepest grief. She found no home that had not lost a loved one.Section 4: The Epiphany at the WaysideWeary and hopeless, Kisa Gotami sat down by the road at nightfall. She watched the city lights flicker up and extinguish into complete darkness. Observing this, she realized that human lives resemble these lights—they flicker briefly and are snuffed out. She reflected on her own grief and acknowledged her selfishness, realizing that death is common to all living beings. She understood that in a world of suffering, the path to immortality requires surrendering all selfishness.Section 5: The Buddha’s Sermon on Mortality and PeaceBuddha explained that human life is brief, troubled, and combined with pain. He presented two vivid analogies:Ripe Fruits: Just as ripe fruits are in constant danger of falling, mortals are always in danger of death.Earthen Vessels: Just as all clay pots made by a potter eventually break, all human lives end in death.Buddha emphasized that neither youth nor age, foolishness nor wisdom, can exempt anyone from death. Weeping and grieving cannot save the dead; they only cause bodily pain and sickness. True peace of mind is achieved by drawing out the \"arrow of lamentation, complaint, and grief.\" One who overcomes sorrow becomes composed, free from grief, and blessed.","content":""},{"heading":"4. Themes & Character AnalysisCore ThemesUniversality of Death and Impermanence: Death is an inescapable law of nature affecting every mortal being regardless of status, age, or wisdom.Transcendence of Selfish Grief: Personal grief becomes destructive when an individual treats their loss as unique. Recognizing that suffering is shared by all humanity allows one to rise above despair.The Path to Inner Peace: Peace of mind comes not from endless mourning or denial, but from drawing out the \"arrow of lamentation\" and accepting life''s natural terms.Inductive Teaching Method: Buddha does not lecture Kisa Gotami directly; instead, he assigns her a practical task that allows her to discover the truth about mortality through her own experience.Character & Spiritual ProfilesSiddhartha Gautama / The Buddha:Traits: Compassionate, enlightened, deeply observant, wise spiritual teacher.Role: Renounces royal comfort to find a solution to human suffering. Demonstrates profound empathy by guiding Kisa Gotami toward self-realization rather than giving abstract lectures.Kisa Gotami:Traits: Loving mother, blinded by grief, persistent, reflective, spiritually open.Role: Represents human grief and denial. Her journey from desperate despair to spiritual understanding serves as the central narrative arc of the sermon.","content":""},{"heading":"5. Important VocabularyWordContextual MeaningSermonA religious discourse or moral address delivered by a preacher.AlmsFood, money, or clothes given to poor people or monks.EnlightenmentA state of supreme spiritual knowledge and awakening.InscrutableImpossible to understand or interpret; mysterious.Repaired toWent to; visited a person or place (archaic usage).ProcureTo obtain or get something with care or effort.FlickeredShone with an unsteady, wavering light.DesolationA state of complete emptiness, grief, or loneliness.MortalsHuman beings bound to die eventually.KinsmenBlood relatives or family members.AfflictedDistressed or burdened with mental or physical suffering.LamentationPassionate expression of grief or sorrow; weeping.ComposedCalm, quiet, and in control of one''s emotions.","content":""},{"heading":"6. Verified Previous-Year & Official Model Paper Questions & Official Board PapersOfficial RBSE Board Examination Paper 2024 (S-02-English)Extract Comprehension Passage Question (Section D) [6 Marks]Read the passage given below and answer the questions that follow:\"Kisa Gotami had an only son and he died. In her grief she carried the dead child to all her neighbours, asking them for medicine and the people said, “She has lost her senses. The boy is dead.” At length, Kisa Gotami met a man who replied to her request. “I cannot give thee medicine for thy child, but I know a physician who can.” And the girl said, “Pray tell me, sir; who is it?” And the man replied to her request. “Go to Sakyamuni, the Buddha.” Kisa Gotami repaired to the Buddha and cried, “Lord and Master give me the medicine that will cure my boy.” The Buddha answered, “I want a handful of mustard-seeds”. And when the girl in her joy promised to procure it, the Buddha added, “The mustard-seeds must be taken from a house where no one has lost a child, husband, parent or friend.” Poor Kisa Gotami now went from house to house, and the people pitied her and said, “Here is mustard-seed; take it!” But when she asked, “Did a son or daughter, a father or mother, die in your family?” they answered her, “Alas! the living are few, but the dead are many. Do not remind us of our deepest grief.” And there was no house but some beloved one had died in it.\"How many sons did Kisa Gotami have?","content":"(A) One\n(B) Two\n(C) Three\n(D) Four\nAnswer:\n(A) OneWhat did Kisa Gotami ask for her dead child?\n\n\n(A) Milk\n(B) Chapati\n(C) Fruits\n(D) Medicine\nAnswer:\n(D) MedicineWho was Sakyamuni?\n\n\n(A) Kisa\n(B) Gotami\n(C) Buddha\n(D) Neighbours\nAnswer:\n(C) BuddhaWhat did Buddha want?\n**Model Answer:** Buddha wanted a handful of mustard seed taken from a house where no one had lost a child, husband, parent, or friend.Which question was asked by Kisa Gotami from the people?\n**Model Answer:** Kisa Gotami asked the villagers whether a son, daughter, father, or mother had died in their family.Write the similar word of the following from the passage: ''Heal''\n**Model Answer:** Cure.Extrapolative Long Answer Question (~80 words) [5 Marks]\n\n**Question:** How was prince Siddhartha transformed into Gautama Buddha? (The Sermon at Benares)\n**Model Answer:** Prince Siddhartha lived a shielded royal life until age twenty-five. While hunting, he encountered a sick man, an aged man, a funeral procession, and a begging monk. Deeply moved by these sights of suffering, he renounced his kingdom, family, and wealth to seek enlightenment. He wandered for seven years and finally sat under a peepal tree at Bodh Gaya, meditating for seven days until he attained spiritual awakening, thus transforming into Gautama Buddha.Official RBSE Secondary Model Examination Paper 2026 (sec_model2026.pdf)Short Answer Question (~60 words) [3 Marks]\n\n**Question:** What is known about the first sermon of Buddha? (The Sermon at Benares)\n**Model Answer:** The Buddha preached his first sermon at the holy city of Benares on the River Ganges. The sermon reflects his wisdom on human suffering and mortality. Through the story of Kisa Gotami, it teaches that death is universal and inevitable, and that peace of mind can only be attained by overcoming selfish grief and accepting the terms of the world."},{"heading":"7. Generated Practice Questions & Predicted QuestionsShort Answer Questions (20–30 words / 2 Marks Each)Why did Kisa Gotami go from house to house after meeting the Buddha?","content":"**Model Answer:** She went from house to house to collect a handful of mustard seed from a home where no one had ever lost a child, husband, parent, or friend.What did Kisa Gotami realize while watching the city lights at nightfall?\n**Model Answer:** Watching the city lights flicker up and extinguish, she realized that human lives are similar—they flicker briefly and are put out. She acknowledged her selfishness in grief, understanding that death is common to all.What analogies does Buddha use to explain the inevitability of human death?\n**Model Answer:** Buddha compares mortals to ripe fruits that are always in danger of falling, and to earthen vessels made by a potter that ultimately end in being broken.According to Buddha, how can a person obtain true peace of mind?\n**Model Answer:** True peace of mind is obtained by drawing out the \"arrow of lamentation, complaint, and grief,\" controlling sorrow, and becoming composed and free from grief.Long Answer / Extrapolative Questions (60–80 words / 3–5 Marks Each)Why did Buddha choose the mustard seed task to teach Kisa Gotami about death instead of lecturing her directly?\n**Model Answer:** Buddha understood that abstract lectures on death would not comfort a grieving mother in deep sorrow. By giving her the practical task of finding mustard seed from a household untouched by death, he allowed her to interact with grieving families firsthand. Experiencing their shared losses helped her realize that death is a universal human reality, leading her to overcome her selfish grief naturally.Discuss the central message of \"The Sermon at Benares\" regarding human grief and mortality.\n**Model Answer:** The chapter conveys that death and decay are inescapable terms of human life. Weeping and mourning cannot bring the dead back to life; instead, they cause physical illness and distress. Buddha teaches that grieving insulates people in their own pain. To achieve true peace of mind, one must accept mortality, surrender selfishness, and pull out the arrow of lamentation."},{"heading":"8. 15-Question MCQ QuizAt what age was Siddhartha Gautama sent away for schooling in sacred scriptures?","content":"(A) 10 years old\n(B) 12 years old\n(C) 16 years old\n(D) 25 years old\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: At twelve, Siddhartha was sent away to study Hindu sacred scriptures.At what age did Prince Siddhartha first encounter the sight of human suffering?\n\n\n(A) 16 years old\n(B) 20 years old\n(C) 25 years old\n(D) 30 years old\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: At about twenty-five, while hunting, he witnessed suffering for the first time.Which of the following was NOT one of the four sights seen by Siddhartha?\n\n\n(A) A sick man\n(B) An aged man\n(C) A blind child\n(D) A monk begging for alms\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: He saw a sick man, an aged man, a funeral procession, and a begging monk.For how many years did Siddhartha wander before sitting under the Peepal tree?\n\n\n(A) Five years\n(B) Seven years\n(C) Ten years\n(D) Twelve years\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He wandered for seven years seeking enlightenment.After how many days of meditation did Siddhartha attain enlightenment?\n\n\n(A) 3 days\n(B) 7 days\n(C) 14 days\n(D) 40 days\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He attained spiritual awakening after meditating for seven days.What name did Buddha give to the Peepal tree where he gained enlightenment?\n\n\n(A) Tree of Peace\n(B) Bodhi Tree (Tree of Wisdom)\n(C) Tree of Life\n(D) Sacred Tree\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He renamed it the Bodhi Tree (\"Tree of Wisdom\").Where did Buddha preach his first sermon?\n\n\n(A) Kapilavastu\n(B) Gaya\n(C) Benares\n(D) Sarnath\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: Buddha preached his first sermon at the city of Benares on the River Ganges.What happened to Kisa Gotami''s child?\n\n\n(A) He fell sick\n(B) He died\n(C) He went missing\n(D) He was injured\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Kisa Gotami''s only son had died.What did Kisa Gotami ask her neighbors for?\n\n\n(A) Food\n(B) Money\n(C) Medicine for her dead child\n(D) Clothes\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: Driven by grief, she went to neighbors asking for medicine to revive her child.What condition did Buddha set for the mustard seed?\n\n\n(A) It must be fresh\n(B) It must come from a house where no one had died\n(C) It must be grown in Benares\n(D) It must be given by a king\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: It had to come from a house where no child, husband, parent, or friend had died.What response did Kisa Gotami receive from the villagers?\n\n\n(A) They refused to give her mustard seed\n(B) They said \"the living are few, but the dead are many\"\n(C) They laughed at her\n(D) They chased her away\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: People offered mustard seed, but reminded her that the dead outnumber the living.What did Kisa Gotami watch while sitting by the road at nightfall?\n\n\n(A) Passing river boats\n(B) City lights flickering up and being extinguished\n(C) Stars in the sky\n(D) Travellers walking past\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: She sat by the wayside watching city lights flicker and extinguish.To what does Buddha compare mortals when speaking of death?\n\n\n(A) Falling leaves\n(B) Ripe fruits in danger of falling\n(C) Melting snow\n(D) Fading flowers\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Buddha compares mortals to ripe fruits that are in early danger of falling.To what does Buddha compare human life regarding the potter''s work?\n\n\n(A) Earthen vessels that end in being broken\n(B) Clay that dries in the sun\n(C) Water stored in a pot\n(D) Wheels turning continuously\nAnswer:\n(A) | Explanation: He notes that all earthen vessels made by a potter end in being broken.According to Buddha, what must a person draw out to find peace of mind?\n\n\n(A) The sword of anger\n(B) The arrow of lamentation, complaint, and grief\n(C) The fear of failure\n(D) The desire for wealth\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Buddha teaches that one must draw out the \"arrow of lamentation, complaint, and grief.\""},{"heading":"9. Syllabus Coverage Checklist & Source References & Source ReferencesCoverage Checklist[x] Official RBSE 2026–27 Syllabus Verification & Inclusion Confirmation.[x] Prescribed NCERT Textbook (First Flight) Identification & Source Mapping.[x] Detailed Overview & Core Focus Areas (Kisa Gotami, Buddha''s teaching, suffering, death, acceptance, universality of loss).[x] Detailed 5-Section Structural Breakdowns.[x] Character and Spiritual Analyses.[x] Contextual Vocabulary Glossary.[x] Authentic Past Paper (RBSE 2024 Code S-02) Comprehension Passage & Question Integration.[x] Model Answers & Generated Practice Questions suitable for Class 10.[x] 15-Question MCQ Quiz with Explanations.Source ReferencesBoard of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE), Ajmer: Class 10 English Syllabus & Blueprint (Session 2026–27).NCERT, New Delhi: First Flight — Class 10 Textbook in English, Chapter 8 (The Sermon at Benares).RBSE Official Secondary Board Examination Paper 2024: Code S-02-English (Passage Q.11 & LA Q.13(ii)).RBSE Official Secondary Model Examination Paper 2026: secmodel2026.pdf (SA Q.13(i)).Missing Information NoteIndividual Chapter Mark Allocation: The official RBSE blueprint assigns marks to Section D as a combined literature pool (15 Marks total for First Flight Prose overall across extracts, short answer, and long answer questions) rather than a fixed individual mark quota per chapter.💡 Next Steps: Would you like me to create the BoardReady package for the final prose lesson (The Proposal by Anton Chekhov) or the attached poem (For Anne Gregory by W. B. Yeats)?keeppinSave to notekeeppincopythumbupthumbdown","content":""}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'english-9-notes',
  'english',
  'english-9',
  'The Proposal',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"The Proposal","subject":"english","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"1. Syllabus Verification & Textbook ConfirmationSyllabus Inclusion Status: Prescribed / Included12.Board: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER, Ajmer)12.Academic Session: 2026–202712.Subject: English (Subject Code: 02 / S-02)12.Prescribed Textbook: NCERT First Flight (Drama / One-Act Play Section, Chapter 9 in 2026–27 Rationalized Edition / Chapter 11 in older printings)23.Chapter Title: The Proposal (originally titled A Marriage Proposal)34.Chapter ID: english-09Subject ID: englishAuthor: Anton Chekhov (Translated from Russian)34.Lesson Type: One-Act Play / Farce / Drama Text4.Mark Weightage Context: Section D (Literature — First Flight Drama & Prose) includes 1 dedicated Short/Long Answer Question (3 Marks) specifically testing the Drama Text (The Proposal), alongside potential passage or MCQ items in the literature paper pool5.","content":""},{"heading":"2. Chapter Metadata & Overview & OverviewMetadataChapter ID: english-09Title: The ProposalAuthor: Anton Chekhov4Genre: One-Act Play / Farce / Satirical Comedy4Setting: Living room in Stepan Stepanovitch Chubukov''s country house in 19th-century Russia4Key Characters:Stepan Stepanovitch Chubukov: A wealthy Russian landowner4.Natalya Stepanovna: Chubukov''s 25-year-old daughter4.Ivan Vassilevitch Lomov: Chubukov''s 35-year-old neighbor, a wealthy, suspicious, and highly hypochondriac landowner4.OverviewThe Proposal is a humorous one-act farce written by Russian playwright Anton Chekhov4. The play satirizes the mercenary nature of marriage among the 19th-century Russian landowning gentry, where marriage was treated primarily as an economic transaction to merge adjacent estates rather than an alliance of romantic love4.The plot centers on Ivan Vassilevitch Lomov, who arrives at the estate of his wealthy neighbor, Stepan Stepanovitch Chubukov, dressed in formal evening clothes to seek the hand of Chubukov''s 25-year-old daughter, Natalya48. Although Chubukov is delighted by the prospect of a profitable alliance, the actual proposal is repeatedly delayed and nearly derailed by petty quarrels4.First, Lomov and Natalya engage in a heated dispute over the ownership of Oxen Meadows, a small strip of land wedged between their properties910. After Lomov collapses from severe heart palpitations and leaves in anger, Natalya learns he came to propose and hysterically demands his return1112. However, as soon as he returns, a second fiery argument breaks out over whose hunting dog is superior—Lomov''s Guess or Natalya''s Squeezer1314. The play reaches a climax when Lomov collapses unconscious from stress15. Fearing he is dead, Chubukov frantically shoves Natalya''s hand into Lomov''s, forcing a formal engagement the moment Lomov revives, while the new couple immediately resume their argument over their dogs1516.","content":""},{"heading":"3. Detailed Section-Wise Explanations","content":""},{"heading":"Scene 1: Lomov’s Arrival and Chubukov’s Initial SuspicionIvan Vassilevitch Lomov arrives at the country house of his neighbor, Stepan Stepanovitch Chubukov, dressed in formal attire consisting of a dress jacket and white gloves811. Chubukov greets him warmly but secretly suspects that Lomov has come to borrow money (\"He’s come to borrow money. Shan’t give him any!\")8.When Lomov stammers nervously, Chubukov urges him to speak out8. Lomov reveals that he has come to ask for the hand of Chubukov''s daughter, Natalya Stepanovna, in marriage8. Overjoyed, Chubukov embraces and kisses Lomov, admitting he has always wished for this match17. Chubukov exits to call Natalya, remarking that she is \"like a lovesick cat\" and will surely consent17.Scene 2: Lomov’s Soliloquy on Marriage and HealthLeft alone, Lomov experiences intense nervous trembling and heart palpitations17. He delivers a soliloquy explaining his pragmatic reasons for marrying417:He is thirty-five years old—a \"critical age\"417.He leads an unstable life troubled by severe hypochondria, muscle spasms, twitching eyebrows, and sudden numbness in his left side1217.He requires a quiet, regular, and settled domestic life417.Natalya Stepanovna is an ideal match because she is an excellent housekeeper, educated, and not bad-looking18.Scene 3: The First Conflict — Dispute Over Oxen MeadowsNatalya enters in an apron and house-dress, welcoming Lomov casually9. Lomov attempts to lead up to his marriage proposal by emphasizing the historical friendship between their families and mentioning that his land touches hers—specifically that his \"Oxen Meadows\" touch her birchwoods9.Natalya immediately interrupts, claiming that Oxen Meadows belong to the Chubukovs, not the Lomovs10. Lomov argues that his aunt''s grandmother gave the temporary free use of the Meadows to the peasants of Natalya''s father''s grandfather in exchange for making bricks19. Natalya rejects his legal explanations, asserting that her family has owned the land for nearly three hundred years20.Chubukov enters and sides with his daughter, calling Lomov a \"landgrabber\" and \"pettifogger\"2122. The argument escalates into shouting and personal insults2223. Overwhelmed by excruciating heart palpitations, Lomov threatens to take the matter to court and staggers out of the house11.Scene 4: The Revelation and Natalya’s HystericsAfter Lomov leaves, Chubukov and Natalya continue to insult him, calling him a \"scarecrow,\" \"villain,\" and \"stuffed sausage\"11. In his anger, Chubuko","content":"<truncated 415881 bytes>\n\nNOTE: The output was truncated because it was too long. Use a more targeted query or a smaller range to get the information you need.\n\nIn his anger, Chubukov lets slip that Lomov had actually come with a formal proposal of marriage for Natalya. The moment Natalya hears that Lomov came to propose, her rage instantly turns into frantic despair. She falls into hysterics, groaning and weeping, demanding that her father immediately bring Lomov back: \"Bring him back! Back! Ah! Bring him here! Fetch him!\" Chubukov clutches his head in exasperation, lamenting the burden of being the father of a grown-up daughter. He rushes out into the courtyard and drags a pale, staggering Lomov back inside."},{"heading":"Scene 5: The Second Dispute — Guess versus Squeezer","content":"Lomov re-enters, weak and clutching his pounding chest, complaining of twitching in his side. To soothe him and prevent another quarrel, Natalya hastily concedes ownership of the Oxen Meadows: \"The Meadows are yours, Ivan Vassilevitch, they are yours! Do sit down... We were wrong.\"\n\nHoping to keep the conversation pleasant, Natalya asks if he plans to go hunting soon. Lomov remarks that he intends to hunt blackcock after the harvest, but laments that his best dog, Guess, has gone lame. When Natalya asks how much he paid, Lomov replies that he paid 125 roubles to Mironov. Natalya laughs scornfully, insisting that 125 roubles was far too much, boasting that her father bought their dog Squeezer for only 85 roubles, and that Squeezer is vastly superior to Guess.\n\nLomov takes deep offense, asserting that Squeezer is \"pug-jawed\" (his lower jaw is shorter than his upper), which severely impairs his grip. Natalya retorts that Squeezer is purebred and young, whereas Guess is old, blind, and as ugly as a worn-out cab-horse. The argument erupts into a furious shouting match with accusations of lies and insults flying across the room."},{"heading":"Scene 6: Lomov’s Collapse and the Forced Engagement","content":"Chubukov re-enters the living room and immediately joins the quarrel, siding aggressively with his daughter. He claims that Squeezer has the finest pedigree in the district while Guess has no stamina and was left far behind by the Count’s dog during the last hunt. Lomov counters that Chubukov’s dog was whipped by the Count’s whipper-in because he was chasing sheep instead of foxes.\n\nInsults escalate rapidly: Lomov accuses Chubukov of being an intriguer, while Chubukov calls Lomov an impudent puppy and his family a line of embezzlers. Overwhelmed by agonizing palpitations, numbness in his limbs, and blurred vision, Lomov gasps: \"My heart... stars before my eyes... Where am I?\" and collapses motionless into an armchair.\n\nSeeing Lomov unresponsive, Natalya panics: \"He''s dead! He''s dead! Doctor, doctor!\" Chubukov fears Lomov has died in his house and that he will be blamed. Desperately looking for water, he splashes Lomov''s face, murmuring that he ought to shoot himself or cut his throat with a razor.\n\nLomov slowly twitches and murmurs: \"Where am I?\" Chubukov wastes not a single second. Seizing Natalya’s hand, he thrusts it forcefully into Lomov’s hand and shouts:\n\"She''s willing! Well, kiss and be damned to you! Hurry up and get married! She’s willing, I give you my blessing!\"\n\nNatalya cries: \"Yes, yes, I''m willing! Kiss each other!\" Lomov, still dazed, kisses her hand and says: \"I understand... My heart... I''m happy, Natalya Stepanovna.\"\nNatalya embraces him, but instantly adds: \"Still, you must admit now that Guess is worse than Squeezer!\"\nLomov retorts obstinately: \"Better!\"\nNatalya yells: \"Worse!\"\nChubukov claps his hands to drown out their shouting, calling out to the servants: \"Champagne! Bring champagne! What a weight off my shoulders!\"\nCurtain falls on their continuing quarrel."},{"heading":"4. Themes & Character Analysis","content":"Core Themes\n- The Mercenary Nature of Bourgeois Marriage: Marriage among the 19th-century Russian landowning gentry was an economic transaction to consolidate adjacent estates rather than an affair of the heart.\n- Petty Vanity and Obstinate Pride: Despite both wanting the marriage desperately, the characters cannot surrender trivial arguments over worthless parcels of land and hunting dogs.\n- Farce and Satire of Upper-Class Hypocrisy: Chekhov uses exaggerated physical comedy, sudden shifts from rage to romantic bliss, and constant insults to mock human pettiness.\n\nCharacter Analysis\n1. Ivan Vassilevitch Lomov:\n- Traits: Anxious, suspicious, hypocritical, hypochondriac (obsessed with imagined heart trouble, twitching eyelids, and insomnia), yet proud and obstinate.\n- Significance: Symbolizes the neurotic decay of the Russian gentry. He seeks marriage not for passion, but for domestic comfort and social duty.\n\n2. Natalya Stepanovna:\n- Traits: Shrewd, obstinate, domineering, sharp-tongued, but desperate to get married at age 25.\n- Significance: Quickly switches from furious hostility over property to desperate longing the moment a marriage proposal is at stake.\n\n3. Stepan Stepanovitch Chubukov:\n- Traits: Flamboyant, opportunistic, quick-tempered, hypocritical, yet practical patriarch.\n- Significance: Eager to marry off his daughter to a wealthy neighbor; ready to force their hands together to seal the deal before Lomov collapses again."},{"heading":"5. Important Vocabulary","content":"- Hypochondriac: A person who is abnormally anxious about their health.\n- Palpitation: Rapid, irregular heartbeat caused by agitation or exertion.\n- Pettifogger: A lawyer or person who deals in petty or underhanded methods.\n- Pug-jawed: Having an underhung or shorter lower jaw than the upper jaw.\n- Intriguer: A person who devises secret plots or schemes.\n- Thoroughbred: An animal of pure breed."},{"heading":"6. Verified Previous-Year & Official Model Paper Questions & Official Board Questions","content":"Official RBSE Board Examination Paper 2024 (S-02-English)\n\n**Question:** Describe the character sketch of Lomov in about 80 words. [5 Marks]\n**Model Answer:** Ivan Vassilevitch Lomov is a 35-year-old wealthy landowner who lives next door to Chubukov. He is a chronic hypochondriac plagued by nervous palpitations, insomnia, and muscle twitches. He decides to propose to Natalya not out of romantic passion, but because he feels 35 is a critical age and he needs a settled, quiet domestic life. Despite his nervous condition, he is exceptionally proud, suspicious, and argumentative, quarreling furiously over Oxen Meadows and hunting dogs.\n\nOfficial RBSE Secondary Model Paper 2026\n\n**Question:** Why did Chubukov shove Natalya''s hand into Lomov''s? [3 Marks]\n**Model Answer:** Chubukov shoved Natalya''s hand into Lomov''s because he was terrified that another quarrel would cause Lomov to collapse and die, ruining the profitable marriage match. To secure the alliance before any more arguments occurred, he forced a hasty verbal engagement the moment Lomov regained consciousness."},{"heading":"7. Generated Practice Questions","content":"Short Answer Questions (20–30 words / 2 Marks Each)\n1. Why does Lomov visit Chubukov in formal evening dress?\n**Model Answer:** Lomov visits Chubukov in a dress-jacket and white gloves to make a formal proposal of marriage for Chubukov''s daughter, Natalya Stepanovna.\n\n2. What causes the first major argument between Lomov and Natalya?\n**Model Answer:** The argument starts over the ownership of Oxen Meadows, a small patch of land wedged between Lomov''s birchwoods and the Burnt Marsh, which both families claim to own.\n\n3. How does Natalya react when she learns Lomov came to propose?\n**Model Answer:** Natalya becomes hysterical, weeping and wailing, reproaching her father for insulting Lomov and demanding that he immediately bring Lomov back.\n\nLong Answer Question (60–80 words / 3–5 Marks)\nDiscuss the humor and irony in the play \"The Proposal\".\n**Model Answer:** The humor arises from the absurd contrast between a formal marriage proposal and the explosive petty quarrels that interrupt it. Both Lomov and Natalya desperately desire the marriage, yet they cannot resist arguing violently over a worthless meadow and whose hunting dog is faster. The supreme irony occurs at the climax: even while accepting the proposal with a kiss, the newly betrothed couple instantly resumes their heated argument over Guess and Squeezer, while Chubukov shouts for champagne."},{"heading":"8. 15-Question MCQ Quiz","content":"1. Who is the author of \"The Proposal\"?\n(A) Guy de Maupassant\n(B) Anton Chekhov\n(C) Gavin Maxwell\n(D) Liam O''Flaherty\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: \"The Proposal\" is a one-act farce by Anton Chekhov.\n\n\n\n2. What is Lomov''s age in the play?\n(A) 25 years\n(B) 30 years\n(C) 35 years\n(D) 40 years\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: Lomov states he is 35 years old, a \"critical age\".\n\n\n\n3. How old is Natalya Stepanovna?\n(A) 20 years\n(B) 25 years\n(C) 28 years\n(D) 32 years\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Chubukov mentions Natalya is 25 years old.\n\n\n\n4. What attire was Lomov wearing when he visited Chubukov?\n(A) Military uniform\n(B) Dress-jacket and white gloves\n(C) Peasant cloak\n(D) Nightgown\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Lomov arrived in evening dress with white gloves.\n\n\n\n5. What was the subject of the first dispute between Lomov and Natalya?\n(A) Oxen Meadows\n(B) Squeezer vs Guess\n(C) The price of wheat\n(D) Dowry amount\nAnswer:\n(A) | Explanation: The initial argument was over ownership of Oxen Meadows.\n\n\n\n6. What did Chubukov initially think Lomov had come for?\n(A) To buy horses\n(B) To borrow money\n(C) To invite him to a hunt\n(D) To propose marriage\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Chubukov suspected Lomov came to borrow money.\n\n\n\n7. Who gave Oxen Meadows for temporary free use according to Lomov?\n(A) His grandfather\n(B) His aunt''s grandmother\n(C) The Russian Tsar\n(D) The village priest\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Lomov argued his aunt''s grandmother gave it to Chubukov''s peasants.\n\n\n\n8. For how long did Natalya claim her family owned the Meadows?\n(A) 50 years\n(B) 100 years\n(C) Nearly 300 years\n(D) 500 years\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: Natalya asserted her family owned it for nearly 300 years.\n\n\n\n9. How much did Lomov pay for his dog Guess?\n(A) 85 roubles\n(B) 100 roubles\n(C) 125 roubles\n(D) 150 roubles\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: Lomov paid 125 roubles to Mironov for Guess.\n\n\n\n10. How much did Chubukov pay for Squeezer?\n(A) 50 roubles\n(B) 85 roubles\n(C) 125 roubles\n(D) 150 roubles\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Chubukov bought Squeezer for 85 roubles.\n\n\n\n11. What anatomical defect did Lomov point out in Squeezer?\n(A) He was blind in one eye\n(B) He was lame\n(C) He was pug-jawed\n(D) He had no tail\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: Lomov claimed Squeezer was pug-jawed.\n\n\n\n12. What physical ailment plagued Lomov throughout the play?\n(A) Fever\n(B) Heart palpitations and muscle spasms\n(C) Asthma\n(D) Gout\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Lomov suffered from nervous heart palpitations.\n\n\n\n13. What did Chubukov call Lomov during the heated argument?\n(A) A scoundrel and pettifogger\n(B) A prince\n(C) A gentle neighbor\n(D) An angel\nAnswer:\n(A) | Explanation: Chubukov insulted Lomov with names like pettifogger and grabber.\n\n\n\n14. What does Chubukov do when Lomov revives from fainting?\n(A) Calls the police\n(B) Joins their hands and blesses the marriage\n(C) Throws water at him again\n(D) Drives him out\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Chubukov hastily thrusts Natalya''s hand into Lomov''s.\n\n\n\n15. What do Lomov and Natalya do immediately after agreeing to marry?\n(A) Dance together\n(B) Resume arguing over whose dog is better\n(C) Thank Chubukov\n(D) Sign the wedding contract\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: They immediately begin bickering over Guess and Squeezer."},{"heading":"9. Syllabus Coverage Checklist & Source References & Source References","content":"Coverage Checklist:\n[x] Official RBSE 2026–27 Syllabus Verification & Inclusion Confirmation\n[x] Prescribed NCERT Textbook (First Flight) Identification & Author Mapping\n[x] Detailed Plot Overview & Core Focus Areas (Marriage negotiation, land, dogs, farce)\n[x] Detailed 6-Scene Structural Breakdown\n[x] Character & Thematic Farce Analysis\n[x] Contextual Vocabulary Glossary\n[x] Authentic Past Paper (RBSE 2024 Code S-02) & Model Paper Integration\n[x] Model Answers & Generated Practice Questions suitable for Class 10\n[x] 15-Question MCQ Quiz with Explanations"}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'english-10-notes',
  'english',
  'english-10',
  'Dust of Snow & Fire and Ice',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Dust of Snow & Fire and Ice","subject":"english","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"1. Syllabus Verification & Textbook Confirmation","content":"- Syllabus Inclusion Status: Prescribed / Included\n- Board: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER, Ajmer)\n- Prescribed Textbook: NCERT First Flight (Poetry Section)\n- Poems: \"Dust of Snow\" & \"Fire and Ice\" by Robert Frost\n- Mark Weightage: Section D (Poetry extracts + Short Answer questions = 8 Marks)"},{"heading":"2. Chapter Metadata & Overview & Overview","content":"- Metadata: Chapter ID: english-10, Subject ID: english, Author: Robert Frost\n- Genre: Modern American Lyric Poetry / Symbolic Verse\n- Setting: A winter landscape with a poisonous hemlock tree (Dust of Snow); Speculative philosophical inquiry into the end of humanity (Fire and Ice)\n\nOverview:\n\"Dust of Snow\" is a brief 8-line lyric that captures how a small, seemingly insignificant natural occurrence can lift an individual out of depressive melancholy. Robert Frost uses unconventional symbols—a crow and a poisonous hemlock tree—to illustrate that nature''s healing touch can come through the most unexpected agents, saving a ruined day.\n\n\"Fire and Ice\" is a 9-line philosophical poem meditating on two apocalyptic forces that could annihilate humankind. Frost equates ''Fire'' with uncontrolled human desire, passion, greed, and lust, while equating ''Ice'' with cold hatred, indifference, cruelty, and rigidity. The poet warns that either emotion, if left unchecked, is sufficient to destroy the world."},{"heading":"3. Detailed Section-Wise Explanations","content":""},{"heading":"Section 1: Dust of Snow — Stanza-by-Stanza Analysis","content":"- Stanza 1: \"The way a crow / Shook down on me / The dust of snow / From a hemlock tree\"\n  The speaker is in a sad, gloomy state of mind, standing under a hemlock tree (a poisonous plant with white flowers). A crow lands on or takes off from a snow-covered branch, causing fine particles of powdery snow (\"dust of snow\") to fall on the poet.\n- Stanza 2: \"Has given my heart / A change of mood / And saved some part / Of a day I had rued.\"\n  The sudden, cold physical sensation acts as an emotional reset. It jolts the poet out of regretful sorrow (\"rued\" = held in regret) and brings a fresh feeling of gratitude, preserving the rest of his day."},{"heading":"Section 2: Fire and Ice — Stanza-by-Stanza Analysis","content":"- Stanza 1: \"Some say the world will end in fire, / Some say in ice. / From what I''ve tasted of desire / I hold with those who favor fire.\"\n  The speaker acknowledges the scientific and theological debate over the world''s end. Drawing from personal experience with human passion and desire, he favors the idea that fiery passions like greed and lust have the explosive power to consume humanity.\n- Stanza 2: \"But if it had to perish twice, / I think I know enough of hate / To say that for destruction ice / Is also great / And would suffice.\"\n  The poet introduces a chilling alternative. If the world had to die twice, cold hatred and emotional detachment (\"ice\") are equally potent and silent killers. Hate builds cold walls between people and is more than enough (\"suffice\") to end civilization.\n\n4. Poetic Devices & Thematic Analysis\n- Rhyme Scheme:\n  - Dust of Snow: ABAB CDCD\n  - Fire and Ice: ABA ABC BCB\n- Key Devices:\n  - Symbolism: Crow (bad omen repurposed as healer), Hemlock (toxic plant providing renewal), Fire (passion/greed), Ice (hate/indifference).\n  - Alliteration: \"Has given my heart\", \"And saved some part\", \"favor fire\".\n  - Enjambment: Thought continues across line breaks without punctuation.\n  - Irony: Inauspicious creatures bringing joy; understated brevity conveying apocalyptic catastrophe."},{"heading":"5. Important Vocabulary","content":"- Hemlock: A poisonous plant with small white flowers.\n- Rued: Held in sorrow or regret; lamented.\n- Perish: Die, decay, or be destroyed completely.\n- Suffice: Be enough or adequate."},{"heading":"6. Verified Previous-Year & Official Model Paper Questions & Official Board Questions","content":"Official RBSE 2024 Paper (S-02-English)\n\n**Question:** What do ''fire'' and ''ice'' stand for in Robert Frost''s poem? [2 Marks]\n**Model Answer:** In Robert Frost''s poem, ''fire'' symbolizes burning human desires, greed, lust, and uncontrolled passion. ''Ice'' symbolizes cold hatred, indifference, rigid cruelty, and emotional detachment.\n\nOfficial RBSE Model Paper 2026\n\n**Question:** How has the poet observed nature in \"Dust of Snow\"? [2 Marks]\n**Model Answer:** The poet rejects traditional conventional symbols of beauty like cuckoos or roses. Instead, he selects a crow and a toxic hemlock tree to show that nature in all its forms has the power to heal human sadness and bring emotional renewal."},{"heading":"7. Generated Practice Questions","content":"Short Answer (2 Marks):\n\n**Question:** What mood change occurs in \"Dust of Snow\"?\n**Model Answer:** The poet begins in a depressed, sorrowful mood, regretting his day. The unexpected falling of snow particles caused by a crow transforms his mood into lightness, optimism, and joy.\n\nLong Answer (3 Marks):\n\n**Question:** Discuss how Frost balances passion and hatred as destructive forces in \"Fire and Ice\".\n**Model Answer:** Frost balances the two destructive emotions by presenting fire as explosive and passionate, while ice is silent, creeping, and stubborn. Both originate inside the human psyche. Greed and lust (fire) can trigger rapid warfare and chaos, while hatred and apathy (ice) freeze human empathy, making both equally capable of destroying civilization."},{"heading":"8. 15-Question MCQ Quiz","content":"1. Who wrote \"Dust of Snow\" and \"Fire and Ice\"?\n(A) Robert Frost\n(B) Leslie Norris\n(C) Walt Whitman\n(D) Robin Klein\nAnswer:\n(A) | Explanation: Both poems are by American poet Robert Frost.\n\n\n2. What tree is mentioned in \"Dust of Snow\"?\n(A) Banyan\n(B) Hemlock\n(C) Oak\n(D) Peepal\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: A poisonous hemlock tree with white flowers is mentioned.\n\n\n3. Which bird shook the snow down?\n(A) Pigeon\n(B) Sparrow\n(C) Crow\n(D) Eagle\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: A crow shook the snow down on the poet.\n\n\n4. What does the \"dust of snow\" represent?\n(A) Cold raindrops\n(B) Tiny particles of snow\n(C) Frost leaves\n(D) Ice blocks\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Dust of snow means fine particles of soft snow.\n\n\n5. What was the poet''s mood before the snow fell?\n(A) Exuberant\n(B) Sad and regretful\n(C) Angry\n(D) Indifferent\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The poet was having a regretful, ruined day.\n\n\n6. What does the word \"rued\" mean?\n(A) Enjoyed\n(B) Held in regret\n(C) Celebrated\n(D) Forgotten\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Rued means regretted or lamented.\n\n\n7. What is the rhyme scheme of \"Dust of Snow\"?\n(A) AABB CCDD\n(B) ABAB CDCD\n(C) ABBA CDDC\n(D) ABCD EFGH\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The 8-line poem follows ABAB CDCD.\n\n\n8. In \"Fire and Ice\", what does \"fire\" symbolize?\n(A) Peace\n(B) Desire and greed\n(C) Cold hatred\n(D) Fear\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Fire represents lust, avarice, and passionate desire.\n\n\n9. In \"Fire and Ice\", what does \"ice\" symbolize?\n(A) Hatred and indifference\n(B) Love\n(C) Hope\n(D) Modesty\nAnswer:\n(A) | Explanation: Ice symbolizes coldness, rigid hatred, and cruelty.\n\n\n10. Who does the poet side with initially in \"Fire and Ice\"?\n(A) Those who favor water\n(B) Those who favor fire\n(C) Those who favor ice\n(D) Neither\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Frost states: \"I hold with those who favor fire.\"\n\n\n11. What does \"perish\" mean?\n(A) Flourish\n(B) Die or be destroyed\n(C) Freeze\n(D) Melt\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Perish means to suffer destruction or death.\n\n\n12. What does \"suffice\" mean?\n(A) Be inadequate\n(B) Be sufficient/enough\n(C) Overflow\n(D) Diminish\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Suffice means to be adequate for a purpose.\n\n\n13. How many lines are there in \"Fire and Ice\"?\n(A) 8\n(B) 9\n(C) 12\n(D) 14\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: \"Fire and Ice\" is a concise 9-line poem.\n\n\n14. What common theme unites both poems?\n(A) Love of animals\n(B) Human emotions and nature''s dual powers\n(C) City life\n(D) War heroes\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Both poems examine inner human psychological states and nature.\n\n\n15. What literary device is present in \"Has given my heart\"?\n(A) Metaphor\n(B) Alliteration\n(C) Hyperbole\n(D) Oxymoron\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Repetition of the ''h'' sound is alliteration."}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'english-11-notes',
  'english',
  'english-11',
  'A Tiger in the Zoo & Amanda!',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"A Tiger in the Zoo & Amanda!","subject":"english","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"1. Syllabus Verification & Textbook Confirmation","content":"- Prescribed in official RBSE Syllabus 2026–27 (Subject Code: 02).\n- First Flight Poetry Pool (Leslie Norris and Robin Klein)."},{"heading":"2. Chapter Metadata & Overview & Overview","content":"- Metadata: Chapter ID: english-11, Subject ID: english\n- Poems: \"A Tiger in the Zoo\" by Leslie Norris; \"Amanda!\" by Robin Klein\n- Themes: Captivity vs. Wild Freedom (Tiger); Parental Control vs. Adolescent Imagination and Yearning for Liberty (Amanda!)."},{"heading":"3. Detailed Section-Wise Explanations","content":""},{"heading":"Section 1: A Tiger in the Zoo (Leslie Norris)","content":"- Contrast of Habitats:\n  - Stanza 1 & 4 & 5: The tiger stalks in his small cage on pads of velvet quiet, imprisoned behind concrete bars, ignoring staring visitors at night while listening to patrolling cars and staring at brilliant stars.\n  - Stanza 2 & 3: What he should be doing: lurking in the shadows, sliding through long grass near the water hole where plump deer pass, and snarling around village houses at the jungle''s edge, terrorizing villagers with his white fangs and claws.\n- Core Message: Wild animals belong in their natural habitats; confining majestic creatures for human amusement robs them of dignity and vitality."},{"heading":"Section 2: Amanda! (Robin Klein)","content":"- Structure: Alternate stanzas of nagging parental instructions contrasted with Amanda''s vivid daydreaming escapes enclosed in parentheses.\n- Instructions: Don''t bite your nails, don''t hunch your shoulders, sit up straight, finish your homework, tidy your room, clean your shoes, don''t eat chocolate, remember your acne, look at me when I''m speaking to you, stop sulking.\n- Daydreams:\n  - Stanza 2: An emerald sea where she is the sole inhabitant—a calm mermaid drifting blissfully.\n  - Stanza 4: An orphan roaming the dusty street, making soft patterns with bare quiet feet—silence is golden, freedom is sweet.\n  - Stanza 6: Rapunzel in a tranquil high tower with no cares—declaring firmly that she will never let down her bright hair.\n\n4. Poetic Devices\n- Rhyme Schemes:\n  - A Tiger in the Zoo: ABCB in each quatrain.\n  - Amanda!: AABA for nagging stanzas; AAA for the parenthetical fantasy stanzas.\n- Literary Devices:\n  - Metaphor: \"pads of velvet quiet\" (tiger''s paws); \"silence is golden, freedom is sweet\".\n  - Personification: The tiger is addressed as \"he\" rather than \"it\".\n  - Allusion: Mermaid, Rapunzel.\n  - Oxymoron: \"quiet rage\"."},{"heading":"5. Important Vocabulary","content":"- Snarling: Making an aggressive, growling sound while showing teeth.\n- Fangs: Long, sharp teeth of animals.\n- Languid: Relaxed, calm, moving slowly.\n- Sole: Single, only.\n- Sulking: Being silently bad-tempered or resentful.\n\n6. Verified Official Board & Practice Questions\nRBSE 2024\n\n**Question:** Where should the tiger be lurking according to the poet? [2 Marks]\n**Model Answer:** According to Leslie Norris, the tiger should be lurking in the shadow of trees and sliding through long grass near the water hole, waiting to hunt plump deer.\n\nOfficial Model Paper 2026\n\n**Question:** Why does Amanda wish to be an orphan? [2 Marks]\n**Model Answer:** Amanda wishes to be an orphan not because she hates her parents, but because she yearns for absolute peace and freedom from constant nagging, wanting to roam freely in the street without adult supervision.\n\n7. 15-Question MCQ Quiz\n1. Who wrote \"A Tiger in the Zoo\"?\n(A) Leslie Norris\n(B) Robin Klein\n(C) John Berryman\n(D) Carl Sandburg\nAnswer:\n(A) 2. Where is the tiger locked?\n\n\n(A) In a cave\n(B) In a concrete cell in a zoo\n(C) In a forest\n(D) In a pit\nAnswer:\n(B) 3. What is meant by \"quiet rage\"?\n\n\n(A) Loud screaming\n(B) Suppressed, helpless anger inside the cage\n(C) Peaceful sleep\n(D) Hunting\nAnswer:\n(B) 4. What animal should the tiger hunt near the water hole?\n\n\n(A) Rabbit\n(B) Plump deer\n(C) Sheep\n(D) Wild boar\nAnswer:\n(B) 5. What does the tiger hear at night?\n\n\n(A) Sounds of monkeys\n(B) Patrolling cars\n(C) Music\n(D) Rain\nAnswer:\n(B) 6. Who wrote the poem \"Amanda!\"?\n\n\n(A) Robin Klein\n(B) Robert Frost\n(C) Ogden Nash\n(D) Adrienne Rich\nAnswer:\n(A) 7. What is Amanda instructed NOT to do in stanza 1?\n\n\n(A) Run in the street\n(B) Bite her nails and hunch shoulders\n(C) Eat apples\n(D) Sing loudly\nAnswer:\n(B) 8. What creature does Amanda imagine being in the emerald sea?\n\n\n(A) Dolphin\n(B) Mermaid\n(C) Shark\n(D) Whale\nAnswer:\n(B) 9. Why is Amanda forbidden from eating chocolate?\n\n\n(A) It causes fever\n(B) It causes acne\n(C) It costs too much\n(D) It spoils lunch\nAnswer:\n(B) 10. In her second daydream, what does Amanda imagine herself to be?\n\n\n(A) An orphan roaming the street\n(B) A princess\n(C) A fairy\n(D) A queen\nAnswer:\n(A) 11. According to Amanda, what is golden and sweet?\n\n\n(A) Honey and bread\n(B) Silence is golden, freedom is sweet\n(C) Money and diamonds\n(D) Sunshine and rain\nAnswer:\n(B) 12. What fairy tale character does Amanda imagine herself as?\n\n\n(A) Cinderella\n(B) Snow White\n(C) Rapunzel\n(D) Sleeping Beauty\nAnswer:\n(C) 13. What promise does Amanda make regarding her hair in the tower?\n\n\n(A) She will cut it\n(B) She will never let down her bright hair\n(C) She will dye it\n(D) She will wash it\nAnswer:\n(B) 14. Why is the speaker annoyed with Amanda at the end?\n\n\n(A) She broke a dish\n(B) She looks moody and sulky\n(C) She failed an exam\n(D) She fell asleep\nAnswer:\n(B) 15. What poetic device is used in \"pads of velvet quiet\"?\n\n\n(A) Simile\n(B) Metaphor\n(C) Hyperbole\n(D) Irony\nAnswer:\n(B)"}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'english-12-notes',
  'english',
  'english-12',
  'A Triumph of Surgery',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"A Triumph of Surgery","subject":"english","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"1. Syllabus Verification & Textbook Confirmation","content":"- Prescribed in RBSE Class 10 English Syllabus 2026–27.\n- Chapter 1 of Footprints Without Feet."},{"heading":"2. Chapter Metadata & Overview & Overview","content":"- Author: James Herriot (British veterinary surgeon and author).\n- Key Characters:\n  - Tricki: A pampered, obese Pekingese dog who becomes seriously ill from overfeeding.\n  - Mrs. Pumphrey: Tricki''s wealthy, excessively affectionate and indulgent mistress.\n  - Mr. James Herriot: The practical, observant veterinary surgeon.\n- Setting: Rural Yorkshire, England, and Herriot''s veterinary clinic.\n\nOverview:\nMrs. Pumphrey lavishes her small pet dog Tricki with rich treats, malt, cod-liver oil, cream cakes, and bowls of Horlicks, believing he suffers from malnutrition. Consequently, Tricki becomes hugely fat, like a bloated sausage with a leg at each corner. Unable to walk and vomiting constantly, he is rescued by Dr. Herriot, who recognizes that Tricki needs no medicine—only strict diet control and rigorous exercise. By keeping Tricki on water for two days and letting him play naturally with other pack dogs, Tricki makes a complete recovery. Mrs. Pumphrey gratefully proclaims the cure \"a triumph of surgery\"."},{"heading":"3. Detailed Section-Wise Explanations","content":""},{"heading":"Section 1: Tricki’s Bloated Condition and Mrs. Pumphrey’s Indulgence","content":"Dr. Herriot is shocked when he encounters Mrs. Pumphrey walking Tricki. The dog has rheumy, bloodshot eyes, a lolling tongue, and no energy. Despite Herriot''s warnings, Mrs. Pumphrey continues giving him sweet cakes, chocolates, and wine."},{"heading":"Section 2: Hospitalization and Common-Sense Treatment","content":"When Tricki refuses to eat his favorite foods and lies panting on a rug, Herriot insists on hospitalizing him for a fortnight. Mrs. Pumphrey almost faints in despair, sending whole wardrobes of tweed coats, bowls, and cushions with him.\nAt the clinic, Herriot puts Tricki on no medical treatment:\n- Day 1 & 2: Plentiful water, no food.\n- Day 3: Tricki whimpers and shows interest in the surrounding dogs (Joe the greyhound, etc.).\n- Day 4 onwards: Tricki competes enthusiastically for his bowl of food and joins in rough-and-tumble games."},{"heading":"Section 3: Mrs. Pumphrey’s Lavish Gifts and the Transformation","content":"Mrs. Pumphrey phones frantically multiple times a day. To build Tricki''s strength, she sends two dozen fresh eggs daily, bottles of wine, and even brandy! Herriot and his staff enjoy these luxuries themselves during lunch.\nAfter two weeks, Tricki is transformed into a lithe, hard-muscled animal. When Mrs. Pumphrey arrives in her chauffeur-driven car, Tricki leaps joyfully into her lap. Tears filling her eyes, she proclaims: \"Oh, Mr. Herriot, how can I ever thank you? This is a triumph of surgery!\""},{"heading":"4. Themes & Character Analysis","content":"- Affection vs. Sensibility: Excessive love without restraint can become toxic and harmful.\n- Common Sense over Medication: Healing often requires simple lifestyle balance, proper nutrition, and physical activity rather than expensive drugs.\n- Satire on Wealthy Gentry: Mrs. Pumphrey''s exaggerated pampering of her dog reflects the idle indulgence of the upper classes."},{"heading":"5. Important Vocabulary","content":"- Bloated: Swollen with fluid or gas.\n- Rheumy: Watery discharge from eyes or nose.\n- Listless: Lacking energy, enthusiasm, or vitality.\n- Convalescing: Recovering health and strength after illness.\n- Distraught: Deeply upset, agitated, or worried.\n\n6. Verified Past Paper & Model Questions\nRBSE 2024\n\n**Question:** Why was Dr. Herriot worried about Tricki? [2 Marks]\n**Model Answer:** Dr. Herriot was worried because Tricki had become immensely fat like a bloated sausage, had bloodshot watery eyes, and could barely walk due to Mrs. Pumphrey''s constant overfeeding.\n\nRBSE Model Paper 2026\n\n**Question:** Why does Mrs. Pumphrey call Tricki''s recovery \"a triumph of surgery\"? [2 Marks]\n**Model Answer:** Mrs. Pumphrey believed Tricki was terminally ill and that Dr. Herriot performed a miraculous surgical operation to cure him, unaware that Tricki was cured purely through diet control and natural exercise without a single incision or medicine.\n\n7. 15-Question MCQ Quiz\n1. Who is the author of \"A Triumph of Surgery\"?\n(A) James Herriot\n(B) Ruskin Bond\n(C) H.G. Wells\n(D) Robert Arthur\nAnswer:\n(A) 2. What kind of animal was Tricki?\n\n\n(A) A cat\n(B) A dog\n(C) An otter\n(D) A monkey\nAnswer:\n(B) 3. What did Mrs. Pumphrey mistakenly believe Tricki suffered from?\n\n\n(A) High fever\n(B) Malnutrition\n(C) Rabies\n(D) Broken leg\nAnswer:\n(B) 4. What did Tricki look like when Dr. Herriot met him on the street?\n\n\n(A) A bloated sausage with four legs\n(B) A lean greyhound\n(C) A skeleton\n(D) A bear cub\nAnswer:\n(A) 5. How long was Tricki to be hospitalized?\n\n\n(A) One week\n(B) A fortnight (two weeks)\n(C) One month\n(D) Two days\nAnswer:\n(B) 6. What did Herriot give Tricki during the first two days at the surgery?\n\n\n(A) Lots of medicine\n(B) Only plenty of water and no food\n(C) Eggs and wine\n(D) Meat and bones\nAnswer:\n(B) 7. What did Mrs. Pumphrey send to build Tricki''s strength during recovery?\n\n\n(A) Fresh eggs, wine, and brandy\n(B) Milk and biscuits\n(C) Fish and bread\n(D) Apples and carrots\nAnswer:\n(A) 8. Who consumed the wine and eggs sent for Tricki?\n\n\n(A) Tricki\n(B) Mrs. Pumphrey\n(C) Dr. Herriot and his partners\n(D) The clinic dogs\nAnswer:\n(C) 9. Into what kind of animal was Tricki transformed after two weeks?\n\n\n(A) A fat lapdog\n(B) A lithe, hard-muscled animal\n(C) A weak puppy\n(D) A fierce guard dog\nAnswer:\n(B) 10. What was Mrs. Pumphrey''s reaction upon seeing the recovered Tricki?\n\n\n(A) She was angry\n(B) Tears shone in her eyes and her lips trembled\n(C) She fainted\n(D) She didn''t recognize him\nAnswer:\n(B) 11. What did Mrs. Pumphrey exclaim at the end of the story?\n\n\n(A) \"A triumph of surgery!\"\n(B) \"You are a fraud!\"\n(C) \"Give me back my dog!\"\n(D) \"How much do I owe you?\"\nAnswer:\n(A) 12. What was Tricki''s only real fault according to Herriot?\n\n\n(A) Barking loudly\n(B) Greed for food\n(C) Biting people\n(D) Running away\nAnswer:\n(B) 13. What breed was Joe, the clinic dog who greeted Tricki?\n\n\n(A) Bulldog\n(B) Greyhound\n(C) German Shepherd\n(D) Poodle\nAnswer:\n(B) 14. Which luxury item did Mrs. Pumphrey NOT send for Tricki?\n\n\n(A) Tweed coats\n(B) Rubber toys\n(C) Silver bowls\n(D) Golden cage\nAnswer:\n(D) 15. What is the central moral lesson of the story?\n\n\n(A) Dogs should never be kept as pets\n(B) Excessive pampering and overfeeding can be dangerous to health\n(C) Veterinarians always charge too much\n(D) Wealth solves all problems\nAnswer:\n(B)"}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'english-13-notes',
  'english',
  'english-13',
  'The Thief''s Story',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"The Thief''s Story","subject":"english","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"1. Syllabus Verification & Textbook Confirmation","content":"- Prescribed in RBSE Class 10 English Syllabus 2026–27.\n- Chapter 2 of Footprints Without Feet."},{"heading":"2. Chapter Metadata & Overview & Overview","content":"- Author: Ruskin Bond (Celebrated Indian author).\n- Key Characters:\n  - Hari Singh: A 15-year-old experienced thief who changes his identity regularly.\n  - Anil: A 25-year-old tall, lean, easy-going, and trusting writer.\n- Setting: A small town in North India; room over Jumna Sweet Shop.\n\nOverview:\nHari Singh befriends Anil at a wrestling match with the intention of robbing him. Anil offers Hari shelter, food, and teaches him how to cook, write his name, and calculate numbers. Anil earns money intermittently through writing for magazines. One evening, Anil returns home with a bundle of 600 rupees (in 50-rupee notes). Hari steals the money while Anil sleeps and rushes to the railway station to catch the 10:30 express to Lucknow. However, an inner moral voice holds him back on the platform. Realizing that education and Anil''s trust are far more valuable than a few stolen notes, Hari walks back in the rain and slips the damp currency back under Anil''s mattress. The next morning, Anil offers Hari a 50-rupee note, aware of what happened but choosing forgiveness over punishment."},{"heading":"3. Detailed Section-Wise Explanations","content":""},{"heading":"Section 1: The Encounter and the Arrangement","content":"Hari Singh, age 15, meets Anil at a wrestling match. Anil looks easy-going, kind, and simple enough for Hari''s opportunistic designs. Hari offers to work for Anil, who admits he cannot afford to pay him but agrees to feed him if Hari can cook. Hari lies that he knows how to cook. The first meal Hari prepares is so terrible that Anil gives it to a stray dog and tells Hari to leave, but Hari smiles charmingly, winning Anil over."},{"heading":"Section 2: Education and Trust","content":"Anil teaches Hari to cook, write his name, write whole sentences, and add numbers. Hari realizes that being literate will allow him to achieve unlimited success. Hari makes a modest profit of about one rupee a day when buying daily groceries, which Anil knows about but overlooks."},{"heading":"Section 3: The Theft and Hesitation at the Station","content":"Anil sells a book to a publisher and brings home a bundle of notes, tucking them under his mattress. Knowing Anil''s careless trust, Hari sneaks in at night, steals the 600 rupees, and runs to the station. The 10:30 express to Lucknow is pulling out, and Hari could easily leap aboard. Yet, something holds him back. He reflects on human faces when robbed: greedy men show fear, rich men anger, poor men acceptance. But Anil’s face would show only sadness for the loss of trust."},{"heading":"Section 4: The Transformation and Return","content":"Hari walks to the maidan and sits on a bench in the pouring rain. His clothes are drenched, and the stolen notes are damp. He realizes that education could make him a genuinely respected man, whereas stealing keeps him a hunted petty criminal. He returns home quietly, creeps in, and slips the notes back under the mattress."},{"heading":"Section 5: Anil''s Silent Forgiveness","content":"The next morning, Anil is already making tea. He hands Hari a wet 50-rupee note, saying: \"I made some money yesterday. Now you will be paid regularly.\" Anil announces that today they will begin writing full sentences. Hari touches the wet note, realizing Anil knows everything, but neither his lips nor his eyes show any anger."},{"heading":"4. Themes & Character Analysis","content":"- Power of Trust and Compassion: Unconditional trust and empathy can reform a hardened criminal more effectively than fear or legal punishment.\n- Value of Education over Illicit Wealth: Education provides dignity, genuine respect, and sustainable self-reliance.\n- Guilt and Conscience: The inner moral transformation of a marginalized adolescent through human kindness."},{"heading":"5. Important Vocabulary","content":"- Flattery: Insincere praise given to further one''s own interests.\n- Intermittently: At irregular intervals; not continuously.\n- Unlined: Smooth; showing no wrinkles, worry, or suspicion (Anil''s face).\n- Drizzle: Light rain falling in fine drops.\n- Spirits: Emotional state, mood, or courage.\n\n6. Verified Official Board & Practice Questions\nRBSE 2024\n\n**Question:** Why did Hari Singh return to Anil after stealing the money? [2 Marks]\n**Model Answer:** Hari Singh returned because his conscience awoke. He realized that Anil had trusted him completely and was teaching him to read and write. Being an educated, respected man was worth far more than 600 stolen rupees.\n\nRBSE Model Paper 2026\n\n**Question:** How did Anil make a living? [2 Marks]\n**Model Answer:** Anil made a living by fits and starts as a freelance writer for magazines. He would borrow money one week and lend it the next, celebrating whenever a cheque arrived.\n\n7. 15-Question MCQ Quiz\n1. Who wrote \"The Thief''s Story\"?\n(A) Ruskin Bond\n(B) Guy de Maupassant\n(C) K.A. Abbas\n(D) Victor Canning\nAnswer:\n(A) 2. How old was Hari Singh when he met Anil?\n\n\n(A) 12 years\n(B) 15 years\n(C) 18 years\n(D) 20 years\nAnswer:\n(B) 3. Where did Hari Singh meet Anil?\n\n\n(A) In a cinema\n(B) At a wrestling match\n(C) At a railway station\n(D) In a bookstore\nAnswer:\n(B) 4. What was Anil''s profession?\n\n\n(A) Doctor\n(B) Lawyer\n(C) Writer\n(D) Merchant\nAnswer:\n(C) 5. How much money did Anil bring home from selling his book?\n\n\n(A) 100 rupees\n(B) 500 rupees\n(C) 600 rupees\n(D) 1000 rupees\nAnswer:\n(C) 6. In what denomination were the stolen notes?\n\n\n(A) 10-rupee notes\n(B) 20-rupee notes\n(C) 50-rupee notes\n(D) 100-rupee notes\nAnswer:\n(C) 7. Where did Anil keep the money?\n\n\n(A) Inside a safe\n(B) Under his mattress\n(C) In his cupboard\n(D) In a locked box\nAnswer:\n(B) 8. Which train was Hari Singh planning to catch?\n\n\n(A) 10:30 Express to Lucknow\n(B) Midnight Express to Delhi\n(C) 9:00 Mail to Jaipur\n(D) Punjab Mail\nAnswer:\n(A) 9. Why did Hari Singh stop on the platform instead of boarding?\n\n\n(A) His ticket was lost\n(B) Police were searching\n(C) An inner feeling and guilt held him back\n(D) The train was full\nAnswer:\n(C) 10. How much daily profit did Hari make on grocery purchases?\n\n\n(A) 50 paise\n(B) About one rupee\n(C) Five rupees\n(D) Ten rupees\nAnswer:\n(B) 11. According to Hari, what expression appears on a trusting person''s face when robbed?\n\n\n(A) Fear\n(B) Anger\n(C) Only sadness for the loss of trust\n(D) Laughter\nAnswer:\n(C) 12. What was the weather like when Hari sat on the bench in the maidan?\n\n\n(A) Hot and sunny\n(B) Chilly night with heavy rain\n(C) Snowy\n(D) Dusty storm\nAnswer:\n(B) 13. How much money did Anil hand Hari the following morning?\n\n\n(A) 10 rupees\n(B) 50 rupees\n(C) 100 rupees\n(D) 600 rupees\nAnswer:\n(B) 14. How did Hari know that Anil knew about the theft?\n\n\n(A) Anil yelled at him\n(B) The 50-rupee note was still damp from the previous night''s rain\n(C) Anil called the police\n(D) The neighbors told him\nAnswer:\n(B) 15. What promised lesson did Anil say they would start that morning?\n\n\n(A) How to write whole sentences\n(B) Cooking sweets\n(C) Wrestling moves\n(D) Account keeping\nAnswer:\n(A)"}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'english-14-notes',
  'english',
  'english-14',
  'Footprints Without Feet',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Footprints Without Feet","subject":"english","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"1. Syllabus Verification & Textbook ConfirmationSyllabus Inclusion Status: Prescribed / Included .Board: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER, Ajmer) .Academic Session: 2026–2027 (Targeting the 2027 Secondary School Examination) .Subject: English (Subject Code: 02 / S-02) .Prescribed Textbook: NCERT Footprints Without Feet (Supplementary Reader, Chapter 5) .Chapter Title: Footprints Without Feet .Chapter ID: english-24Subject ID: englishAuthor: H.G. Wells (Herbert George Wells) .Lesson Type: Supplementary Reader / Science Fiction & Moral Tale .Mark Weightage Context: Section D (Literature — Footprints Without Feet) carries 12 Marks in total across:5 Factual MCQs ($\\text{1 mark } \\times 5 = 5\\text{ Marks}$) .2 Short Answer Questions ($\\text{2 marks } \\times 2 = 4\\text{ Marks}$) .1 Character/Plot/Situation Long Answer Question ($\\text{3 Marks}$) .","content":""},{"heading":"2. Chapter Metadata & Overview & OverviewMetadataChapter ID: english-24Title: Footprints Without FeetAuthor: H.G. WellsGenre: Science Fiction / Moral & Psychological NarrativeKey Settings: Streets of London, a big London department store, a theatrical shop in Drury Lane, and the rural village of Iping in SussexKey Characters:Griffin: A brilliant but lawless, short-tempered scientist who discovers the secret of human invisibility.Mrs. Hall: The inquisitive, money-minded, and firm landlady of the local inn at Iping.Mr. Hall: Mrs. Hall''s husband who assists at the inn.Rev. Mr. Bunting: The clergyman at Iping whose house is burglarized.Mr. Bobby Jaffers: The dutiful village constable who attempts to arrest Griffin.OverviewFootprints Without Feet by H.G. Wells is a classic science-fiction story that warns against the dangers of misusing scientific discoveries . The narrative follows Griffin, a brilliant scientist who carries out experiments to make the human body transparent and invisible. He swallows rare chemical drugs, causing his body to become as transparent as a sheet of glass while remaining as solid as glass.However, rather than using his discovery for the welfare of humanity, Griffin reveals a deeply lawless and vengeful nature. When his landlord attempts to evict him, Griffin burns the landlord''s house down in revenge. To escape detection, he sheds his clothes and becomes a naked, invisible wanderer in the freezing London winter.After seeking warmth and stolen supplies in a London department store and robbing a theatrical shop in Drury Lane (disguising his invisible head with bandages, dark glasses, a false nose, and side-whiskers), Griffin flees the city noise to the quiet village of Iping. He rents two rooms at Mrs. Hall''s inn. His eccentric behavior, strange appearance, and sudden cash transactions arouse suspicion. When his stolen money runs out, Griffin robs the clergyman''s house. Suspicion falls directly on him, leading to a dramatic confrontation with Mrs. Hall and Constable Jaffers. Griffin removes his clothes and bandages, turning into a \"headless\" and invisible force, overpowering the village constable and escaping once again into thin air .","content":""},{"heading":"3. Detailed Section-Wise Explanations                     GRIFFIN''S ESCALATION OF LAWLESSNESS","content":"│\n  ┌───────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┐\n  ▼                                   ▼                                   ▼\nPHASE 1: THE DISCOVERY      PHASE 2: LONDON ESCAPADES     PHASE 3: IPING & ESCAPE\n• Formulates invisibility   • Arson; wanders naked.       • Takes rooms at Iping inn.\n  drug.                     • Robs store & Drury Lane     • Robs clergyman''s desk.\n• Becomes transparent as      theatrical shop for         • Knocks out Constable Jaffers;\n  sheet of glass.             clothes & disguise.           escapes invisible."},{"heading":"Section 1: The Discovery and Arson in LondonThe Scientific Breakthrough: Griffin carries out years of research and successfully formulates rare drugs that can make the human body completely transparent.Lawless Temperament: Griffin is a \"lawless person\". His landlord dislikes him and tries to eject him from the house. In revenge, Griffin sets fire to the house.The Cost of Invisibility: To walk away unseen, Griffin has to strip off all his clothes. Consequently, he becomes a homeless, penniless, naked wanderer in London during the mid-winter cold.Section 2: Adventures in the London Store and Drury LaneThe Muddy Footprints: Two young boys in London are astonished to see fresh, muddy footprints appearing on a doorstep without seeing any person making them. They follow the prints until the marks fade completely.Shelter in the Store: Seeking relief from the icy cold, Griffin sneaks into a big London department store at closing time. He breaks open boxes, clothes himself in warm garments, shoes, and an overcoat, and feeds on cold meat, coffee, sweets, and wine from the grocery store, falling asleep on a pile of quilts.Discovery and Flight: The next morning, store assistants discover him sleeping. Panic-stricken, Griffin strips off his newly found clothes to become invisible again and escapes into the cold air.Drury Lane Disguise: To cover his invisible face and head, Griffin enters a theatrical shop in Drury Lane. He steals bandages to wrap around his forehead, dark spectacles, a false nose, big bushy side-whiskers, and a large hat. Before leaving, he callously attacks the shopkeeper from behind and robs him of all his money.Section 3: The Arrival at Iping VillageSeeking Solitude: Wanting to get away from crowded London, Griffin takes a train to the rural village of Iping and books two rooms at the local inn owned by Mrs. Hall.Mrs. Hall''s Suspicion: Mrs. Hall tries to be friendly, but Griffin curtly tells her that he came to Iping for solitude and does not wish to be disturbed. His bandages and strange appearance make the villagers view him as an eccentric eccentric with a facial deformity.Running Out of Cash: Griffin pays his inn bill in advance with stolen money. When the cash runs out, he lies to Mrs. Hall, claiming he is expecting a remittance (money transfer) at any moment.Section 4: The Clergyman''s House Burglary & The Extraordinary ChairThe Theft: Early one morning, the clergyman (Rev. Mr. Bunting) and his wife hear noises in their study. They hear the chink of money being taken from the desk. Holding a poker, the clergyman bursts into the room shouting \"Surrender!\", only to find the room completely empty. The money has vanished without a soul in sight.The Inn Room Investigation: That same morning, Mr. and Mrs. Hall find Griffin''s bedroom door wide open. Investigating inside, they notice his bedclothes are cold, and his bandages and clothes are scattered across the floor.The Supernatural Attack: Suddenly, Mrs. Hall hears a sniff close to her ear. Moments later, the hat on the bedpost leaps up and dashes itself into her face! The bedroom chair then springs to life, charging at her legs and pushing both Mr. and Mrs. Hall out of the room before locking the door behind them. Mrs. Hall faints, convinced the room is haunted by spirits.Section 5: The Climax and the Invisible EscapeThe Confrontation: After the burglary news spreads, suspicion falls squarely on Griffin because he suddenly produces ready cash to pay his outstanding inn bill. Mrs. Hall directly demands to know how he exited an empty room and how he entered a locked one.Losing Temper: Furious at her questioning, Griffin shouts: \"You don’t understand who or what I am!\" He rips off his bandages, false nose, spectacles, and whiskers. The horrified villagers in the bar find themselves staring at a headless man!Constable Jaffers'' Arrival: Bobby Jaffers, the village constable, arrives with a warrant to arrest the suspect. Jaffers is amazed to find he has to arrest a man without a head, but he firmly resolves to do his duty.The Final Struggle: A chaotic struggle ensues. Griffin strips off his garments one by one. As each garment falls, he becomes increasingly invisible. Jaffers attempts to grapple with an unseen opponent, receiving blows from nowhere until he is knocked unconscious. Griffin escapes into the countryside, invisible and free once more.","content":""},{"heading":"4. Themes & Character Analysis & Key ConceptsCore ThemesMisuse of Scientific Knowledge: Science is a powerful tool meant for the betterment of society. When wielded by an irresponsible, self-centered individual, it becomes a dangerous force of destruction.Lawlessness vs. Social Order: Invisibility releases Griffin from social accountability. Without moral integrity, the absence of fear of punishment leads directly to crime, theft, and violence.The Isolation of Arrogance: Griffin''s intellectual superiority makes him detached, antisocial, and megalomaniacal, eventually reducing him to a hunted, naked fugitive.Character Analysis1. GriffinTraits: Brilliant, secretive, short-tempered, arrogant, lawless, and violent.Duality: A scientific genius who achieves an unprecedented discovery, yet morally bankrupt. His invisibility reveals his inner darkness—he uses his power to commit arson, theft, assault, and terrorize innocent villagers.2. Mrs. HallTraits: Practical, hospitable, observant, money-minded, and courageous.Role: As the landlady of the Iping inn, she tolerates Griffin''s rudeness as long as he pays cash. However, when she suspects foul play and encounters supernatural occurrences, she confronts him directly.3. Bobby JaffersTraits: Dutiful, brave, conscientious, and uncompromising.Role: The village constable who demonstrates high professional integrity. He attempts to arrest Griffin regardless of whether the suspect has a head or is invisible, stating that a warrant must be executed.","content":""},{"heading":"5. Important VocabularyWordContextual MeaningTransparentAllowing light to pass through so that objects behind can be distinctly seen.GazingLooking steadily and intently at something in surprise or admiration.EccentricUnconventional, strange, or unusual in behavior or appearance.SolitudeThe state or situation of being alone, away from other people.RemittanceA sum of money sent in payment or as a gift.ClergymanA male priest, minister, or religious official in the Christian Church.PokerA metal rod used for stirring a fire.IndignantlyIn a manner indicating anger or annoyance at unfair treatment.HystericAffected by wildly uncontrollable emotion or panic.HeadlessHaving no head (describing Griffin when his head garments are removed).","content":""},{"heading":"6. Verified Previous-Year & Official Model Paper Questions Questions & Official Board PapersOfficial RBSE Secondary Model Examination Paper 2026 (secmodel2026.pdf)Question 1: Multiple Choice Question (Section D) [1 Mark] ","content":"**Question:** What discovery did Griffin make ? (Footprints without Feet) \n(A) He could change metals into gold \n(B) He could make himself invisible \n(C) He could fly in the sky\n(D) He could read minds\nAnswer:\n(B) He could make himself invisible Explanation: Griffin carried out experiments to prove that the human body could become transparent and invisible .Official RBSE Secondary Board Paper 2024 (S-02-English)Question 2: Short Answer Question (Section D) [2 Marks] \n\n**Question:** Why were the two boys in London surprised and fascinated? (Footprints without Feet) \n**Model Answer:** The two boys were surprised and fascinated because they saw fresh, muddy footprints appearing step-by-step on the stairs of a house, but could not see the person making them .Question 3: Short Answer Question (Section D) [2 Marks] \n\n**Question:** Why did Griffin set fire to his landlord’s house? (Footprints without Feet) \n**Model Answer:** Griffin set fire to his landlord''s house in revenge because his landlord disliked him and tried to eject him from the property ."},{"heading":"7. Generated Practice Questions & Predicted QuestionsShort Answer Questions (20–30 words / 2 Marks Each)How did Griffin become invisible?","content":"**Model Answer:** Griffin swallowed certain rare chemical drugs he had formulated, causing his body to become as transparent and solid as a sheet of glass .Why was Griffin wandering the streets of London without clothes or money?\n**Model Answer:** After setting fire to his landlord''s house, Griffin had to strip off all his clothes to become invisible and escape without being caught .What extraordinary sight did the clergyman and his wife witness in their study?\n**Model Answer:** They heard the chink of money being taken from the desk, but when they opened the door, the room was completely empty with no one in sight.Why did Mrs. Hall think her guest was an eccentric scientist?\n**Model Answer:** She thought he was eccentric because of his strange wrapped bandages, dark spectacles, refusal to talk, and his desire for absolute solitude .Long Answer Questions (60 words / 3 Marks Each)\"Griffin was a brilliant scientist, but a lawless person.\" Justify this statement with examples from the text.\n**Model Answer:** Griffin proved his scientific brilliance by discovering a formula to make the human body transparent . However, his lawless nature ruined his achievement . He committed arson by setting fire to his landlord''s house, robbed a London store, assaulted and robbed a shopkeeper in Drury Lane, stole money from a clergyman''s desk in Iping, and knocked out the village constable . Instead of serving society, he used science to commit crimes .Describe the scene in Mrs. Hall’s inn when Griffin’s room appeared to be haunted.\n**Model Answer:** Finding Griffin''s bedroom door open, Mr. and Mrs. Hall went inside to investigate . Suddenly, Mrs. Hall heard a sniff near her ear, and Griffin''s hat leaped off the bedpost, striking her face . The bedroom chair then came alive, pushing them both out of the room and locking the door . Mrs. Hall fainted, believing the furniture was inhabited by spirits ."},{"heading":"8. 15-Question MCQ QuizWho is the author of \"Footprints Without Feet\"?","content":"(A) Ruskin Bond\n(B) H.G. Wells\n(C) Robert Arthur\n(D) Victor Canning\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The story was written by H.G. Wells .What discovery did Griffin make?\n\n\n(A) How to make metals into gold\n(B) How to make the human body invisible\n(C) How to cure incurable diseases\n(D) How to travel in time\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Griffin discovered a way to make the human body transparent .Why did Griffin set fire to his landlord''s house?\n\n\n(A) By accident\n(B) In revenge because the landlord tried to eject him\n(C) To test his invisibility\n(D) To claim insurance money\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He burned the house in revenge after the landlord tried to evict him .What did Griffin have to do to become completely invisible?\n\n\n(A) Wear a special cap\n(B) Take off all his clothes\n(C) Stay in the dark\n(D) Close his eyes\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: His body was transparent, but any clothes he wore remained visible .Where did Griffin first seek warmth and shelter in London?\n\n\n(A) At an inn\n(B) Inside a big London department store\n(C) At a friend''s house\n(D) In a church\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He slipped into a big London store at closing time .In which street did Griffin find a theatrical goods shop?\n\n\n(A) Baker Street\n(B) Drury Lane\n(C) Oxford Street\n(D) Regent Street\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He visited a theatrical shop in Drury Lane .Which item did Griffin NOT use to disguise his invisible head?\n\n\n(A) Bandages\n(B) Dark spectacles\n(C) False nose\n(D) A iron helmet\nAnswer:\n(D) | Explanation: He used bandages, dark spectacles, a false nose, side-whiskers, and a large hat .What was the name of the village Griffin escaped to from London?\n\n\n(A) Iping\n(B) Sussex\n(C) Oxford\n(D) Bristol\nAnswer:\n(A) | Explanation: Griffin took a train to the rural village of Iping .Who was the landlady of the local inn at Iping?\n\n\n(A) Mrs. Pumphrey\n(B) Mrs. Hall\n(C) Mrs. Bunting\n(D) Mrs. Danby\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Mrs. Hall was the innkeeper''s wife .Whose house was burglarized in Iping early in the morning?\n\n\n(A) Constable Jaffers'' house\n(B) The clergyman''s (Rev. Mr. Bunting''s) house\n(C) The doctor''s house\n(D) The mayor''s house\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Griffin stole money from the clergyman''s study desk .What object leapt up and dashed itself into Mrs. Hall''s face in the bedroom?\n\n\n(A) A pillow\n(B) The bedpost hat\n(C) A mirror\n(D) A heavy book\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The hat on the bedpost sprang up into her face .Why was Constable Bobby Jaffers surprised when he arrived to arrest Griffin?\n\n\n(A) Griffin was armed with a gun\n(B) He had to arrest a man without a head\n(C) The inn was closed\n(D) Griffin had already died\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Griffin had removed his head bandages, revealing a headless body .How did Griffin manage to overpower Constable Jaffers and the villagers?\n\n\n(A) By using a knife\n(B) By stripping off his garments one by one to become invisible\n(C) By locking them in a cellar\n(D) By jumping out of a high window\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He stripped off his clothes to become an unseen, invisible attacker .What is described as being \"as transparent as a sheet of glass\" in the story?\n\n\n(A) Griffin''s spectacles\n(B) Griffin''s body after taking the drugs\n(C) The window at the inn\n(D) The bottle in Drury Lane\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Griffin''s body became transparent like glass .What central message does H.G. Wells convey through \"Footprints Without Feet\"?\n\n\n(A) Science should be banned\n(B) Invisibility makes a person powerful and happy\n(C) Scientific power without moral responsibility leads to self-destruction and lawlessness\n(D) Landlords are always wrong\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: The story demonstrates that irresponsible science leads to lawlessness and chaos ."},{"heading":"9. Syllabus Coverage Checklist & Source References & Source ReferencesCoverage Checklist[x] Official RBSE 2026–27 Syllabus Verification & Chapter Inclusion Confirmation .[x] Prescribed NCERT Textbook (Footprints Without Feet) Identification & Author Mapping .[x] Detailed Plot Overview & Core Focus Areas (Griffin''s invisibility, scientific misuse, thefts, conflict, character motivations, consequences of irresponsible science) .[x] Detailed Section-Wise Narrative Breakdown .[x] Themes & Character Sketches (Griffin, Mrs. Hall, Constable Jaffers) .[x] Contextual Vocabulary Glossary .[x] Authentic Past Paper (RBSE 2024 Code S-02) & Model Paper 2026 Integration .[x] Model Answers & Generated Practice Questions suitable for Class 10 .[x] 15-Question MCQ Quiz with Explanations .Source ReferencesNCERT, New Delhi: Footprints Without Feet — Class 10 Supplementary Reader, Chapter 5 (Footprints Without Feet by H.G. Wells) .Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE), Ajmer: Class 10 English Syllabus & Blueprint (Session 2026–27) .RBSE Official Secondary Board Examination Paper 2024: Code S-02-English (SA Q. 17(iii) & Q. 18(ii)) .RBSE Official Secondary Model Examination Paper 2026: secmodel2026.pdf (MCQ Q. 20(i)) .RBSE Official Research Report: Curriculum, Assessment Architecture, and Source Library (2026–2027 Session) .Missing Information NoteIndividual Lesson Mark Allocation: In Section D (Supplementary Reader), the 12 Marks total are distributed across all 9 prescribed lessons of Footprints Without Feet through 5 MCQs, 2 SAQs, and 1 LAQ on the 80-mark final paper, rather than an isolated fixed quota per individual story .💡 Next Steps: Would you like me to prepare the BoardReady learning package for Chapter 6 (The Making of a Scientist by Robert W. Peterson) or Chapter 7 (The Necklace by Guy de Maupassant)?keeppinSave to notekeeppincopythumbupthumbdownCreate the complete BoardReady learning package for RBSE Class 10 English, academic session 2026–27, for the lesson \"The Making of a Scientist\".","content":"Book: Footprints Without Feet\nLesson type: Supplementary Reader\nChapter ID: english-25\nSubject ID: english\nExplanation language: English\nRequired learning focus: Richard Ebright, curiosity, early interests, scientific investigation, mentors, discipline and achievements supported by the text.\nFirst verify that the lesson is included in the current official RBSE syllabus and confirm the prescribed textbook edition. If the lesson is excluded, report that and do not present it as required board content.\nGenerate chapter metadata, a clear overview, detailed section-wise explanations, themes, character analysis or poetic analysis as appropriate, important vocabulary, verified previous-year questions, practice questions, predicted practice questions and a 15-question MCQ quiz.\nUse the prescribed textbook and available official board papers. Do not invent quotations, events, previous-year questions, years or marks. Separate exact past questions from generated practice. Provide model answers and explanations suitable for Class 10. Respect copyright by summarising and explaining rather than reproducing the entire lesson.\nFinish with a syllabus coverage checklist, source references and missing information. Format the output for BoardReady editorial review. If the output is too long, produce one section at a time while preserving the chapter ID. Thoughts expand_more\\n"}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'english-15-notes',
  'english',
  'english-15',
  'Bholi',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Bholi","subject":"english","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"1. Syllabus Verification & Textbook ConfirmationSyllabus Inclusion Status: Prescribed / Included .Board: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER, Ajmer) .Academic Session: 2026–2027 (Targeting the 2027 Secondary School Examination) .Subject: English (Subject Code: 02 / S-02) .Prescribed Textbook: NCERT Footprints Without Feet (Supplementary Reader, Chapter 8) .Chapter Title: Bholi .Chapter ID: english-27Subject ID: englishAuthor: K.A. Abbas (Khwaja Ahmad Abbas) .Lesson Type: Supplementary Reader / Social Realism & Female Empowerment Narrative .Mark Weightage Context: Section D (Literature — Footprints Without Feet) carries 12 Marks in total across:5 Factual MCQs ($\\text{1 mark } \\times 5 = 5\\text{ Marks}$) .2 Short Answer Questions ($\\text{2 marks } \\times 2 = 4\\text{ Marks}$) .1 Character/Plot/Situation Long Answer Question ($\\text{3 Marks}$) .","content":""},{"heading":"2. Chapter Metadata & Overview & OverviewMetadataChapter ID: english-27Title: BholiAuthor: K.A. AbbasGenre: Short Story / Social Realism & Female EmpowermentKey Setting: A developing Indian village (experiencing rural modernization with the opening of a primary school for girls)Key Characters:Sulekha (Bholi): The fourth daughter of Numberdar Ramlal; neglected, pockmarked, and stammering as a child, she transforms through education into a courageous, independent woman.Numberdar Ramlal: Bholi''s father, a prosperous revenue official in the village who worries about social status and Bholi''s marriage.Ramlal''s Wife (Bholi''s Mother): A conservative, traditional woman who considers daughters a burden and believes girls should not be educated.The Primary School Teacher: A compassionate, soft-spoken, encouraging mentor who nurtures Bholi''s self-confidence and self-respect.Bishambhar Nath: A prosperous, middle-aged, limping grocer from another village who demands a heavy dowry to marry Bholi.Laxmi: The family''s old cow who was sold away, making Bholi fearful of leaving home on her first day of school.OverviewBholi by K.A. Abbas is an inspiring narrative about the transformative power of education and the fight against deep-rooted social evils like gender bias, physical discrimination, and dowry .The protagonist, whose real name is Sulekha, suffers two tragic childhood setbacks: at ten months old, an accidental fall off a cot damages part of her brain, leaving her mentally backward; at two years old, a severe attack of smallpox permanently disfigures her body with deep, ugly pockmarks. Because she stammers when she learns to speak at age five, other children make fun of her, leading her to stay silent and earning her the nickname \"Bholi\" (the simpleton).While her brothers and sisters are healthy, good-looking, and sent to school or married off, Bholi is completely neglected. Her life reaches a turning point when the village Tehsildar inaugurates a new primary school for girls and commands Ramlal, as a government official (Numberdar), to set an example by sending his daughters to school. Ramlal''s wife refuses to send her good-looking daughters, fearing no one will marry educated girls. Instead, she sends Bholi, reasoning that she has \"no chance of getting married\" anyway.At school, Bholi encounters a gentle, soft-spoken teacher whose warmth and encouragement change everything. The teacher patiently helps Bholi speak without stammering, gives her books, and promises that education will make her the most respected person in the village.Years later, Ramlal arranges Bholi''s marriage to Bishambhar Nath, a wealthy, middle-aged, limping grocer with grown-up children. During the wedding ceremony, when the veil slips and Bishambhar sees Bholi''s pockmarked face, he refuses to marry her unless Ramlal pays him 5,000 rupees in cash as dowry. Ramlal places his turban at Bishambhar''s feet, weeping and begging for mercy, but the greedy groom stands firm.Seeing her father humiliated, Bholi steps forward with fierce dignity. Speaking in a clear, confident, unstammering voice, she flings the wedding garland into the sacred fire and refuses to marry a greedy coward. When shocked villagers accuse her of being shameless, Bholi boldly declares that she will remain unmarried, care for her parents in their old age, and teach in the same school that gave her a new life .","content":""},{"heading":"3. Detailed Section-Wise Explanations                       BHOLI''S TRANSFORMATION JOURNEY","content":"│\n  ┌──────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┐\n  ▼                                  ▼                                  ▼\nPHASE 1: NEGLECTED CHILD     PHASE 2: SCHOOL & MENTORSHIP      PHASE 3: FEMALE EMPOWERMENT\n• Smallpox at age 2; pock-   • Sent to school as a burden.     • Refuses greedy Bishambhar''s\n  marks & brain damage.      • Teacher inspires confidence.      ₹5,000 dowry demand.\n• Called ''Bholi'' (simpleton).• Overcomes stammer; learns to    • Chooses self-respect &\n• Neglected by parents.        read & write.                     vows to become a teacher."},{"heading":"Section 1: Early Childhood Neglect and Physical ImpairmentTragic Infancy: Sulekha was a healthy, fair baby at birth. However, at 10 months old, she fell off a cot on her head, damaging part of her brain and delaying her mental growth.Smallpox Attack: At two years of age, she suffered a severe smallpox attack. Only her eyes were saved; the rest of her body was covered in permanent, deep black pockmarks.Social Isolation: She did not learn to talk until she was five, and when she did, she stammered badly. Other children mimicked and teased her, causing her to withdraw into total silence. Her family called her \"Bholi\" (the simpleton) and treated her as a hopeless burden.Section 2: The Opening of the Girls'' Primary SchoolThe Tehsildar''s Order: When a primary school for girls opened in the village, the Tehsildar came to perform the opening ceremony. He told Ramlal that as a Numberdar (revenue official), he must set a public example by sending his daughters to school.The Mother''s Opposition: Ramlal''s wife protested, believing that educating girls ruins their marriage prospects. However, fearing the Tehsildar''s authority, she suggested sending Bholi: \"Let the teachers at school worry about her.\"The First Day of School: Bholi was terrified, remembering how their old cow, Laxmi, had been taken away and sold. But when she was given a bath, oil was rubbed into her matted hair, and she was dressed in a clean, hand-me-down dress belonging to her sister Champa, Bholi realized she was being taken to a better place.Section 3: The Teacher''s Encouragement and TransformationThe Classroom Experience: On her first day, Bholi sat in a corner of the classroom, fascinated by the colorful pictures of horses, goats, parrots, and cows on the wall.The Gentle Voice: When the teacher asked her name, Bholi tried to answer but stammered (\"Bho-Bho-Bholi\") and burst into tears. Instead of laughing like others, the teacher spoke in a soft, soothing, affectionate voice that Bholi had never heard in her life.The Promise of Literacy: The teacher patted her affectionately and promised: \"Put the fear out of your heart and you will be able to speak like everyone else.\" She gave Bholi a picture book, promising that within a month she would read it, then move to bigger books, earning the respect of the entire village.Section 4: The Marriage Proposal of Bishambhar NathYears of Progress: Years passed, and the village transformed into a small town. The primary school became a high school, a cinema hall opened under a tin shed, and a cotton ginning mill was established.Accepting the Proposal: Ramlal received a marriage proposal for Bholi from Bishambhar Nath, a prosperous grocer from another village . Bishambhar was middle-aged, limped while walking, was a widower, and had grown-up children from his first wife.Parental Calculation: Bholi''s parents accepted the proposal without consulting Bholi because Bishambhar did not ask for dowry initially and was wealthy enough to own a big shop, a house, and thousands in the bank . They felt it was Bholi''s only chance to marry, given her pockmarked face and lack of sense .Section 5: The Wedding, Dowry Demand, and Bholi''s Bold StandThe Wedding Procession: Bishambhar arrived in a grand procession with a brass band playing popular tunes. Ramlal was overjoyed to see such pomp and splendor.The Shocker: As the bride stood at the altar, a woman slipped back Bholi''s silken veil. Bishambhar caught a glimpse of her pockmarked face. He immediately halted the ceremony and greedily demanded 5,000 rupees in cash from Ramlal to marry her.Ramlal''s Humiliation: Ramlal placed his turban—the symbol of his family honor—at Bishambhar''s feet, weeping and begging him to take 2,000 rupees. Bishambhar remained adamant: \"Keep your daughter then. Five thousand or we go back.\"The Rebellion: Ramlal brought out the 5,000 rupees and placed the notes at the groom''s feet. But as Bishambhar raised the garland, Bholi''s hand struck out like lightning, knocking the garland into the sacred fire.The Declaration: In a clear, firm, and steady voice without a single stammer, Bholi commanded her father to take back his money. She declared that she was willing to marry a middle-aged man for her father''s honor, but she would never accept a greedy, despicable coward as her husband.The Future: When an old woman called her a \"shameless girl\", Bholi retorted with quiet dignity. The wedding party dispersed in confusion. Bholi reassured her weeping father that she would care for him and her mother in their old age and teach in the same school where she learned everything . Looking on from a corner, her school teacher felt a quiet sense of deep fulfillment, like an artist contemplating her masterpiece.","content":""},{"heading":"4. Themes & Character Analysis & Key ConceptsCore ThemesEducation as an Instrument of Empowerment: Education transforms Bholi from a helpless, stammering, insecure child into an articulate, confident woman capable of making independent moral choices .Social Discrimination and Gender Bias: The story exposes how patriarchal families prioritize sons while treating daughters—especially those with physical flaws—as useless economic burdens .Resistance Against Dowry and Social Evils: Bholi''s refusal to marry Bishambhar sends a powerful message against extortionate dowry demands and forced, unequal marriages .The Impact of Compassionate Mentorship: The teacher''s patience, gentle voice, and belief in Bholi prove that positive reinforcement can unlock hidden potential in neglected children .Character Analysis1. Sulekha / BholiEvolution: From a timid, brain-damaged, stammering victim of smallpox pockmarks to a proud, courageous, articulate young woman.Key Traits: Resilient, observant, dignified, dutiful, and uncompromising when her self-respect is threatened .2. The School TeacherTraits: Gentle, empathetic, encouraging, wise, and visionary .Role: Acts as the true architect of Bholi''s life . Unlike Bholi''s parents, she treats Bholi with affection and respect, giving her the courage to overcome her stammer and stand up against social injustice .3. Bishambhar NathTraits: Greedy, opportunistic, arrogant, self-centered, and mercenary.Role: Represents the dark reality of social extortion. He attempts to exploit Ramlal''s desperation by demanding 5,000 rupees as compensation for marrying a pockmarked girl.4. Numberdar Ramlal and his WifeTraits: Traditional, status-conscious, weak-willed, and indifferent parents .Role: Reflect patriarchal societal norms that value daughters only as marriage commodities and view education as unnecessary or harmful for women .","content":""},{"heading":"5. Important VocabularyWordContextual MeaningSimpletonA foolish, gullible, or easily deceived person (referring to Bholi''s nickname).PockmarksDeep, pitted scars left on the skin by smallpox or acne.StammerSpeak with sudden involuntary pauses and a tendency to repeat initial letters.NumberdarA village revenue official responsible for collecting taxes.MattedTangled into a thick, dense mass (referring to Bholi''s uncombed hair).SoothingHaving a gently calming, comforting effect.GinningThe process of separating cotton fibers from their seeds.ProsperousFinancially successful, wealthy, and thriving.ContemptibleDeserving of contempt, scorn, or intense dislike; despicable.MasterpieceA work of outstanding artistry, skill, or achievement.","content":""},{"heading":"6. Verified Previous-Year & Official Model Paper Questions Questions & Official Board PapersOfficial RBSE Secondary Board Paper 2024 (S-02-English)Question 1: Long Answer Question (Section D) [5 Marks in 2024 Format] ","content":"**Question:** How did Bholi’s teacher play an important role in changing the course of her life ? (Bholi) \n**Model Answer:** Bholi’s teacher played a crucial role in transforming her life from a neglected, stammering child into a confident, self-respecting woman :Kindness & Affection: On Bholi''s first day at school, when everyone else mocked her stammering, the teacher spoke in a soft, affectionate voice that Bholi had never heard before .Building Confidence: She patted Bholi gently, encouraging her to speak without fear and reassuring her that practice would cure her stammer completely .Empowerment Through Education: The teacher gave Bholi books, promising that education would earn her more respect than anyone else in the village . This constant guidance enabled Bholi to stand up against Bishambhar''s greedy dowry demands years later .Question 2: Multiple Choice Question (Section D) [1 Mark] \n\n**Question:** What was the name of Bholi ? (Bholi) \n(A) Sunita \n(B) Surekha \n(C) Sulekha \n(D) Mangla \nAnswer:\n(C) Sulekha Explanation: Bholi''s real birth name was Sulekha, though everyone called her Bholi (the simpleton) .Official RBSE Secondary Model Examination Paper 2026 (sec_model2026.pdf)Question 3: Short Answer Question (Section D) [2 Marks] \n\n**Question:** Why do Bholi''s parents accept Bishambhar''s marriage proposal ? (Bholi) \n**Model Answer:** Bholi''s parents accept Bishambhar''s marriage proposal because he was a wealthy grocer with a big shop, a house of his own, and thousands in the bank who initially did not ask for dowry . They felt that given Bholi''s pockmarked face and lack of intelligence, no one else would ever marry her .Question 4: Multiple Choice Question (Section D) [1 Mark] \n\n**Question:** What does Bholi aspire to become after refusing to marry Bishambhar ? (Bholi) \n\n\n(A) a lawyer \n(B) a doctor \n(C) a teacher \n(D) an engineer \nAnswer:\n(C) a teacher Explanation: Bholi declares that she will teach in the same school where she received her education and confidence ."},{"heading":"7. Generated Practice Questions & Predicted QuestionsShort Answer Questions (20–30 words / 2 Marks Each)Why was Sulekha nicknamed ''Bholi''?","content":"**Model Answer:** An accidental fall off a cot in infancy damaged part of her brain, making her mentally slow. Because she appeared simple and backward, everyone nicknamed her ''Bholi'' (the simpleton).Why did Ramlal''s wife agree to send Bholi to school?\n**Model Answer:** She agreed because the Tehsildar commanded Ramlal to send his daughters. Fearing no one would marry educated girls, she sent Bholi, reasoning she had no marriage prospects anyway.Why did Bholi refuse to marry Bishambhar Nath at the wedding altar?\n**Model Answer:** She refused because Bishambhar humiliated her father and greedily demanded a 5,000-rupee cash dowry upon seeing her pockmarks. She refused to marry a greedy, heartless coward.Long Answer Questions (60 words / 3 Marks Each)How does the story \"Bholi\" showcase the issue of gender discrimination and dowry in Indian society?\n**Model Answer:** The story exposes how families treat daughters as financial burdens. Bholi’s parents neglect her health and education, sending her to school only to satisfy the Tehsildar. Later, they try to marry her to a middle-aged widower simply to get rid of her. The wedding scene highlights the evil of dowry when Bishambhar demands 5,000 rupees to marry a pockmarked girl, demonstrating how women are treated as commodities.Describe Bholi''s moral growth and final decision at the climax of the narrative.\n**Model Answer:** Bholi evolves from an insecure, stammering child into a confident, self-respecting woman through education. When Bishambhar demands 5,000 rupees and humiliates her father, Bholi flings her wedding garland into the sacred fire. She boldly rejects the greedy groom, asserting that she will earn her own living, serve her aging parents, and teach at her village school."},{"heading":"8. 15-Question MCQ QuizWho is the author of the story \"Bholi\"?","content":"(A) Ruskin Bond\n(B) K.A. Abbas\n(C) Guy de Maupassant\n(D) Victor Canning\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: \"Bholi\" was written by Khwaja Ahmad Abbas (K.A. Abbas) .What was Bholi''s real birth name?\n\n\n(A) Mangla\n(B) Champa\n(C) Sulekha\n(D) Radha\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: Her real name was Sulekha .At what age did Bholi suffer an attack of smallpox?\n\n\n(A) Ten months\n(B) Two years\n(C) Five years\n(D) Seven years\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Smallpox struck her at two years of age, leaving deep pockmarks.What was Ramlal''s official designation in the village?\n\n\n(A) Tehsildar\n(B) Sarpanch\n(C) Numberdar\n(D) School principal\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: Ramlal was a revenue official known as a Numberdar.What was the name of the family''s old cow?\n\n\n(A) Laxmi\n(B) Gauri\n(C) Kamdhenu\n(D) Champa\nAnswer:\n(A) | Explanation: The old cow taken away and sold was named Laxmi.Who performed the opening ceremony of the girls'' primary school in the village?\n\n\n(A) Numberdar Ramlal\n(B) The Tehsildar\n(C) Bishambhar Nath\n(D) The Headmistress\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The Tehsildar inaugurated the new primary school.Why did Bholi''s mother agree to send Bholi to school?\n\n\n(A) She wanted Bholi to become a doctor\n(B) She feared no one would marry educated girls, but Bholi had no marriage prospects anyway\n(C) Bholi insisted on going\n(D) The teacher paid her money\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: She thought Bholi''s pockmarked face meant she had no chance of marriage regardless.How did Bholi''s teacher treat her on her first day at school?\n\n\n(A) She scolded her for stammering\n(B) She spoke in a soft, comforting voice and encouraged her\n(C) She made her sit outside\n(D) She ignored her completely\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The teacher spoke with unprecedented warmth and encouragement .What profession did Bishambhar Nath pursue?\n\n\n(A) Teacher\n(B) Grocer / Shopkeeper\n(C) Lawyer\n(D) Doctor\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Bishambhar was a prosperous grocer from a neighboring village .What physical flaw did Bishambhar Nath have?\n\n\n(A) He was blind in one eye\n(B) He limped while walking\n(C) He had no hair\n(D) He was deaf\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Bishambhar was middle-aged and limped.Why did Bishambhar demand dowry at the wedding altar?\n\n\n(A) Ramlal did not arrange a brass band\n(B) He saw Bholi''s pockmarked face when her veil slipped\n(C) Bholi refused to speak\n(D) His house burned down\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Seeing her pockmarks, he exploited the moment to demand cash.How much cash dowry did Bishambhar demand from Ramlal?\n\n\n(A) 2,000 rupees\n(B) 5,000 rupees\n(C) 10,000 rupees\n(D) 50,000 rupees\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Bishambhar insisted on receiving 5,000 rupees in cash.What symbolic gesture of submission did Ramlal make to Bishambhar?\n\n\n(A) He gave him his shop key\n(B) He placed his turban at Bishambhar''s feet\n(C) He washed his feet with water\n(D) He gave him gold rings\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Ramlal placed his turban at the groom''s feet, begging for mercy.What did Bholi do with the wedding garland?\n\n\n(A) Put it around Bishambhar''s neck\n(B) Threw it into the sacred fire\n(C) Gave it to her mother\n(D) Tore it into pieces\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: She flung the garland into the wedding fire in protest.What did Bholi resolve to do after rejecting the marriage proposal?\n\n\n(A) Become a lawyer in town\n(B) Serve her parents and teach in her village school\n(C) Open a grocery shop\n(D) Move to the city alone\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: She decided to care for her parents and work as a school teacher ."},{"heading":"9. Syllabus Coverage Checklist & Source References & Source ReferencesCoverage Checklist[x] Official RBSE 2026–27 Syllabus Verification & Chapter Inclusion Confirmation .[x] Prescribed NCERT Textbook (Footprints Without Feet) Identification & Author Mapping .[x] Detailed Plot Overview & Core Focus Areas (Bholi''s childhood, education, teacher, confidence, social discrimination, marriage proposal, final decision) .[x] Detailed Section-Wise Narrative Breakdown .[x] Themes & Character Sketches (Bholi, Teacher, Bishambhar, Ramlal) .[x] Contextual Vocabulary Glossary.[x] Authentic Past Paper (RBSE 2024 Code S-02) & Model Paper 2026 Question Integration .[x] Model Answers & Generated Practice Questions suitable for Class 10.[x] 15-Question MCQ Quiz with Explanations .Source ReferencesNCERT, New Delhi: Footprints Without Feet — Class 10 Supplementary Reader, Chapter 8 (Bholi by K.A. Abbas) .Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE), Ajmer: Class 10 English Syllabus & Blueprint (Session 2026–27) .RBSE Official Secondary Board Examination Paper 2024: Code S-02-English (LAQ Q. 17(i) & MCQ Q. 19(iii)) .RBSE Official Secondary Model Examination Paper 2026: secmodel2026.pdf (SAQ Q. 18(ii) & MCQ Q. 20(v)) .RBSE Official Research Report: Curriculum, Assessment Architecture, and Source Library (2026–2027 Session) .Missing Information NoteIndividual Lesson Mark Allocation: In Section D (Supplementary Reader), the 12 Marks total are distributed across all 9 prescribed lessons of Footprints Without Feet through 5 MCQs, 2 SAQs, and 1 LAQ on the 80-mark final paper, rather than an isolated fixed quota per individual story .💡 Next Steps: Would you like me to prepare the BoardReady learning package for Chapter 9 (The Book That Saved the Earth by Claire Boiko) to complete the entire Supplementary Reader syllabus, or generate a consolidated character comparison sheet covering key protagonists across Footprints Without Feet?keeppinSave to notekeeppincopythumbupthumbdownCreate the complete BoardReady learning package for RBSE Class 10 English, academic session 2026–27, for the lesson \"The Book That Saved the Earth\".","content":"Book: Footprints Without Feet\nLesson type: Supplementary Reader\nChapter ID: english-28\nSubject ID: english\nExplanation language: English\nRequired learning focus: Think-Tank, Noodle, the Martian invasion plan, misunderstanding of books, humour, dramatic elements and the role of knowledge.\nFirst verify that the lesson is included in the current official RBSE syllabus and confirm the prescribed textbook edition. If the lesson is excluded, report that and do not present it as required board content.\nGenerate chapter metadata, a clear overview, detailed section-wise explanations, themes, character analysis or poetic analysis as appropriate, important vocabulary, verified previous-year questions, practice questions, predicted practice questions and a 15-question MCQ quiz.\nUse the prescribed textbook and available official board papers. Do not invent quotations, events, previous-year questions, years or marks. Separate exact past questions from generated practice. Provide model answers and explanations suitable for Class 10. Respect copyright by summarising and explaining rather than reproducing the entire lesson.\nFinish with a syllabus coverage checklist, source references and missing information. Format the output for BoardReady editorial review. If the output is too long, produce one section at a time while preserving the chapter ID. Thoughts expand_more\\n"}]}'::jsonb,
