INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'english-16-notes',
  'english',
  'english-16',
  'Grammar & Writing Skills',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Grammar & Writing Skills","subject":"english","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"Full Chapter Revision Notes","content":"# RBSE Class 10 English: Chapter 16 — Grammar & Writing Skills\n* **Board**: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER, Ajmer)\n* **Subject**: English (Subject Code: 02 / S-02)\n* **Section**: Section B (Writing — 13 Marks) & Section C (Grammar — 10 Marks)\n* **Chapter ID**: `english-16`\n\n---\n\nBoardReady Learning Package: RBSE Class 10 English (2026–27)\n\n1. Syllabus & Weightage Breakdown\n- Section B (Writing — 13 Marks total):\n  1. Letter Writing (Official / Informal / Email) — 5 Marks (Word limit: ~80 words)\n  2. Story Writing (based on given outline) — 4 Marks (Word limit: ~80 words)\n  3. Short Paragraph / Picture Composition — 4 Marks (Word limit: ~60 words)\n- Section C (Grammar — 10 Marks total):\n  1. Tenses (Correct form of verbs) — 4 Marks (4 MCQs / fill in the blanks)\n  2. Reported Speech (Direct and Indirect Speech) — 2 Marks\n  3. Conjunctions (and, as well as, not only...but also, either...or, neither...nor, so...that, too...to) — 2 Marks\n  4. Relative Pronouns (who, whom, whose, which, where, that) — 2 Marks\n  5. Active and Passive Voice & Framing Questions / Question Tags — 2 Marks\n\n2. Essential Grammar Rules & Model Questions\nRule 1: Tenses (Correct Verb Forms)\n- Universal Truth / Habitual Action -> Simple Present (V1 / V1+s/es). E.g., The sun rises in the east.\n- Specific past time indicators (yesterday, ago, last year, in 2010) -> Simple Past (V2). E.g., India became free in 1947.\n- Just, already, yet, so far, recently -> Present Perfect (has/have + V3). E.g., He has just arrived.\n- Look!, Listen!, at present, now -> Present Continuous (is/am/are + V1+ing). E.g., Listen! Someone is knocking.\n- Two past actions connected by ''before''/''after'': The past perfect (had + V3) comes before simple past (V2). E.g., The train had left before we reached the station.\n\nRule 2: Direct and Indirect Speech\n- Reporting verb in Past (said):\n  - Simple Present -> Simple Past\n  - Present Continuous -> Past Continuous\n  - Present Perfect -> Past Perfect\n  - Simple Past -> Past Perfect\n- Connectives: ''that'' for statements; ''if''/''whether'' for yes/no questions; wh-word for wh-questions; ''to + V1'' for imperatives.\n\nRule 3: Active and Passive Voice\n- Object of active becomes subject of passive.\n- Appropriate form of ''be'' + Past Participle (V3).\n- E.g., Active: \"She wrote a letter.\" -> Passive: \"A letter was written by her.\"\n\n3. Writing Section Formats & Model Samples\nSample 1: Formal Letter / Email Writing (5 Marks)\nTo: principal.gsss@rajasthan.gov.in\nSubject: Application for issue of Transfer Certificate (T.C.)\nRespected Sir,\nWith due respect, I beg to state that my father, who is a government servant, has been transferred from Ajmer to Jaipur. Our whole family is shifting there next week. Therefore, I cannot continue my studies in your esteemed school.\nKindly issue me my Transfer Certificate (T.C.) and character certificate at an early date so that I may seek admission in a government school at Jaipur. All my school dues have been cleared.\nThanking you.\nYours obediently,\nRahul Sharma (Class 10-A, Roll No. 18)\n\nSample 2: Story Writing (4 Marks)\nTitle: The Honest Woodcutter\nOutline: A poor woodcutter... cuts wood by the river... axe falls into water... weeps... Mercury appears... brings gold axe... woodcutter refuses... brings silver axe... refuses... brings iron axe... happily accepts... god pleased with honesty... gives all three axes...\nMoral: Honesty is the best policy.\n\n4. 15-Question MCQ Quiz on Grammar\n1. The sun _______ in the east. (rise)\n(A) rose\n(B) rises\n(C) is rising\n(D) had risen\nAnswer:\n(B) 2. India _______ freedom in 1947. (get)\n\n\n(A) got\n(B) gets\n(C) had got\n(D) has got\nAnswer:\n(A) 3. She _______ her homework just now. (complete)\n\n\n(A) completed\n(B) has completed\n(C) is completing\n(D) had completed\nAnswer:\n(B) 4. The patient had died before the doctor _______. (arrive)\n\n\n(A) arrived\n(B) had arrived\n(C) arrives\n(D) was arriving\nAnswer:\n(A) 5. Listen! The bird _______. (sing)\n\n\n(A) sang\n(B) is singing\n(C) sings\n(D) has sung\nAnswer:\n(B) 6. He said to me, \"Where do you live?\" (Indirect Speech)\n\n\n(A) He asked me where I lived.\n(B) He asked me where did I live.\n(C) He told me where I live.\n(D) He asked me that where I lived.\nAnswer:\n(A) 7. Ram said, \"I am reading a novel.\" (Indirect Speech)\n\n\n(A) Ram said that he is reading a novel.\n(B) Ram said that he was reading a novel.\n(C) Ram asked if he was reading a novel.\n(D) Ram told that he read a novel.\nAnswer:\n(B) 8. Change into passive: \"The peon rang the bell.\"\n\n\n(A) The bell rang by the peon.\n(B) The bell was rung by the peon.\n(C) The bell is rung by peon.\n(D) The bell had rung.\nAnswer:\n(B) 9. Change into passive: \"Open the door.\"\n\n\n(A) Let the door be opened.\n(B) You should open door.\n(C) The door is opened.\n(D) Let door opened.\nAnswer:\n(A) 10. Combine with ''who'': \"This is the boy. He won the gold medal.\"\n\n\n(A) This is the boy who won the gold medal.\n(B) This is the boy whom won the gold medal.\n(C) This is the boy whose won the gold medal.\n(D) This is the boy which won medal.\nAnswer:\n(A) 11. He is _______ weak _______ walk.\n\n\n(A) so...that\n(B) too...to\n(C) neither...nor\n(D) either...or\nAnswer:\n(B) 12. Give the question tag: \"She is a teacher, _______?\"\n\n\n(A) is she\n(B) isn''t she\n(C) wasn''t she\n(D) doesn''t she\nAnswer:\n(B) 13. Frame question for answer: \"I live in Jaipur.\"\n\n\n(A) Where do you live?\n(B) Where are you living?\n(C) How do you live?\n(D) Why do you live?\nAnswer:\n(A) 14. Neither Mohan _______ his friends were present.\n\n\n(A) or\n(B) nor\n(C) and\n(D) but\nAnswer:\n(B) 15. He worked hard _______ he failed.\n\n\n(A) but\n(B) because\n(C) so\n(D) and\nAnswer:\n(A) \n\n"}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'english-17-notes',
  'english',
  'english-17',
  'A Question of Trust',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"A Question of Trust","subject":"english","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"1. Syllabus Verification & Textbook ConfirmationSyllabus Inclusion Status: Prescribed / Included1.Board: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER, Ajmer)4.Academic Session: 2026–2027 (Targeting the 2027 Secondary School Examination)4.Subject: English (Subject Code: 02 / S-02)4.Prescribed Textbook: NCERT Footprints Without Feet (Supplementary Reader, Chapter 4)7.Chapter Title: A Question of Trust7.Chapter ID: english-23Subject ID: englishAuthor: Victor Canning7.Lesson Type: Supplementary Reader / Crime Fiction & Dramatic Irony7.Mark Weightage Context: Section D (Literature — Footprints Without Feet) carries 12 Marks in total across:5 Factual MCQs ($\\text{1 mark } \\times 5 = 5\\text{ Marks}$)12.2 Short Answer Questions ($\\text{2 marks } \\times 2 = 4\\text{ Marks}$)1.1 Character/Plot/Situation Long Answer Question ($\\text{3 Marks}$)1.","content":""},{"heading":"2. Chapter Metadata & Overview & OverviewMetadataChapter ID: english-23Title: A Question of TrustAuthor: Victor CanningGenre: Crime Mystery / Short Story / Dramatic IronyKey Setting: Shotover Grange (a wealthy country house)Key Characters:Horace Danby: A 50-year-old unmarried, respectable locksmith who secretly robs one safe a year to buy rare, expensive books.The Young Lady in Red: A clever, charming, confident woman thief who poses as the owner of Shotover Grange.Sherry: The pet dog at Shotover Grange.The Housekeeper: Horace''s elderly housekeeper who worries about his health.OverviewA Question of Trust by Victor Canning is a humorous and ironic crime story that turns the phrase \"honor among thieves\" on its head7.The protagonist, Horace Danby, is a 50-year-old locksmith who is widely regarded as a good, respectable citizen16. However, he hides a secret double life: he suffers from a passion for rare, expensive books and plans one careful bank/safe robbery every year to fund his collection19.For his annual robbery, Horace targets Shotover Grange, meticulously studying its layout, wiring, and schedule for two weeks. When the servants leave for the movies, Horace breaks in. However, flowers on the table trigger his severe hay fever, causing him to sneeze repeatedly. Hearing the sneezes, a graceful young woman in a red dress enters, carrying the pet dog Sherry. She poses convincingly as the lady of the house, treating Horace with cool authority.When Horace begs for mercy, she promises to let him walk free if he opens the safe for her, claiming she forgot the combination and needs her jewels for a party that night. Naively trusting her, Horace takes off his gloves and cracking tools to open the safe. Two days later, Horace is arrested by the police—his fingerprints were found all over the room. The real owner of Shotover Grange turns out to be a 60-year-old gray-haired woman. Horace realizes he was outsmarted by a rival thief, ending up as an assistant librarian in prison, deeply angered whenever anyone mentions \"honor among thieves\"7.","content":""},{"heading":"3. Detailed Section-Wise Explanations                     HORACE DANBY''S DOWNFALL","content":"│\n  ┌─────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┐\n  ▼                             ▼                             ▼\nPHASE 1: THE PLANNING     PHASE 2: THE ENCOUNTER        PHASE 3: THE DECEPTION\n• Studies Shotover       • Sneezes from hay fever     • Woman poses as lady \n  Grange for 2 weeks.      triggered by flowers.        of the house.\n• Target: ₹15,000 in     • Young woman in red dress   • Asks Horace to open safe;\n  jewels for rare books.   enters with Sherry.          he removes gloves!\n                                                              │\n                                                              ▼\n                                                        PHASE 4: ARREST\n                                                      • Fingerprints left behind.\n                                                      • Real owner is 60 yrs old.\n                                                      • Prison librarian role."},{"heading":"Section 1: Horace Danby’s Double LifePublic Identity: Horace Danby is 50 years old, unmarried, and lives with a housekeeper who worries about his health. He runs a successful lock-making business with two assistants and is viewed by society as good and respectable16.Secret Passion: Horace is an avid collector of rare, valuable books. Because he cannot afford them on his legitimate income, he robs one safe every year19. He plans each theft so meticulously that he steals just enough to last 12 months, purchasing his desired books through an agent19.Ethical Boundary: Horace is not a habitual, violent criminal; he robs only wealthy people, never uses weapons, and hates the idea of prison.Section 2: Planning the Shotover Grange RobberyThe Target: Horace decides to rob the safe at Shotover Grange, which contains jewels worth about 15,000 pounds. Selling them even for half price would buy the three rare books coming up for auction in autumn.Preparation: Horace spends two full weeks studying the house—its rooms, electric wiring, paths, and garden. He learns that the family is in London and the two servants are out at the movies.Entry: He cuts the burglar alarm wire, puts on a pair of rubber gloves, and enters through the kitchen door using a duplicate key.Section 3: The Robbery and Hay Fever AttackThe Flaw in Execution: Horace notices a large bowl of flowers sitting on the table in the drawing-room. Suffering from hay fever, the pollen causes a tickle in his nose, forcing him to sneeze loudly again and again.The Arrival of the Lady in Red: A quiet, confident voice speaks to him from the doorway: \"What is it? A cold or hay fever?\" A young, charming woman dressed in a bright red dress steps into the room, calming the barking pet dog, Sherry.The Impersonation: She acts completely at ease, speaking with the authority of the mistress of the house returning unexpectedly from London.Section 4: The Misplaced Trust and the TrapHorace''s Plea: Panicked at the threat of prison, Horace begs her to let him go, promising never to steal again.The Bargain: The woman claims she needs her jewels that very night for a party, but forgot the numerical combination to her own safe. She promises to let Horace leave quietly if he opens the safe for her.The Fatal Mistake: Eager to escape arrest, Horace agrees. In his rush and excitement, he takes off his rubber gloves to handle his fine tools and open the safe without leaving glove marks on the dial. He hands her the jewels and leaves happily, believing he has secured his freedom.Section 5: The Arrest and Final IronyThe Investigation: Two days later, a policeman arrests Horace for the jewel robbery at Shotover Grange.The Evidence: Horace''s fingerprints were found all over the drawing-room, table, and safe because he had opened it bare-handed!The Revelation: When Horace tries to explain that he opened the safe for the owner''s wife, the real mistress of the house—a 60-year-old, sharp-tongued woman with gray hair—denies his story completely.Outcome: Horace is sentenced to prison, where he becomes the assistant librarian. He frequently thinks about the clever young lady in red who tricked him, getting furious whenever anyone speaks of \"honor among thieves\"7.","content":""},{"heading":"4. Themes & Character Analysis & Key ConceptsCore ThemesDramatic Irony & The Cheat Cheated: Horace believes he is the smartest thief in the room, yet he falls victim to a far more cunning, theatrical imposter7.Deceptive Appearances: Just as Horace''s respectable locksmith facade hides a thief, the young woman''s charming, elegant demeanor hides a ruthless criminal16.Misplaced Trust & \"Honor Among Thieves\": Horace naively trusts a fellow criminal based on a verbal promise, proving that there is no guaranteed honor in the world of crime7.Character Analysis1. Horace DanbyTraits: Meticulous, polite, non-violent, educated, naive, book-loving19.Duality: A \"respectable\" citizen who makes locks by day, but breaks safes once a year to fuel his intellectual addiction to rare literature16. His fatal flaw is his gullibility when under pressure.2. The Young Lady in RedTraits: Clever, calm, manipulative, highly observant, theatrical, fearless.Role: The ultimate antagonist who uses psychology, charm, and Horace''s hay fever against him. She tricks Horace into doing the physical work and leaving incriminating fingerprints while she walks away with 15,000 pounds in jewels.","content":""},{"heading":"5. Important VocabularyWordContextual MeaningLocksmithA skilled person who makes, repairs, and opens locks11.Hay feverAn allergic condition caused by pollen/dust, causing sneezing and watery eyes.GrangeA large country house with farm buildings attached (e.g., Shotover Grange).ConnoisseurAn expert judge in matters of fine art, taste, or rare books.SafeA strong metal cabinet with a combination lock used to store money or jewels.FingerprintsUnique ridge marks on human fingertips used by police for identification.Honor among thievesThe idea that even criminals maintain a code of honesty among themselves.","content":""},{"heading":"6. Verified Previous-Year & Official Model Paper Questions Questions & Official Board PapersOfficial RBSE Board Paper 2024 (S-02-English)Question 1: Short Answer Question (Section D) [2 Marks]16","content":"**Question:** “Horace Danby was good and respectable – but not completely honest.” Explain. [A Question of Trust]16\n**Model Answer:** Horace Danby was considered good and respectable because he was a 50-year-old successful locksmith who ran a good business16. However, he was not completely honest because he secretly robbed one safe every year to buy rare and expensive books16.Question 2: Short Answer Question (Section D) [2 Marks]19\n\n**Question:** Why does Horace Danby steal every year ? (A Question of Trust)19\n**Model Answer:** Horace Danby steals every year because he has a passionate love for rare and expensive books19. He steals just enough money to buy the books coming up for auction, managing his expenses through a secret agent19.Official RBSE Board Paper 2025 (S-02-English)Question 3: Long Answer / Character Comparison (Section D) [3 Marks]24\n\n**Question:** Character evaluation of Horace Danby and Hari Singh.24\n**Model Answer:** Both Horace Danby and Hari Singh are thieves, but they differ in motivation and character24:Horace Danby: A 50-year-old respectable locksmith who steals once a year exclusively to fund his passion for rare books16. He is non-violent, plans methodically, but is tricked by a clever rival thief7.Hari Singh: A 15-year-old experienced thief who steals for survival7. Unlike Horace, Hari undergoes a moral transformation when touched by Anil''s trust and literacy, choosing honesty over crime7.Official RBSE Secondary Model Examination Paper 2026 (sec_model2026.pdf)Question 4: Multiple Choice Question (Section D) [1 Mark]11\n\n**Question:** What was Horace Danby''s profession ? (A Question of Trust)11\n(A) doctor11\n(B) locksmith11\n(C) watch repairer11\n(D) owner of a rare book business11\nAnswer:\n(B) locksmith11Explanation: Horace Danby ran a successful lock-making business as a locksmith11."},{"heading":"7. Generated Practice Questions & Predicted QuestionsShort Answer Questions (20–30 words / 2 Marks Each)How did the flowers in the drawing-room ruin Horace Danby''s plan?","content":"**Model Answer:** Horace suffered from hay fever. The fragrance of the flowers in the drawing-room triggered a severe allergy attack, causing him to sneeze repeatedly and alert the lady in red.Why did Horace Danby remove his rubber gloves while opening the safe?\n**Model Answer:** Horace removed his gloves to handle his fine cracking tools easily when the young lady asked him to open the safe, accidentally leaving his fingerprints everywhere.Why does Horace Danby get angry whenever anyone mentions ''honor among thieves''?\n**Model Answer:** He gets angry because he was outsmarted and framed by a fellow thief—the young lady in red—who tricked him into opening the safe and stole the jewels herself.Long Answer Questions (60 words / 3 Marks Each)Describe how the young lady in red tricked Horace Danby into opening the safe.\n**Model Answer:** The young lady in red entered the drawing-room with extreme confidence and composure, holding the pet dog Sherry. She spoke with the natural authority of the house owner. When Horace pleaded to be spared from prison, she offered to let him go if he opened the safe, claiming she forgot the combination. Naively trusting her, Horace removed his gloves, cracked the safe, and handed her the jewels, leaving his fingerprints for the police.How does \"A Question of Trust\" demonstrate the concept of dramatic irony?\n**Model Answer:** The story uses dramatic irony by presenting a thief who believes he has planned the perfect robbery, only to be outwitted by a rival thief. Horace Danby takes great pride in his meticulous preparation and non-violent ethics. Yet, he fails to recognize that the charming woman in red is an imposter. The ultimate irony is that Horace ends up in prison for a robbery where he walked away empty-handed."},{"heading":"8. 15-Question MCQ QuizWho wrote the short story \"A Question of Trust\"?","content":"(A) Ruskin Bond\n(B) Victor Canning\n(C) Robert Arthur\n(D) James Herriot\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: \"A Question of Trust\" was written by Victor Canning7.How old was Horace Danby?\n\n\n(A) 25 years old\n(B) 30 years old\n(C) 50 years old\n(D) 60 years old\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: Horace Danby was about fifty years old and unmarried.What was Horace Danby''s primary profession?\n\n\n(A) Doctor\n(B) Locksmith\n(C) Teacher\n(D) Book seller\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Horace ran a successful lock-making business as a locksmith11.What physical health problem did Horace Danby suffer from?\n\n\n(A) Asthma\n(B) Hay fever\n(C) Diabetes\n(D) High blood pressure\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Horace suffered from hay fever, an allergy triggered by flower pollen.Why did Horace Danby rob a safe every year?\n\n\n(A) To buy expensive cars\n(B) To buy rare and expensive books\n(C) To pay off gambling debts\n(D) To build a big mansion\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He stole exclusively to purchase rare, valuable books19.Which house did Horace Danby target for his annual robbery?\n\n\n(A) Shotover Grange\n(B) London House\n(C) Paris Hotel\n(D) Crimson Manor\nAnswer:\n(A) | Explanation: Horace studied and targeted Shotover Grange.How much were the jewels in the Shotover Grange safe worth?\n\n\n(A) 5,000 pounds\n(B) 10,000 pounds\n(C) 15,000 pounds\n(D) 50,000 pounds\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: Textually, the jewels were worth about 15,000 pounds.What was the name of the pet dog at Shotover Grange?\n\n\n(A) Tricki\n(B) Sherry\n(C) Joe\n(D) Caesar\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The dog at Shotover Grange was named Sherry.Where was the safe located at Shotover Grange?\n\n\n(A) In the bedroom cupboard\n(B) Behind a poor painting in the drawing-room\n(C) In the basement vault\n(D) Under the floorboards\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The safe sat in the drawing-room behind a poor painting.What color dress was the young woman wearing when she confronted Horace?\n\n\n(A) Blue\n(B) Red\n(C) Black\n(D) Yellow\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: She entered dressed in a bright red dress.Why did Horace take off his rubber gloves?\n\n\n(A) Because his hands were sweating\n(B) To open the safe for the young lady\n(C) To pet the dog Sherry\n(D) To write a note\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He foolishly took off his gloves to open the safe dial for her.How did the police track down Horace Danby two days later?\n\n\n(A) The lady reported his vehicle number\n(B) Through his fingerprints left all over the room\n(C) A neighbor saw him enter\n(D) He dropped his wallet\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: His bare fingerprints were found all over the drawing-room.How old was the REAL owner of Shotover Grange?\n\n\n(A) 25 years old\n(B) 40 years old\n(C) 60 years old\n(D) 80 years old\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: The real owner was a 60-year-old, gray-haired woman.What job does Horace Danby do in prison at the end of the story?\n\n\n(A) Lock repairer\n(B) Assistant librarian\n(C) Cook\n(D) Gardener\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Horace becomes the assistant librarian in prison.What phrase makes Horace Danby angry at the end of the story?\n\n\n(A) \"Practice makes perfect\"\n(B) \"Honor among thieves\"\n(C) \"Honesty is the best policy\"\n(D) \"Crime does not pay\"\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He gets furious when anyone talks about \"honor among thieves\"7."},{"heading":"9. Syllabus Coverage Checklist & Source References & Source ReferencesCoverage Checklist[x] Official RBSE 2026–27 Syllabus Verification & Chapter Inclusion Confirmation1.[x] Prescribed NCERT Textbook (Footprints Without Feet) Identification & Author Mapping7.[x] Detailed Plot Overview & Core Focus Areas (Horace Danby, robbery, woman posing as owner, deception, irony, consequences of misplaced trust)7.[x] Detailed Section-Wise Narrative Breakdown7.[x] Themes & Character Sketches (Horace Danby, Young Lady in Red)16.[x] Contextual Vocabulary Glossary11.[x] Authentic Past Paper (RBSE 2024 & 2025 Code S-02) & Model Paper 2026 Integration11.[x] Model Answers & Generated Practice Questions suitable for Class 107.[x] 15-Question MCQ Quiz with Explanations11.Source ReferencesNCERT, New Delhi: Footprints Without Feet — Class 10 Supplementary Reader, Chapter 4 (A Question of Trust by Victor Canning)7.Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE), Ajmer: Class 10 English Syllabus & Blueprint (Session 2026–27)4.RBSE Official Secondary Board Examination Paper 2025: Code S-02-English (Character Evaluation)24.RBSE Official Secondary Board Examination Paper 2024: Code S-02-English (Q. 17(ii), Q. 18(iii))16.RBSE Official Secondary Model Examination Paper 2026: secmodel2026.pdf (MCQ Q. 20(iii))11.RBSE Official Research Report: Curriculum, Assessment Architecture, and Source Library (2026–2027 Session)4.Missing Information NoteIndividual Lesson Mark Allocation: In Section D (Supplementary Reader), the 12 Marks total are distributed across all 9 prescribed lessons of Footprints Without Feet through 5 MCQs, 2 SAQs, and 1 LAQ on the 80-mark final paper, rather than an isolated fixed quota per individual story1.💡 Next Steps: Would you like me to prepare the BoardReady learning package for Chapter 5 (Footprints Without Feet by H.G. Wells) or Chapter 6 (The Making of a Scientist by Robert W. Peterson)?keeppinSave to notekeeppincopythumbupthumbdownCreate the complete BoardReady learning package for RBSE Class 10 English, academic session 2026–27, for the lesson \"Footprints Without Feet\".","content":"Book: Footprints Without Feet\nLesson type: Supplementary Reader\nChapter ID: english-24\nSubject ID: english\nExplanation language: English\nRequired learning focus: Griffin''s invisibility, scientific misuse, the thefts, conflict, character motivations and the consequences of irresponsible science.\nFirst verify that the lesson is included in the current official RBSE syllabus and confirm the prescribed textbook edition. If the lesson is excluded, report that and do not present it as required board content.\nGenerate chapter metadata, a clear overview, detailed section-wise explanations, themes, character analysis or poetic analysis as appropriate, important vocabulary, verified previous-year questions, practice questions, predicted practice questions and a 15-question MCQ quiz.\nUse the prescribed textbook and available official board papers. Do not invent quotations, events, previous-year questions, years or marks. Separate exact past questions from generated practice. Provide model answers and explanations suitable for Class 10. Respect copyright by summarising and explaining rather than reproducing the entire lesson.\nFinish with a syllabus coverage checklist, source references and missing information. Format the output for BoardReady editorial review. If the output is too long, produce one section at a time while preserving the chapter ID. Thoughts expand_more\\n"}]}'::jsonb,
  'approved',
  '["RBSE Official Curriculum 2026-27"]'::jsonb
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET
  content = EXCLUDED.content,
  status = 'approved';
INSERT INTO public.content_items (id, subject_id, chapter_id, chapter_name, content_type, content, status, source_refs)
VALUES (
  'english-18-notes',
  'english',
  'english-18',
  'Footprints Without Feet',
  'notes',
  '{"title":"Footprints Without Feet","subject":"english","readTime":"12 mins","sections":[{"heading":"1. Syllabus Verification & Textbook ConfirmationSyllabus Inclusion Status: Prescribed / Included .Board: Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE / BSER, Ajmer) .Academic Session: 2026–2027 (Targeting the 2027 Secondary School Examination) .Subject: English (Subject Code: 02 / S-02) .Prescribed Textbook: NCERT Footprints Without Feet (Supplementary Reader, Chapter 5) .Chapter Title: Footprints Without Feet .Chapter ID: english-24Subject ID: englishAuthor: H.G. Wells (Herbert George Wells) .Lesson Type: Supplementary Reader / Science Fiction & Moral Tale .Mark Weightage Context: Section D (Literature — Footprints Without Feet) carries 12 Marks in total across:5 Factual MCQs ($\\text{1 mark } \\times 5 = 5\\text{ Marks}$) .2 Short Answer Questions ($\\text{2 marks } \\times 2 = 4\\text{ Marks}$) .1 Character/Plot/Situation Long Answer Question ($\\text{3 Marks}$) .","content":""},{"heading":"2. Chapter Metadata & Overview & OverviewMetadataChapter ID: english-24Title: Footprints Without FeetAuthor: H.G. WellsGenre: Science Fiction / Moral & Psychological NarrativeKey Settings: Streets of London, a big London department store, a theatrical shop in Drury Lane, and the rural village of Iping in SussexKey Characters:Griffin: A brilliant but lawless, short-tempered scientist who discovers the secret of human invisibility.Mrs. Hall: The inquisitive, money-minded, and firm landlady of the local inn at Iping.Mr. Hall: Mrs. Hall''s husband who assists at the inn.Rev. Mr. Bunting: The clergyman at Iping whose house is burglarized.Mr. Bobby Jaffers: The dutiful village constable who attempts to arrest Griffin.OverviewFootprints Without Feet by H.G. Wells is a classic science-fiction story that warns against the dangers of misusing scientific discoveries . The narrative follows Griffin, a brilliant scientist who carries out experiments to make the human body transparent and invisible. He swallows rare chemical drugs, causing his body to become as transparent as a sheet of glass while remaining as solid as glass.However, rather than using his discovery for the welfare of humanity, Griffin reveals a deeply lawless and vengeful nature. When his landlord attempts to evict him, Griffin burns the landlord''s house down in revenge. To escape detection, he sheds his clothes and becomes a naked, invisible wanderer in the freezing London winter.After seeking warmth and stolen supplies in a London department store and robbing a theatrical shop in Drury Lane (disguising his invisible head with bandages, dark glasses, a false nose, and side-whiskers), Griffin flees the city noise to the quiet village of Iping. He rents two rooms at Mrs. Hall''s inn. His eccentric behavior, strange appearance, and sudden cash transactions arouse suspicion. When his stolen money runs out, Griffin robs the clergyman''s house. Suspicion falls directly on him, leading to a dramatic confrontation with Mrs. Hall and Constable Jaffers. Griffin removes his clothes and bandages, turning into a \"headless\" and invisible force, overpowering the village constable and escaping once again into thin air .","content":""},{"heading":"3. Detailed Section-Wise Explanations                     GRIFFIN''S ESCALATION OF LAWLESSNESS","content":"│\n  ┌───────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┐\n  ▼                                   ▼                                   ▼\nPHASE 1: THE DISCOVERY      PHASE 2: LONDON ESCAPADES     PHASE 3: IPING & ESCAPE\n• Formulates invisibility   • Arson; wanders naked.       • Takes rooms at Iping inn.\n  drug.                     • Robs store & Drury Lane     • Robs clergyman''s desk.\n• Becomes transparent as      theatrical shop for         • Knocks out Constable Jaffers;\n  sheet of glass.             clothes & disguise.           escapes invisible."},{"heading":"Section 1: The Discovery and Arson in LondonThe Scientific Breakthrough: Griffin carries out years of research and successfully formulates rare drugs that can make the human body completely transparent.Lawless Temperament: Griffin is a \"lawless person\". His landlord dislikes him and tries to eject him from the house. In revenge, Griffin sets fire to the house.The Cost of Invisibility: To walk away unseen, Griffin has to strip off all his clothes. Consequently, he becomes a homeless, penniless, naked wanderer in London during the mid-winter cold.Section 2: Adventures in the London Store and Drury LaneThe Muddy Footprints: Two young boys in London are astonished to see fresh, muddy footprints appearing on a doorstep without seeing any person making them. They follow the prints until the marks fade completely.Shelter in the Store: Seeking relief from the icy cold, Griffin sneaks into a big London department store at closing time. He breaks open boxes, clothes himself in warm garments, shoes, and an overcoat, and feeds on cold meat, coffee, sweets, and wine from the grocery store, falling asleep on a pile of quilts.Discovery and Flight: The next morning, store assistants discover him sleeping. Panic-stricken, Griffin strips off his newly found clothes to become invisible again and escapes into the cold air.Drury Lane Disguise: To cover his invisible face and head, Griffin enters a theatrical shop in Drury Lane. He steals bandages to wrap around his forehead, dark spectacles, a false nose, big bushy side-whiskers, and a large hat. Before leaving, he callously attacks the shopkeeper from behind and robs him of all his money.Section 3: The Arrival at Iping VillageSeeking Solitude: Wanting to get away from crowded London, Griffin takes a train to the rural village of Iping and books two rooms at the local inn owned by Mrs. Hall.Mrs. Hall''s Suspicion: Mrs. Hall tries to be friendly, but Griffin curtly tells her that he came to Iping for solitude and does not wish to be disturbed. His bandages and strange appearance make the villagers view him as an eccentric eccentric with a facial deformity.Running Out of Cash: Griffin pays his inn bill in advance with stolen money. When the cash runs out, he lies to Mrs. Hall, claiming he is expecting a remittance (money transfer) at any moment.Section 4: The Clergyman''s House Burglary & The Extraordinary ChairThe Theft: Early one morning, the clergyman (Rev. Mr. Bunting) and his wife hear noises in their study. They hear the chink of money being taken from the desk. Holding a poker, the clergyman bursts into the room shouting \"Surrender!\", only to find the room completely empty. The money has vanished without a soul in sight.The Inn Room Investigation: That same morning, Mr. and Mrs. Hall find Griffin''s bedroom door wide open. Investigating inside, they notice his bedclothes are cold, and his bandages and clothes are scattered across the floor.The Supernatural Attack: Suddenly, Mrs. Hall hears a sniff close to her ear. Moments later, the hat on the bedpost leaps up and dashes itself into her face! The bedroom chair then springs to life, charging at her legs and pushing both Mr. and Mrs. Hall out of the room before locking the door behind them. Mrs. Hall faints, convinced the room is haunted by spirits.Section 5: The Climax and the Invisible EscapeThe Confrontation: After the burglary news spreads, suspicion falls squarely on Griffin because he suddenly produces ready cash to pay his outstanding inn bill. Mrs. Hall directly demands to know how he exited an empty room and how he entered a locked one.Losing Temper: Furious at her questioning, Griffin shouts: \"You don’t understand who or what I am!\" He rips off his bandages, false nose, spectacles, and whiskers. The horrified villagers in the bar find themselves staring at a headless man!Constable Jaffers'' Arrival: Bobby Jaffers, the village constable, arrives with a warrant to arrest the suspect. Jaffers is amazed to find he has to arrest a man without a head, but he firmly resolves to do his duty.The Final Struggle: A chaotic struggle ensues. Griffin strips off his garments one by one. As each garment falls, he becomes increasingly invisible. Jaffers attempts to grapple with an unseen opponent, receiving blows from nowhere until he is knocked unconscious. Griffin escapes into the countryside, invisible and free once more.","content":""},{"heading":"4. Themes & Character Analysis & Key ConceptsCore ThemesMisuse of Scientific Knowledge: Science is a powerful tool meant for the betterment of society. When wielded by an irresponsible, self-centered individual, it becomes a dangerous force of destruction.Lawlessness vs. Social Order: Invisibility releases Griffin from social accountability. Without moral integrity, the absence of fear of punishment leads directly to crime, theft, and violence.The Isolation of Arrogance: Griffin''s intellectual superiority makes him detached, antisocial, and megalomaniacal, eventually reducing him to a hunted, naked fugitive.Character Analysis1. GriffinTraits: Brilliant, secretive, short-tempered, arrogant, lawless, and violent.Duality: A scientific genius who achieves an unprecedented discovery, yet morally bankrupt. His invisibility reveals his inner darkness—he uses his power to commit arson, theft, assault, and terrorize innocent villagers.2. Mrs. HallTraits: Practical, hospitable, observant, money-minded, and courageous.Role: As the landlady of the Iping inn, she tolerates Griffin''s rudeness as long as he pays cash. However, when she suspects foul play and encounters supernatural occurrences, she confronts him directly.3. Bobby JaffersTraits: Dutiful, brave, conscientious, and uncompromising.Role: The village constable who demonstrates high professional integrity. He attempts to arrest Griffin regardless of whether the suspect has a head or is invisible, stating that a warrant must be executed.","content":""},{"heading":"5. Important VocabularyWordContextual MeaningTransparentAllowing light to pass through so that objects behind can be distinctly seen.GazingLooking steadily and intently at something in surprise or admiration.EccentricUnconventional, strange, or unusual in behavior or appearance.SolitudeThe state or situation of being alone, away from other people.RemittanceA sum of money sent in payment or as a gift.ClergymanA male priest, minister, or religious official in the Christian Church.PokerA metal rod used for stirring a fire.IndignantlyIn a manner indicating anger or annoyance at unfair treatment.HystericAffected by wildly uncontrollable emotion or panic.HeadlessHaving no head (describing Griffin when his head garments are removed).","content":""},{"heading":"6. Verified Previous-Year & Official Model Paper Questions Questions & Official Board PapersOfficial RBSE Secondary Model Examination Paper 2026 (secmodel2026.pdf)Question 1: Multiple Choice Question (Section D) [1 Mark] ","content":"**Question:** What discovery did Griffin make ? (Footprints without Feet) \n(A) He could change metals into gold \n(B) He could make himself invisible \n(C) He could fly in the sky\n(D) He could read minds\nAnswer:\n(B) He could make himself invisible Explanation: Griffin carried out experiments to prove that the human body could become transparent and invisible .Official RBSE Secondary Board Paper 2024 (S-02-English)Question 2: Short Answer Question (Section D) [2 Marks] \n\n**Question:** Why were the two boys in London surprised and fascinated? (Footprints without Feet) \n**Model Answer:** The two boys were surprised and fascinated because they saw fresh, muddy footprints appearing step-by-step on the stairs of a house, but could not see the person making them .Question 3: Short Answer Question (Section D) [2 Marks] \n\n**Question:** Why did Griffin set fire to his landlord’s house? (Footprints without Feet) \n**Model Answer:** Griffin set fire to his landlord''s house in revenge because his landlord disliked him and tried to eject him from the property ."},{"heading":"7. Generated Practice Questions & Predicted QuestionsShort Answer Questions (20–30 words / 2 Marks Each)How did Griffin become invisible?","content":"**Model Answer:** Griffin swallowed certain rare chemical drugs he had formulated, causing his body to become as transparent and solid as a sheet of glass .Why was Griffin wandering the streets of London without clothes or money?\n**Model Answer:** After setting fire to his landlord''s house, Griffin had to strip off all his clothes to become invisible and escape without being caught .What extraordinary sight did the clergyman and his wife witness in their study?\n**Model Answer:** They heard the chink of money being taken from the desk, but when they opened the door, the room was completely empty with no one in sight.Why did Mrs. Hall think her guest was an eccentric scientist?\n**Model Answer:** She thought he was eccentric because of his strange wrapped bandages, dark spectacles, refusal to talk, and his desire for absolute solitude .Long Answer Questions (60 words / 3 Marks Each)\"Griffin was a brilliant scientist, but a lawless person.\" Justify this statement with examples from the text.\n**Model Answer:** Griffin proved his scientific brilliance by discovering a formula to make the human body transparent . However, his lawless nature ruined his achievement . He committed arson by setting fire to his landlord''s house, robbed a London store, assaulted and robbed a shopkeeper in Drury Lane, stole money from a clergyman''s desk in Iping, and knocked out the village constable . Instead of serving society, he used science to commit crimes .Describe the scene in Mrs. Hall’s inn when Griffin’s room appeared to be haunted.\n**Model Answer:** Finding Griffin''s bedroom door open, Mr. and Mrs. Hall went inside to investigate . Suddenly, Mrs. Hall heard a sniff near her ear, and Griffin''s hat leaped off the bedpost, striking her face . The bedroom chair then came alive, pushing them both out of the room and locking the door . Mrs. Hall fainted, believing the furniture was inhabited by spirits ."},{"heading":"8. 15-Question MCQ QuizWho is the author of \"Footprints Without Feet\"?","content":"(A) Ruskin Bond\n(B) H.G. Wells\n(C) Robert Arthur\n(D) Victor Canning\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The story was written by H.G. Wells .What discovery did Griffin make?\n\n\n(A) How to make metals into gold\n(B) How to make the human body invisible\n(C) How to cure incurable diseases\n(D) How to travel in time\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Griffin discovered a way to make the human body transparent .Why did Griffin set fire to his landlord''s house?\n\n\n(A) By accident\n(B) In revenge because the landlord tried to eject him\n(C) To test his invisibility\n(D) To claim insurance money\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He burned the house in revenge after the landlord tried to evict him .What did Griffin have to do to become completely invisible?\n\n\n(A) Wear a special cap\n(B) Take off all his clothes\n(C) Stay in the dark\n(D) Close his eyes\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: His body was transparent, but any clothes he wore remained visible .Where did Griffin first seek warmth and shelter in London?\n\n\n(A) At an inn\n(B) Inside a big London department store\n(C) At a friend''s house\n(D) In a church\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He slipped into a big London store at closing time .In which street did Griffin find a theatrical goods shop?\n\n\n(A) Baker Street\n(B) Drury Lane\n(C) Oxford Street\n(D) Regent Street\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He visited a theatrical shop in Drury Lane .Which item did Griffin NOT use to disguise his invisible head?\n\n\n(A) Bandages\n(B) Dark spectacles\n(C) False nose\n(D) A iron helmet\nAnswer:\n(D) | Explanation: He used bandages, dark spectacles, a false nose, side-whiskers, and a large hat .What was the name of the village Griffin escaped to from London?\n\n\n(A) Iping\n(B) Sussex\n(C) Oxford\n(D) Bristol\nAnswer:\n(A) | Explanation: Griffin took a train to the rural village of Iping .Who was the landlady of the local inn at Iping?\n\n\n(A) Mrs. Pumphrey\n(B) Mrs. Hall\n(C) Mrs. Bunting\n(D) Mrs. Danby\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Mrs. Hall was the innkeeper''s wife .Whose house was burglarized in Iping early in the morning?\n\n\n(A) Constable Jaffers'' house\n(B) The clergyman''s (Rev. Mr. Bunting''s) house\n(C) The doctor''s house\n(D) The mayor''s house\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Griffin stole money from the clergyman''s study desk .What object leapt up and dashed itself into Mrs. Hall''s face in the bedroom?\n\n\n(A) A pillow\n(B) The bedpost hat\n(C) A mirror\n(D) A heavy book\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: The hat on the bedpost sprang up into her face .Why was Constable Bobby Jaffers surprised when he arrived to arrest Griffin?\n\n\n(A) Griffin was armed with a gun\n(B) He had to arrest a man without a head\n(C) The inn was closed\n(D) Griffin had already died\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Griffin had removed his head bandages, revealing a headless body .How did Griffin manage to overpower Constable Jaffers and the villagers?\n\n\n(A) By using a knife\n(B) By stripping off his garments one by one to become invisible\n(C) By locking them in a cellar\n(D) By jumping out of a high window\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: He stripped off his clothes to become an unseen, invisible attacker .What is described as being \"as transparent as a sheet of glass\" in the story?\n\n\n(A) Griffin''s spectacles\n(B) Griffin''s body after taking the drugs\n(C) The window at the inn\n(D) The bottle in Drury Lane\nAnswer:\n(B) | Explanation: Griffin''s body became transparent like glass .What central message does H.G. Wells convey through \"Footprints Without Feet\"?\n\n\n(A) Science should be banned\n(B) Invisibility makes a person powerful and happy\n(C) Scientific power without moral responsibility leads to self-destruction and lawlessness\n(D) Landlords are always wrong\nAnswer:\n(C) | Explanation: The story demonstrates that irresponsible science leads to lawlessness and chaos ."},{"heading":"9. Syllabus Coverage Checklist & Source References & Source ReferencesCoverage Checklist[x] Official RBSE 2026–27 Syllabus Verification & Chapter Inclusion Confirmation .[x] Prescribed NCERT Textbook (Footprints Without Feet) Identification & Author Mapping .[x] Detailed Plot Overview & Core Focus Areas (Griffin''s invisibility, scientific misuse, thefts, conflict, character motivations, consequences of irresponsible science) .[x] Detailed Section-Wise Narrative Breakdown .[x] Themes & Character Sketches (Griffin, Mrs. Hall, Constable Jaffers) .[x] Contextual Vocabulary Glossary .[x] Authentic Past Paper (RBSE 2024 Code S-02) & Model Paper 2026 Integration .[x] Model Answers & Generated Practice Questions suitable for Class 10 .[x] 15-Question MCQ Quiz with Explanations .Source ReferencesNCERT, New Delhi: Footprints Without Feet — Class 10 Supplementary Reader, Chapter 5 (Footprints Without Feet by H.G. Wells) .Board of Secondary Education, Rajasthan (RBSE), Ajmer: Class 10 English Syllabus & Blueprint (Session 2026–27) .RBSE Official Secondary Board Examination Paper 2024: Code S-02-English (SA Q. 17(iii) & Q. 18(ii)) .RBSE Official Secondary Model Examination Paper 2026: secmodel2026.pdf (MCQ Q. 20(i)) .RBSE Official Research Report: Curriculum, Assessment Architecture, and Source Library (2026–2027 Session) .Missing Information NoteIndividual Lesson Mark Allocation: In Section D (Supplementary Reader), the 12 Marks total are distributed across all 9 prescribed lessons of Footprints Without Feet through 5 MCQs, 2 SAQs, and 1 LAQ on the 80-mark final paper, rather than an isolated fixed quota per individual story .💡 Next Steps: Would you like me to prepare the BoardReady learning package for Chapter 6 (The Making of a Scientist by Robert W. Peterson) or Chapter 7 (The Necklace by Guy de Maupassant)?keeppinSave to notekeeppincopythumbupthumbdownCreate the complete BoardReady learning package for RBSE Class 10 English, academic session 2026–27, for the lesson \"The Making of a Scientist\".","content":"Book: Footprints Without Feet\nLesson type: Supplementary Reader\nChapter ID: english-25\nSubject ID: english\nExplanation language: English\nRequired learning focus: Richard Ebright, curiosity, early interests, scientific investigation, mentors, discipline and achievements supported by the text.\nFirst verify that the lesson is included in the current official RBSE syllabus and confirm the prescribed textbook edition. If the lesson is excluded, report that and do not present it as required board content.\nGenerate chapter metadata, a clear overview, detailed section-wise explanations, themes, character analysis or poetic analysis as appropriate, important vocabulary, verified previous-year questions, practice questions, predicted practice questions and a 15-question MCQ quiz.\nUse the prescribed textbook and available official board papers. Do not invent quotations, events, previous-year questions, years or marks. Separate exact past questions from generated practice. Provide model answers and explanations suitable for Class 10. Respect copyright by summarising and explaining rather than reproducing the entire lesson.\nFinish with a syllabus coverage checklist, source references and missing information. Format the output for BoardReady editorial review. If the output is too long, produce one section at a time while preserving the chapter ID. Thoughts expand_more\\n"}]}'::jsonb,
