Q19 (OR). Discuss the origin, spread, and major features of the Non-Cooperation Movement
(1920–1922).',
  'Main Q: 1. Causes: Irwin''s vague Dominion Status offer rejected; Lahore Congress (1929) passed ''Purna',
  'Main Q: 1. Causes: Irwin''s vague Dominion Status offer rejected; Lahore Congress (1929) passed ''Purna
Swaraj'' resolution; Gandhi''s 11 demands including salt tax abolition rejected by Lord Irwin. 2. Dandi March:
On 12 March 1930, Gandhi marched 240 miles from Sabarmati to Dandi with 78 volunteers; broke salt law on
6 April 1930 by manufacturing salt from seawater. 3. Movement Spread: Boycott of foreign cloth, picketing
of liquor shops, refusal of peasants to pay revenue/chaukidari taxes, violation of forest laws. 4. Repression
& Pact: Brutal British crackdown (1,00,000 arrests); Gandhi-Irwin Pact signed in March 1931 suspending
movement for 2nd Round Table Conference. [1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4 Marks]
OR Q: 1. Causes: Rowlatt Act (1919), Jallianwala Bagh Massacre, and Khilafat issue united under Gandhiji''s
leadership at Nagpur Congress (Dec 1920). 2. Town Spread: Students left government schools, lawyers
gave up practice, foreign cloth boycotted and burnt in bonfires; foreign cloth imports halved between
1921–1922. 3. Countryside Spread: Baba Ramchandra led peasant struggle in Awadh against Talukdars;
Alluri Sitarama Raju led tribal armed resistance in Gudem Hills (AP). 4. Withdrawal: Abruptly called off by
Gandhiji in Feb 1922 following the Chauri Chaura violent incident where 22 policemen were burnt alive. [1 + 1
+ 1 + 1 = 4 Marks]',
