) VALUES (
  'social-science-pdf-predicted-20',
  'social-science',
  'social-science-predicted',
  'PDF predicted question bank',
  'Q20. [Geography - 4 Marks] Classify Iron Ore deposits in India into four major belts and
explain their strategic industrial importance.
OR
Q20 (OR). Distinguish between Conventional and Non-Conventional sources of energy with two
examples and arguments for energy conservation.',
  'Main Q: 1. Odisha-Jharkhand Belt: High-grade hematite ore in Badampahar mines (Mayurbhanj/Kendujhar)',
  'Main Q: 1. Odisha-Jharkhand Belt: High-grade hematite ore in Badampahar mines (Mayurbhanj/Kendujhar)
and Gua/Noamundi (Singhbhum). 2. Durg-Bastar-Chandrapur Belt: Very high-grade hematite in Bailadila
hills (Chhattisgarh); exported to Japan and South Korea via Visakhapatnam port. 3.
Ballari-Chitradurga-Chikkamagaluru-Tumakuru Belt: Kudremukh mines in Karnataka (100% export unit
transported as slurry pipeline to Mangaluru port). 4. Maharashtra-Goa Belt: Includes Goa and Ratnagiri
(Maharashtra); ores efficiently exported through Marmagao port. [1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4 Marks]
OR Q: 1. Conventional Sources: Traditional, exhaustible, pollution-causing energy sources used for decades
(e.g., Coal, Petroleum, Natural Gas). 2. Non-Conventional Sources: Renewable, non-polluting,
inexhaustible modern energy sources (e.g., Solar, Wind, Biogas, Tidal energy). 3. Need for Conservation:
Economic growth depends on energy; commercial reserves (coal/oil) are fast depleting. 4. Conservation
Measures: Using public transport, switching off idle electricity, using energy-saving devices (LEDs), and
expanding non-conventional solar/wind plants. [1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4 Marks]

