  'social-science-pdf-predicted-21',
  'social-science',
  'social-science-predicted',
  'PDF predicted question bank',
  'Q21. [Political Science - 4 Marks] Examine the four major forms of Power Sharing in modern
democracies with suitable real-world examples.
OR
Q21 (OR). Discuss the key features of Indian Federalism and explain the division of legislative
powers between Union and State Governments.',
  'Main Q: 1. Horizontal Distribution: Power shared among different organs of government (Legislature,',
  'Main Q: 1. Horizontal Distribution: Power shared among different organs of government (Legislature,
Executive, Judiciary) creating a system of Checks and Balances. 2. Vertical Distribution: Power shared
among governments at different levels (Central/Union, State/Regional, and Local Self-Government). 3. Social
Groups: Power shared among different religious/linguistic groups (e.g., Community Government in Belgium,
Reserved Constituencies in India). 4. Political Parties & Pressure Groups: Competition among parties and
coalition governments (e.g., NDA/INDIA alliances) ensuring power does not remain in one hand. [1 + 1 + 1 +
1 = 4 Marks]
OR Q: 1. Union List: Subjects of national importance (Defence, Foreign Affairs, Banking, Currency); Union
Parliament alone makes laws (97 subjects). 2. State List: Subjects of state/local importance (Police, Trade,
Agriculture, Irrigation); State Legislature alone makes laws (66 subjects). 3. Concurrent List: Subjects of
common interest (Education, Forests, Trade Unions, Marriage); Both Parliament and State can legislate, but
Union law prevails in case of conflict. 4. Residuary Powers: Cyber law, IT, and new subjects arising
post-constitution belong exclusively to the Union Government. [1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4 Marks]',
